window.PhoenixSiteSearchIndex={"generated_at_utc":"2026-07-30T15:22:12.306581Z","count":91,"pages":[{"title":"UFO Report Databases","url":"/ufo-report-databases/","description":"UFO report databases and catalogues are not a single, unified body of evidence.","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"UFO Report Databases UFO Report Databases UFO Report Databases UFO Report Databases Untitled UFO report databases and catalogues are not a single, unified body of evidence. UFO report databases and catalogues are not a single, unified body of evidence. They are a patchwork of witness report repositories, government case files, investigative records, bibliographies and archival finding aids, each built for a different purpose. UFO Report Databases ufo report databases Introduction For most readers, the best starting points are the National UFO Reporting Center for searchable first hand accounts, France’s GEIPAN for publicly documented investigations and classifications, and national archives in the United States, United Kingdom and Canada for historical government records. MUFON and UFOCAT add substantial civilian research material, but access conditions, duplicated entries and uneven documentation complicate large scale analysis. No catalogue, however large, establishes that its reports describe extraordinary craft: a report records an observation or allegation, not a verified explanation. What the main catalogues actually contain The word “database” can conceal important differences. A collection of unverified online submissions is not equivalent to an archive of contemporary government correspondence, while an index pointing to a case file is not the same as a structured dataset ready for statistical analysis. The principal resources fall into four broad types: Public reporting databases , such as the National UFO Reporting Center and MUFON , collect accounts submitted directly by witnesses. Investigated case databases , most notably GEIPAN, publish cases after a defined review and classification process. Historical government archives , including Project Blue Book records and British and Canadian files, preserve documents created by military, intelligence and civil authorities. Research catalogues , such as UFOCAT, bring together references drawn from books, periodicals, investigators’ files and earlier databases. This distinction affects what can reasonably be inferred. A reporting database can reveal what people choose to report, where and when they report it, and how they describe an experience. It cannot, without extensive verification and contextual data, measure how often genuinely unexplained objects occur. The most useful public report databases National UFO Reporting Center The US based National UFO Reporting Center, usually abbreviated to NUFORC , has received reports since 1974. Its public databank allows browsing by event date, location and reported shape, while individual records generally include the time, place, duration, description and date of submission. NUFORC says it has processed more than 180,000 reports over its history, making it one of the largest freely accessible collections of first hand UFO or unidentified anomalous phenomenon accounts. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsThe NUFORC Databank is the largest independently collected set of UFO / UAP sighting reports available on the interne... Its greatest strengths are accessibility and chronological reach. A reader can move quickly from a national or state index to individual narratives, and an interactive map provides a geographical view of the collection. The database is useful for finding clusters of reports, recurring descriptions or accounts associated with a particular date. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org NUFORC Reports by LocationLOCATION, REPORT COUNT. USA Unspecified, 120. USA Alaska, 675. USA Alabama, 1523. USA Arkansas, 1363. U... The principal limitation is self selection. Witnesses decide whether to report, sometimes months or years after an event, and reporting rates are affected by publicity, internet access, local population and awareness of NUFORC. Entries may contain valuable detail, but their presence in the database does not indicate that they have been independently confirmed. Academic researchers using NUFORC data therefore treat it as public reporting data rather than a census of objects in the sky. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov This research uses data from the National UFO Reporting Center (NUFORC) online. NUFORC was formed in 1974...Read more... Mutual UFO Network The Mutual UFO Network, or MUFON, operates a Case Management System that combines witness submissions with the work of volunteer field investigators. Its public website displays recent reports and selected historical case material, but broader access to narratives and attachments in the searchable system is generally a benefit of paid membership. MUFON’s own terms state that active members may access specified parts of the database, including photographs, videos and documents. [MUFON] mufon.com Open source on mufon.com. MUFON is potentially useful when a researcher wants more than a short witness narrative. Some cases may contain interview records, investigator conclusions, images or supporting files. Yet coverage and investigative depth vary because reports are handled across a large volunteer organisation. Restricted access also makes the dataset less transparent and less reproducible than a fully open repository. NUFORC and MUFON should not simply be added together to produce a larger total. The same incident may be submitted to both organisations, copied into later compilations or reported independently by several witnesses. Without case level deduplication, apparent patterns can partly reflect repeated records rather than separate events. GEIPAN’s investigated case model GEIPAN is a unit of France’s national space agency, CNES. Created in 1977, it collects, analyses, archives and publishes information about reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena in France. Unlike a minimally moderated submission archive, its public case database is organised around investigation and an explicit classification system. [CNES] cnes.fr Open source on cnes.fr. Cases are assigned to four main categories: A: the phenomenon has been identified after investigation. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr Source details in endnotes. B: it has probably been identified. C: it remains unidentified because the available information is inadequate. D: it remains unidentified after investigation. The separation of categories C and D is especially important. “Unidentified” can mean either that the evidence is too poor to reach a conclusion or that a comparatively well documented case resisted the available explanations. Combining those outcomes would exaggerate the significance of incomplete reports. As of 25 June 2026, GEIPAN’s published statistics covered 3,368 classified cases. About 28 per cent were class A, 38.8 per cent class B, 30.1 per cent class C and 3.1 per cent class D. These figures are dynamic and change when cases are added or reconsidered. They indicate that most published cases are identified or probably identified, while the proportion remaining unexplained after investigation is relatively small. [Geipan] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. GEIPAN is one of the strongest databases for readers interested in how investigations reach conclusions rather than merely in what witnesses reported. Its limitations include a predominantly French geographical scope, variable evidence quality inherited from the original reports, and the difficulty of comparing its classifications directly with systems used by other organisations. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Historical government catalogues Project Blue Book and the US National Archives Project Blue Book was the United States Air Force’s principal public UFO investigation programme from 1952 until 1969, following the earlier Projects Sign and Grudge. Its records include sighting reports, correspondence, investigative material and analytical documents. The files were later transferred to the US National Archives, where they form one of the most important historical collections for studying official responses to post war UFO reports. [osi.af.mil] osi.af.mil project blue book part 1 ufo reports project blue book part 1 ufo reports The collection is often described through its summary numbers: 12,618 reports were recorded, of which 701 remained classified as unidentified when the programme ended. Those figures should not be read as 701 demonstrations of unusual technology. “Unidentified” meant that Blue Book did not arrive at a satisfactory identification from the information available; it did not specify the cause. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Project Blue Book Project Blue Book Project Blue Book is valuable because many files are contemporary records rather than later retellings. Researchers can examine dates, interview summaries, military routing slips and the reasoning behind proposed explanations. Nevertheless, the programme’s methods and priorities changed over time,"},{"title":"Report Maps","url":"/report-maps/","description":"Maps can reveal where reports concentrate, but population, publicity, database reach, and repeated records can create misleading hotspots.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases","level":2,"text":"Report Maps Do UFO Hotspot Maps Show Real Patterns? UFO Archives Report Maps Report Maps Do UFO Hotspot Maps Show Real Patterns? Maps can reveal where reports concentrate, but population, publicity, database reach, and repeated records can create misleading hotspots. Maps can reveal where reports concentrate, but population, publicity, database reach, and repeated records can create misleading hotspots. UFO Report Databases mechanism theme cluster dataset evidence What Geographic Visualizations Reveal Population and Reporting Access Distinguishing Hotspots From Data Artifacts ufo report databases report maps Introduction UFO hotspot maps show where reports have accumulated, not necessarily where unusual objects occur most often. A dense patch of markers may reflect a large population, good internet access, strong awareness of a particular reporting centre, extensive media coverage or several witnesses submitting separate accounts of the same event. It may also be partly manufactured by the mapping process itself: reports naming only a town can be placed at the same central coordinate, creating an artificial cluster. That does not make report maps useless. They can reveal where a database reaches the public, identify episodes that generated many accounts and help investigators compare reports by time, direction and description. The crucial step is to treat geography as a pattern requiring explanation, rather than as proof of a physical hotspot. NUFORC itself cautions that its global map contains only reports submitted to NUFORC and that city level locations tend to cluster because many witnesses provide no more precise position. [NUFORC] nuforc.org UFO Sightings Map NUFORCDecember 18, 2023 — As NUFORC is a US based organization, the map clearly shows that most of our sighting repor... Published: December 18, 2023 What Geographic Visualisations Reveal A basic UFO map answers a narrow but valuable question: where are the records in this particular collection? It can show concentrations around major cities, coastlines, transport corridors or well known sighting locations. Adding a time filter can reveal whether a concentration built gradually or appeared during one evening. Filters for reported shape, duration or classification can show whether neighbouring markers describe broadly similar observations or merely happen to share a location. These distinctions matter because a place with many unrelated reports is different from a tightly timed group of compatible accounts. NUFORC’s own spatial analysis notes that a high report count at one location is not automatically a meaningful hotspot, particularly when the reported forms differ substantially. Its databank also separates event date from submission and processing, allowing analysts to avoid confusing a burst of late submissions with a burst of observations. [NUFORC] nuforc.org A high number of reports in one spot does not necessarily mean it is a hotspot if there are many different types of shapes reported in th... A map becomes more informative when reports can be compared with possible explanatory data. The available variables might include: population and settlement density; [arxiv.org] arxiv.org Source details in endnotes. airports, military ranges and common flight paths; launch schedules and satellite visibility; astronomical conditions and cloud cover; major roads, tourist areas and outdoor recreation sites; the date a reporting service began operating or became widely publicised. A peer reviewed study of more than 98,000 public US reports, largely drawn from NUFORC, modelled reporting against environmental and social conditions rather than reading marker density at face value. It found that sky visibility and the opportunities people have to observe the sky affect the spatial distribution of reports. The broader lesson is that a report requires both something to be noticed and someone willing and able to record it. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NUFORC discusses hoax and joke reports, which are said to be PubMed Central (PMC)An environmental analysis of public UAP sightings and sky...by RM Medina · 2023 · Cited by 27 — The dependent variab... France’s GEIPAN database illustrates another useful mapping approach. Its public case search connects locations to investigated files and classifications, while its statistics cover classified and published cases rather than every unprocessed approach to the organisation. A GEIPAN backed spatial study modelled French reports using population density, airports, moisture and other local variables. It found that several factors were statistically associated with report intensity, while some residual concentrations remained unexplained by that particular model. Such residuals are research questions, not automatic evidence of extraordinary activity. [Geipan] geipan.fr Distribution of Observation Cases by Type, Dynamic Statistics... Region names were not updated to maintain historical continuity in the... Population Is Only the First Denominator Raw totals nearly always favour places containing more people. Ten thousand observers have more opportunities to notice and report something than one thousand observers, so comparing states, counties or countries by report count alone can be seriously misleading. Population adjusted rates are therefore a better starting point, but even reports per head do not measure equal exposure. People do not spend equal amounts of time looking at dark skies. Urban residents may encounter more aircraft, advertising drones and light reflections but suffer greater light pollution. Rural residents may see clearer skies yet have less awareness of a particular reporting website. Holiday areas can contain many temporary observers who are absent from resident population figures. A military aircrew, astronomer or night shift worker also has a different opportunity to see aerial lights from someone who spends most evenings indoors. The correct denominator depends on the question. Reports per resident might help compare broad regions, whereas reports per hour of observable darkness, per active observer or per aircraft movement would address different mechanisms. Those more precise exposure figures are rarely available in UFO catalogues. Population normalisation should therefore be presented as a partial correction, not a magic conversion from reports into incident rates. The geographic unit also changes the result. A state level map can hide strong variation within states; a city map can exaggerate small municipalities with few residents; county boundaries may divide one metropolitan area into several apparently different zones. Spatial researchers call this sensitivity to the chosen boundaries the modifiable areal unit problem : patterns can strengthen, weaken or move when the same observations are grouped into different areas. Robust hotspot methods consequently test patterns at more than one scale rather than relying on whichever county or postcode map looks most striking. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. Reporting Access Creates Its Own Geography A database map also charts the reach of the organisation collecting the reports. NUFORC openly states that its map is dominated by the United States because it is a US based reporting centre; the United Kingdom and the Netherlands are among the next most represented countries. That distribution should not be mistaken for a global survey conducted with equal coverage, language support and public awareness everywhere. [NUFORC] nuforc.org UFO Sightings Map NUFORCDecember 18, 2023 — As NUFORC is a US based organization, the map clearly shows that most of our sighting repor... Published: December 18, 2023 Access changes over time as well as space. A telephone hotline, English language website, mobile application and government reporting portal each attract different users. Publicity following a news story can introduce thousands of people to a service that they had never previously known existed. Changes in submission forms, moderation practices or database publication schedules can then produce apparent rises or falls that partly reflect the collection system. This creates a basic selection problem: the mapped records represent people who observed something, interpreted it as worth reporting, found a particular organisation, completed its form and had the entry retained or published. People who dismissed an observation, reported it elsewhere or lacked access remain invisible. Comparisons between NUFORC, MUFON , GEIPAN and historical government collections must therefore account for their different constituencies and operating procedures rather than combining their marker totals as though they came from one uniform survey. Publicity can also alter perception before a report is made. The rapid spread of “flying saucer” stories in 1947 was followed by a major surge of similarly framed reports across the United States. Historians and social researchers debate"},{"title":"US Archives","url":"/us-archives/","description":"The US National Archives brings together UFO and UAP records from many agencies, formats, and record groups beyond Project Blue Book.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases","level":2,"text":"US Archives Where America's Government UFO Records Are Kept UFO Archives US Archives US Archives Where America's Government UFO Records Are Kept The US National Archives brings together UFO and UAP records from many agencies, formats, and record groups beyond Project Blue Book. The US National Archives brings together UFO and UAP records from many agencies, formats, and record groups beyond Project Blue Book. UFO Report Databases governance theme cluster entity Records Beyond Project Blue Book Digitized Files and Bulk Metadata How to Navigate Multiple Agencies ufo report databases us archives Introduction The US National Archives does not hold America’s UFO records in one complete, unified file. Project Blue Book is the best known collection, but federal material is dispersed across Air Force, Navy, intelligence, aviation, space, nuclear regulatory, diplomatic and presidential holdings. Records also appear in different forms: investigative case files, memoranda, photographs, radar related material, press briefings, films, audio recordings and later reviews of disputed incidents. The National Archives and Records Administration, or NARA, now provides a central UFO and unidentified anomalous phenomena portal, but that portal is principally a finding aid across existing archival structures. A newer statutory collection, Record Group 615 , is bringing digital copies supplied by federal agencies into one designated location. Even so, researchers must usually search both RG 615 and the older agency record groups in which UFO related records were originally created and preserved. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and...24 Apr 2025 — NARA has records related to unidentified flyi... W. Glenn Dennis Interview, 11/19/1990 Channel: US National Archives Open on YouTube Records Beyond Project Blue Book Project Blue Book remains the most substantial single body of historical US government UFO case material. The Air Force transferred its records to the National Archives in 1975 after the programme ended, and NARA holds administrative files, sanitised case files, photographs, films, sound recordings and related investigative series. Blue Book recorded 12,618 sightings, of which 701 were categorised as unidentified, but those figures describe the programme’s classifications rather than proof of extraordinary vehicles. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK The project closed in 1969 and we have no...Read more... Treating Blue Book as the whole federal archive, however, misses how government responsibilities were divided. Air Force intelligence and investigative offices generated several distinct series alongside the familiar case files. These include Office of Special Investigations records from 1948–1968, reports from the 4602nd Air Intelligence Service Squadron, early Air Intelligence Reports, administrative correspondence and physical artefacts. The later Roswell Report research produced another group of source files, moving images and recorded interviews covering material gathered between the 1940s and 1990s. [National Archives] archives.gov Relating to Unidentified Flying Object, 1986 (FAA—Japan Airlines Flight 1628) (National... Other agencies preserved records because a report touched their own operational responsibilities rather than because they ran a dedicated UFO programme: The Federal Aviation Administration holds a series concerning the widely publicised 1986 Japan Airlines Flight 1628 incident over Alaska. Its archival setting reflects air traffic control, aviation safety and public information responsibilities rather than an Air Force case management system. The Navy appears through records of installations and shore commands, including a 1959 collection filed under an internal subject classification. NASA holdings include mission transcripts, photographs, interviews and recordings in which UFO claims or terminology arose during spaceflight, public affairs or agency activity. CIA and defence records include films, briefings and material created in intelligence or military information contexts. The US Information Agency and Agency for Global Media preserved interviews, broadcasts and overseas facing programmes about UFO controversies. NARA’s own administrative records document how the archives handled public interest in Blue Book and related enquiries. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... This distribution matters because an archival record’s location reveals why the government created or retained it. A pilot report in FAA files may emphasise communications and traffic control data; an intelligence file may focus on foreign perceptions or security implications; a public affairs recording may document what officials said rather than what investigators privately concluded. Searching only for formal “UFO investigations” can therefore omit records filed under aviation incidents, intelligence reports, correspondence, psychological strategy, press relations or technical programmes. Photographic cataloguing requires similar caution. NARA’s UFO pages include genuine sighting photographs, documentation of experimental aircraft and images deliberately fabricated during scientific studies to test photographic interpretation. A catalogue result labelled “flying saucer” may therefore depict an alleged phenomenon, a research exercise, a model, a conventional aircraft or simply an image described using the language of its period. The title is a route into the record, not an authentication verdict. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... 5:00 Maj. Gen. John A. Samford's Statement on \"Flying Saucers\", Pentagon, Washington, DC, 07/31/1952 Channel: US National Archives · Views: 334.6K · Uploaded: September 2013 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube Record Group 615 Changes the Filing System Congress required NARA to establish the Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection through sections 1841–1843 of the 2024 National Defense Authorization Act. The resulting Record Group 615 is intended to contain copies of government, government provided or government funded records relating to UAP, technologies of unknown origin and non human intelligence, excluding temporarily non attributed objects as defined by the legislation. Federal agencies were instructed to review and organise relevant holdings, create digital copies and prepare them for transfer. [National Archives] archives.gov Open source on archives.gov. RG 615 does not simply relocate every original document into a new physical archive. NARA accepts digital copies for the statutory collection, while agencies continue to manage the source records under their normal records retention schedules. Permanent originals may later enter their traditional agency record groups; temporary originals may eventually be destroyed according to approved schedules even though a statutory digital copy has been transmitted to RG 615. This creates a two layer system: a subject based UAP collection for access and an agency based archival structure preserving the records’ administrative provenance. [National Archives] archives.gov Open source on archives.gov. As of NARA’s May 2026 update, RG 615 listed transferred series from the Federal Aviation Administration, Nuclear Regulatory Commission, Office of the Director of National Intelligence, Office of the Secretary of Defense, National Security Agency, Department of State and Federal Bureau of Investigation. The collection is being expanded on a rolling basis, so its present contents should not be read as a definitive list of every agency that holds or has ever created relevant material. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Record Group 615: Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection National Archives National Archives Record Group 615: Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection National Archives The transfer rules also explain why public releases may not be identical to the government’s complete copies. Agencies must review records for access restrictions. Where only part of a record can be disclosed, they are instructed to transfer both a redacted public version and an unredacted version. Records withheld in full can also be transferred as “postponed” material. The existence of an RG 615 series therefore does not necessarily mean that every page, attachment or data field is immediately visible online. [National Archives] archives.gov Open source on archives.gov. NARA’s governance role is important but limited. It receives, preserves, describes and provides access to records; it does not ordinarily reinvestigate the underlying sighting or endorse the originating agency’s"},{"title":"British Files","url":"/british-files/","description":"British UFO records are scattered across departmental series, making search terms, dates, and government provenance essential to finding useful files.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases","level":2,"text":"British Files How to Find UFO Files in Britain's Archives UFO Archives British Files British Files How to Find UFO Files in Britain's Archives British UFO records are scattered across departmental series, making search terms, dates, and government provenance essential to finding useful files. British UFO records are scattered across departmental series, making search terms, dates, and government provenance essential to finding useful files. UFO Report Databases implementation pillar entity Which Departments Created UFO Records Searching the Discovery Catalogue Reading Files in Their Administrative Context ufo report databases british files Introduction Britain’s official UFO records are not stored in one complete “UFO archive”. They are scattered across files created by the Ministry of Defence, the Air Ministry and Royal Air Force, the Foreign Office, the Meteorological Office and related government bodies. The most effective way to find them is therefore to search The National Archives’ Discovery catalogue by department code, date and historically appropriate terminology , rather than relying on a single search for “UFO”. The National Archives recommends concentrating on the record departments DEFE, AIR, FCO and BJ , while trying terms such as “UFO”, “ unidentified flying”, “flying saucer” and “saucers”. Researchers should then open the catalogue hierarchy around each result to see which office created the file, what administrative series it belongs to and whether it records sightings, policy, parliamentary correspondence, intelligence assessment or meteorological explanation. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National ArchivesSearch Discovery, our catalogue in DEFE, AIR, FCO and BJ for records of UFOs, using keywords... Which Departments Created UFO Records? The departmental code at the start of a British archive reference is not merely a shelf label. It identifies the government organisation responsible for creating, using or inheriting the record. That provenance is essential because two files containing similar sighting reports may have served very different purposes. DEFE: Ministry of Defence policy and case handling DEFE is the principal code for Ministry of Defence records. It contains much of the surviving correspondence associated with the department’s UFO responsibilities, including sighting reports, briefing papers, parliamentary questions, public letters, internal policy discussions and records of how officials handled press and Freedom of Information enquiries. The files are distributed among several DEFE series rather than assembled as a single collection. DEFE 24 , for example, contains registered files produced by Defence Secretariat branches and their predecessors. Catalogue entries include subjects such as UFO reports, parliamentary correspondence, Air Defence Ground Environment reports and policy discussions. DEFE 31 contains intelligence related material, including files catalogued as “UFO policy” and “UFO incidents”. [Discovery Archive] discovery.nationalarchives.gov.uk Immediate source of acquisition: From 2003 Ministry of Defence... Selection and destruction information: Collection theme 2.2.1.3... This distinction matters. A DEFE 24 file may reveal how a secretariat answered a minister or member of the public, whereas a DEFE 31 file may show how defence intelligence assessed whether reports had military significance. Neither category should automatically be treated as a scientific investigation. The office function, correspondence trail and stated purpose of the file determine what its contents can establish. The best known example is the Rendlesham Forest material. Relevant documents appear in more than one file, including a Ministry of Defence memorandum in DEFE 24/1948 and parliamentary or public correspondence in nearby DEFE 24 files. Searching only for “Rendlesham” may find the prominently catalogued document, but browsing the surrounding series can reveal how the incident was subsequently discussed, copied and reframed within government. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk defe 241948 defe 241948 AIR: the Air Ministry and Royal Air Force AIR covers records of the Air Ministry and Royal Air Force. It is especially important for early official correspondence, reports involving RAF stations, air defence concerns and material created before the unified Ministry of Defence assumed its later form. One prominent catalogue reference is AIR 2/17318 , which includes correspondence concerning UFO sightings and early public claims about vehicles from other planets. The National Archives uses the file as an example of the letters reaching the Air Ministry during the 1960s. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk ufo reports ufo reports AIR files can be difficult to locate through modern vocabulary alone. Contemporary officials might have used “flying saucer”, “unidentified object”, “aerial phenomenon”, “unusual light” or a description tied to a location or RAF station. A researcher investigating a particular event should therefore combine subject terms with the place, year, airfield, squadron or command involved. AIR also contains vast quantities of unrelated aviation material, so an unrestricted search can produce false matches. “Saucer”, for instance, may appear in descriptions having nothing to do with sightings, while searches for aircraft types can return accident reports and operational records. Limiting the date range and examining the catalogue hierarchy helps distinguish a genuine UFO related file from an accidental keyword match. FCO: overseas reports and diplomatic correspondence FCO represents the Foreign and Commonwealth Office and its predecessors. UFO material appears here when a report reached the British government through an embassy, consulate, overseas administration or foreign government rather than through a domestic defence channel. These records can provide a different perspective from DEFE files. A diplomatic post might forward a local newspaper report, ask London how to answer an enquiry, transmit information from another government or record public concern about an event abroad. The file’s purpose may consequently be diplomatic reporting or correspondence management rather than investigation of the sighting itself. FCO searches are most productive when paired with a country, city, embassy or approximate year. Searching “UFO” across the entire department may retrieve only descriptions in which cataloguers used that exact word. Searching for “flying saucer”, “unidentified flying”, “aerial phenomenon” or a known location can expose material described in older or less standardised language. The National Archives specifically identifies FCO as one of the departmental groups in which UFO records should be sought. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National ArchivesSearch Discovery, our catalogue in DEFE, AIR, FCO and BJ for records of UFOs, using keywords... BJ: Meteorological Office explanations and observations BJ is the departmental code for Meteorological Office records. Its presence in the UFO research guide is a reminder that unidentified objects were often considered alongside weather balloons, unusual clouds, atmospheric optics, meteors and other natural or instrument related phenomena. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National ArchivesSearch Discovery, our catalogue in DEFE, AIR, FCO and BJ for records of UFOs, using keywords... Meteorological records may not resemble conventional UFO case files. They can include technical observations, correspondence about possible explanations or documentation created because defence officials consulted weather specialists. This makes BJ valuable when testing an interpretation rather than merely collecting dramatic witness accounts. A report of a luminous object, for example, may appear in a defence correspondence file while relevant balloon launches or atmospheric conditions are recorded elsewhere. Discovery will not automatically connect those records. The researcher must search across departments using the event’s date, place and descriptive details. 8:37 UFO file release February 2010 Channel: The National Archives UK · Views: 454.7K · Uploaded: February 2010 · Length: 8 minutes Open on YouTube Searching the Discovery Catalogue Discovery includes descriptions of records held by The National Archives and thousands of other archives. A broad keyword search therefore covers much more than central government UFO files. Selecting “Held by The National Archives” prevents local collections, personal papers and institutional archives from overwhelming the results when the goal is to study official British government provenance. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives Searching for records using Discovery Type in the name of a government"},{"title":"MUFON","url":"/mufon/","description":"MUFON cases may include interviews, images, and investigator findings, although paid access and uneven volunteer work limit transparency.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases","level":2,"text":"MUFON How Much Evidence Does a MUFON Case Hold? UFO Archives MUFON MUFON How Much Evidence Does a MUFON Case Hold? MUFON cases may include interviews, images, and investigator findings, although paid access and uneven volunteer work limit transparency. MUFON cases may include interviews, images, and investigator findings, although paid access and uneven volunteer work limit transparency. UFO Report Databases implementation pillar entity From Witness Submission to Field Investigation What Members Can Access Why Case Quality Varies ufo report databases mufon Introduction A MUFON case can hold much more than a witness’s original account. The Mutual UFO Network’s Case Management System may combine a submitted narrative with photographs, video, drawings, documents, follow up interviews, measurements, investigator notes and a final assessment. That makes MUFON distinct from catalogues that function mainly as public collections of unexamined reports. MUFON attempts to turn a submission into an investigated case file. [MUFON] mufon.com Search Database Terms and ConditionsThe Mutual UFO Network (MUFON) allows active members to access certain parts of our Case Managem... The difficulty is that much of this added material is not openly available. MUFON reserves searchable access to substantial parts of the database for paying members, while investigator only information is more tightly restricted. Outsiders may therefore see that a case exists without being able to inspect the evidence, reasoning or correspondence behind its classification. The system can preserve unusually rich civilian casework, but restricted access and uneven volunteer practice make its conclusions harder to audit than those of a fully public archive. [MUFON] mufon.com MUFON: HomeRead and Search MUFON Case Management System Reports; Monthly MUFON e Journal... Access to The Observer Network, MUFON's... MUFON MEMBER ACCESS TO OUR 145,000 CASE DATABASE Channel: Mutual UFO Network (MUFON) Open on YouTube From Witness Submission to Field Investigation A MUFON case begins with information supplied by a member of the public. The reporting form asks for the circumstances of the observation and allows witnesses to attach supporting material such as photographs, video, drawings and documents. MUFON says personal information entered in the report text is treated as confidential, although its attachment notice warns that anonymity cannot be guaranteed when uploaded files themselves contain identifying details. [MUFON] mufon.com CMS IFO Info Personal information in the text of the report you submit is confidential.... The launch vehicle itself has also resulted in reports whe... The resulting entry is not necessarily the finished case. MUFON’s stated procedure is to assign reports to trained field investigators, who contact witnesses and seek details that may not have been captured adequately in the online form. A recent MUFON analysis describes investigators as being expected to establish contact within 72 hours, although actual response times will depend on staffing and local conditions. [MUFON] mufon.com Witnesses submit detailed reports through the MUFON CMS, after which each case is assigned to a trained field investigator who is require... The follow up can add several layers of evidence: A witness interview , allowing the investigator to clarify chronology, viewing direction, apparent movement and uncertainties in the original account. Basic angular and positional estimates , including elevation, azimuth, apparent size, brightness and possible distance. Environmental checks , such as the weather, astronomical objects, aircraft activity, satellites, launches or other events visible at the relevant time and place. Media examination , including review of image metadata, visual artefacts or ordinary objects that may account for what appears in a photograph. Site work , where appropriate, involving an inspection of the reported location, sight lines or claimed physical traces. A written finding , recording the investigator’s reasoning and proposed case disposition. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific MethodA MUFON Field Investigator then interviews the witnesses who made the report and collects additional informatio... MUFON explicitly directs investigators to consider mistaken identification before treating a report as unexplained. Its own discussion of method lists aircraft, drones, astronomical objects, Chinese lanterns and reflected light among the common sources of apparently anomalous sightings. This is important because the word “investigated” does not mean that a case has been validated as extraordinary. In many instances, investigation is the process through which an initially puzzling report is given an ordinary explanation. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific MethodA MUFON Field Investigator then interviews the witnesses who made the report and collects additional informatio... When no satisfactory identification is reached, the file may retain an unknown or unexplained disposition. Such a label is best understood as a statement about the investigation’s result, not the nature of the object. It may indicate a genuinely unusual observation, but it can also reflect missing data, a delayed report, an unavailable witness, insufficient imagery or an investigator’s inability to distinguish among several plausible explanations. What Evidence Can Accumulate in One Case? The strongest MUFON files are potentially richer than the short narratives visible in many UFO reporting catalogues. A well developed case can preserve the original submission, subsequent witness answers and the investigator’s attempt to reconstruct the observation. MUFON’s database terms specifically refer to narratives, photographs, videos, Microsoft Word documents and PDF files, while the organisation’s historical description says that investigator reports and witness statements are stored together in the Case Management System. [MUFON] mufon.com Search Database Terms and ConditionsThe Mutual UFO Network (MUFON) allows active members to access certain parts of our Case Managem... That combination matters because the most useful evidence is often not the striking image shown in isolation. It is the surrounding record: when the original file was obtained, whether it was compressed or edited, where the camera was pointing, whether other witnesses were interviewed separately, what aircraft or astronomical objects were present, and why an investigator accepted or rejected a proposed identification. A case with several independent observations, original media, precise timing and a documented elimination process may therefore offer considerably more analytical value than a vivid but unsupported story. Conversely, a long case file is not automatically a strong one. Repeated correspondence, speculative interpretation and derivative images can increase its size without improving its evidential value. This distinction is easily lost when researchers work only from headline fields or case summaries. MUFON’s archive reportedly contained more than 140,000 witness statements and case reports by 2024, but aggregate size says little about how many entries have complete interviews, usable attachments or reproducible findings. The database is a mixture of submissions and investigative records rather than a uniformly measured scientific dataset. [Guinness World Records] guinnessworldrecords.com Guinness World RecordsLargest civilian UFO investigative groupThe reports these investigators write, as well as witness statements collec... Older records add another complication. MUFON materials describe the Case Management System as a major improvement over earlier paper based handling, and the organisation has worked to incorporate reports created before the online system existed. Digitised historical entries may preserve valuable information, but they cannot necessarily reproduce missing attachments, undocumented investigative decisions or fields that were never recorded in a standardised form. [MUFON] mufon.com A Brief History of Mufon A Brief History of Mufon 48:03 MUFON Unmasks the Truth! UFO's Over Earth Discovery Channel Channel: DiscoveryChannelInd · Views: 540.0K · Uploaded: May 2025 · Length: 48 minutes Open on YouTube What Members Can Access MUFON does not operate its full case archive as a completely open public database. Its terms state that active members may access specified portions of the Case Management System, including report narratives and attached photographs, videos and documents. The organisation describes this access as a privilege of paid membership and places copyright restrictions on copying or redistributing the material. [MUFON] mufon.com Search Database Terms and ConditionsThe Mutual UFO Network (MUFON) allows active members to access certain parts of our Case Managem... MUFON currently advertises the ability to read and search Case Management System reports as a membership benefit. Its Enhanced membership is"},{"title":"Search by Date","url":"/search-by-date/","description":"A strong date-based search checks public reports, investigated cases, government files, and local context without assuming matching entries are independent.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases","level":2,"text":"Search by Date How to Trace UFO Reports From One Night UFO Archives Search by Date Search by Date How to Trace UFO Reports From One Night A strong date based search checks public reports, investigated cases, government files, and local context without assuming matching entries are independent. A strong date based search checks public reports, investigated cases, government files, and local context without assuming matching entries are independent. UFO Report Databases implementation decision cluster population context Start With Date and Location Indexes Cross Check Archives and Case Databases Separate Corroboration From Duplication ufo report databases search by date Introduction Researching a UFO sighting from a particular night is less about finding one dramatic database entry than reconstructing what was visible, reported and recorded at a specific place and time. Begin with the event date, local time and location, then search public report indexes for nearby accounts. Next, compare those entries with investigated cases, government files, astronomical records, aircraft activity, meteor reports, satellite re entries, weather and contemporary local news. The crucial discipline is to distinguish matching records from independent corroboration . The same witness statement may be copied between catalogues, summarised in a newspaper and later entered into a historical database. Conversely, several separately filed reports may describe one meteor, aircraft formation or satellite train. A high report count therefore does not automatically mean that many independent objects or events were observed. The aim is to build a dated evidence file whose sources, witnesses and possible relationships are explicit. Start With Date and Location Indexes A date search works best when it begins with a precise search frame rather than a broad query such as “UFOs in 1997”. Record the following before opening any catalogue: 1. Calendar date and approximate local time 2. Town, county, state, province or nearest landmark 3. Direction faced and apparent direction of travel 4. Duration 5. Witness location or route 6. Whether the date is exact, estimated or recalled years later These details determine which records can genuinely be compared. A sighting at 11.50 pm may be filed under the following day after conversion to Coordinated Universal Time, commonly abbreviated to UTC. A witness travelling across a time zone boundary may also give a local time that does not match an aviation, meteor or satellite record. Searches should therefore cover at least the reported date, the adjoining calendar dates and a reasonable time window on either side. Search both occurrence and submission dates The National UFO Reporting Center, or NUFORC , is a useful first stop because its public databank provides indexes and individual first hand reports. Its reporting form distinguishes the date and time of the sighting from the details supplied when the account is submitted. Individual records likewise display an “occurred” time and a separate “reported” time. One NUFORC entry, for example, records an event on 29 November 2006 that was not submitted until 12 February 2008. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsThe NUFORC Databank is the largest independently collected set of UFO / UAP sighting repor... That distinction matters. Searching only the date on which a story appeared online may miss an event that happened months or decades earlier. Conversely, a burst of submissions after a television programme or local news report may create the appearance of a dated cluster even though the underlying sightings occurred at different times. Use several forms of the date when searching general websites and digitised archives: 14 March 1986 UFO \"14 March 1986\" lights sky \"March 14, 1986\" unidentified object town name plus date [aerospace.org] aerospace.org Source details in endnotes. county or neighbouring towns plus date date plus descriptive terms such as fireball , formation , hovering light or aircraft Older reports may use “flying saucer”, “mysterious lights”, “strange aircraft” or “aerial phenomenon” rather than UFO or unidentified anomalous phenomenon. Expand geographically before expanding thematically NUFORC offers location indexes and a map, but a phenomenon visible high in the sky may generate reports tens or hundreds of kilometres apart. Its location index is therefore better treated as an entry point than as a hard boundary. Begin with the named town, then inspect neighbouring counties, states or regions during the same time window. [NUFORC] nuforc.org NUFORC Reports by LocationLOCATION, REPORT COUNT. USA Unspecified, 120. USA Alaska, 676. USA Alabama, 1529. USA Arkansas, 1... The likely search radius depends on the description. A low, apparently nearby object may warrant a tight local search. A brilliant meteor, rocket re entry or high altitude plume can be visible across several regions. The American Meteor Society’s event catalogue demonstrates this scale by grouping geographically separated witness reports into common events, with dates, local and UTC times, duration, brightness and other observations. [American Meteor Society] fireball.amsmeteors.org American Meteor SocietyAmerican Meteor Society Fireball reportsReports found: 3615 in the last 30 days. Date & Time Local Date & Time C... Do not reject a possible match solely because witnesses named different locations. Their entries usually record where they stood, not where the phenomenon physically was. Cross Check Archives and Case Databases Once public reports have supplied candidate entries, move to sources that may contain investigation notes, official correspondence or contemporary documentation. Each type answers a different question: a witness database establishes that someone filed an account; an investigation file may show what checks were performed; an archive may establish what an authority knew at the time. Look for investigated versions of the case France’s GEIPAN , part of the national space agency CNES, collects, investigates, classifies, publishes and archives reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena. Published files may contain anonymised testimony, supporting documents and an investigative conclusion rather than only a short narrative. GEIPAN assigns a case number once a report is formally recorded, which makes that identifier valuable for tracking later references to the same material. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr in our investigation database.Read more... When a public report appears to match a GEIPAN or other investigated case, compare: exact time and municipality; witness number and occupations; duration and apparent motion; drawings, photographs or maps; investigative correspondence; final classification and its stated basis. Do not rely on the classification label alone. Read the accompanying reasoning to see whether the explanation rests on strong physical or positional evidence, a plausible but unconfirmed identification, or insufficient information. NUFORC also publishes selected investigation reports. These can distinguish the incident time from later interviews and fieldwork, helping the researcher separate original observation from retrospective interpretation. [NUFORC] nuforc.org recent investigation reports recent investigation reports 2:12 UFO Project Blue Book at National Archives Museum Channel: US National Archives · Views: 352.7K · Uploaded: December 2019 · Length: 2 minutes 12 seconds Open on YouTube Search government records by date range and agency For United States cases from 1947 to 1969, Project Blue Book records are an obvious comparison source. The US National Archives holds the project’s textual records and directs researchers to associated files, while the FBI Vault provides a separate collection of Project Blue Book material. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National Archives Project BLUE BOOK A date based archive search should include more than the word “UFO”. Try the date alongside: Air Force base or airport names; radar station names; military unit or command; “unidentified aircraft”; “unknown track”; “aerial object”; “intelligence report”; “operations log”; “incident report”. Government files are often arranged by department, series or administrative period rather than by sighting date. The relevant document may therefore sit inside a monthly correspondence file, intelligence summary or policy folder whose catalogue description never mentions the town or incident. The US National Archives now groups UAP related holdings across multiple record groups rather than treating Project Blue Book as the entire historical collection. Its topic pages also identify photographs, film, sound recordings and presidential library material, some of which is digitised and some of which requires further archival work. [National Archives] archives.gov Open source on archives.gov. For British sightings, the"},{"title":"Reporting Bias","url":"/reporting-bias/","description":"Public UFO databases measure who chooses to report and under what conditions, not the true frequency of unexplained objects.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases","level":2,"text":"Reporting Bias Why More UFO Reports Do Not Mean More UFOs UFO Archives Reporting Bias Reporting Bias Why More UFO Reports Do Not Mean More UFOs Public UFO databases measure who chooses to report and under what conditions, not the true frequency of unexplained objects. Public UFO databases measure who chooses to report and under what conditions, not the true frequency of unexplained objects. UFO Report Databases mechanism theme cluster mechanism Who Decides to File a Report Publicity, Population, and Internet Effects Safer Ways to Interpret Report Counts ufo report databases reporting bias Introduction A rise in UFO reports does not necessarily mean that more unusual objects are appearing in the sky. Public databases such as the National UFO Reporting Center record reports made by people who noticed something, decided it was sufficiently unusual, knew where to submit an account and were willing to do so. Everyone else—including witnesses who dismissed an event, feared ridicule, lacked internet access or never heard of the database—is absent. This is self selection bias: inclusion depends partly on the witness’s own choices and circumstances rather than on a neutral system observing every event. Report totals therefore measure a chain of human behaviour as well as activity in the sky. They can reveal when and where people file accounts, what attracts attention and how descriptions change, but they cannot be treated as a direct count of unexplained objects. Research using large UFO datasets has found patterns associated with media exposure, reporting delays, internet availability, population and opportunities to see the sky, all of which can alter the database without any corresponding change in the underlying phenomenon. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com In this study, we used data from 80,332 cases of UFO sightings reported from 1906 to 2014 as a tool...Read more... Who Decides to File a Report A sighting enters a public database only after several informal filters have already operated. A person must be outdoors or near a window, look in the right direction, notice the object, judge it to be unusual, remember enough detail, find a reporting channel and decide that submitting an account is worth the effort. Each stage removes potential observations from the record. The result is not a random sample of the population. People with an existing interest in UFOs may be more likely to recognise the name of a reporting organisation, spend time completing a form or interpret an ambiguous light as worthy of documentation. Conversely, professional observers may decide not to report because they fear reputational damage. People who quickly identify an aircraft, planet or satellite normally have no reason to submit anything, while those left uncertain are disproportionately represented. The database is therefore selected not merely by who sees the sky, but by who remains puzzled and motivated afterwards. That distinction matters because the population of reporters may differ systematically from the population of witnesses. A study involving 206 participants who said they had or had not seen an unidentified anomalous phenomenon found that only 28 per cent of those reporting a sighting experience had told any organisation or authority, and only 14 per cent had used a specialist UFO reporting organisation. The authors identified stigma and the perceived absence of a legitimate reporting route as important barriers. The sample was not designed to estimate a national reporting rate, but it demonstrates how many claimed observations may never reach the catalogues most often used for statistical analysis. [Cardiff Met Research Explorer] pure.cardiffmet.ac.uk Cardiff Met Research Explorer Who sees UFO’s? The relationship between unidentified anomalous phenomena sightings and personality factors Cardiff Metropolitan University Research Ex... NASA’s independent UAP study reached a similar conclusion from an institutional perspective. It described the negative perception surrounding UAP reporting as an obstacle to data collection and said that stigma almost certainly causes data attrition. It also noted that civilian reporting lacks a single standardised system, producing records that may be sparse, incomplete or weakly vetted. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science Independent Study Team Report NASA ScienceIndependent Study Team ReportSeptember 13, 2023 — NASA's very involvement in UAP will play a vital role in reducing stigma as... Published: September 13, 2023 Self selection can work in both directions: Under reporting occurs when witnesses fear ridicule, professional consequences or unwanted attention, or simply regard the experience as too trivial to record. Over representation of highly motivated witnesses occurs when people with strong interest, concern or certainty are more likely to complete a submission. Selective reporting of ambiguous events occurs because clearly identified objects usually disappear from the reporting pool before a form is opened. Repeat participation may occur when enthusiasts submit several observations, meaning that a count of reports is not necessarily a count of independent observers. Channel selection occurs when different groups choose different destinations—civilian databases, police, aviation authorities, social media or no formal channel at all. These mechanisms do not prove that individual reports are mistaken. They show why the collection as a whole cannot automatically be generalised to the public or treated as a complete record of aerial events. Publicity, Population and Internet Effects Media attention changes reporting behaviour Publicity can increase report totals by changing what people notice, how they interpret it and whether they remember an older experience. A statistical study of more than 80,000 UFO records from 1906 to 2014 examined the delay between the reported event and the date on which the account was filed. It found that new submissions were sensitive to media broadcasting and to ordinary daily activity patterns. This supports the view that a surge in database entries may partly be a surge in attention and willingness to report, rather than a surge in contemporaneous sightings. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com In this study, we used data from 80,332 cases of UFO sightings reported from 1906 to 2014 as a tool...Read more... Publicity can affect current and retrospective reports differently. After a documentary, news story or official hearing, some people may look more carefully at the sky and submit new observations quickly. Others may decide that an event from years earlier now seems relevant or socially acceptable enough to report. When those accounts are plotted by the date of the alleged sighting rather than the submission date, the later publicity that prompted their appearance can become invisible. This creates a basic analytical hazard: event date and report date answer different questions. Event date concerns when the witness says the observation occurred. Submission date concerns when that person chose to enter the reporting system. Combining them without examining the time lag can make historical patterns appear more complete and contemporaneous than they really are. Media effects should not be assumed to explain every increase. One study examining whether social conditions during the COVID 19 pandemic were associated with reporting found that reports rose in 2020 but had little or no relationship with the pandemic measures tested. The authors also highlighted the introduction of Starlink satellite trains as a competing explanation for some reports. This is a useful warning against replacing one simplistic interpretation—“more reports mean more UFOs”—with another—“all increases are caused by publicity”. Reporting totals can change because of several mechanisms at once, including new objects in the sky, altered routines, greater attention and easier submission. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate(PDF) Social factors and UFO reports: was the SARS Co V Research Gate(PDF) Social factors and UFO reports: was the SARS Co V More people create more opportunities to report Raw maps of UFO reports tend to give large, populous areas a visual advantage. More residents mean more potential observers, more journeys at night, more outdoor activity and more chances for an ambiguous object to be noticed. A county with 1,000 reports is not necessarily more unusual than one with 100 if its population and observation opportunities are ten times larger. Researchers analysing NUFORC reports have therefore used population standardised rates rather than raw totals. A 2023 environmental study modelled reports from 2001 to 2020 while accounting for population and factors affecting the view of the sky, including cloud cover, light pollution, tree cover, airports and military installations. Its approach reflects a central principle: report density must be separated, as far as possible, from the"},{"title":"Canada Files","url":"/canada-files/","description":"Canada's historical UFO records offer a separate national trail of military and civil documentation for comparing official reporting practices.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases","level":2,"text":"Canada Files What Canada's UFO Archives Preserve Canada Files Canada Files What Canada's UFO Archives Preserve Canada's historical UFO records offer a separate national trail of military and civil documentation for comparing official reporting practices. Canada's historical UFO records offer a separate national trail of military and civil documentation for comparing official reporting practices. UFO Report Databases historical comparative pillar entity Military and Civilian Record Sources How Canadian Files Are Organized Comparisons With US and British Archives ufo report databases canada files Introduction Canada’s government UFO archives preserve a national record of how military, police, transport and scientific agencies handled reports of unusual objects in the sky. Rather than forming one continuous investigation programme, the files document a shifting administrative system: early Cold War studies, military and police inquiries, civil aviation reports, scientific assessments and, eventually, the transfer of surviving material into public archives. Library and Archives Canada provides online access to approximately 9,500 digitised documents created between 1947 and the early 1980s. The wider collection is estimated at about 15,000 pages and draws principally from the Department of National Defence, Department of Transport, National Research Council and Royal Canadian Mounted Police. Its value lies less in proving extraordinary explanations than in showing who reported incidents, which agencies became involved, what evidence was collected and how official responsibility changed over time. [canada.ca] canada.ca Canada's UFOs: The search for the unknown2 Mar 2026 — The Library and Archives Canada collection of government records on UFOs was... Military and Civilian Record Sources Canada’s UFO holdings reflect the country’s dispersed approach to aerial anomaly reporting. Different departments encountered reports for different operational reasons, so the surviving archive contains several overlapping kinds of record rather than one standardised national case file. Department of National Defence records include correspondence, intelligence assessments, military sighting reports, reporting procedures and files associated with investigations by the Royal Canadian Air Force and other defence organisations. Defence interest was generally practical: an unidentified object might be an aircraft, balloon, meteorological event, foreign platform or other occurrence relevant to airspace security. The presence of a military document therefore indicates that a report entered a defence channel, not that officials accepted an exotic interpretation. Department of Transport records include material associated with civil aviation, telecommunications and Project Magnet . Established in 1950 under Department of Transport engineer Wilbert Smith, Project Magnet examined whether reports of flying saucers might reveal useful information about geomagnetism or propulsion. It was officially authorised but remained small and was closely associated with Smith’s personal theories and initiative rather than a broad, sustained departmental programme. [utoronto.ca] utias.utoronto.ca U of T Aerospace Studies History Wilbert Smith, who headed up Project Magnet in 1950.Read more... National Research Council records became especially important after responsibility for receiving UFO reports was transferred from defence authorities in 1967. The NRC collected information from the public, police, government departments and municipalities, often filing it as “Non Meteoritic Sightings”. Astronomer Peter Millman and other scientific staff frequently suggested conventional possibilities such as planets, meteors, stars or optical effects. The NRC continued receiving reports until 1995, when it stopped collecting them and transferred the associated material to the national archives. [ISED Canada] ised isde.canada.ca report sky canada project Defence, the responsibility for overseeing UFO reports was transferred to the NRC, which then became...Read more... Royal Canadian Mounted Police records often supply the ground level investigative detail missing from higher level correspondence. RCMP officers interviewed witnesses, checked locations, forwarded reports and liaised with military or scientific departments. Their files can include witness statements, sketches, observations by officers and notes about physical traces. These records are particularly useful because they show how an unusual report was handled before later retellings altered or simplified it. The result is an archive built from administrative pathways rather than from a single investigative philosophy. A report might begin with a civilian witness, pass through local police, reach the military and end in an NRC scientific file. Another might remain only as correspondence within one department. That unevenness is central to understanding the collection. 1:14:36 UFOs Part 1 — Canadian Reports, Research & Disclosure Channel: Curiouscast Podcasts · Views: 89 · Uploaded: January 2024 · Length: 1 hour 14 minutes Open on YouTube Two Early Projects, Two Different Purposes Canada’s best known official initiatives were Project Magnet and Project Second Storey , both active during the early 1950s. They are often grouped together, but they were structurally and intellectually different. Project Magnet was driven by Wilbert Smith’s belief that UFO reports might contain information relevant to geomagnetic propulsion. With Department of Transport approval, Smith used government facilities and established an observation station at Shirley’s Bay near Ottawa. The project reviewed sightings and attempted to detect measurable physical effects, but its modest resources, reliance on Smith and increasingly speculative conclusions limited its institutional standing. Formal government support ended in 1954, although Smith continued related work privately. [wikipedia.org] Wikipedia Project Magnet (Canada Project Magnet (Canada Project Second Storey was an interdepartmental committee sponsored by the Defence Research Board. It brought together scientific and military personnel to consider reports, discuss classification methods and advise the government on whether a larger investigation was justified. Its emphasis was administrative and evaluative: collecting, cataloguing and correlating reports, then determining whether the subject warranted further official action. The committee concluded that a large scale programme was not justified and ceased operating in 1954. [utoronto.ca] utias.utoronto.ca 1960s dr gordon patterson establishes the utias ufo project 1960s dr gordon patterson establishes the utias ufo project These projects reveal an important feature of the Canadian archive. Official interest did not necessarily mean agreement about what UFOs were. Some participants saw a possible scientific puzzle; others treated reports as an air defence or intelligence concern; still others regarded them primarily as mistaken observations requiring routine explanation. The files preserve those disagreements rather than a single government conclusion. Nor did the closure of the two projects end federal documentation. Defence departments continued receiving and sometimes investigating reports, while the NRC later became the main civilian destination. Canada’s record is therefore better understood as a succession of changing responsibilities than as one programme comparable in scale or continuity to the United States Air Force’s Project Blue Book . [ISED Canada] ised isde.canada.ca report sky canada project Defence, the responsibility for overseeing UFO reports was transferred to the NRC, which then became...Read more... 47:59 The Shag Harbour UFO Incident Full Documentary Channel: Ocean Digital Entertainment · Views: 530.1K · Uploaded: September 2015 · Length: 47 minutes Open on YouTube What the Case Files Actually Preserve The Canadian online collection contains correspondence, memoranda, reports, procedures, forms and documents concerning individual sightings. Some records identify a clear place and date; others discuss policy or reporting arrangements without referring to one event. Only about half contain a specific sighting location, and inconsistent original dating means that searches by place or event date can miss relevant documents. [Canada] canada.ca Canada's UFOs: The search for the unknown2 Mar 2026 — The Library and Archives Canada collection of government records on UFOs was... Shag Harbour The Shag Harbour incident of 4 October 1967 is one of the strongest examples of how different official sources can converge around a single report. Witnesses near Shag Harbour, Nova Scotia, described lights descending towards or entering the water. The RCMP, Canadian Forces and maritime authorities became involved because the initial concern was that an aircraft might have crashed. Library and Archives Canada identifies the episode as the country’s"},{"title":"Database Comparison","url":"/database-comparison/","description":"Database totals and unexplained rates cannot be compared fairly until differences in intake, geography, investigation, classification, and access are","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases","level":2,"text":"Database Comparison Which UFO Database Best Fits Your Question? UFO Archives Database Comparison Database Comparison Which UFO Database Best Fits Your Question? Database totals and unexplained rates cannot be compared fairly until differences in intake, geography, investigation, classification, and access are Database totals and unexplained rates cannot be compared fairly until differences in intake, geography, investigation, classification, and access are UFO Report Databases historical comparative decision cluster population context Public Reports, Investigations, and Archives Why Totals Are Not Directly Comparable Matching a Database to Research Goals ufo report databases database comparison Introduction UFO databases cannot be ranked fairly by headline totals or by the percentage of cases labelled “unexplained”. A public reporting site, an investigative agency and a historical archive are counting different things. One may record every submitted narrative, another may publish only cases that have passed through a formal inquiry, while a third preserves documents created under a discontinued government programme. Their totals therefore describe the reach and rules of the repository, not the underlying frequency of unusual objects. The right database depends on the question. The National UFO Reporting Center, or NUFORC, is well suited to studying public reporting patterns and witness language. France’s GEIPAN is stronger for examining documented investigations and classification decisions. Project Blue Book and national archives are most useful for reconstructing how governments handled reports in particular periods. MUFON and UFOCAT offer extensive civilian research material, but access restrictions, uneven documentation and deliberate duplication make their headline counts especially easy to misread. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsThe NUFORC Databank is the largest independently collected set of UFO / UAP sighting repor... Public Reports, Investigations and Archives The first comparison to make is not between database sizes, but between their units of collection . Is one row a witness submission, an investigated incident, a source reference or an archival file? Those categories may look similar in a search result, yet they support very different conclusions. NUFORC records public submissions. Its reporting form asks witnesses for the event date, time, location, duration, number of witnesses, description and supporting media. Its public indexes organise reports by date, location and other fields, making the collection useful for tracing when people reported certain shapes, descriptions or clusters of events. The underlying population, however, consists of people who knew about NUFORC and chose to contact it; it is not a complete sample of everyone who saw something unusual. [NUFORC] nuforc.org UFO Sighting Report Form NUFORCNational UFO Reporting Center – Sighting Report Form · SIGHTING DATE AND TIME · SIGHTING LOCATION... That distinction matters even within a single entry. NUFORC displays both when an event allegedly occurred and when it was reported. A recollection submitted decades later may sit beside an account filed the same evening. Researchers interested in media driven reporting waves, memory effects or seasonal submission patterns must therefore separate event date from submission date rather than treating all records as contemporaneous observations. NUFORC itself periodically adds large batches of reports, so a spike in database publication can also reflect processing schedules rather than a sudden wave of sightings. [NUFORC] nuforc.org 1039 new reports posted 1039 new reports posted MUFON combines submissions with volunteer investigation. Its Case Management System stores the original witness form and, where a case is followed up, a field investigator’s findings. MUFON describes separate forms for issues such as landing traces, electromagnetic effects, animal reactions and other reported features. This creates richer potential case files than a narrative only repository, but the depth of investigation can vary according to investigator availability, witness cooperation and the evidence supplied. [MUFON] mufon.com A NEWCOMER'S GUIDE TO MUFON A NEWCOMER'S GUIDE TO MUFON MUFON’s database is also not equivalent to a fully open public dataset. The organisation states that active paid members may access specified parts of the system, including narratives and attachments, while reuse and distribution are restricted by its terms. Consequently, an outside researcher may be able to inspect selected recent reports without having the same access needed to audit classifications, attachments or historical coverage across the whole collection. [MUFON] mufon.com Search Database Terms and ConditionsThe Mutual UFO Network (MUFON) allows active members to access certain parts of our Case Managem... GEIPAN publishes investigated cases under a defined classification system. The French agency assesses both the consistency of the information and the residual strangeness after inquiry. Its familiar A, B, C and D classes distinguish identified cases, probably identified cases, cases lacking sufficient reliable information and cases remaining unidentified after investigation. The resulting database is therefore not simply an inbox of public claims: its labels reflect a stated evaluation process. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification8. How does GEIPAN classify observation cases? The classification process is done according a quantitative and qualit... Even here, “unidentified” does not mean “extraordinary craft”. It means that the agency did not establish an adequate conventional explanation from the available evidence under its own method. A case can remain unresolved because observations are unusual and well documented, but another repository might leave a similar report unresolved because no one investigated it at all. Counting both as the same category would erase the most important methodological difference. Government archives preserve records rather than operate live reporting systems. The US National Archives holds Project Blue Book material covering reports investigated by the US Air Force from 1947 to 1969. The UK National Archives holds Ministry of Defence sighting reports, correspondence, policy papers and parliamentary material. These collections answer historical questions about what officials received, recorded, investigated and retained; they do not provide a current, uniform multinational census of sightings. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects25 Jun 2024 — From 1947 to 1969, a total of 12, 618 sightings were report... Archives also organise information at several levels. One catalogue record may describe a folder, microfilm roll or series containing numerous reports. Conversely, one incident may produce several documents: a witness letter, police communication, internal memorandum, technical assessment and later correspondence. Counting catalogue entries as sightings would therefore be as misleading as counting every page in a case file as a separate event. UFOCAT is primarily a reference catalogue. The Center for UFO Studies explicitly warns that it often contains multiple records for the same sighting because its purpose was to index all available sources: an original file, a periodical article and a later book account might each receive an entry. Duplication is therefore partly a design feature rather than simply poor data cleaning. UFOCAT is valuable for locating the literature around a case, but its total number of records should not be treated as a total number of unique incidents. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO StudiesUFOCATUFOCAT is a catalog of published and unpublished UFO sighting reports. It often contains multiple entries for... Why Totals Are Not Directly Comparable A database total is produced by a chain of filters. Comparing two totals without mapping those filters is like comparing hospital admissions with calls to a medical advice line: both concern health events, but they do not count the same population. Intake changes the population NUFORC and MUFON depend heavily on voluntary witness reporting. Their geographical distributions are shaped by population, internet use, language, publicity, familiarity with the organisation and confidence that a report will be taken seriously. A high count in one US state may reflect more observers and greater reporting awareness rather than a higher incidence of anomalous objects. Academic work using NUFORC records therefore controls for environmental, demographic and visibility related variables rather than interpreting raw counts as direct exposure rates. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Government programmes use different gates. Project Blue Book received reports within a"},{"title":"Duplicates","url":"/duplicates/","description":"One incident can appear in several databases or through multiple witnesses, creating false clusters unless records are carefully matched and deduplicated.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases","level":2,"text":"Duplicates When One UFO Sighting Becomes Many Records UFO Archives Duplicates Duplicates When One UFO Sighting Becomes Many Records One incident can appear in several databases or through multiple witnesses, creating false clusters unless records are carefully matched and deduplicated. One incident can appear in several databases or through multiple witnesses, creating false clusters unless records are carefully matched and deduplicated. UFO Report Databases critique risk decision cluster mechanism How Duplicate Reports Arise Clues for Matching the Same Incident How Duplication Distorts Trends ufo report databases duplicates Introduction A single UFO sighting can become several database records without representing several separate events. One witness may submit accounts to both the National UFO Reporting Center ( NUFORC ) and the Mutual UFO Network ( MUFON ); several witnesses may report the same rocket launch independently; and a later catalogue may create separate entries for an investigator’s file, a magazine article and a book that all describe one incident. The resulting overlap can make ordinary events look like dense UFO “waves” unless researchers distinguish records, witnesses, sources and underlying incidents. This is not merely a clerical nuisance. Duplicate reports can inflate sighting totals, exaggerate geographical and temporal clusters, and give copied claims the appearance of independent corroboration. Yet aggressive deduplication creates the opposite danger: merging genuinely independent witnesses and discarding evidence that could help reconstruct an event. Reliable analysis therefore needs event level matching rather than a simple search for identical text. How Duplicate Reports Arise The central problem is that different databases do not always count the same unit. One system may treat each witness submission as a record; another may organise several witnesses under one investigated case; a bibliographic catalogue may preserve each published source separately. Record totals drawn from these systems are therefore not directly comparable. The same witness reports twice Witnesses can contact more than one organisation, particularly after a widely publicised incident. Their accounts may then appear in NUFORC, MUFON, a local investigative group and a newspaper archive. The wording may be nearly identical if the witness copied the original statement, but it may also change because each reporting form asks different questions or because the second account was written later. Even within one organisation, a person may submit an update, a corrected date or a second account containing photographs. Unless the later record is explicitly linked to the first, an automated analysis may count both. Submission dates are especially misleading: NUFORC distinguishes the date of occurrence from the date on which the account was reported, and some sightings are entered years after the alleged event. Matching only by upload date would therefore miss many obvious connections. [NUFORC] nuforc.org UFO Sighting Report Form NUFORCData Bank · Map · Gallery · File a UFO Report... Use a word processor to carefully compose and sp... Several witnesses report one visible event A mass observation naturally produces many records. A rocket launch, meteor, satellite re entry or formation of aircraft may be visible across several towns, prompting independent reports with different locations, times and descriptions. These are not duplicate testimonies in the ordinary sense: they are separate observations. They are nevertheless duplicate event counts if the research question is “How many distinct aerial events occurred?” NUFORC illustrated this problem after an October 2018 rocket launch. It noted that people watching the same event supplied markedly different times, dates and descriptions, demonstrating why simple exact matching is unreliable. A deduplication rule requiring identical timestamps or object shapes would have split that one launch into numerous apparent incidents. [NUFORC] nuforc.org 2018 Posts NUFORCDecember 31, 2018 — In the case of the rocket launch, all the witnesses were watching the same event at the same... Published: December 31, 2018 French research using historical GEPAN and GEIPAN material makes the same distinction formally. Jean Pierre Rospars treated repeated reports from the same witnesses as one evaluation, but recognised that one physical event—particularly a meteor or satellite re entry—could generate numerous separate reports. His technical notes distinguish per report assessments from per event assessments, showing that the choice of unit changes the statistical dataset. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr Open source on cnes geipan.fr. Later catalogues multiply the source trail UFOCAT , maintained by the Center for UFO Studies, is a particularly clear example of intentional duplication. CUFOS describes it primarily as a catalogue of published and unpublished reports and states that it often includes several entries for one sighting—for example, an original case file, a periodical article and a later book account. The duplication serves a bibliographic purpose: it helps researchers locate every source relating to an incident. It does not mean that each entry is an independent observation. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO StudiesUFOCATUFOCAT is a catalog of published and unpublished UFO sighting reports. It often contains multiple entries for... The UFOCAT codebook explains that a typical record represents one witness or group, one event and one source, but acknowledges unavoidable confusion among those categories. It also preserves information as given in the original source even when inaccuracies are known, because the catalogue is designed to lead users back to source material rather than silently rewrite it. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies This creates a copying chain. An early witness statement may be summarised by an investigator, condensed in a newsletter, retold in a book and then indexed from each publication. Four database rows can therefore descend from one testimony. Counting the rows as four confirmations would mistake repetition for corroboration. 14:40 10 Cases From Project Blue Book: The CIA's Hunt For UFOs Channel: TopTenz · Views: 271.1K · Uploaded: February 2019 · Length: 14 minutes Open on YouTube Clues for Matching the Same Incident No single field reliably identifies duplicates. Dates may be wrong, locations may reflect where different witnesses stood, and object descriptions can diverge sharply. A defensible match combines several clues and records the uncertainty rather than forcing every pair into “same” or “different”. The most useful comparison fields are: Occurrence time, with tolerance. Reports a few minutes apart may describe the same event, especially when witnesses estimated the time. Time zones, daylight saving rules and midnight crossings must be normalised before comparison. Geographical relationship. Nearby towns can refer to one event, but widely separated observers may also see a high altitude rocket plume or re entry. Matching should consider distance, direction of view and the event’s possible visibility footprint. Trajectory and duration. A west to east movement lasting three minutes is often more discriminating than a generic label such as “light” or “circle”. Narrative details. Unusual sequences—three orange lights separating, a sudden spiral, a fading plume—can connect reports even when structured fields disagree. Witness and source history. Matching names, contact details or photographs can reveal repeat submissions, while identical phrasing may indicate that one publication copied another. Known external events. Launch schedules, meteor observations, aircraft activity and satellite re entries can provide an event anchor around which multiple reports should be grouped. UFOCAT’s own designers used coarse geographical coding as one safeguard. The codebook says that different place names for versions of the same event usually resolve to the same region, state and county, and that limiting summary statistics to one similar record per county per date removes many duplications. This is useful for broad tabulation, but it remains a blunt rule: two genuinely distinct sightings can occur in the same county on one date, while one large event may cross several counties. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies Modern record linkage offers a better model. Instead of demanding exact equality, probabilistic matching assigns a likelihood that records refer to the same underlying entity or event. It can combine partially agreeing fields, tolerate errors and retain uncertainty for later analysis. Research on record linkage also stresses “blocking”: narrowing comparisons to plausible candidates before detailed matching, because checking every record against every other record quickly becomes computationally unmanageable. [arXiv] arxiv.org"},{"title":"GEIPAN","url":"/geipan/","description":"GEIPAN separates identified, poorly documented, and genuinely unresolved cases through a public investigation and classification system.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases","level":2,"text":"GEIPAN Why GEIPAN's UFO Classifications Matter UFO Archives GEIPAN GEIPAN Why GEIPAN's UFO Classifications Matter GEIPAN separates identified, poorly documented, and genuinely unresolved cases through a public investigation and classification system. GEIPAN separates identified, poorly documented, and genuinely unresolved cases through a public investigation and classification system. UFO Report Databases evidence pillar policy intervention How GEIPAN Investigates Reports The Difference Between Classes A, B, C, and D What the Published Statistics Show ufo report databases geipan Introduction GEIPAN’s value among UFO report databases lies not in the number of sightings it collects, but in what happens after a report is received. The French government unit investigates each usable account, tests ordinary explanations and assigns a public classification indicating whether the phenomenon was identified, probably identified, too poorly documented to judge, or still unexplained after inquiry. That distinction prevents two very different outcomes — “insufficient evidence” and “genuinely unresolved” — from being merged into one misleading category. The published figures also place the mystery in proportion. As of 25 June 2026, GEIPAN’s online statistics covered 3,368 classified cases: 28.0% were fully identified, 38.8% probably identified, 30.1% lacked enough evidence for a conclusion, and 3.1% remained unidentified after investigation. A Class D result therefore represents a small residual, not proof of an extraordinary craft or extraterrestrial origin. [Geipan] geipan.fr Statistiques GEIPAN Statistiques GEIPAN... Jacques Vallée on the Status of a Classified UAP Database The Sol Forum Channel: The Sol Foundation Open on YouTube How GEIPAN Investigates Reports GEIPAN is a technical unit of France’s national space agency, CNES. It collects, analyses, archives and publishes reports of unusual aerospace phenomena, while removing identifying information about witnesses. Its work is supported by the police and gendarmerie, roughly 20 trained volunteer investigators and specialists in fields such as astronomy, meteorology, image analysis, psychology and aerospace operations. It can also request information including weather records , aircraft movements and military radar data. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPANGEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives information on u... That institutional access can turn an apparently extraordinary sighting into a reconstructable event. In one example described by GEIPAN, witnesses reported a large, apparently stationary, saucer like form with a cone of light. Radar information established that a C 130 Hercules transport aircraft had been in the area. Investigators then considered why it had not looked like an aircraft: indirect moonlight illuminated the rounded fuselage more persistently than the wings, while lit treetops contributed to the impression of a downward beam. The conclusion did not merely name an aeroplane; it attempted to explain the specific features that had made the aeroplane seem anomalous. [Geipan] geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Mission & Geipan GEIPAN... Reports normally begin with a detailed technical questionnaire. Investigators compare the claimed time, direction, duration, movement and appearance with possible astronomical, meteorological, aeronautical and human causes. Photographs or videos may be checked for image artefacts, manipulation and perspective effects. When necessary, investigators interview witnesses, reconstruct viewing geometry and visit the location. About 10% of cases have historically required field investigation, while potential D1 and D2 cases receive an on site inquiry and cognitive interview as part of the current methodology. [Geipan] geipan.fr baisse cas d Forte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années14 Dec 2016 — Depuis 2008, le GEIPAN établit la classification A, B, C, D1/D2 au... Witness testimony remains central but is not treated as a direct recording of external reality. GEIPAN explicitly considers visual limitations, mistaken estimates of distance and speed, emotional reactions, memory changes and the influence of familiar cultural imagery. Its investigators are trained in cognitive interviewing, a method intended to recover detail without steering witnesses towards a preferred interpretation. This matters because even an honest and experienced observer may accurately report what an event looked like while being mistaken about its size, distance or nature. [Geipan] geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Mission & Geipan GEIPAN... The process ends with an investigation report and classification, not simply an acceptance or rejection of the witness’s account. Case pages may publish questionnaires, police statements, technical notes, photographs, maps, expert analyses and the reasoning behind the conclusion. This makes GEIPAN closer to a public collection of investigative files than to an open submission UFO database, although the depth and quality of documentation still vary between cases and historical periods. [CNES] cnes.fr GEIPANGEIPAN, the French UAP research and information group created by CNES in 1977, collects, analyses and archives information on u... The Difference Between Classes A, B, C and D GEIPAN’s classification rests on two linked concepts: residual strangeness and consistency . Residual strangeness describes how far the observation remains from a known explanation after possible hypotheses have been tested. Consistency reflects both the amount of information available and its reliability — for example, the number and independence of witnesses, the precision of their accounts, and the presence of photographs, radar records or physical traces. [Geipan] geipan.fr La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN La méthodologie de classification au GEIPAN GEIPAN... This creates a crucial safeguard: the stranger a claimed event appears, the stronger its evidence must be before it is classified as unexplained. A dramatic narrative with weak documentation should not receive the same status as an anomalous event supported by coherent testimony and independent records. Where strangeness is high but consistency is inadequate, the proper result is Class C rather than Class D. [Geipan] geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Mission & Geipan GEIPAN... The four principal classes mean: Class A — perfectly identified: The investigation provides evidence amounting to a near conclusive identification. Examples include a particular planet, aircraft, satellite, balloon or atmospheric event whose position and behaviour match the observation. Class B — probably identified: A conventional explanation fits the main facts and is judged likely, but some detail or confirming evidence is missing. Class B acknowledges uncertainty without treating the case as unexplained. Class C — not identifiable because evidence is insufficient: The account cannot support a reliable conclusion. The report may be too late, vague or incomplete, or may lack dependable information about direction, time, movement or viewing conditions. Class D — unidentified after investigation: The case remains anomalous despite an inquiry, and its evidence is considered sufficiently consistent to justify that conclusion. Since 2008, GEIPAN has sometimes divided this category into D1 for strange cases and D2 for very strange cases with stronger consistency. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr nown phenomena hypotheses. · Consistency (C): This is the product of the... Class C is therefore not a halfway stage between an identified case and an extraordinary one. It is an evidential dead end. GEIPAN’s own glossary notes that C cases should, strictly speaking, be considered separately from the A–B–D scale because investigators have been unable to make a supported judgement about them. A blurry photograph with no reliable time or direction may be deeply puzzling to its owner, yet still be Class C because too little information exists to test what it depicts. [Geipan] geipan.fr Le glossaire du Geipan GEIPANIls résultent en effet d’enquêtes sur lesquelles le GEIPAN n’a pas pu se prononcer, à la différence... Class D is also narrower than the everyday meaning of “unexplained”. It does not mean that investigators have ruled out every natural, technological or human cause. It means that no tested hypothesis adequately explains the evidence at the time of classification. GEIPAN states that classifications may be revised when new information or better analytical tools become available, and that unresolved cases require periodic re evaluation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr nown phenomena hypotheses. · Consistency (C): This is the product of the... 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube What the Published Statistics Show GEIPAN’s dynamic statistics, updated on 25 June"},{"title":"Unidentified","url":"/unidentified/","description":"A case remains unidentified when available evidence does not support a satisfactory explanation, which is not the same as confirming an extraordinary craft.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases","level":2,"text":"Unidentified What Does Unidentified Actually Mean? UFO Archives Unidentified Unidentified What Does Unidentified Actually Mean? A case remains unidentified when available evidence does not support a satisfactory explanation, which is not the same as confirming an extraordinary craft. A case remains unidentified when available evidence does not support a satisfactory explanation, which is not the same as confirming an extraordinary craft. UFO Report Databases critique risk theme cluster policy intervention Administrative Labels Versus Explanations Poor Evidence and Resistant Cases Common Misreadings of Unresolved Totals ufo report databases unidentified Introduction “Unidentified” is an investigative status, not a conclusion about extraterrestrial origin. In UFO and unidentified anomalous phenomenon databases, it usually means that the available evidence does not justify a satisfactory identification. The missing explanation may reflect poor imagery, incomplete witness information, absent sensor data, uncertain timing or simply the practical limits of the investigation. It does not establish that the observation involved an extraordinary vehicle, still less one from another world. This distinction matters because unresolved totals are often presented as though every unidentified entry has survived a single, rigorous test for alien technology. In reality, government agencies and civilian catalogues apply different categories, evidential thresholds and closure rules. Some separate genuinely resistant cases from reports that are merely too incomplete to assess; others publish witness narratives without making a formal determination at all. A responsible reading therefore begins with the catalogue’s definitions, not with the apparent drama of the word “unidentified”. Administrative Labels Versus Explanations A classification records what an investigative body could conclude under its own procedures. It does not, by itself, identify the cause of the event. This is clearest in the language used by Britain’s Ministry of Defence: records for which no ordinary explanation could be established remained “unidentified rather than extraterrestrial”. The National Archives’ guide also stresses that the military meaning of a UFO was simply something seen in the sky that the observer did not recognise; “unidentified aerial phenomenon” did not imply an object of extraterrestrial origin. [National Archives] cdn.nationalarchives.gov.uk Open source on nationalarchives.gov.uk. Modern US practice makes the same distinction, although with a different administrative system. The All domain Anomaly Resolution Office, or AARO, publishes cases labelled “unresolved” even when it considers their observed behaviour unremarkable. Several official imagery assessments describe physical objects whose appearance and performance did not warrant further analysis, but whose precise identity could not be established from the available footage. Another case was closed as “not anomalous” because its behaviour matched an ordinary aircraft, even though the data were insufficient to determine its exact type or origin. [AAROM] aaro.mil MUAP ImageryThe video footage associated with this report is insufficient for AARO to render a determination on its subject matter. T... This produces several possible outcomes that are easily collapsed in public discussion: Identified or resolved: investigators judge that the evidence supports a conventional explanation, such as a balloon, bird, aircraft, satellite or atmospheric effect. Probably identified: the proposed explanation fits strongly, but absolute confirmation is unavailable. Insufficient information: the record lacks enough reliable detail to support either an identification or a meaningful anomaly assessment. Unresolved or unidentified: investigators cannot make a satisfactory attribution under the catalogue’s rules. Not anomalous but unattributed: the observation appears ordinary in performance, although its exact identity remains unknown. These categories are not interchangeable. An unresolved report may be evidentially weak, technically ordinary or genuinely resistant to the explanations tested so far. The label alone does not reveal which. France’s GEIPAN system illustrates why definitions must be read carefully. GEIPAN, a unit of the French space agency CNES, distinguishes cases with insufficient data from stronger unidentified cases. Its classification includes category C for a phenomenon that cannot be identified because information is lacking, D1 for an unidentified case of moderate strangeness and evidential consistency, and D2 for one judged both more unusual and better supported—for example, by several independent witnesses or photographs. The organisation also states that classifications are provisional and may change when new information becomes available. [Geipan] geipan.fr FAQ Press English  D1:an unidentified phenomenon but of average strangeness and consistency (only one witness or no photo forexample). D2:an unidentified... A civilian reporting site may work differently again. The National UFO Reporting Center’s databank is principally a repository of first hand submissions. The presence of an account there shows that someone reported an observation; it does not mean that investigators have confirmed the event, tested every ordinary explanation or assigned the case a formal unresolved status. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC NUFORCData Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021 — The NUFORC Databank is the largest independently collected set of UFO... Published: September 6, 2021 The practical rule is simple: before treating an “unidentified” total as evidence, establish who applied the label, what evidence was required, what alternatives were examined and whether incomplete reports were placed in a separate category. Poor Evidence and Resistant Cases The most important division is not between “identified” and “unidentified”, but between cases that resist explanation despite good evidence and cases that remain open because the evidence is too poor to decide anything. A short, blurred video may show that something occupied part of a sensor’s field of view without establishing its distance, size, speed or physical nature. Those quantities are connected: apparent rapid motion may result from camera movement or a nearby object, while an impressive estimated size may depend on an assumed distance. Without reliable range data, calibrated sensor information, environmental conditions and the observing platform’s movement, investigators may be unable to reconstruct what happened. NASA’s independent UAP study made this evidential problem central. NASA says that the limited supply of consistent, detailed and curated observations prevents firm scientific conclusions about the nature of UAP. Its public guidance adds that there is no evidence that UAP are extraterrestrial and that, without extensive data, verifying or explaining an observation is nearly impossible. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP What types of scientific data currently collected and archived by NASA or other civilian government entities should be synthesized and an... AARO’s recent reporting shows how heavily resolution depends on data quality and analytical tools. In its fiscal year 2025 report, the office said that it received 319 reports and resolved 370 cases when older holdings were included; every resolved case was attributed to ordinary objects or events, including balloons, satellites, birds, aircraft, drones, a commercial rocket launch and a manned jet pack. A new analytical capability allowed 238 reports to be resolved as satellite flaring. The same report said that a lack of timely, actionable sensor data continued to constrain case resolution. [AAROM] aaro.mil MAARO FY25 Consolidated Annual Report on UAP MAARO FY25 Consolidated Annual Report on UAP The satellite flare example is particularly instructive. To a pilot, a brief bright orb appearing at high altitude may look unlike a familiar aircraft or star. Once analysts model the geometry of sunlight reflecting from satellite surfaces, reports that once appeared puzzling can become identifiable. AARO found that many high altitude civilian pilot reports matched this effect, demonstrating that an unresolved inventory may shrink when new comparison data or modelling methods become available. [AAROM] aaro.mil MAARO FY25 Consolidated Annual Report on UAP MAARO FY25 Consolidated Annual Report on UAP This does not mean that every difficult case has already been explained. A well documented observation can remain resistant after serious investigation, and that is a legitimate reason for further study. However, resistance to the explanations tested is not positive evidence for a particular untested cause. The logical position remains: the case is unresolved. GEIPAN’s re examination of older records shows why even apparently stronger cases should not be treated as permanently"},{"title":"NUFORC","url":"/nuforc/","description":"NUFORC offers an unusually large collection of witness accounts, but its patterns reflect reporting behavior as well as events in the sky.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases","level":2,"text":"NUFORC What Can NUFORC Reports Really Reveal? UFO Archives NUFORC NUFORC What Can NUFORC Reports Really Reveal? NUFORC offers an unusually large collection of witness accounts, but its patterns reflect reporting behavior as well as events in the sky. NUFORC offers an unusually large collection of witness accounts, but its patterns reflect reporting behavior as well as events in the sky. UFO Report Databases critique risk pillar dataset evidence What Each Public Report Contains Why Self Selection Shapes the Data Useful Questions the Database Can Answer ufo report databases nuforc Introduction The National UFO Reporting Center (NUFORC) offers something few UFO catalogues can match: a large, freely searchable collection of first hand sighting accounts stretching across decades. Founded in 1974, the centre says it has processed more than 180,000 reports, while its public databank allows readers to browse entries by date, place and reported shape. That scale makes NUFORC useful for tracing how unusual aerial experiences are described, when reports increase and which locations or visual forms recur. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsThe NUFORC Databank is the largest independently collected set of UFO / UAP sighting reports available on the interne... Its size does not make it a census of unidentified objects. NUFORC records what people decide to submit, sometimes long after an event, using details that witnesses estimate rather than instruments measure. Publicity, internet access, population, available leisure time, changing technology and familiar objects such as Starlink satellites can all alter the number and character of reports. Researchers can therefore learn a great deal from NUFORC, provided they treat it primarily as a database of reported experiences and reporting behaviour , not as a direct measure of extraordinary activity in the sky. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect On the dynamics of reporting data: A case study of UFO sightings These data are freely available on the Kaggle platform [14] and were curated by The National UFO Reporting Center (NUFORC) [15].... It h... What Each Public Report Contains A NUFORC entry is more structured than an anecdote posted on social media. The public indexes commonly show the date and time of the event, city, state or country, reported shape, a short summary and the date on which the entry was posted. Researchers who extracted the database for a national spatial study identified fields including event date, city, state, country, shape, duration, summary, posting date and whether an image was present. [Nature] nature.com An environmental analysis of public UAP sightings and sky view potential Scientific Reports... Newer individual records can contain considerably more detail. Depending on what the witness supplies, fields may include: the occurrence and submission times; the number of observers; the observer’s claimed professional background; a more precise location description; [nuforc.org] nuforc.org NUFORC UFO Sighting 196975August 20, 2023 — 9 Apr 2026 — No of observers: 2. Location: Calgary, AB, Canada Location details... Viewed Fr... Published: August 20, 2023 colour, apparent shape and estimated size; [nuforc.org] nuforc.org Source details in endnotes. whether the sighting was made from land or an aircraft; direction, elevation and estimated distance; estimated speed and duration; reported characteristics, such as lights, sound or unusual movement; a free text narrative and sometimes photographs or video. A recent public entry, for example, displays separate fields for the event time, reporting time, two observers, viewing direction, elevation, distance, estimated size and speed, followed by the witness’s narrative. The presence of these fields is valuable because it preserves distinctions that a one line “sighting count” would erase. It does not guarantee that the estimates are physically accurate. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org NUFORC UFO Sighting 196975August 20, 2023 — 9 Apr 2026 — No of observers: 2. Location: Calgary, AB, Canada Location details... Viewed Fr... Published: August 20, 2023 Event date and report date answer different questions One of the most important NUFORC fields is the submission date. An event may be entered immediately, several days later or decades afterwards. NUFORC has published batches containing both contemporary observations and old experiences that witnesses had only recently chosen to report. In one update, 489 of 722 newly posted entries concerned recent events, while the remainder described earlier sightings. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org 722 new ufo reports 722 new ufo reports That distinction affects almost every time series analysis. Counting reports by event date approximates when witnesses say something happened. Counting them by submission or posting date reflects when people were motivated to contact NUFORC and when staff processed the records. Mixing the two can create artificial peaks, especially when old accounts are added in batches or publication is delayed. NUFORC has itself noted backlogs and intervals in which large groups of accumulated submissions were posted together. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org 1039 new reports posted 1039 new reports posted Historical entries are not necessarily worthless. They can preserve memories that were never recorded elsewhere and help locate witnesses or compare regional narratives. Their evidential limits are simply different. A report written thirty years after an event is more exposed to memory reconstruction, uncertain dates and later cultural influences than a statement recorded that night. Structure is not the same as verification A report can be detailed, internally coherent and sincerely written without establishing what produced the observation. Apparent distance, size and speed are especially difficult to estimate when the witness does not know the object’s true dimensions or range. A light that appears to move rapidly may instead be nearer, farther away or affected by the observer’s own movement and lack of visual reference points. NUFORC says staff review reports and now assigns tiers, with the highest category reserved for accounts judged especially dramatic or anomalous. The organisation also uses explanatory labels including aircraft, balloons, camera anomalies, drones, meteors, planets or stars, rockets, satellites, Starlink and hoaxes. These judgements add useful editorial information, but they should not be mistaken for a uniform forensic investigation of every case. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsThe NUFORC Databank is the largest independently collected set of UFO / UAP sighting reports available on the interne... The database therefore contains several layers that researchers must keep separate: 1. what the witness says was observed; 2. the witness’s interpretation of it; 3. any category or explanation added during NUFORC review; 4. any later independent examination using flight, satellite, weather or astronomical data. Treating all four layers as equivalent can make an unresolved account look confirmed or make a tentative editorial suggestion look conclusive. 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube Why Self Selection Shapes the Data NUFORC does not draw a random sample of people and ask whether they saw anything unusual. Witnesses find the centre, decide that their experience is worth reporting, complete the form and accept that much of the account may become public. This process is called self selection : participation depends on the individual rather than on a controlled sampling design. Self selection matters because reporters may differ systematically from non reporters. They may be more interested in UFOs, more comfortable discussing unusual experiences, more aware of NUFORC or more motivated by a recent news story. Other observers may dismiss the same light, report it to a different organisation, tell only friends or never mention it because they fear ridicule. General research on online self selected samples warns that such participation can produce coverage and selection bias, limiting claims about the wider population. [JMIR] jmir.org Open source on jmir.org. Consequently, a state with twice as many NUFORC reports does not necessarily experience twice as many unusual objects. It may have more people, clearer skies, greater internet access, stronger public interest or a higher tendency to report. Opportunity to see the sky changes the numbers A national study of more than 98,000 NUFORC reports from the contiguous United States between 2001 and 2020 found that reported sightings were associated with conditions that give people a better opportunity to observe the sky. Reports tended to be more common in areas with greater sky visibility, while factors such as tree cover and artificial night sky brightness"},{"title":"Blue Book","url":"/blue-book/","description":"Project Blue Book preserved thousands of military-era reports, but an unidentified ruling meant no satisfactory explanation was reached, not that aliens were","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases","level":2,"text":"Blue Book What Did Project Blue Book Leave Unexplained? UFO Archives Blue Book Blue Book What Did Project Blue Book Leave Unexplained? Project Blue Book preserved thousands of military era reports, but an unidentified ruling meant no satisfactory explanation was reached, not that aliens were Project Blue Book preserved thousands of military era reports, but an unidentified ruling meant no satisfactory explanation was reached, not that aliens were UFO Report Databases historical comparative case family event time window How the Air Force Recorded Sightings What the 701 Unidentified Cases Mean Strengths and Gaps in the Surviving Files ufo report databases blue book Introduction Project Blue Book left 701 sightings officially “ unidentified ”, but that label did not mean the United States Air Force had found evidence of alien spacecraft. It meant that investigators considered the available report sufficiently detailed to evaluate, yet could not match the described object or motion confidently to a known aircraft, balloon, astronomical body, meteorological effect or other conventional cause. The distinction matters because “unidentified” was an administrative conclusion about a case file, not a positive finding about what had been observed. Blue Book and its immediate predecessors recorded 12,618 reports from 1947 to 1969. Most received conventional explanations; others were placed in an “insufficient data” category because crucial information was missing. The remaining 701 form a historically valuable but uneven catalogue of unresolved military era observations. They preserve what witnesses said, what investigators checked and where the inquiry stopped—not proof that every unexplained report described a single extraordinary phenomenon. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National Archives... How the Air Force Recorded Sightings Project Blue Book was the Air Force’s principal public UFO investigation programme from March 1952 until December 1969, following the earlier Project Sign and Project Grudge investigations. Its stated objectives were to determine whether reported objects threatened US security and whether they displayed scientifically or technically significant characteristics. Reports could originate with military personnel, pilots, weather observers, astronomers, police officers or members of the public. [National Archives] archives.gov After an extensive interagency partnership between the two entities to redact personally identifiable information from Blue Book case fil... The official process was supposed to begin at the Air Force base nearest the reported event. Local personnel gathered statements and contextual information before forwarding the material to the Blue Book office at Wright Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio. Where the initial inquiry produced no clear answer, Blue Book could undertake further analysis and consult Air Force scientific, technical, aviation and tracking resources. Its staff checked such possibilities as military and civilian flights, high altitude balloons, satellites, astronomical objects, meteors, searchlights, reflections, radar anomalies and unusual weather effects. [WHS ESD] esd.whs.mil proj b1 proj b1 A typical file could contain a reporting form or questionnaire, witness statements, sketches, maps, weather information, correspondence with military units, calculations, photographs, newspaper cuttings and an investigator’s evaluation. The quantity and quality varied sharply. Some incidents generated many pages and inquiries involving several agencies; others survived as little more than a short message or partially completed form. This variation is central to interpreting Blue Book as a database. The collection was not produced through a single, stable research protocol operating under controlled conditions. Reporting procedures, available staff, investigative priorities and classification habits changed over more than two decades. A case entered in 1952 may therefore have been documented and assessed differently from a superficially similar report in 1967. Three outcome categories By 1966, the Air Force publicly described three broad evaluation categories: Identified: enough specific information had been obtained to connect the sighting with a conventional object or phenomenon. Insufficient data: essential details—such as precise time, position, duration, weather or manner of disappearance—were absent, preventing a meaningful determination. Unidentified: the report apparently contained enough pertinent information to test plausible explanations, but the reported appearance or movement could not be correlated with a known object or phenomenon. [WHS ESD] esd.whs.mil proj b1 proj b1 This framework shows why “unidentified” and “insufficient data” were not officially interchangeable. An incomplete account was supposed to be placed in the latter category rather than counted among the unexplained. In practice, however, the boundary could be subjective: investigators still had to decide whether the information was adequate, whether proposed identifications were strong enough and how much follow up a report warranted. 2:26 Project Blue Book: Declassified The True Story of the D.C. UFO Sightings History Channel: HISTORY · Views: 393.7K · Uploaded: March 2019 · Length: 2 minutes 26 seconds Open on YouTube What the 701 Unidentified Cases Mean The figure of 701 is the final Air Force total for unresolved reports among 12,618 cases—about 5.6 per cent of the catalogue. It represents the accumulation of case rulings across the full 1947–69 reporting period, including files inherited from the investigations that preceded the formal creation of Blue Book. It should not be read as 701 confirmed physical objects, 701 encounters supported by radar, or 701 incidents of equal credibility. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National Archives... Some unidentified files involved a single visual witness. Others included several observers, military personnel, pilots or apparent radar information. Some described distant lights with little measurable detail; others contained estimates of direction, speed, altitude, shape or manoeuvres. The surviving group is consequently a case family rather than a uniform dataset. What its members share is the Air Force’s failure—or inability—to assign a satisfactory explanation under the procedures applied at the time. Several interpretations of an unidentified ruling are possible: No conventional explanation fitted the reported facts well enough. A plausible explanation existed, but investigators could not establish it with confidence. Records needed to verify an explanation were unavailable, incomplete or classified. The witness description contained errors that could not be reconstructed later. The investigation ended before decisive evidence could be obtained. The report described an unusual event for which the surviving file remains genuinely inconclusive. None of these possibilities alone establishes extraterrestrial origin. The Air Force’s formal position was that no Blue Book case showed a threat to national security, scientific principles beyond contemporary knowledge or evidence that the unidentified objects were extraterrestrial vehicles. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National Archives... That conclusion was broader than saying every report had been solved. Blue Book could simultaneously acknowledge unresolved individual cases while maintaining that the total collection did not support a particular extraordinary explanation. This is the most important distinction for readers using the files as a historical catalogue: a residual set of unknowns challenges claims that the programme explained everything, but it does not by itself validate any single theory about what remained. Original document Project Blue Book FBI file Federal Bureau of Investigation · Wikimedia Commons · Unknown date · 118281892 A primary Project Blue Book–related document scan, directly relevant to an article about what Blue Book left unexplained. Public domain · View original record A Statistical Test That Did Not Eliminate the Unknowns One of Blue Book’s most ambitious attempts to analyse its records systematically was Special Report No. 14, completed in 1955 from work carried out with the Battelle Memorial Institute. Rather than concentrating on one celebrated incident, the study coded thousands of reports by characteristics such as date, location, duration, colour, shape, speed and observational quality. Cases were divided into explained reports, unknowns and reports lacking sufficient information. [CIA] cia.gov CIA(ANALYSIS OF REPORTS OF UNIDENTIFIED AERIAL...PROJECT BLUE BOOK ASDIA fele SPECIAL REPORT NO."},{"title":"Reports vs Files","url":"/reports-vs-files/","description":"A first-hand account records what someone says they saw, while an investigated file adds checks, context, competing explanations, and a documented conclusion.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases","level":2,"text":"Reports vs Files Is a UFO Report the Same as Evidence? UFO Archives Reports vs Files Reports vs Files Is a UFO Report the Same as Evidence? A first hand account records what someone says they saw, while an investigated file adds checks, context, competing explanations, and a documented conclusion. A first hand account records what someone says they saw, while an investigated file adds checks, context, competing explanations, and a documented conclusion. UFO Report Databases evidence decision cluster dataset evidence What Witness Narratives Preserve What Investigation Adds Choosing the Right Source for a Question ufo report databases reports vs files Introduction A UFO report is not the same thing as evidence that an extraordinary object was present. A witness narrative preserves what a person remembers seeing: the appearance, movement, duration, setting and emotional force of an event. An investigated case file starts with that account but adds questions the original report may not answer—exact time and location, viewing geometry , weather, aircraft and satellite activity, sensor behaviour, independent witnesses , photographs, radar data and possible conventional explanations. This distinction is essential when using UFO report databases and catalogues. A searchable testimony archive is often the best source for studying how sightings are experienced and described. A completed investigative file is usually better for judging what probably happened. Even then, a conclusion of “ unidentified ” normally means that the available information did not support a firm identification; it does not, by itself, establish exotic technology or extraterrestrial origin. NASA has similarly warned that inconsistent reporting, incomplete metadata and poorly calibrated or single source observations prevent firm scientific conclusions. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov NASA Science... What Witness Narratives Preserve First hand accounts are the raw material from which most UFO cases begin. Repositories such as the National UFO Reporting Center, or NUFORC , preserve reports submitted by members of the public, often in the witness’s own language. Entries commonly record a date, place, duration, reported shape and free text description. Their value lies partly in this immediacy: they retain details that may be shortened, standardised or lost when a case is later summarised. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsThe NUFORC Databank is the largest independently collected set of UFO / UAP sighting repor... A narrative can preserve several kinds of information particularly well: Perceived sequence: whether a light appeared suddenly, changed direction, divided into several objects or vanished. Viewing circumstances: whether the observer was driving, standing outdoors, looking through glass or using binoculars. Sensory impressions: colour, brightness, apparent size, sound and perceived speed. Human response: surprise, fear, uncertainty, attempts to alert others or efforts to photograph the event. Contemporary vocabulary: the comparisons witnesses naturally reach for, such as “triangle”, “star like”, “cigar shaped” or “a row of lights”. These details matter because later investigators need to reconstruct not merely what the witness concluded, but what was actually observed. “It was a craft” is an interpretation; “three steady white lights kept the same angular separation for about two minutes” is a potentially testable description. Witness accounts also have historical and cultural value even when the object is eventually identified. They show what unfamiliar aircraft, satellites, balloons, astronomical objects and atmospheric effects looked like to observers before an explanation was known. A large report database can therefore reveal reporting patterns, common descriptions and changes in public attention. Researchers have, for example, used NUFORC records to examine geographical and environmental patterns in public UAP reporting. Such work treats the database as a record of reported observations, not as a direct count of unexplained objects. [Nature] nature.com This research uses data from the National UFO Reporting Center (NUFORC) online. NUFORC was formed in 1974...Read more... A narrative is not a neutral measurement The strength of a first hand report is also its central limitation: it records human perception and memory rather than an instrument calibrated event. Apparent size and speed are especially difficult to estimate without knowing distance. A small nearby object and a large distant object can occupy the same angle in the sky. A stationary light may seem to move when clouds pass or when the observer’s eyes and head shift. Duration estimates can expand under stress, while a report written years later may incorporate subsequent discussion, media coverage or repeated retelling. Online report collections introduce further selection effects. Witnesses choose whether, when and where to report; some observations are submitted immediately, others decades later. Publicity, internet access, population density and awareness of a reporting organisation all influence which experiences enter the database. Research on self selected online reporting more generally identifies selection bias, information bias and confounding as persistent methodological problems. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Research Conducted Using Data Obtained through Online...by ACJW Janssens · 2012 · Cited by 100 — Research relying on... None of this makes testimony worthless. It means the correct question is usually not “Is the witness honest?” but “Which parts of this account can be checked, and what uncertainties remain?” A sincere witness can accurately describe an experience while misidentifying its cause. What Investigation Adds An investigated file converts an observation into a case that can be tested against other information. The process varies between organisations, but the key change is the introduction of comparison: the witness’s account is placed beside external records, alternative hypotheses and an explicit conclusion. France’s GEIPAN provides one of the clearest public examples. It begins with direct witness testimony and a technical questionnaire designed to collect enough detail to open an investigation. A case may then be assigned to an approved investigator, and supporting material such as sketches, photographs or video can be added. GEIPAN publishes both sighting material and investigative conclusions while protecting witness anonymity. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. The investigative file may add: A structured interview: clarifying direction of view, angular elevation, duration, object behaviour and the order of events. Site reconstruction: checking sightlines, landmarks, reflections, obstructions and distances. Environmental context: weather, cloud cover, wind, astronomical objects and atmospheric conditions. Traffic checks: aircraft routes, helicopters, drones, balloons and satellites expected at the recorded time. Independent evidence: separate witness statements, photographs, video, radar or other sensor data. Hypothesis testing: asking whether each proposed explanation accounts for the whole observation rather than merely one feature. A documented outcome: identified, probably identified, insufficient information or unexplained after investigation. GEIPAN’s classification system explicitly considers both the residual strangeness of a case after investigation and the consistency or quality of the available information. Its categories distinguish identified or probably identified events from cases with inadequate data and cases that remain unexplained despite sufficient information. The system also allows some cases to be revisited if new material becomes available. [Geipan] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. That separation between “not explained” and “not explainable” is crucial. A file may remain open because the witness supplied only an approximate date, because no photograph survives, because radar data were not retained or because a potentially relevant aircraft record is unavailable. An unresolved label describes the state of the evidence, not the nature of the object. 11:43 Watch: Witnesses recount UFO sightings in opening statements at House hearing Channel: CBS News · Views: 143.9K · Uploaded: September 2025 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube The same sighting can look less extraordinary after reconstruction One of the most useful features of an investigated file is that it can test apparent motion and performance rather than accepting them at face value. A striking modern example involved several commercial pilots who reported unfamiliar lights over the Pacific in August 2022. The event included multiple witnesses and mobile phone imagery, features that might initially appear strongly corroborative. Researchers later combined satellite orbital data with aircraft"},{"title":"UFOCAT","url":"/ufocat/","description":"UFOCAT connects reports scattered across books, periodicals, investigator files, and older databases, but its entries often require source-by-source","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases","level":2,"text":"UFOCAT How UFOCAT Connects Decades of Case Research UFO Archives UFOCAT UFOCAT How UFOCAT Connects Decades of Case Research UFOCAT connects reports scattered across books, periodicals, investigator files, and older databases, but its entries often require source by source UFOCAT connects reports scattered across books, periodicals, investigator files, and older databases, but its entries often require source by source UFO Report Databases historical comparative pillar dataset evidence What Sources Feed the Catalogue Using References to Trace Original Records Coverage Gaps and Duplicate Material ufo report databases ufocat Introduction UFOCAT is best understood not as a clean list of unique sightings, but as a bibliographic map of civilian UFO research. Its purpose is to connect descriptions of the same incident scattered across investigators’ files, specialist periodicals, books, newspaper accounts and earlier catalogues. A single event may therefore appear several times, with each entry pointing to a different source rather than representing a separate sighting. That design makes UFOCAT unusually valuable for historical research. It can help a reader move backwards from a later summary to an earlier report, compare how an account changed in circulation and identify which civilian organisation or investigator preserved the surviving evidence. It also creates serious complications: duplicate material inflates simple totals, source quality varies sharply, some records remain confidential, and an index entry does not guarantee that the underlying document is complete or readily accessible. CUFOS, which maintains the catalogue, explicitly advises researchers to treat UFOCAT as a guide to original records rather than as a self sufficient body of evidence. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... What Sources Feed the Catalogue UFOCAT began in 1967 during the University of Colorado UFO project, commonly known as the Condon Committee. Psychologist David Saunders developed the catalogue, while Jacques Vallée contributed an existing computer file of roughly 6,000 cases. Saunders transferred his version to the Center for UFO Studies, or CUFOS, in 1976; Fred Merritt maintained it until 1982, when the cost of keeping it on an IBM mainframe forced the organisation to place it into storage. Donald Johnson reconstructed the database after obtaining a diskette copy in 1990 and combining it with an older magnetic tape backup to replace records that had been lost during conversion. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... That history matters because UFOCAT accumulated material through successive layers of civilian research rather than through one consistent reporting system. UFOCAT 2023 contains more than 300,000 entries, of which CUFOS identifies more than 192,000 as primary UFO reports. The difference between those figures reflects the catalogue’s central principle: several records can refer to one incident when different publications, investigations or later summaries preserve distinct versions of it. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... The catalogue recognises several broad source forms: Investigation files , including structured witness forms, correspondence, interview notes and reports prepared by field investigators. Civilian periodicals , such as organisational bulletins and specialist magazines that published case reports, updates or correspondence. Books and compiled case lists , often written years after an incident and sometimes dependent on earlier unpublished material. Newspapers and wire reports , which may preserve the earliest public account but can omit technical detail or compress several witnesses into one story. Earlier computer catalogues and private research collections , which supplied batches of cases already selected and coded by other researchers. The codebook ranks investigation files among the preferred forms of input because they are more likely to contain fuller documentation with fewer stages of retelling. Published sources remain important, however, especially where original files have disappeared or were never deposited in an archive. UFOCAT’s value lies partly in retaining both levels and making their relationship visible. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies This structure also reflects the social organisation of twentieth century UFO research. NICAP, the National Investigations Committee on Aerial Phenomena, maintained a large investigator network and collected reports until 1980. APRO, the Aerial Phenomena Research Organization, operated from 1952 until the late 1980s with investigators and consultants in several countries. CUFOS continued collecting and analysing cases after the closure of the US Air Force’s Project Blue Book . The surviving records of these groups now sit alongside personal collections, correspondence, recordings, clippings and research notes preserved by the National UFO Historical Records Center. [www.nufohrc.org] nufohrc.org Open source on nufohrc.org. UFOCAT therefore connects not only publications but research communities. A witness account might have entered the system through a local investigator, been forwarded to APRO or NICAP, summarised in a bulletin, cited in a later book and eventually coded from several of those sources. What looks like repetition in the database may reveal the transmission path by which the case survived. Using References to Trace Original Records The most important UFOCAT fields are those that identify where a statement came from and how closely it stands to the original event. Each record has a permanent unique number, while linked identifiers group entries concerning the same incident. The X2 field indicates the record’s position within that group: zero marks the selected primary entry, one an independent investigation not chosen as primary, two a source dependent on another account, and three material further removed from the original report. Filtering for zero can reduce obvious double counting, although it does not resolve every ambiguity about whether two similar reports describe the same event. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies The SOURCE field identifies the direct item from which information was coded, such as a book, periodical or investigation file. A companion location field records a page, publication date, acquisition number or other internal reference intended to help the user find the original documentation. The ISOURCE field records the indirect source credited by that item: for example, a book may be the direct source used by the coder while an investigator’s report cited in the book is the indirect source. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies This distinction allows a researcher to reconstruct a reference chain. Consider a hypothetical case whose UFOCAT block contains: 1. an investigator’s contemporary report; 2. an account in an APRO bulletin; 3. a newspaper article; 4. a paragraph in a later survey book. The book may be easiest to obtain, but it is not necessarily the best evidence. UFOCAT can indicate that the book depends on the bulletin, which in turn drew on the investigator’s file. A careful researcher would compare all four, checking whether the date, duration, witness count, object description or claimed physical effects changed between versions. The catalogue also records how information was obtained. Its coding distinguishes, among other things, an on the scene investigation, a standardised witness form, a first person account in a scientific publication, a witness letter and a newspaper interview. These categories do not automatically establish reliability, but they reveal how many interpretive steps separate the database entry from the person who reported the event. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT deliberately preserves much source wording even when the compiler suspects it is inaccurate. The codebook explains that records normally reflect the details supplied by the source, with flags used for suspected errors and approximations. This is appropriate for a historical catalogue because quietly correcting conflicting dates or locations would erase evidence of how a case was reported. It means, however, that researchers must distinguish between what allegedly happened and what a particular document claimed. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies The catalogue is therefore most effective as a discovery and comparison tool. It can answer questions such as: Which publications mention this event? Is there evidence of an independent investigation? Does a later author cite a traceable earlier record? Are several apparently separate entries actually versions of one case? Which"},{"title":"RAF Searches","url":"/raf-searches/","description":"Early RAF sightings are easier to locate when searches combine period language with airfields, commands, locations and narrow date ranges.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / British Files","level":3,"text":"RAF Searches How to Find Early RAF UFO Reports British Files RAF Searches RAF Searches How to Find Early RAF UFO Reports Early RAF sightings are easier to locate when searches combine period language with airfields, commands, locations and narrow date ranges. Early RAF sightings are easier to locate when searches combine period language with airfields, commands, locations and narrow date ranges. UFO Report Databases implementation single mechanism mechanism Why modern UFO terms miss older records Combining airfields, places and commands Avoiding false matches in aviation files ufo report databases british files raf searches Introduction Finding early Royal Air Force (RAF) UFO reports requires a different search strategy from looking for later Ministry of Defence (MOD) UFO files. Before the term \"UFO\" became standard in British official records, Air Ministry and RAF correspondence often described sightings using everyday language such as flying saucer , unidentified object , unusual light or simply by referring to a particular RAF station, command or locality. Many early records were also destroyed under routine retention policies before 1967, making surviving material uneven and difficult to discover. As a result, the most productive approach is to combine historical terminology with specific places, RAF organisations and tightly defined date ranges rather than relying on modern UFO keywords alone. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... Why modern UFO terms miss older records Researchers often begin by searching Discovery, The National Archives' catalogue, for \"UFO\". While this works well for later MOD material, it is much less effective for the early post war period. Several factors explain why: The expression \"flying saucer\" dominated British reporting after 1947. Air Ministry staff frequently described events as \"unidentified object\" , \"unusual light\" , \"aerial phenomenon\" or similar observational language. Many catalogue descriptions were written decades after the records themselves and may contain only brief summaries. Some files are catalogued primarily under administrative subjects rather than the phenomenon being reported. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... The National Archives specifically recommends searching multiple historical terms, including \"UFO\", \"unidentified flying\", and \"saucers\", across the AIR and DEFE departments rather than assuming modern terminology appears consistently. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... Combine airfields, places and RAF organisations For early RAF related sightings, place names usually provide stronger search anchors than the word \"UFO\". Instead of searching only for a phenomenon, combine: an RAF station name a nearby town or county the relevant command or Air Ministry office a narrow date range For example, searches built around: RAF Lakenheath RAF Woodbridge RAF Bentwaters RAF Mildenhall Fighter Command Bomber Command Air Ministry Whitehall often locate administrative correspondence that a generic \"UFO\" search would overlook. This approach reflects how the records were created. Officials were generally concerned with operational reporting, station administration or air defence rather than maintaining a dedicated UFO archive. Consequently, a catalogue entry may emphasise the originating office or location rather than the unusual sighting itself. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... 3:56 UFO file release March 2009 Channel: The National Archives UK · Views: 128.6K · Uploaded: March 2009 · Length: 3 minutes 56 seconds Open on YouTube Search within narrow date windows Using broad date ranges frequently produces large numbers of unrelated aviation records. Instead, search around known reporting periods or specific incidents: a single day if an exact sighting date is known one calendar month for newspaper reported events one year when reconstructing a sequence of reports Discovery allows searches to be filtered by date, which can dramatically reduce irrelevant results. The National Archives also notes that browsing surrounding records is often as important as keyword searching because catalogue descriptions may be sparse. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives Searching for records using Discovery National ArchivesSearching for records using Discovery The National Archives... A useful pattern is: 1. Identify a reported sighting date. 2. Search the station or locality. 3. Limit results to several weeks before and after the event. 4. Browse neighbouring catalogue references within the same AIR or DEFE series. Neighbouring files often concern the same administrative correspondence even when their titles differ. Use historical language from the period Contemporary newspapers, parliamentary correspondence and witness letters influenced the language used by officials. Useful historical search terms include: flying saucer flying saucers unidentified object unidentified flying object [archives.gov] archives.gov ecord Group or Collection National ArchivesDecember 12, 2025 — RECORDS RELATED TO UNIDENTIFIED FLYING OBJECTS (UFOS) AND UNIDENTIFIED A... Published: December 12, 2025 unusual light mysterious light aerial phenomenon strange object luminous object These phrases should be combined with place names rather than searched in isolation. For example, \"flying saucer Lakenheath\" or \"unusual light Norfolk\" is more likely to retrieve relevant catalogue entries than simply \"flying saucer\". The National Archives' own research guidance explicitly recommends searching with historically appropriate vocabulary because official terminology evolved over time. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... 9:47 UFO file release October 2008 Channel: The National Archives UK · Views: 19.5K · Uploaded: October 2008 · Length: 9 minutes Open on YouTube AIR files often hide relevant material Early RAF related reports are not necessarily filed under obvious UFO headings. A good example is AIR 2/17318 , an Air Ministry file containing correspondence on UFO sightings and letters discussing possible vehicles from other planets during the early 1960s. Rather than being a register of investigations, it preserves public correspondence alongside official replies and conventional explanations such as Venus, weather balloons, satellites and aircraft. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFO reports National ArchivesUFO reports The National Archives... This illustrates an important research principle: file titles may refer broadly to correspondence while the contents include individual sightings linked to particular places and dates. Avoid false matches in aviation records Searching RAF locations inevitably retrieves many ordinary operational documents. Common false positives include: flying accidents unidentified aircraft during exercises radar tracking records aircraft recognition training meteorological observations air traffic administration Not every reference to an \"unidentified aircraft\" concerns a UFO report. During the Cold War, the phrase often referred to unidentified military or civilian aircraft detected by radar rather than unexplained aerial phenomena. Reading the catalogue description carefully—and then browsing the surrounding series—is usually more productive than relying solely on keyword hits. Discovery's guidance stresses that many record descriptions are brief, meaning browsing remains an essential part of archival research. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives Searching for records using Discovery National ArchivesSearching for records using Discovery The National Archives... Build searches around known RAF locations A practical workflow is to begin with the location and expand only as necessary. For example: Research goalEffective search combinationEarly reports from an RAF stationStation name + \"flying saucer\" + yearLocalised sightingTown or county + \"unusual light\" + AIRAdministrative correspondenceAir Ministry + place + dateAir defence interestFighter Command + location + unidentified object This method mirrors how Air Ministry records were organised by office responsibility rather than by a modern subject classification. Browse beyond individual search results One of the most valuable techniques is to move from an individual record into its surrounding catalogue hierarchy. Discovery allows researchers to browse neighbouring references within the same series, revealing related correspondence created by the same office over the same period. This is particularly valuable where catalogue descriptions are minimal or use inconsistent terminology. For early RAF"},{"title":"Search Radius","url":"/search-radius/","description":"Nearby towns, counties, roads, and border regions can reveal related reports that an exact-place search misses.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Search by Date","level":3,"text":"Search Radius How Far From the Sighting Should You Search? Search by Date Search Radius Search Radius How Far From the Sighting Should You Search? Nearby towns, counties, roads, and border regions can reveal related reports that an exact place search misses. Nearby towns, counties, roads, and border regions can reveal related reports that an exact place search misses. UFO Report Databases implementation bounded topic population context Expanding from town to neighboring settlements Choosing a distance based on movement and duration Reconciling roads, junctions, counties, and local place names ufo report databases search by date search radius Introduction When searching UFO report databases for a sighting on a particular date, an exact town name is rarely enough. Witnesses looking at the same object may describe their location by different towns, villages, motorways, counties or landmarks, even though they observed the same event. A search that remains fixed to one place can therefore miss relevant reports, while a search that expands too widely risks mixing unrelated incidents from the same evening. The aim is not to search as far as possible, but to widen the geographic area in measured stages that match what was realistically visible and how the object was described. Expand from the Named Town Before Expanding the Date Most public UFO catalogues organise reports by the location entered by the witness rather than by the actual position of the observed object. A bright object high in the sky may therefore appear in multiple databases under different towns. A practical search sequence is: 1. Search the exact town or city named by the witness. 2. Search neighbouring towns and villages within the same local area. 3. Search the surrounding county or administrative region. 4. Search adjacent counties or states if the object appeared high above the horizon or travelled a significant distance. 5. Finally, search broader regional terms if contemporary newspapers or archives commonly used them. This staged approach reduces the chance of overlooking duplicate descriptions while avoiding unnecessary expansion into unrelated reports. NUFORC itself provides both location indexes and broader state or country indexes, illustrating that reports can be explored through multiple geographic levels rather than a single locality. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 Match the Search Radius to What Was Seen There is no universal search distance because different aerial phenomena are visible over dramatically different areas. Short Duration, Apparently Nearby Objects Keep the initial search relatively local when witnesses describe: an object hovering over a neighbourhood something apparently below cloud level low altitude movement between nearby landmarks an object interacting with trees, buildings or hills In these cases, neighbouring settlements a few kilometres away are usually more valuable than distant regional searches because perspective changes rapidly over short distances. Long Distance or High Altitude Events Increase the search radius substantially when reports describe: brilliant lights high in the sky objects crossing much of the visible horizon very long flight paths multiple observers over a wide area unusually bright flashes Large meteors, satellite re entries and rocket related phenomena frequently generate reports across several counties or even multiple states because the event occurs at very high altitude. The American Meteor Society routinely combines reports from geographically separated witnesses into single reconstructed fireball events, demonstrating why a wider geographic search is often essential before concluding that several independent UFO sightings occurred. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor SocietyAmerican Meteor Society Member Area Report a Fireball... 15:14 Inside the AI Alien Hunting Project at Harvard Channel: Bloomberg Originals · Views: 542.8K · Uploaded: June 2025 · Length: 15 minutes Open on YouTube Use Travel Distance and Viewing Duration as Clues Witness descriptions often contain enough information to estimate how widely to search. A report lasting only a few seconds with little apparent movement usually points towards either a brief local event or a fast astronomical phenomenon. Start locally before widening. By contrast, a report lasting several minutes in which an object appeared to cross much of the sky is more likely to have been visible from many surrounding communities. Those are the cases where neighbouring counties, districts or administrative regions deserve equal attention. Similarly, if witnesses describe an object moving from the direction of one town towards another, search both places even if the observer remained stationary. Another witness may have reported the same event from the object's apparent destination. Search Roads, Junctions and Local Landmarks Many reports are not filed under towns at all. Witnesses travelling by car frequently identify locations using: motorway numbers highway junctions named roundabouts bridges service stations mountain passes lakes reservoirs parks airports Someone else observing the same object from home may instead report the nearest town. Modern geographical datasets such as the Ordnance Survey's named feature collections show how roads, junctions and other named places exist alongside conventional settlements in geographic indexing. These alternative place names can be valuable search terms when reviewing reports or digitised newspaper archives. [OS Documentation] docs.os.uk Documentation Named Features OS National Geographic Database OS DocumentationNamed Features OS National Geographic Database... Expect Administrative Boundaries to Create Artificial Gaps Database boundaries rarely reflect how people actually observe the sky. A witness standing only a few hundred metres from a county or state border may file under one jurisdiction, while another observer of the same event files under another. Historical newspapers may further complicate matters by identifying the location using the nearest larger town rather than the precise village. For this reason, always search: neighbouring counties bordering states or provinces where applicable historic county names for older cases regional newspapers serving several communities This is especially important for rural sightings where small settlements may not be familiar to later researchers. 40:43 The Galileo Project's First Data on Half a Million Objects with Avi Loeb Channel: Event Horizon · Views: 68.1K · Uploaded: November 2024 · Length: 40 minutes Open on YouTube Reconcile Different Place Names for the Same Area Historical reports often use names that differ from modern mapping. Examples include: former county boundaries merged municipalities alternative spellings abbreviated place names local nicknames military bases rather than nearby civilian towns A witness might describe a location as \"near the old airfield\", while another reports the nearest village. Newspaper editors may shorten or simplify the place name further. Searching each plausible geographic label greatly improves the chance of finding matching reports without expanding into unrelated regions. Distinguish Multiple Locations from Multiple Events Finding reports from five nearby towns does not automatically mean five separate incidents occurred. Instead, compare: reported direction of travel local observation time estimated duration brightness colour shape weather conditions witness descriptions If these characteristics align closely, several geographically separated reports may represent independent observations of one aerial event rather than several unrelated UFO sightings. Conversely, reports from neighbouring towns that differ substantially in timing or description may indicate distinct incidents despite their geographic proximity. 6:49 More Evidence for UAPs! Scientists Afraid to Speak Out Channel: Sabine Hossenfelder · Views: 377.3K · Uploaded: March 2026 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube A Practical Search Strategy An efficient search radius grows in deliberate stages rather than all at once: Begin with the exact settlement named in the original account. Add neighbouring towns and villages. Include major roads, junctions and landmarks mentioned by witnesses. Search the wider county or administrative region. Extend across bordering counties or states if the object was described as high, bright or travelling a long distance. Compare the resulting reports before deciding whether they represent one shared event or multiple independent sightings. This progressive approach balances completeness against precision, increasing the likelihood of locating related reports while reducing false connections created by overly broad geographic searches. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to How Far From the Sighting"},{"title":"Busy Airspace","url":"/busy-airspace/","description":"Busy airspace can increase unfamiliar lights, observer attention, and report volume without proving that anomalous objects prefer those locations.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Report Maps","level":3,"text":"Busy Airspace Do Military Zones Really Attract UFO Reports? Report Maps Busy Airspace Busy Airspace Do Military Zones Really Attract UFO Reports? Busy airspace can increase unfamiliar lights, observer attention, and report volume without proving that anomalous objects prefer those locations. Busy airspace can increase unfamiliar lights, observer attention, and report volume without proving that anomalous objects prefer those locations. UFO Report Databases evidence bounded topic population context How ordinary flight activity produces reports What airport and military comparisons have found Why proximity does not establish a cause ufo report databases report maps busy airspace Introduction Maps of UFO or unidentified anomalous phenomenon (UAP) reports often show apparent clusters around airports, military airfields and training ranges. At first glance, these concentrations can appear to suggest that unusual objects are attracted to such locations. The available evidence points to a more cautious interpretation. Busy airspace places large numbers of aircraft, helicopters, drones, flares, satellites, military exercises and trained observers into the same landscape, creating more opportunities both for genuine observations and for understandable misidentifications. Statistical studies of public reporting databases consistently find that aviation activity influences where reports are submitted, but they do not demonstrate that unexplained phenomena preferentially occur near airports or military facilities. [Nature] nature.com An environmental analysis of public UAP sightings and sky view potential Scientific ReportsDecember 14, 2023... Published: December 14, 2023 Understanding this distinction is essential when interpreting UFO databases and catalogues. A hotspot on a map reflects where reports accumulated within a reporting system, not necessarily where anomalous objects are objectively more common. Christopher Mellon on military's UFO encounters Channel: CNBC Television Open on YouTube Do Military Zones Really Attract UFO Reports? Military bases and civilian airports are among the most closely watched parts of the sky. Pilots, air traffic controllers, ground crews, radar operators and local residents all observe aircraft movements regularly, increasing the likelihood that unusual looking objects will be noticed and reported. Several factors contribute to this effect: Aircraft arrive and depart from multiple directions throughout the day and night. Military exercises introduce unfamiliar flight patterns, high performance aircraft, aerial refuelling, parachute operations and electronic warfare activities. Training often occurs after dark, when navigation lights, afterburners and formation flying can appear unusual from the ground. Temporary restrictions, test flights and classified programmes mean that observers may see aircraft they cannot readily identify. These conditions increase both the number of aerial objects present and the number of people actively watching them. Higher report totals therefore become an expected consequence of greater exposure rather than evidence that unexplained objects favour those locations. [Nature] nature.com An environmental analysis of public UAP sightings and sky view potential Scientific ReportsDecember 14, 2023... Published: December 14, 2023 1:44 Pentagon UFO Files Reveal Chilling Encounters Near Secret Military Bases WION Originals Channel: WION · Views: 5.5K · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 1 minute 44 seconds Open on YouTube How Ordinary Flight Activity Produces Reports Many common aviation activities can resemble classic UFO descriptions under particular viewing conditions. Landing aircraft approaching an airport can appear almost stationary when flying directly towards an observer because their forward motion is compressed by perspective. Bright landing lights may remain visible for several minutes before suddenly seeming to move sideways as the aircraft changes angle. Military aircraft introduce additional complexity. Fast jets may accelerate rapidly, climb steeply or disappear suddenly into cloud. Formation flights can create patterns of lights that seem unlike conventional aircraft, especially when navigation lights become visible or hidden as the formation changes direction. Other routine sources of reports include: Helicopters hovering with powerful searchlights. Military illumination flares descending slowly beneath parachutes. Airborne refuelling operations involving several aircraft. Large transport aircraft flying unusual military routes. High altitude aircraft reflecting sunlight after local sunset. Increased drone operations around airports and security zones. Each of these can generate sincere reports from witnesses unfamiliar with the specific operation being observed. What Airport and Military Comparisons Have Found Recent statistical research has examined whether airports and military activity help explain the geographical distribution of UFO reports rather than simply noting where reports occur. A peer reviewed study analysing nearly 99,000 U.S. reports submitted between 2001 and 2020 modelled reporting rates against environmental variables including airport density, military installations, cloud cover, tree cover and light pollution. The researchers found that reports were more common where opportunities to observe aircraft were greater and where the sky was more visible, supporting the interpretation that human observation conditions play an important role in shaping report distributions. Airport density showed a positive association with reporting after accounting for other environmental factors. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)An environmental analysis of public UAP sightings and sky view potential PMC... Government reporting shows a different but related pattern. The U.S. Department of Defense's All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) notes that its own data are strongly affected by collection bias because military personnel and sensors operate extensively in restricted airspace. As reporting has expanded to include more commercial aviation, the geographical distribution has become broader, illustrating how changes in who reports can alter apparent hotspots without implying any change in the underlying phenomenon. [AARO] aaro.mil FY23 Consolidated Annual Report Annual Report to Congress on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena... Other spatial analyses have reached similarly cautious conclusions. NUFORC 's own mapping work explicitly warns that clusters identify places where many compatible reports occurred, not proof of military testing, extraterrestrial activity or any other particular cause. The maps are intended as starting points for investigation rather than answers in themselves. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Spatial Hotspots in NUFORC Reports – An Analysis NUFORCNovember 8, 2023... Published: November 8, 2023 Research using France's GEIPAN database likewise found that factors such as population density and environmental characteristics help explain report intensity, while some residual clusters remain after modelling. Those remaining concentrations represent questions for further investigation, not automatic evidence that military airspace attracts unexplained objects. [arXiv] arxiv.org Spatial Point Pattern Analysis of the Unidentified Aerial Phenomena in FranceSeptember 2, 2015... Published: September 2, 2015 US military stumped by UFO sightings over bases: Ex defense official Elizabeth Vargas Reports Channel: NewsNation Open on YouTube Why Proximity Does Not Establish a Cause One of the most common mistakes in interpreting UFO hotspot maps is confusing geographical correlation with causation. A cluster near an airport may result from several overlapping influences: Thousands of aircraft movements each week. Large nearby populations. Better opportunities to view the sky from open surroundings. Greater public awareness of unusual aircraft. Higher likelihood that multiple witnesses independently report the same event. Exactly the same caution applies to military installations. Even where report densities appear elevated, they do not show that military activity creates unexplained phenomena or that anomalous objects preferentially visit military sites. They demonstrate only that reports occur more frequently in those environments after all the observational and reporting factors have combined. Some studies have even found differing relationships depending on which military variables are examined. For example, analyses have distinguished between permanent military installations and designated military operations areas used for flight training, highlighting that different aspects of military aviation can influence reporting in different ways. Such findings reinforce the need to examine specific mechanisms rather than treating all military locations as a single category. [SkyWatch Center] skywatchcenter.com Sky Watch Center RAND Corp study of"},{"title":"Database Maps","url":"/database-maps/","description":"The same world can look very different when one catalogue uses public submissions and another relies on government investigation.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Report Maps","level":3,"text":"Database Maps Why UFO Databases Map Different Worlds Report Maps Database Maps Database Maps Why UFO Databases Map Different Worlds The same world can look very different when one catalogue uses public submissions and another relies on government investigation. The same world can look very different when one catalogue uses public submissions and another relies on government investigation. UFO Report Databases historical comparative bounded topic dataset evidence Who each reporting system reaches How intake and publication rules differ What cross database comparisons can reveal ufo report databases report maps database maps Introduction UFO maps are not neutral pictures of where unusual aerial phenomena occur. They are visualisations of where particular reporting systems collect, investigate and publish reports. As a result, the same event can appear prominently in one database yet be absent from another, while entire regions may seem quiet simply because no widely used reporting network operates there. Understanding a UFO map therefore begins with understanding the reporting system behind it. Different catalogues were built for different purposes. Some exist to encourage public reporting, others to investigate selected cases, and some publish only reports that meet specific administrative or technical requirements. These design choices shape every map they produce, affecting geographic coverage, case density and the kinds of reports available for analysis. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFAQ GEIPAN GEIPANFAQ GEIPAN Who Each Reporting System Reaches The first source of variation is the audience that each reporting system serves. Public reporting databases such as the National UFO Reporting Center ( NUFORC ) are designed to accept reports directly from witnesses with relatively few barriers to submission. This produces a large archive that captures everything from brief lights in the sky to lengthy narrative accounts. Because reporting is voluntary, however, the database reflects where people know about NUFORC, feel motivated to report and have internet or telephone access as much as it reflects the underlying distribution of sightings. [narcap.org] narcap.org Platte, John Michael Guttierez, Ted Roe, Ryan Graves, 2023 — National Aviation Reporting Center on Anomalous PhenomenaJune 14, 2023... Published: June 14, 2023 By contrast, France's GEIPAN , which operates within the French space agency CNES, uses a structured intake process centred on a detailed technical questionnaire. Witnesses submit supporting material where available, investigations follow a defined methodology and only eligible aerospace phenomena are accepted. The resulting map represents reports that have entered this official investigative process rather than every observation members of the public considered unusual. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANFAQ GEIPAN GEIPANFAQ GEIPAN Other organisations occupy different positions between these models. MUFON accepts public submissions but also assigns many cases to volunteer investigators for follow up. Its published material therefore reflects both public reporting and organisational investigation, while access to detailed case information is more restricted than in completely open databases. [UAPedia Unlocking New Realities] uapedia.ai Unlocking New Realities NUFORC & MUFON: Civilian Data Sources Unlocking New RealitiesNUFORC & MUFON: Civilian Data Sources UAPediaNovember 7, 2025... Published: November 7, 2025 These institutional differences mean that two databases covering the same country can legitimately produce different geographical patterns even before any comparison of the reports themselves begins. 52:34 Use ASRS to Report UAP and UFO Encounters Episode 234 Channel: Flight Safety Detectives · Views: 3.7K · Uploaded: August 2024 · Length: 52 minutes Open on YouTube How Intake and Publication Rules Change the Map Reporting systems differ not only in who can submit reports but also in what eventually appears on a public map. Several design choices have particularly large effects: Submission requirements. A simple online form encourages many reports, whereas lengthy questionnaires reduce participation but often improve data quality. Screening before publication. Some organisations publish reports soon after receipt, while others investigate or classify cases before release. Eligibility rules. Government systems may reject reports outside their remit, such as paranormal claims or observations lacking sufficient information. Privacy policies. Some organisations anonymise reports or withhold precise locations, affecting how points appear geographically. Resource constraints. Limited investigators or staff can create delays, meaning publication dates do not necessarily match observation dates. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMethodology GEIPAN GEIPANMethodology GEIPAN GEIPAN illustrates how publication policy can reshape a national map. Straightforward cases may be resolved quickly and not proceed to publication, while investigated files are anonymised, classified and released only after processing. Consequently, its public map reflects completed case handling rather than a live feed of incoming reports. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMethodology GEIPAN GEIPANMethodology GEIPAN Conversely, a public submission database may display many recent reports because its objective is rapid collection rather than exhaustive investigation. This creates a map with greater temporal immediacy but more variation in report quality and completeness. [narcap.org] narcap.org Platte, John Michael Guttierez, Ted Roe, Ryan Graves, 2023 — National Aviation Reporting Center on Anomalous PhenomenaJune 14, 2023... Published: June 14, 2023 Why National Boundaries Often Reflect Institutions Rather Than Phenomena International comparisons require particular caution because reporting infrastructure varies dramatically between countries. A country with an active official investigation programme, established civilian reporting organisations and widespread public awareness is likely to accumulate many more records than a country without comparable institutions. Differences in language, media attention, trust in government, internet access and cultural attitudes towards reporting also influence participation. This means that an apparent national \"hotspot\" may indicate an efficient reporting ecosystem rather than an unusually high frequency of unexplained aerial observations. Likewise, sparse reporting from another country may reflect the absence of a well known reporting pathway instead of an absence of sightings. The resulting maps therefore measure reporting opportunity as much as reported experience. Pentagon has new surveillance system for UFO reports NewsNation Now Channel: NewsNation Open on YouTube What Cross Database Comparisons Can Reveal Comparing multiple catalogues is often more informative than relying on any single database. When similar clusters appear independently across different reporting systems, researchers gain greater confidence that they are observing a genuine reporting pattern rather than an artefact created by one organisation's procedures. Differences between databases can also reveal institutional effects. For example: A concentration appearing only in a public submission database may reflect local publicity or reporting campaigns. A cluster surviving both public reporting and official investigation deserves closer examination because it persists despite different intake and filtering rules. Regions that disappear entirely when moving from an open database to an investigated database may indicate that many reports were resolved during case assessment rather than representing fundamentally different observations. Researchers also use cross database comparisons to identify duplicate reports, reconcile differing classifications and estimate reporting biases before conducting statistical analyses. Because many databases contain overlapping cases submitted to multiple organisations, careful matching is necessary to avoid counting the same event more than once. [Pia] p i a.com Pia SCU 10.5281/zenodo.10287332 The Reported Shape, SDecember 11, 2023... Published: December 11, 2023 Reading UFO Maps as Products of Reporting Systems The most useful question to ask of any UFO map is not simply, \"Where are the sightings?\" but \"What reporting system produced this pattern?\" Every catalogue embodies decisions about who can report, what information is required, which cases are investigated, what is published and how locations are displayed. Those institutional choices influence the resulting geography just as surely as the underlying reports themselves. Rather than treating competing databases as contradictory, they are better understood as complementary views of different reporting populations. Public submission systems reveal broad witness participation, investigative systems document more structured case assessment, and comparisons between them"},{"title":"Geocoding Bias","url":"/geocoding-bias/","description":"Geocoding broad place names to a single downtown point can make scattered reports look like a concentrated urban hotspot.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Report Maps","level":3,"text":"Geocoding Bias When Map Pins Manufacture a UFO Hotspot Report Maps Geocoding Bias Geocoding Bias When Map Pins Manufacture a UFO Hotspot Geocoding broad place names to a single downtown point can make scattered reports look like a concentrated urban hotspot. Geocoding broad place names to a single downtown point can make scattered reports look like a concentrated urban hotspot. UFO Report Databases mechanism single mechanism mechanism How place names become coordinates Why default map points create clusters Ways to test and correct geocoding bias ufo report databases report maps geocoding bias Introduction UFO report maps are only as accurate as the locations assigned to each report. When a witness says only \"Manchester\", \"Phoenix\" or \"Lyon\", a database often has to convert that place name into a single pair of latitude and longitude coordinates before it can display the report on a map. This process, known as geocoding, can unintentionally create dense clusters of markers in city centres even when the original sightings were scattered across suburbs, industrial estates, rural outskirts or nearby villages. The result is an apparent urban \"hotspot\" that reflects a mapping decision rather than the true distribution of observations. [Google for Developers] developers.google.com for Developers Geocoding API v4 overview Google for Developers Google for DevelopersGeocoding API v4 overview Google for DevelopersJuly 20, 2026... Published: July 20, 2026 For anyone interpreting UFO catalogues, this distinction is crucial. A concentration of map pins may indicate nothing more than the database assigning multiple reports with vague locations to the same default point. When Map Pins Manufacture a UFO Hotspot How place names become coordinates A UFO report rarely arrives with survey grade coordinates. Older reports may provide only a town name, while many modern online submissions include a city but omit a street address for privacy. Before the report can appear on an interactive map, the place description must be translated into coordinates through a geocoding service or a gazetteer—a database of named places. [Google for Developers] developers.google.com for Developers Geocoding API overview Google for Developers Google for DevelopersGeocoding API overview Google for Developers... When the location is imprecise, the geocoder must choose a representative point for the named place. Depending on the mapping system, that point may be: the administrative centre of the city; a calculated geographic centroid; the historic town centre; the centre of the mapped urban boundary; or another standard reference point associated with the place name. None of these locations necessarily corresponds to where the witness actually made the observation. They merely provide a convenient coordinate that allows the record to be displayed on a map. [Google for Developers] developers.google.com for Developers Geocode an address Geocoding API Google for Developers Google for DevelopersGeocode an address Geocoding API Google for DevelopersJuly 20, 2026... Published: July 20, 2026 6:20 First Ever UFO Database Reveals Strange Pattern (S21) Ancient Aliens History Channel: HISTORY · Views: 118.8K · Uploaded: September 2025 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube Why default map points create clusters The bias appears when many reports share the same broad place name. Imagine fifty witnesses over several decades reporting sightings from different parts of Birmingham. Some observations occur near the airport, others from northern suburbs, parks or surrounding countryside. If every report simply lists \"Birmingham\" and the database assigns them all to one central coordinate, every marker will occupy essentially the same position. On the finished map this produces what looks like a remarkably dense hotspot. In reality, the reports may be spread across hundreds of square kilometres. The distortion becomes stronger because interactive maps display overlapping markers as a single dense stack or a visually striking cluster. Zooming out reinforces the illusion that the city centre experienced an exceptional concentration of sightings when, in fact, the clustering originated during data processing rather than in the observations themselves. NUFORC explicitly cautions that its public map contains city level locations and that reports tend to cluster where only a city name is available, illustrating exactly this form of geocoding artefact rather than necessarily indicating a genuine concentration of events. [Google for Developers] developers.google.com for Developers Geocoding API overview Google for Developers Google for DevelopersGeocoding API overview Google for Developers... Why the Bias Matters for UFO Analysis Artificial clustering affects several common interpretations of UFO databases. First, it can exaggerate apparent urban hotspots. Researchers comparing cities may conclude that one downtown area generated unusually large numbers of reports, when many reports actually originated throughout the wider metropolitan region. Second, spatial statistics can become misleading. Techniques designed to identify genuine geographic concentrations assume that mapped coordinates approximate the observation location. If hundreds of records share identical fallback coordinates, statistical hotspot analyses may detect significance driven partly by duplicated locations rather than by an underlying phenomenon. Third, comparisons with environmental features become less reliable. Analysts may incorrectly associate sightings with airports, military facilities, coastlines or geological features simply because the assigned city centre coordinate happens to lie near those landmarks, even though the original observation occurred elsewhere. This is why careful spatial studies distinguish between precise observation coordinates and approximate locality references whenever possible, rather than treating every point as equally accurate. [Google for Developers] developers.google.com for Developers Geocode an address Geocoding API Google for Developers Google for DevelopersGeocode an address Geocoding API Google for DevelopersJuly 20, 2026... Published: July 20, 2026 Pentagon launches new UFO database Channel: FOX 13 Seattle Open on YouTube Ways to Test and Correct Geocoding Bias Researchers have several practical methods for identifying whether city centre pins are manufacturing apparent hotspots. Inspect repeated coordinates. If dozens or hundreds of reports from a large city share exactly the same latitude and longitude, they almost certainly reflect a default geocoding choice rather than identical observation locations. Record spatial uncertainty. Instead of treating every coordinate as equally precise, databases can distinguish between exact GPS positions, street level locations, neighbourhood level locations and city level approximations. This allows analyses to account for different levels of positional confidence. Use larger geographic units. If reports only identify a municipality, mapping them as belonging to that administrative area rather than a single point often provides a more honest representation. Compare coordinate precision. Analysts can test whether apparent hotspots persist after excluding reports with only city level locations. If the hotspot disappears, the clustering was probably driven by geocoding rather than observation patterns. Preserve the original location description. Retaining the witness's text alongside derived coordinates allows later researchers to improve the geocoding as better geographic information becomes available, rather than treating an estimated point as permanent. These approaches are common practice in geographic information systems because geocoding is recognised as an interpretive step rather than a neutral conversion of text into location. [google.com] developers.google.com for Developers Geocode an address Geocoding API Google for Developers Google for DevelopersGeocode an address Geocoding API Google for DevelopersJuly 20, 2026... Published: July 20, 2026 A Map Pin Is Not the Observation The most important lesson is that a map marker represents the database's best available estimate of location, not necessarily the place where the witness stood. In UFO catalogues, many reports originate from incomplete place descriptions, forcing compilers to choose a representative coordinate. That decision is essential for mapping, but it also introduces the possibility of artificial hotspots. For this reason, dense clusters in city centres should be interpreted cautiously. They may indicate genuine concentrations of reports, but they may equally reflect the routine geocoding practice of assigning broad place names to a single default point. Distinguishing between those two possibilities is a necessary first step before drawing conclusions from the geography of UFO reports. Analyzing UFO Risk with PostGIS and H3 with Martin Davis Channel: Crunchy Data Open on YouTube"},{"title":"Small Numbers","url":"/small-numbers/","description":"A few reports can produce an eye-catching per-capita rate in a small county even when the underlying evidence is thin.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Report Maps","level":3,"text":"Small Numbers Can a Few Reports Make a Hotspot? Report Maps Small Numbers Small Numbers Can a Few Reports Make a Hotspot? A few reports can produce an eye catching per capita rate in a small county even when the underlying evidence is thin. A few reports can produce an eye catching per capita rate in a small county even when the underlying evidence is thin. UFO Report Databases critique risk bounded topic population context Why raw totals favor large populations How small denominators inflate local rates When smoothing and uncertainty change rankings ufo report databases report maps small numbers Introduction Population adjusted UFO maps are an improvement over simple report totals because they recognise that places with more people will usually generate more reports. However, dividing reports by population creates a different statistical problem. In sparsely populated counties or districts, only a handful of reports can produce an apparently exceptional per capita rate, making a rural area look like a major hotspot even when the underlying evidence is very limited. This is a classic \"small numbers\" problem found throughout epidemiology, demography and crime mapping. The same statistical caution that applies to disease rates or accident rates also applies to UFO report databases . A high rate calculated from three reports among a few hundred residents is far less stable than a similar rate calculated from hundreds of reports in a large city. As a result, population adjusted hotspot maps should be interpreted alongside the underlying report counts, measures of uncertainty and, where possible, statistical smoothing techniques rather than by ranking rates alone. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Disease mapping PubMed Central (PMC)Disease mapping PMCJanuary 1, 2010... Published: January 1, 2010 Why Raw Totals Favour Large Populations Raw counts naturally concentrate around large metropolitan areas because more people are available both to observe unusual aerial phenomena and to submit reports. This does not necessarily imply that cities experience more genuinely unexplained events; it largely reflects greater opportunity for observation and reporting. Population adjustment attempts to compensate for this bias by expressing reports relative to the number of residents. Within UFO databases, this is an important first correction because otherwise the largest cities dominate almost every map. However, the adjustment only removes one source of distortion. It does not account for the reliability of the calculated rate, which depends heavily on how many reports generated it. Reported UFO sightings across the US Backscroll Channel: NewsNation Open on YouTube Can a Few Reports Make a Hotspot? The answer is yes. Small populations produce unstable rates because every additional report changes the denominator far more dramatically than it would in a densely populated area. Consider a simplified example: County A has 1 report and 500 residents. County B has 200 reports and 500,000 residents. County A's per capita rate appears five times higher despite relying on a single observation. If one additional witness files a report, its calculated rate immediately doubles. If the original report is later removed as a duplicate or mislocated, the apparent hotspot disappears entirely. By contrast, adding or removing one report from County B barely changes its calculated rate. The estimate is therefore much more stable and more likely to reflect a persistent pattern rather than statistical chance. For this reason, statisticians routinely distinguish between high rates supported by many observations and high rates created by very small sample sizes . The latter should be treated as hypotheses requiring further investigation rather than convincing evidence of an unusually active location. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Small Area Estimation for Disease Prevalence Mapping PubMed Central (PMC)Small Area Estimation for Disease Prevalence Mapping PMC... 51:24 Breaking Down UAP Footage with the Head of The Pentagon’s UAP Taskforce, Dr. Jon Kosloski Channel: StarTalk · Views: 1.5M · Uploaded: August 2025 · Length: 51 minutes Open on YouTube How Small Denominators Inflate Local Rates Small area mapping has long recognised that crude rates become increasingly variable as population size falls. The mathematical reason is straightforward: when the denominator is small, random variation has a much larger proportional effect. For UFO reporting, several situations can produce misleading rural hotspots: One exceptional evening. A meteor, satellite train or military exercise may generate several reports in a lightly populated county, creating an unusually high annual rate. Multiple witnesses to one event. Several reports describing the same incident may all be counted separately if the database records individual submissions. Repeat reporters. Although many databases attempt quality control, repeated submissions from the same area can disproportionately influence local rates where total numbers are otherwise tiny. Boundary effects. Counties and municipalities differ enormously in both land area and resident population. Two neighbouring places experiencing similar observations may end up with very different per capita rates simply because their administrative populations differ. These effects do not imply that rural hotspots are false. Rather, they indicate that extreme rankings become increasingly uncertain as the underlying counts become smaller. This is one reason why statistical agencies rarely publish small area rate maps without additional methods to address instability. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Disease mapping PubMed Central (PMC)Disease mapping PMCJanuary 1, 2010... Published: January 1, 2010 When Smoothing and Uncertainty Change Rankings Modern spatial analysis rarely relies on crude local rates alone. Instead, researchers often apply smoothing methods that reduce the influence of random fluctuations in sparsely populated areas. One widely used approach is Empirical Bayes smoothing , which partially adjusts extreme local rates towards the regional average when the available evidence is weak. Areas supported by many observations change very little, while areas based on only one or two observations are moderated more substantially. Similar Bayesian spatial methods also allow neighbouring areas to contribute information, reducing the likelihood that isolated spikes are mistaken for meaningful clusters. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Mapping disease and mortality rates using empirical Bayes estimators Mapping disease and mortality rates using empirical Bayes estimators PubMed... In practice, smoothing frequently changes hotspot rankings: isolated rural peaks often become less extreme; persistent clusters supported by numerous reports remain prominent; confidence increases that surviving hotspots represent genuine spatial patterns rather than statistical noise. Researchers in public health use these techniques because crude small area maps can overemphasise random variation. The same reasoning applies when analysing geographically referenced UFO reports. [wiley.com] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Online Library BAYESIAN SMOOTHING OF RATES IN SMALL GEOGRAPHIC AREAS Wiley Online LibraryBAYESIAN SMOOTHING OF RATES IN SMALL GEOGRAPHIC AREAS Cressie 1995 Journal of Regional Science Wiley Online... 1:06:54 The UFO Evidence I'm Taking Seriously Channel: Astrum Extra · Views: 495.5K · Uploaded: October 2025 · Length: 1 hour 6 minutes Open on YouTube Reading Rural Hotspots More Carefully A high per capita UFO reporting rate is therefore best viewed as the beginning of an investigation, not its conclusion. Before interpreting a rural county as unusually significant, several questions are worth asking: How many reports actually produced the reported rate? Were they independent events or multiple accounts of the same incident? Did they occur across many years or during a single evening? Does the hotspot remain after applying smoothing or uncertainty estimates? Does the pattern persist across independent databases, or only within one catalogue? These checks help distinguish genuinely persistent concentrations from statistical artefacts created by sparse populations and limited observations. Population adjustment remains an essential improvement over raw report totals, but it is not the final step. The most reliable geographic analyses combine per capita measures with minimum report thresholds, uncertainty estimates and spatial smoothing so that eye catching rural hotspots are judged by both their rate and the strength of the evidence supporting them. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Can a Few Reports Make a Hotspot?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Art of Statistics: How to Learn from Data By David"},{"title":"Time Filters","url":"/time-filters/","description":"A permanent-looking cluster may disappear when reports are grouped by hour, revealing one shared meteor, satellite train, balloon, or re-entry.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Report Maps","level":3,"text":"Time Filters Is That Hotspot Just One Night? Report Maps Time Filters Time Filters Is That Hotspot Just One Night? A permanent looking cluster may disappear when reports are grouped by hour, revealing one shared meteor, satellite train, balloon, or re entry. A permanent looking cluster may disappear when reports are grouped by hour, revealing one shared meteor, satellite train, balloon, or re entry. UFO Report Databases implementation single case aspect event time window Why cumulative maps hide short lived events How time windows expose shared sightings Clues that multiple reports describe one cause ufo report databases report maps time filters Introduction A map showing thousands of UFO reports collected over decades can give the impression that certain places are permanent hotspots. In many cases, however, that impression changes dramatically once the data are filtered by time. A cluster that appears highly significant on a cumulative map may shrink to a single evening, a few hours, or even a few minutes, revealing that dozens of witnesses were independently reporting the same meteor, satellite train, rocket launch, atmospheric re entry or other shared event rather than repeated unexplained activity over many years. Time filtering is therefore one of the most important analytical tools in UFO report databases because it distinguishes persistent reporting patterns from short lived observational episodes. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Spatial Hotspots in NUFORC Reports – An Analysis NUFORCNovember 8, 2023... Published: November 8, 2023 Is That Hotspot Just One Night? Geographic maps combine reports from many different dates into a single image. This is useful for showing where a database has accumulated observations, but it also hides the chronology of those reports. A town with 200 reports over forty years is fundamentally different from a town where 60 reports arrived within two hours on the same evening. Without temporal filtering, both situations can appear as equally dense clusters. Once reports are grouped by hour, day or week, investigators can immediately see whether they are examining a long term reporting pattern or a single event that produced many independent accounts. This distinction prevents analysts from mistaking a temporary surge in witnesses for evidence of an enduring geographic anomaly. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Spatial Hotspots in NUFORC Reports – An Analysis NUFORCNovember 8, 2023... Published: November 8, 2023 NUFORC 's database structure illustrates why this matters. It indexes reports by both the event date and the date the report was submitted, allowing researchers to separate when something was seen from when people later chose to report it. That distinction helps avoid confusing a backlog of submissions with a genuine spike in observations. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO Sightings... How Time Windows Expose Shared Sightings The simplest implementation is to compare several time windows over the same area. A cumulative map may show years of reports concentrated around one location. Applying progressively narrower filters often reveals very different stories: Multi year view: identifies long term reporting density. Monthly view: highlights periods of increased activity. Daily view: separates independent events occurring weeks apart. Hourly view: often exposes a single shared observation witnessed by many people. When many reports appear within a narrow time window and describe similar movement, brightness, direction and duration, investigators have reason to suspect that multiple witnesses observed the same underlying event rather than unrelated phenomena. Conversely, if reports from the same location occur months or years apart and describe very different objects, they are better treated as unrelated cases that merely share geography. Time therefore becomes a filtering variable rather than simply another field in the database. Tracking UFOs (UAP) at Colombia's Ancient Portal Channel: History Fuzz Open on YouTube Why One Event Can Create an Apparent Hotspot Many well understood sky events naturally generate bursts of reports across large areas. Examples include: bright fireballs and meteor events; satellite trains such as newly launched Starlink groups; rocket launches and upper stage re entries; high altitude balloons; unusual atmospheric optical effects. These events are visible simultaneously over wide regions, meaning hundreds of observers may independently report essentially the same object. When every report is plotted without considering time, the result can resemble a persistent hotspot despite representing only one evening's observations. This is why modern reporting organisations increasingly encourage witnesses to compare the sighting time with satellite passes, launch schedules and other known astronomical events before treating a report as anomalous. NUFORC's own reporting guidance specifically highlights Starlink satellite trains and rocket launches as common sources of multiple reports occurring during the same period. [NUFORC] nuforc.org report a ufo File a UFO Report NUFORCSeptember 13, 2021... Published: September 13, 2021 Clues That Multiple Reports Describe One Cause Time filtering becomes especially powerful when combined with other report fields. Investigators look for combinations such as: observation times within minutes of one another; similar directions of travel; matching descriptions of colour, shape or brightness; comparable duration estimates; witnesses spread across neighbouring towns observing the same trajectory. None of these factors alone proves that reports concern the same object. Together, however, they often reveal a coherent event moving across a landscape rather than numerous unrelated incidents. In contrast, reports sharing only the same location but differing widely in date, appearance and behaviour are much less likely to represent a single phenomenon. UFO Cover Up Exposed: Navy Pilot Forces Government To Admit UFOs Are Real Ryan Graves Channel: Dr. Mayim Bialik Open on YouTube Time Filters Improve Database Interpretation Temporal filtering also reduces several common analytical errors. A sudden increase in reports may reflect: a highly visible astronomical event; widespread media attention encouraging submissions; social media rapidly spreading awareness of an unusual sight; delayed reporting after investigators publicise a reporting service. Separating observation time from submission time helps identify these effects. A database may receive dozens of reports on the same day even though the sightings themselves occurred weeks apart, or it may receive reports over several weeks describing one widely observed event. Keeping these timelines distinct prevents false conclusions about when activity actually occurred. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO Sightings... From Static Maps to Event Based Analysis Time filters shift UFO databases from being simple collections of points on a map to tools for reconstructing real world observation events. Rather than asking only, \"Where are the reports?\", investigators can ask, \"Which reports belong to the same episode?\" That approach produces more meaningful comparisons with meteor records, satellite predictions, rocket launches and atmospheric re entry data, because those phenomena are defined by precise times as well as locations. A permanent looking hotspot that dissolves into one tightly grouped evening is not necessarily less interesting, but it represents a different kind of pattern: a shared observational event rather than evidence that one location consistently generates unusual reports. By treating time as an essential analytical filter alongside geography, UFO databases become better at distinguishing enduring reporting trends from brief episodes that naturally attract many witnesses. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Spatial Hotspots in NUFORC Reports – An Analysis NUFORCNovember 8, 2023... Published: November 8, 2023 The Truth Behind 75 Years of UFO Encounters Alien Files Reopened Full Season 1 Marathon Channel: SpaceRip Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Is That Hotspot Just One Night?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book UFOs: Generals, Pilots, and Government Officials Go on the Re... By Leslie Kean NEW YORK TIMES BESTSELLER • Impeccably researched, this riveting journalistic investigation separates fact from fiction, and documents th... See on Amazon Book The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects By Edward J Ruppelt The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects is a book by Edward J. Ruppelt which described the study of UFOs by United States Air Force fro... See on Amazon Book The Signal and the Noise: Why So Many Predictions Fail—but So... By Nate Silver This work explores the history, art and science of prediction. See on Amazon Book The UFO Experience: A Scientific Inquiry By Joseph"},{"title":"JAL 1628 Files","url":"/jal-1628-files/","description":"FAA records on Japan Airlines Flight 1628 preserve an aviation-centered view of the 1986 Alaska encounter, including traffic-control and public-information","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / US Archives","level":3,"text":"JAL 1628 Files What FAA Files Add to the JAL 1628 Case US Archives JAL 1628 Files JAL 1628 Files What FAA Files Add to the JAL 1628 Case FAA records on Japan Airlines Flight 1628 preserve an aviation centered view of the 1986 Alaska encounter, including traffic control and public information FAA records on Japan Airlines Flight 1628 preserve an aviation centered view of the 1986 Alaska encounter, including traffic control and public information UFO Report Databases evidence single case event time window The Encounter and Its Archival Setting What Aviation Records Emphasize Limits of Reading an FAA File as a UFO Verdict ufo report databases us archives jal 1628 files Introduction The Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) archive relating to Japan Airlines Flight 1628 occupies a distinctive place within the wider US National Archives UFO collections because it preserves an aviation record rather than a dedicated UFO investigation. The files concern the well known encounter reported by the crew of Japan Airlines Cargo Flight 1628 over Alaska on 17 November 1986, but their primary purpose was to document how the FAA responded to an operational event affecting controlled airspace. As a result, the collection offers valuable insight into air traffic control, communications, radar information, interviews and public information handling, while stopping well short of providing an official verdict about what the crew encountered. Rather than answering whether the sighting involved an extraordinary object, the archive reveals how a civil aviation agency documented, evaluated and managed an unusual report within its regulatory responsibilities. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... The Encounter and Its Archival Setting On the evening of 17 November 1986, the crew of Japan Airlines Flight 1628 reported observing unusual illuminated objects while flying over Alaska. The event attracted considerable attention because it involved an experienced international cargo crew, occurred within controlled airspace, and generated communications with FAA air traffic controllers during the flight. Within the National Archives, the incident appears not as an Air Force investigative case but as a Federal Aviation Administration series entitled Information Releases Relating to Unidentified Flying Object, 1986 (FAA—Japan Airlines Flight 1628) . This organisational detail is important. The collection exists because the FAA was responsible for managing aircraft operating safely within the National Airspace System, recording operational communications and responding to public enquiries, not because the agency maintained a programme for investigating unidentified aerial phenomena as a separate scientific subject. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... That archival context helps explain why researchers find different kinds of evidence here than they would in Project Blue Book . Instead of classification reports or intelligence assessments, the FAA preserved the operational record generated while an unusual event unfolded. What Aviation Records Emphasise The FAA collection is valuable precisely because it captures the practical documentation produced during an aviation incident. According to the National Archives catalogue, the series includes: FAA information releases issued after the incident. Radar related material documenting what controllers observed or reviewed. Interviews with members of the flight crew. Administrative records connected with the agency's handling of the event. [UAP Records Archive] uap archive.org faa japan airlines 1628 1986 UAP Records ArchiveJapan Airlines Flight 1628 UFO Records, 1986 (FAA) — NARA UAP Records Archive... These records allow researchers to reconstruct several aspects of the FAA's response. Air Traffic Control Communications The archive documents how controllers interacted with the flight while the crew reported unusual objects. This provides a chronological operational record rather than recollections written years later. For aviation historians, these communications are often more informative than later public debates because they show what information was available to controllers in real time, what questions were asked, and how standard procedures were followed while maintaining flight safety. 47:05 UAP hotspots: Are the US and Japan working together to research UAPs? Reality Check Channel: NewsNation · Views: 293.6K · Uploaded: September 2025 · Length: 47 minutes Open on YouTube Radar Material One of the most frequently discussed elements of the case is the radar evidence. The FAA records include radar related documentation generated during the agency's review. Importantly, these materials document what radar operators examined and how radar information entered the administrative record; they do not amount to a definitive technical proof that an unidentified craft was independently tracked throughout the encounter. Different analysts have interpreted the radar material differently over the years, making the underlying FAA documentation especially valuable because it preserves the original operational evidence instead of later summaries. [UAP Records Archive] uap archive.org faa japan airlines 1628 1986 UAP Records ArchiveJapan Airlines Flight 1628 UFO Records, 1986 (FAA) — NARA UAP Records Archive... Crew Interviews The archived interviews preserve statements from the flight crew while memories remained relatively fresh. These interviews are useful because they distinguish between: direct observations made by individual crew members; questions posed by FAA personnel; clarifications added during follow up interviews. Researchers can therefore compare witness testimony with air traffic communications and radar documentation rather than relying solely on later retellings. Public Information Management The series also contains FAA information releases prepared for the public. These documents illustrate another important function of the agency: balancing transparency with caution during an event that attracted international media attention. Rather than treating the incident as evidence for any particular explanation, the FAA documented what had been reported, what records existed, and how the agency intended to communicate publicly. [UAP Records Archive] uap archive.org faa japan airlines 1628 1986 UAP Records ArchiveJapan Airlines Flight 1628 UFO Records, 1986 (FAA) — NARA UAP Records Archive... What Makes This Archive Different from Project Blue Book Readers sometimes expect every federal UFO related collection to resemble Project Blue Book. The Flight 1628 archive demonstrates why that assumption is misleading. Project Blue Book was created specifically to catalogue and assess reports of unidentified flying objects. The FAA files were created because an aircraft operating within the civil aviation system reported something unusual that affected operational communications. That distinction changes the character of the surviving evidence. The FAA files prioritise: aviation safety; controller actions; communications; operational chronology; administrative accountability. They do not attempt to provide a comprehensive scientific or intelligence explanation for the reported object. Consequently, the archive is especially valuable for reconstructing what happened operationally, even if it cannot resolve broader questions about the nature of the sighting. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... 21:37 Japan Airlines 1628 UFO Encounter, 1986 Channel: Think Anomalous · Views: 151.9K · Uploaded: February 2022 · Length: 21 minutes Open on YouTube Limits of Reading an FAA File as a UFO Verdict One of the most common misunderstandings surrounding the Flight 1628 archive is the belief that the existence of official records constitutes government confirmation of an extraordinary object. The archival evidence supports a more cautious conclusion. The FAA preserved the documentation because the reported event fell within its operational responsibilities. Maintaining interviews, radar material and information releases demonstrates that the agency regarded the report as significant enough to document carefully. It does not mean the agency reached a definitive conclusion about what the crew observed. [UAP Records Archive] uap archive.org faa japan airlines 1628 1986 UAP Records ArchiveJapan Airlines Flight 1628 UFO Records, 1986 (FAA) — NARA UAP Records Archive... Similarly, researchers should distinguish between: documented observations , which are well preserved; interpretations of those observations , which remain disputed. Later"},{"title":"Bulk UAP Data","url":"/bulk-uap-data/","description":"NARA's bulk packages combine digital files with JSON metadata, but they require substantial cleaning before they can support statistical or computational study.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / US Archives","level":3,"text":"Bulk UAP Data What You Can Actually Do With NARA's UAP Downloads US Archives Bulk UAP Data Bulk UAP Data What You Can Actually Do With NARA's UAP Downloads NARA's bulk packages combine digital files with JSON metadata, but they require substantial cleaning before they can support statistical or computational study. NARA's bulk packages combine digital files with JSON metadata, but they require substantial cleaning before they can support statistical or computational study. UFO Report Databases mechanism single dataset dataset evidence What the Download Packages Include Why File Size and Format Matter Common Limits in Computational Analysis ufo report databases us archives bulk uap data Introduction The US National Archives and Records Administration (NARA) has made large collections of UFO and unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) records available as bulk downloads rather than requiring researchers to retrieve each catalogue item individually. These packages are significant because they combine the underlying digital files—such as PDFs, photographs, video and audio—with machine readable JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) metadata describing each catalogue entry. This structure makes the collection suitable for computational research as well as traditional archival study, although substantial data preparation is usually required before meaningful large scale analysis becomes possible. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesBulk Downloads for Records Related to Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) National ArchivesApril 24, 2025... Published: April 24, 2025 Unlike browsing the online catalogue one record at a time, bulk downloads allow researchers to build local databases, automate searches, compare metadata across agencies and preserve reproducible datasets. At the same time, the collections were designed primarily as archival exports rather than research ready datasets, so researchers must understand both their strengths and their limitations. What the Download Packages Include NARA's UAP bulk downloads are generated directly from the National Archives Catalog. Each package normally contains two complementary components: The digital objects themselves, including PDFs, scanned documents, photographs, moving images and audio where available. A JSON metadata export describing every catalogue record included in the package. This metadata typically contains identifiers such as National Archives Identifiers (NAIDs), titles, dates, record group information, descriptive text, object locations and links back to the catalogue. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesBulk Downloads for Records Related to Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) National ArchivesApril 24, 2025... Published: April 24, 2025 The packages span multiple federal agencies rather than a single historical investigation. Examples include records originating from the Federal Aviation Administration, Office of the Secretary of Defense, Office of the Director of National Intelligence and Nuclear Regulatory Commission, alongside material from older archival collections already available through the catalogue. As new transfers enter Record Group 615 under the UAP Records Collection, additional packages can be added or existing ones expanded. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesBulk Downloads for Records Related to Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) National ArchivesApril 24, 2025... Published: April 24, 2025 An important feature is that metadata and files remain linked through stable archival identifiers. Researchers can therefore reproduce analyses and cite the original archival source instead of relying on copies hosted elsewhere. Explosive Proof the Government Hid UFOs for 80 Years Channel: The Dr. Phil Podcast Open on YouTube Why File Size and Format Matter Bulk downloads fundamentally change how researchers can work with the archive, but they also introduce practical challenges. Individual ZIP archives vary considerably in size, from relatively small collections of only a few megabytes to packages exceeding 100 MB, while the complete UAP collection across all record groups can amount to hundreds of gigabytes or more than a terabyte depending on which digital objects are included. Local storage, bandwidth and processing power therefore become important considerations for anyone intending to analyse the entire corpus. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesBulk Downloads for Records Related to Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) National ArchivesApril 24, 2025... Published: April 24, 2025 The diversity of file formats also affects research methods: PDFs often contain scanned documents rather than searchable text, requiring optical character recognition (OCR). Photographs may contain little or no textual information beyond catalogue descriptions. Video and audio require specialised processing before their contents become searchable. JSON metadata is already machine readable and can usually be imported directly into databases or programming environments. Because metadata is structured while many primary documents are not, researchers often begin with metadata driven analysis before processing the underlying files. 41:31 All the videos from Pentagon's first batch of UFO files Channel: Face the Nation · Views: 61.7K · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 41 minutes Open on YouTube How Researchers Use the Metadata The JSON exports make several kinds of computational research possible without immediately reading every document manually. Researchers can: Build searchable local catalogues indexed by NAID, date, agency or record group. Measure how records are distributed across agencies and historical periods. Identify duplicate catalogue entries or records that appear in multiple series. Track additions as NARA updates the bulk downloads. Link archival records to external datasets, such as congressional releases, Freedom of Information Act disclosures or agency publications, using identifiers and descriptive metadata. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesBulk Downloads for Records Related to Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) National ArchivesApril 24, 2025... Published: April 24, 2025 This workflow is particularly valuable because the metadata can be queried in milliseconds, allowing researchers to narrow thousands of records to a manageable subset before examining the original documents in detail. The metadata also improves reproducibility. Instead of reporting that \"several FAA documents were examined,\" researchers can specify exactly which NAIDs or catalogue entries formed the dataset, making later verification much easier. Common Limits in Computational Analysis Although the bulk downloads support data driven research, they should not be mistaken for a clean analytical dataset. Several limitations routinely require additional work. Scanned rather than born digital documents. Many historical records are image scans with inconsistent OCR quality. Text extraction errors can distort keyword frequencies, named entity recognition or automated topic modelling. Inconsistent descriptions. Catalogue metadata was created over many years by different agencies and archivists. Titles, date formats and descriptive practices are not always standardised, making straightforward statistical comparison difficult. Incomplete online coverage. NARA notes that some records cannot be included because of copyright or other intellectual property restrictions. In those cases, only the catalogue description is available, and researchers must consult the originals in the National Archives research rooms. Computational studies therefore analyse only the publicly digitised portion of the archive. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesBulk Downloads for Records Related to Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) National ArchivesApril 24, 2025... Published: April 24, 2025 Collection level rather than document level metadata. Some metadata describes an entire series instead of every individual page or file, limiting fine grained quantitative analysis. Rolling updates. NARA updates the UAP bulk downloads at least three times each year, with additional updates when new material becomes available. Analyses performed at different dates may therefore use different underlying datasets unless researchers preserve version information. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesBulk Downloads for Records Related to Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) National ArchivesApril 24, 2025... Published: April 24, 2025 These characteristics mean that substantial cleaning, normalisation and validation are usually necessary before applying statistical or machine learning techniques. The secret history of America's UFO 'legacy program' explained Reality Check Channel: NewsNation Open on YouTube Turning Archive Exports into Research Datasets A typical computational workflow involves several stages before analysis begins. Researchers"},{"title":"Record Group 615","url":"/record-group-615/","description":"Record Group 615 creates a central UAP collection without replacing the older agency files where the original records remain.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / US Archives","level":3,"text":"Record Group 615 Why UFO Records Now Live in Two Archives US Archives Record Group 615 Record Group 615 Why UFO Records Now Live in Two Archives Record Group 615 creates a central UAP collection without replacing the older agency files where the original records remain. Record Group 615 creates a central UAP collection without replacing the older agency files where the original records remain. UFO Report Databases governance single policy policy intervention What Record Group 615 Contains Why Original Agency Files Still Matter How Researchers Should Search Both Systems ufo report databases us archives record group 615 Introduction Record Group (RG) 615 changes how researchers access United States government UFO and unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) records by creating a single National Archives collection for digital copies submitted under the 2024 National Defense Authorization Act (NDAA). It does not replace the long established agency record groups where the original files were created and preserved. Instead, it adds a second access point designed to make cross government UAP records easier to discover and retrieve. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection: Frequently Asked Questions National Archives National ArchivesUnidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection: Frequently Asked Questions National ArchivesApril 24, 2025 This distinction is essential for anyone researching UFO archives. Before RG 615, relevant material was dispersed across Air Force, intelligence, aviation, diplomatic and other federal collections. After the NDAA, agencies must identify qualifying UAP records, create digital copies and transfer those copies to a dedicated National Archives collection while continuing to maintain their original records according to normal federal records management practices. The result is effectively a dual archive model rather than a wholesale migration of historical files. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection: Frequently Asked Questions National Archives National ArchivesUnidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection: Frequently Asked Questions National ArchivesApril 24, 2025 What Record Group 615 Contains RG 615 was established by the National Archives and Records Administration (NARA) to implement Sections 1841–1843 of the 2024 NDAA. The legislation directs federal agencies to identify records relating to unidentified anomalous phenomena, prepare digital copies and transfer them into a central archival collection for public access where disclosure is permitted. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... Unlike traditional record groups that usually represent the permanent records of a single department or agency, RG 615 functions as a government wide aggregation point. It brings together digital copies originating from many different organisations, including defence, intelligence, aviation, scientific and diplomatic bodies, provided the records meet the statutory definition established by Congress. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection: Frequently Asked Questions National Archives National ArchivesUnidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection: Frequently Asked Questions National ArchivesApril 24, 2025 Several features distinguish the collection: It contains digital copies , not the physical archival originals. It accepts records from multiple federal agencies instead of one creating organisation. Records are added on a rolling basis as agencies complete review and transfer. Publicly releasable material is intended to become available through the National Archives Catalog after accessioning. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection: Frequently Asked Questions National Archives National ArchivesUnidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection: Frequently Asked Questions National ArchivesApril 24, 2025 The legislation also requires agencies to transfer both redacted public versions and corresponding unredacted copies when records contain information that cannot yet be fully released, allowing NARA to preserve a complete archival record while providing the maximum lawful public access. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection: Frequently Asked Questions National Archives National ArchivesUnidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection: Frequently Asked Questions National ArchivesApril 24, 2025 8:39 Congress pushes for UFO disclosure Channel: Scripps News · Views: 47.1K · Uploaded: December 2023 · Length: 8 minutes Open on YouTube Why Original Agency Files Still Matter A common misunderstanding is that RG 615 replaces existing archival holdings. It does not. The original records generally remain within the agency record groups that document how and why they were created. An Air Force investigation remains part of Air Force archival history. An FAA operational file continues to belong within aviation records. Intelligence documents remain linked to their originating organisations. RG 615 simply provides an additional, centralised collection of qualifying digital copies. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection: Frequently Asked Questions National Archives National ArchivesUnidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection: Frequently Asked Questions National ArchivesApril 24, 2025 Keeping the original archival context remains important because record groups preserve relationships that researchers often need: surrounding correspondence; administrative history; related case files; filing structures; provenance showing which office created the record and for what operational purpose. Those relationships can provide evidence that is less visible when viewing an isolated copy inside a thematic collection. A UAP report may make more sense when examined alongside the wider aviation investigation, intelligence assessment or administrative correspondence preserved in the originating agency's files. This reflects a longstanding archival principle that records gain meaning from their original context rather than from individual documents alone. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... Top 10 Declassified UAP Secrets Channel: WatchMojo.com Open on YouTube How Researchers Should Search Both Systems For comprehensive research, RG 615 should be treated as a starting point rather than the only destination. A practical search strategy is: Two congressmen stonewalling UAP transparency bill, expert says NewsNation Prime Channel: NewsNation · Views: 113.9K · Uploaded: December 2023 Open on YouTube 1. Search RG 615 for recently transferred UAP records from multiple agencies. 2. Identify the originating department or office listed in the metadata. 3. Search the relevant historical record group for related files, earlier investigations, administrative correspondence and supporting documentation. 4. Compare both collections where digital copies and original archival series overlap. National Archives This approach is particularly valuable because the central collection may contain only the qualifying UAP related material, whereas the originating record group can include broader operational records that explain why a document was created, who reviewed it and how it fit into agency activities. Researchers should also expect uneven coverage while transfers continue. Congress required agencies to review their holdings, identify qualifying records and transfer publicly releasable digital copies according to NARA guidance, but agencies complete this work on different schedules. Consequently, RG 615 expands incrementally rather than appearing fully populated at once. National Archives Why the Two Archive Model Improves Access Although maintaining two archival locations may initially seem more complicated, it addresses two different research needs simultaneously. The original agency record groups preserve provenance, administrative history and complete institutional context. RG 615 improves discoverability by giving researchers a single catalogue location where UAP related records from multiple agencies can be found without first knowing which department created them. National Archives In practical terms, the new system shifts the first question from \"Which agency might have investigated this?\" to \"Has a copy already been transferred into the central UAP collection?\" If the answer is yes, researchers gain a convenient entry point while still retaining the option to consult the original agency"},{"title":"Search Beyond Blue","url":"/search-beyond-blue/","description":"Useful UFO records are scattered across aviation, intelligence, naval, space, diplomatic and public-affairs collections rather than one master file.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / US Archives","level":3,"text":"Search Beyond Blue Where UFO Records Hide Outside Blue Book US Archives Search Beyond Blue Search Beyond Blue Where UFO Records Hide Outside Blue Book Useful UFO records are scattered across aviation, intelligence, naval, space, diplomatic and public affairs collections rather than one master file. Useful UFO records are scattered across aviation, intelligence, naval, space, diplomatic and public affairs collections rather than one master file. UFO Report Databases implementation bounded topic population context Which Agencies Created Relevant Records How Administrative Purpose Shapes File Content Search Terms That Find Overlooked Material ufo report databases us archives search beyond blue Introduction Searching the US National Archives for UFO records requires a different approach from simply browsing Project Blue Book . Although Blue Book is the best known collection, it represents only one Air Force investigation programme. Federal agencies created and preserved records according to their own operational responsibilities, meaning that reports connected to unidentified aerial phenomena are dispersed across aviation, intelligence, naval, scientific, diplomatic and public information collections. The most effective research strategy is therefore to search by the government function that generated the document, not merely by the phrase \"UFO\". The National Archives' modern UAP portal and Record Group 615 provide useful entry points, but many historically important records remain in the record groups where they originated. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Records Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archi National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... Which Agencies Created Relevant Records? Understanding why an agency created a document is often the quickest way to locate overlooked material. Different departments recorded sightings for very different reasons, and those purposes shaped how records were catalogued. Federal Aviation Administration (FAA): Air traffic safety, pilot reports and controller communications. The Japan Airlines Flight 1628 incident over Alaska, for example, appears as FAA information releases because it concerned civil aviation operations rather than an Air Force investigation. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... Central Intelligence Agency (CIA): Intelligence assessments, foreign reporting, security implications and analytical memoranda rather than routine sighting investigations. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA): Mission transcripts, astronaut communications, technical documentation, interviews and public affairs material where UFO claims became part of agency activities or public enquiries. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... US Navy: Installation records, shore command correspondence and subject files generated during routine naval administration instead of a dedicated UFO programme. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... Office of the Secretary of Defense and Air Force headquarters: Policy memoranda, intelligence coordination, public statements and administrative files that explain how reports were evaluated and communicated. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... US Information Agency and US Agency for Global Media: Broadcasts, interviews and overseas information programmes discussing UFO controversies from a public diplomacy perspective. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... National Archives administrative records: Internal correspondence documenting public requests for Blue Book records, archival processing and reference services rather than UFO investigations themselves. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... The National Archives' UAP portal lists these record groups precisely because no single agency owned every aspect of the subject. Instead, each organisation preserved records generated during its normal work. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Records Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archi National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... 41:31 All the videos from Pentagon's first batch of UFO files Channel: Face the Nation · Views: 61.7K · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 41 minutes Open on YouTube How Administrative Purpose Shapes File Content Researchers often miss valuable records because they expect every document to resemble a case file. In reality, archival organisation follows administrative function. A radar operator's observation may appear inside air defence records because the military regarded it as an operational matter. A pilot's encounter may be preserved in aviation safety files because regulators focused on flight procedures. Intelligence agencies might retain summaries discussing foreign reactions or possible adversary technology rather than the sighting itself. Public affairs offices frequently preserved press briefings, interview transcripts and correspondence responding to public interest instead of investigative evidence. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... This explains why researchers who search only for \"Project Blue Book\" or \"UFO investigation\" often overlook records describing exactly the same incident from another administrative perspective. A single historical event may therefore generate several complementary records: an operational report; intelligence analysis; administrative correspondence; public information material; photographs or motion pictures; later historical reviews. Viewed together, these records often provide a fuller picture than any individual investigation file. Search Terms That Find Overlooked Material Searching broadly with only \"UFO\" frequently produces incomplete results because older records used different terminology or filed sightings under operational subjects. Useful search terms include: unidentified flying object [archives.gov] archives.gov National Archives Record Group Explorer National Archives National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... unidentified aerial phenomenon unidentified anomalous phenomena [archives.gov] archives.gov Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection: Frequently Asked Questions National ArchivesApril 24, 2025 — UNIDENTIFIED ANOMALOU... Published: April 24, 2025 flying saucer unidentified aircraft unidentified object aerial observation radar observation air intelligence air defence aviation incident pilot report unknown aircraft visual sighting information release public affairs correspondence guided missile (for early Cold War air defence files where unidentified objects were evaluated alongside missile concerns) Combining these terms with agency names, record group numbers or known incidents generally produces better catalogue results than relying upon \"UFO\" alone. The National Archives catalogue also benefits from searching by document type—such as photographs, motion pictures, sound recordings or correspondence—because many collections are indexed primarily by format rather than subject. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesRecords Related to Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs) and Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) at the National Archiv... Did the CIA Destroy an Alien Message? The 1958 UFO Mystery WION Podcast Channel: WION Open on YouTube Build Searches Around Incidents Rather Than Programmes Many researchers begin with Project Blue Book because it is familiar. A more productive method is to begin with a specific event. For example: Search the Japan Airlines Flight 1628 encounter through FAA records rather"},{"title":"Redacted Records","url":"/redacted-records/","description":"A listed UAP series may include public redactions, sealed attachments or postponed records that are not yet available for full inspection.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / US Archives","level":3,"text":"Redacted Records Why Listed UAP Files May Still Be Hidden US Archives Redacted Records Redacted Records Why Listed UAP Files May Still Be Hidden A listed UAP series may include public redactions, sealed attachments or postponed records that are not yet available for full inspection. A listed UAP series may include public redactions, sealed attachments or postponed records that are not yet available for full inspection. UFO Report Databases critique risk single mechanism mechanism How Agencies Prepare Public and Restricted Copies What Postponed Records Are Why Catalogue Presence Does Not Equal Full Access ufo report databases us archives redacted records Introduction Finding a UAP ( Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena) record listed in the US National Archives does not necessarily mean every page, attachment or image is available for public inspection. Under the framework established by the 2024 National Defense Authorization Act (NDAA), federal agencies must identify UAP related records, review them for disclosure, and transfer digital copies to the National Archives. During that review, agencies can determine that a record is fully releasable, releasable with redactions, or postponed from public release altogether if it meets specific legal standards. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesGuidance to Federal Agencies on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection National ArchivesMay 8, 2024... Published: May 8, 2024 For researchers using the National Archives' UAP collections or Record Group (RG) 615, this distinction is crucial. A catalogue entry is evidence that a record exists within the collection, but it is not proof that every part of that record is publicly accessible. Understanding why records are redacted or postponed helps avoid mistaken conclusions about what archival listings actually reveal. How Agencies Prepare Public and Restricted Copies The UAP disclosure process requires considerably more than simply uploading government files. Before records reach the National Archives, each federal agency must conduct an access review for every identified UAP record. The review places each record into one of three broad categories: Fully releasable: the complete record can be published without restriction. Restricted in part: sensitive passages are removed from the public version, while the complete, unredacted copy is also transferred to the National Archives for controlled custody. Postponed in full: the entire record remains unavailable to the public because disclosure would meet one of the statutory grounds for postponement. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesGuidance to Federal Agencies on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection National ArchivesMay 8, 2024... Published: May 8, 2024 An important feature of the current system is that agencies are expected to transfer both versions when a document is only partially restricted. The public receives the redacted copy, while the National Archives also receives the complete version, preserving the original record for future review rather than permanently altering it. This differs from older assumptions that redaction meant the original document had effectively disappeared. The agencies also prepare extensive metadata describing each record, allowing the National Archives to catalogue both public and restricted material consistently even when some content cannot yet be displayed publicly. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesGuidance to Federal Agencies on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection National ArchivesMay 8, 2024... Published: May 8, 2024 26:47 UFOs Unlocked: Inside the Pentagon’s secret files This Is America Channel: Al Jazeera English · Views: 99.7K · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 26 minutes Open on YouTube What Postponed Records Are A postponed record is not the same as a missing record. Under the UAP Records Collection provisions of the NDAA, postponement means the government has determined that immediate public release would satisfy one of the statutory grounds allowing temporary withholding. The law sets out specific categories rather than permitting open ended secrecy. Examples include situations where disclosure would reveal: an intelligence officer or protected intelligence source; intelligence methods that remain in active use; information whose release would demonstrably harm national security or foreign relations; protected personal information under the Privacy Act; information whose release would create a substantial and unwarranted invasion of personal privacy; or confidential relationships between government agencies and cooperating individuals or foreign governments. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Specific Access Restriction Authority List National Archives National ArchivesSpecific Access Restriction Authority List National ArchivesMay 6, 2026... Published: May 6, 2026 The National Archives has published formal postponement codes corresponding to these statutory categories. These codes standardise how restricted UAP records are described and explain why disclosure has been delayed rather than permanently denied. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Specific Access Restriction Authority List National Archives National ArchivesSpecific Access Restriction Authority List National ArchivesMay 6, 2026... Published: May 6, 2026 Importantly, postponement does not necessarily imply extraordinary content. The reasons may relate entirely to intelligence practices, operational methods, privacy obligations or diplomatic sensitivities rather than to the reported phenomenon itself. Why Catalogue Presence Does Not Equal Full Access One of the easiest mistakes for researchers is assuming that because a document appears in an archive catalogue, every component of that document must be available. Archival catalogues primarily describe holdings. They answer questions such as: Does the government acknowledge possessing this record? Which agency created it? When was it created? What collection does it belong to? They do not guarantee unrestricted access to every page, attachment, photograph or annex. A catalogue entry may therefore coexist with: redacted text; withheld appendices; sealed attachments; classified images; restricted technical annexes; or an entirely postponed document whose metadata remains publicly visible. The existence of a catalogue description is therefore evidence of record management rather than complete disclosure. Researchers should distinguish carefully between record existence and record accessibility , as the two are not equivalent under federal archival practice. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesGuidance to Federal Agencies on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Collection National ArchivesMay 8, 2024... Published: May 8, 2024 41:31 All the videos from Pentagon's first batch of UFO files Channel: Face the Nation · Views: 61.7K · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 41 minutes Open on YouTube Why Redactions Should Be Interpreted Carefully Redactions often attract speculation, but their presence alone provides very little information about a document's underlying significance. Across the federal government, sensitive information is routinely removed for reasons that have little connection to the substantive topic being discussed. Common examples include: names of intelligence personnel; technical collection capabilities; operational procedures; classified communications methods; personally identifying information; and information protected by existing federal statutes. The National Archives' general FOIA procedures illustrate this broader practice. Reviewers examine documents line by line, removing only the portions that remain legally exempt while releasing as much information as possible. Where classified information belongs to another agency, NARA itself cannot declassify it and must refer the material to the originating agency for review. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Freedom of Information Act Requests National Archives National ArchivesFreedom of Information Act Requests National ArchivesJuly 16, 2025... Published: July 16, 2025 Consequently, the presence of blacked out passages should not automatically be interpreted as evidence of concealed extraordinary findings. In many cases, the protected material concerns standard government equities rather than the reported UAP event itself. The Difference Between Temporary Restriction and Permanent Secrecy Another common misconception is that postponed records have disappeared indefinitely. The current UAP framework is designed around preservation as well as disclosure. Agencies are instructed to identify, organise and transfer digital copies of UAP records—including restricted ones—to the National Archives rather than excluding them from the collection altogether. Publicly releasable copies are made available online, while restricted records remain subject to"},{"title":"Mo D File Purpose","url":"/mo-d-file-purpose/","description":"Ministry of Defence UFO records can document public correspondence, policy handling or intelligence assessment rather than one unified investigation.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / British Files","level":3,"text":"Mo D File Purpose What Ministry of Defence UFO Files Actually Prove British Files Mo D File Purpose Mo D File Purpose What Ministry of Defence UFO Files Actually Prove Ministry of Defence UFO records can document public correspondence, policy handling or intelligence assessment rather than one unified investigation. Ministry of Defence UFO records can document public correspondence, policy handling or intelligence assessment rather than one unified investigation. UFO Report Databases critique risk bounded topic dataset evidence How administrative purpose shapes the record Public correspondence versus intelligence assessment Reading surrounding files for missing context ufo report databases british files mo d file purpose Introduction Ministry of Defence (MoD) UFO files are often treated as though they document a single, continuous government investigation into unexplained aerial phenomena. The surviving records show something more complicated. Over several decades, the purpose of these files changed repeatedly in response to shifting defence priorities, public interest, parliamentary scrutiny, freedom of information requirements and administrative practice. As a result, two files containing similar UFO reports may have been created for entirely different reasons and carry very different evidential value. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... For anyone using the British UFO collections in The National Archives' Discovery catalogue, understanding why a file was created is often more important than the sighting it contains. A file assembled to answer Members of Parliament or members of the public should not be read in the same way as one produced by Defence Intelligence Staff to assess possible military significance. How administrative purpose shapes the record The MoD's UFO records evolved alongside changing institutional responsibilities rather than a fixed investigative mission. During the early Cold War, unidentified aerial reports attracted attention because any unknown object might represent a foreign aircraft, missile or intelligence threat. Defence interest therefore centred on national security rather than proving or disproving extraterrestrial explanations. As reports accumulated, different branches created records for different operational needs, including intelligence assessment, ministerial briefing, public correspondence and policy management. [National Archives] media.nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFO files at The National Archives The National Archives National Archives UFO files at The National Archives The National Archives This gradual diversification explains why the Discovery catalogue contains UFO material across several record series rather than a single coherent archive. The file reference identifies not simply where the record is stored, but which office produced it and for what administrative purpose. An important historical change occurred in record retention itself. Until 1967, MoD policy was generally to destroy UFO files after five years, meaning many early records no longer exist. From 1970 onwards, surviving files were increasingly retained because of growing public interest and eventual transfer to The National Archives. That policy change altered the character of the archive: later decades contain many more administrative and correspondence records than the fragmented early period. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... Public correspondence versus intelligence assessment One of the most common research mistakes is assuming every MoD UFO file records an official investigation. In practice, many files primarily document how officials managed public interest. These often include: replies to letters from the public; responses to parliamentary questions; press enquiries; briefing notes for ministers; internal guidance on consistent public messaging; Freedom of Information handling in later years. Such files reveal government communication strategy and administrative workload rather than independent evidence about the reported event itself. The National Archives explicitly notes that surviving records consist largely of policy papers, parliamentary business, correspondence and sighting reports, not a unified investigative programme. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... By contrast, records created within Defence Intelligence Staff—particularly those associated with DI55 and preserved mainly within DEFE 31—served a different function. Their purpose was to determine whether unusual reports might indicate foreign military technology, missile activity, satellite re entry or another defence concern. Intelligence officers were interested in unexplained reports only insofar as they might reveal capabilities relevant to UK defence planning. [National Archives] media.nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFO files at The National Archives The National Archives National Archives UFO files at The National Archives The National Archives The difference matters because identical witness reports may appear in both administrative and intelligence files, but the surrounding documentation—and therefore the historical meaning—is entirely different. 46:54 The 1993 Cosford Incident: Nick Pope’s Turning Point on UFOs Channel: Eyes On Cinema · Views: 73.4K · Uploaded: February 2026 · Length: 46 minutes Open on YouTube Why similar files can carry different evidential weight Discovery catalogue entries often appear similar at first glance, yet the creating office fundamentally changes how researchers should interpret them. A Defence Secretariat file may contain: correspondence chains; drafting comments; ministerial submissions; media handling advice; filing decisions. These documents demonstrate how government responded institutionally to public concern. A Defence Intelligence file, meanwhile, may include: technical evaluation; operational analysis; consideration of radar or military reporting; assessment of possible defence implications. Neither category automatically establishes what witnesses actually observed. Instead, each reveals how a different part of government processed the same information according to its own responsibilities. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives Intelligence and security services National ArchivesIntelligence and security services The National Archives... This distinction also helps explain why some researchers reach conflicting conclusions from the same archive. One person may read a communications file and conclude the MoD dismissed UFO reports, while another studies intelligence papers showing genuine interest in rare unexplained incidents with possible defence significance. Both observations can be accurate because they concern different administrative functions. Reading surrounding files for missing context The Discovery catalogue is most useful when researchers examine neighbouring files rather than relying on one headline reference. Individual incidents often generated several separate records: the original report; intelligence assessment; parliamentary correspondence; ministerial briefing; later public enquiries; press handling papers. Viewed separately, each file presents only one administrative perspective. Read together, they reveal how an incident moved through different parts of government. The National Archives highlights this pattern in several well known cases. For example, files associated with the Rendlesham Forest incident include both documentation relating to the event itself and substantial later correspondence generated by continuing public and parliamentary interest. Many surviving records therefore document the government's subsequent handling of the incident rather than fresh investigation of the original sightings. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFO reports National ArchivesUFO reports The National Archives... 3:56 UFO file release March 2009 Channel: The National Archives UK · Views: 128.6K · Uploaded: March 2009 · Length: 3 minutes 56 seconds Open on YouTube The gradual shift from investigation to records management Over time, the balance of work carried out by the MoD UFO desk changed noticeably. As Cold War priorities evolved and repeated reviews found no evidence that reported UFOs posed a defence threat, increasing amounts of staff time were devoted to handling correspondence, media enquiries and parliamentary business. The files increasingly reflect administrative management rather than active investigation of unexplained aerial events. This explains why later collections often contain extensive communications records despite relatively limited technical assessment. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National"},{"title":"Overseas Reports","url":"/overseas-reports/","description":"Overseas UFO files often reveal how embassies relayed local claims and managed enquiries rather than how officials investigated the events.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / British Files","level":3,"text":"Overseas Reports Why British Embassies Filed UFO Reports British Files Overseas Reports Overseas Reports Why British Embassies Filed UFO Reports Overseas UFO files often reveal how embassies relayed local claims and managed enquiries rather than how officials investigated the events. Overseas UFO files often reveal how embassies relayed local claims and managed enquiries rather than how officials investigated the events. UFO Report Databases governance bounded topic population context How sightings entered diplomatic channels Local press reports and foreign government messages Separating diplomatic reporting from investigation ufo report databases british files overseas reports Introduction British UFO files are often associated with Ministry of Defence sighting reports, but a less obvious strand of the archival record comes from British embassies and diplomatic posts overseas. These records rarely show diplomats conducting scientific or military investigations into unexplained aerial phenomena. Instead, they reveal how unusual claims moved through government communication channels, how overseas events generated questions for London, and how officials distinguished between reporting foreign developments and opening a formal British investigation. For researchers using The National Archives' Discovery catalogue, overseas UFO material is therefore valuable less as evidence about the sightings themselves than as evidence of diplomatic practice. Embassy correspondence illustrates how local press coverage, foreign government statements, public enquiries and requests from British nationals abroad entered Whitehall, while also showing the limits of what the Foreign and Commonwealth Office (FCO) and the Ministry of Defence (MOD) considered to be their respective responsibilities. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... How sightings entered diplomatic channels British embassies routinely reported developments in their host countries that might affect British interests or generate official enquiries. UFO stories occasionally met that threshold, particularly when they became major national news, prompted parliamentary debate overseas or resulted in questions directed at British officials. The route was generally administrative rather than investigative: A major sighting attracted extensive local media attention. British citizens or journalists asked the embassy whether the UK possessed relevant information. The embassy forwarded the correspondence or summarised local reporting to London. The Foreign Office or MOD decided whether a reply was necessary. In most cases, officials simply confirmed whether Britain held relevant information or whether the event had any defence significance. This process reflects normal diplomatic reporting rather than the creation of a specialised UFO intelligence network. Embassy staff acted as communicators between overseas developments and Whitehall, not as investigators attempting to verify extraordinary claims. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk foreign commonwealth correspondence and records from 1782 National ArchivesForeign Office and Foreign and Commonwealth Office records from 1782 The National Archives... Why embassies sometimes became involved Embassies were especially likely to report UFO related matters when they overlapped with broader diplomatic concerns. Public and media interest Large sighting waves often generated extensive newspaper coverage and public correspondence. British missions abroad monitored significant local stories as part of their routine political and media reporting. If ministers in London were likely to receive questions, diplomatic posts could provide context about how the issue was being discussed locally. This explains why surviving files often contain newspaper summaries, translations or references to foreign reporting rather than technical assessments of the reported objects themselves. 48:15 The British UFO Files: Secret Government Investigations Revealed Channel: Documentary Overload · Views: 796 · Uploaded: April 2026 · Length: 48 minutes Open on YouTube Foreign government communications Occasionally, foreign authorities exchanged information or responded to British enquiries concerning unusual aerial reports. Such correspondence was generally administrative, addressing whether an event had implications for aviation safety, defence or bilateral communications. The existence of diplomatic correspondence should not be interpreted as evidence that Britain endorsed the underlying UFO claims. In archival practice, governments routinely exchange information about events that later prove mundane or remain unresolved. Consular enquiries British nationals living or travelling overseas sometimes contacted embassies seeking information after well publicised sightings. Diplomatic staff could forward these enquiries to London, especially when members of the public believed the MOD possessed classified information. These files reveal as much about public expectations of government as about the reported phenomena themselves. The Belgian UFO wave as a diplomatic example One of the clearest examples of overseas reporting entering British official files concerns Belgium's well known UFO wave between 1989 and 1990. Thousands of reports of large triangular objects led the Belgian Air Force to investigate several incidents, including radar tracking and the scrambling of F 16 aircraft. The events attracted substantial international attention and inevitably generated enquiries reaching the British government. The MOD's own records show that Britain was not formally informed by Belgium during the events because there was no evidence of a threat requiring international notification. Nevertheless, British officials later dealt with numerous public questions about the Belgian incidents, including parliamentary enquiries. Correspondence from this period includes communication with the British Embassy in Belgium alongside responses explaining the MOD's position. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFO reports National ArchivesUFO reports The National Archives... This distinction is important. The diplomatic record demonstrates that: the embassy functioned as a communication channel; public interest travelled internationally; British officials responded to enquiries; none of this automatically amounted to a British investigation of the Belgian sightings. Researchers sometimes mistake diplomatic correspondence for evidence of operational cooperation. The surviving files instead suggest that the principal British activity consisted of answering questions and maintaining awareness of a major overseas news story. Local press reports versus official evidence One recurring feature of overseas UFO files is the careful separation between press reporting and official knowledge. Embassies frequently summarised prominent stories appearing in local newspapers because such coverage could affect British interests or generate enquiries from London. However, diplomats generally avoided treating media reports as verified evidence. This distinction appears throughout government record keeping: Press accounts documented what was being reported publicly. Diplomatic correspondence recorded what officials were being asked to explain. MOD responses addressed whether Britain possessed relevant defence information. Foreign government communications reflected official positions rather than confirmation of extraordinary claims. Recognising these different document types helps researchers avoid reading administrative correspondence as proof that officials accepted the reported events at face value. 9:47 UFO file release October 2008 Channel: The National Archives UK · Views: 19.5K · Uploaded: October 2008 · Length: 9 minutes Open on YouTube Separating diplomatic reporting from investigation Perhaps the most important lesson from overseas UFO files is that reporting and investigation were different governmental functions. The Foreign Office existed to monitor developments abroad, maintain relations with foreign governments and respond to enquiries affecting British interests. The MOD, by contrast, considered UFO reports only insofar as they might indicate a defence or air defence issue. Consequently, a file containing embassy correspondence may document: questions from journalists; requests from Members of Parliament; letters from members of the public; copies of foreign government statements; or summaries of international media coverage. None of these necessarily indicate that Britain launched an operational investigation. The National Archives similarly emphasises that UFO related records are dispersed across departments, including DEFE, AIR and FCO, because each organisation created documents for its own administrative purpose rather than contributing to a single, unified UFO investigation. Understanding"},{"title":"Rendlesham Files","url":"/rendlesham-files/","description":"The Rendlesham incident appears across memoranda and later correspondence, showing how one event was copied, reframed and debated inside government.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / British Files","level":3,"text":"Rendlesham Files How Government Records Reframed Rendlesham British Files Rendlesham Files Rendlesham Files How Government Records Reframed Rendlesham The Rendlesham incident appears across memoranda and later correspondence, showing how one event was copied, reframed and debated inside government. The Rendlesham incident appears across memoranda and later correspondence, showing how one event was copied, reframed and debated inside government. UFO Report Databases historical comparative single case aspect event time window The original defence memorandum trail Later parliamentary and public correspondence What neighboring files add to the case ufo report databases british files rendlesham files Introduction The Rendlesham Forest incident is often treated as a single Ministry of Defence (MoD) file, but the archival record tells a different story. Instead of remaining in one dossier, the incident migrated through several government recordkeeping contexts over decades. It began as a military memorandum describing unusual lights, later became a subject of parliamentary scrutiny, generated repeated correspondence with members of the public, and eventually emerged as one of the flagship examples used by The National Archives to explain Britain's released UFO records. This progression makes Rendlesham an instructive case for anyone using the Discovery catalogue: understanding the incident requires following how different government offices copied, summarised and reinterpreted the same event for different administrative purposes rather than expecting one definitive investigative file. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... The original defence memorandum trail The foundation of the official British record is Lieutenant Colonel Charles Halt's memorandum dated 13 January 1981. Written by the deputy base commander of the United States Air Force at RAF Woodbridge after the December 1980 events, it described unexplained lights observed by American personnel stationed at the joint UK US bases of RAF Bentwaters and RAF Woodbridge. The memorandum entered the MoD because the bases were located in Britain, even though the witnesses were serving with the United States Air Force. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFO reports National ArchivesUFO reports The National Archives... Within the National Archives, this document is catalogued in DEFE 24/1948 , a Defence Secretariat registered file rather than an intelligence case file. That placement is significant. The file reflects how the Defence Secretariat handled correspondence and policy matters, not evidence that the government established a large investigative programme devoted to the incident. The catalogue description itself presents the memorandum as official correspondence concerning unexplained lights rather than as the beginning of an extensive technical inquiry. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFO reports National ArchivesUFO reports The National Archives... Researchers examining the file therefore encounter two different layers of documentation: the original Halt memorandum reporting the observations; MoD administrative handling of that report within its normal correspondence system. This distinction is easy to miss when the memorandum is reproduced outside its archival context. 1:06:52 Rendlesham Forest UFO sighting: Eyewitness Colonel Charles Halt Channel: 7 News Spotlight · Views: 707.9K · Uploaded: May 2024 · Length: 1 hour 6 minutes Open on YouTube Later parliamentary and public correspondence As public interest increased through the 1990s and 2000s, Rendlesham gradually shifted from being an operational report into a recurring subject of parliamentary questions. These later records reveal less about the original sightings than about how the government consistently explained its earlier decisions. Successive ministers gave remarkably similar answers in both the House of Commons and House of Lords. They stated that: the Halt memorandum had been assessed by staff responsible for air defence; officials concluded it contained nothing of defence significance; no further investigation by the MoD was considered necessary on national security grounds. [Hansard] hansard.parliament.uk Hansard Rendlesham Forest (Incident Rendlesham Forest (Incident) Hansard UK ParliamentJuly 24, 1996... Published: July 24, 1996 Those exchanges effectively created a second documentary layer. Rather than reopening the evidence, parliamentary files repeated and clarified the department's administrative position. The emphasis shifted away from what witnesses reported and towards explaining why the ministry had not pursued the matter further. The same pattern appears in responses to members of the public. According to the National Archives' guide to UFO records, much of the surviving material concerns policy, parliamentary business and correspondence rather than investigations of unexplained phenomena themselves. Rendlesham therefore became one of many subjects discussed repeatedly in letters drafted years after the original event. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... Why the wording changes across files One of the most useful lessons from the Rendlesham records is that identical events are described differently depending on why a document was created. The original memorandum is observational. It records what personnel believed they saw and the sequence of events as reported shortly afterwards. Secretariat files become administrative. They record receipt, circulation and handling of the memorandum inside the ministry. Parliamentary material becomes explanatory. Ministers summarise the earlier assessment, justify departmental decisions and answer specific questions raised by MPs or peers. Public correspondence becomes communicative. Officials explain existing policy to individual correspondents rather than reassessing the underlying incident. Each version therefore compresses or reframes the previous one. Details important to witnesses may disappear from later correspondence because they are irrelevant to answering a parliamentary question, while procedural explanations become more prominent over time. This is a normal feature of government recordkeeping rather than evidence that records were necessarily altered or replaced. [nationalarchives.gov.uk] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... 22:11 TRUE STORY: When The US Airforce Encountered UFO's Our Life Channel: True Lives · Views: 334.1K · Uploaded: June 2023 · Length: 22 minutes Open on YouTube What neighbouring files add to the case Browsing around DEFE 24/1948 in the Discovery catalogue often proves more informative than reading the famous memorandum in isolation. Neighbouring Defence Secretariat files contain material relating to: responses to parliamentary questions; briefing notes prepared for ministers; correspondence with journalists and members of the public; broader MoD policy on handling UFO reports. These surrounding files demonstrate how Rendlesham gradually became an administrative reference point. Officials increasingly cited earlier conclusions instead of re evaluating the original evidence, creating a documentary trail in which later files repeatedly point back to the same 1981 memorandum. This explains why searches confined to the word \"Rendlesham\" may overlook valuable contextual records filed under broader subjects such as UFO policy or parliamentary correspondence. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... Discovery catalogue lessons from the Rendlesham example For researchers using the Discovery catalogue, Rendlesham illustrates several practical principles. First, catalogue provenance matters as much as document content. A record's department and series indicate why it exists and what function it served inside government. Second, one historical event rarely remains in one archival location. Rendlesham appears in operational correspondence, Defence Secretariat files, parliamentary business and later responses to public enquiries, each reflecting different administrative purposes. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... Third, later references should not automatically be treated as new evidence. Many are summaries of earlier decisions rather than independent investigations. For example, parliamentary answers in 1996, 1997, 2001 and later years consistently restated that the Halt memorandum had been assessed as having no defence significance, while also confirming that the MoD held no additional official investigative material beyond the memorandum itself and associated papers. [parliament.uk] hansard.parliament.uk Hansard Rendlesham Forest"},{"title":"Weather Records","url":"/weather-records/","description":"Meteorological files can help test whether balloons, clouds, meteors or atmospheric effects match the date and place of a reported sighting.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / British Files","level":3,"text":"Weather Records Can Weather Records Explain a UFO Sighting? British Files Weather Records Weather Records Can Weather Records Explain a UFO Sighting? Meteorological files can help test whether balloons, clouds, meteors or atmospheric effects match the date and place of a reported sighting. Meteorological files can help test whether balloons, clouds, meteors or atmospheric effects match the date and place of a reported sighting. UFO Report Databases evidence single dataset dataset evidence Matching sighting details to weather observations Checking balloons and atmospheric phenomena Limits of retrospective meteorological explanations ufo report databases british files weather checks Introduction Weather records are one of the most useful independent datasets for evaluating British UFO reports. While Ministry of Defence (MoD) files often contain witness descriptions and official correspondence, they rarely include a complete meteorological analysis. Researchers therefore strengthen an investigation by matching the reported date, time and location with historical weather observations. This approach can determine whether a sighting is consistent with known atmospheric conditions, weather balloons, cloud formations, astronomical visibility or other natural phenomena rather than relying solely on eyewitness testimony. The National Archives and the Met Office preserve these records separately, making cross referencing an essential part of serious archive research. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... Matching sighting details to weather observations The starting point is to establish the most precise information available from the UFO report itself. Useful details include: Exact date and local time. Geographic location or grid reference. Direction of observation. Estimated altitude and movement. Reported weather conditions from the witness. Many official British UFO report forms already include basic weather information supplied by the witness, such as cloud cover, visibility or mist. These observations should not be treated as definitive evidence, but they provide a useful starting point for comparison with independent meteorological records. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFO reports National ArchivesUFO reports The National Archives... Researchers then compare the report against historical weather datasets, including: Surface observations from nearby weather stations. [ncei.noaa.gov] ncei.noaa.gov weather balloon weather balloon Synoptic weather charts. Cloud cover and visibility records. Wind speed and direction at different altitudes. Rainfall and thunderstorm activity. Sunset, twilight and moon phase data where relevant. The Met Office's National Meteorological Archive preserves daily weather reports dating back to 1860, together with original observation forms, weather charts and other historical records that can be consulted when investigating older cases. [Met Office] metoffice.gov.uk Met Office National Meteorological Archive Met OfficeNational Meteorological Archive Met Office... Met Office National Meteorological Library and Archive Introduction Channel: Met Office Science and Services Open on YouTube Checking balloons and atmospheric phenomena One of the most common questions raised during historical UFO investigations is whether the reported object could have been a weather balloon. Weather balloons do not simply drift randomly. Their movement depends upon wind conditions at different heights, so investigators compare reported object movement with archived wind profiles where available. If a witness describes an object travelling steadily with the prevailing winds, ascending naturally and eventually disappearing into cloud, a balloon becomes a plausible explanation. Conversely, if the reported motion is directly against measured upper level winds or includes abrupt manoeuvres unsupported by the available evidence, the balloon hypothesis becomes weaker. The National Archives notes that official correspondence attached to many early British UFO reports routinely considered explanations including weather balloons, satellites and the planet Venus before treating a case as unexplained. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFO reports National ArchivesUFO reports The National Archives... Weather records can also help identify atmospheric optical effects that witnesses may not have recognised, including: Lenticular clouds illuminated by low angle sunlight. Ice crystal halos and sundogs. Temperature inversions affecting the appearance of lights. Mist layers obscuring distance and apparent speed. Lightning within distant storm systems. Because these phenomena depend on specific meteorological conditions, archived observations can either support or undermine a proposed explanation. Connecting separate government records An important feature of British archive research is that UFO correspondence and meteorological observations were usually created by different organisations. A Ministry of Defence file may record: the witness account, internal correspondence, military assessments, replies to Members of Parliament or the public. Meanwhile, the relevant weather information may be preserved within the Met Office archive as daily observations or synoptic charts rather than inside the UFO file itself. The investigator therefore has to reconstruct the event by combining records produced independently for entirely different administrative purposes. This separation is one reason why archive based UFO research often requires multiple catalogue searches rather than examination of a single government file. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... Five amazing finds from the Met Office archive Channel: Met Office Learn About Weather Open on YouTube A practical example of weather comparison Suppose an archived report describes a bright stationary light seen over eastern England shortly after sunset. A weather based investigation might proceed by asking: 1. Was visibility unusually clear or reduced by haze? 2. Was there a strong temperature inversion capable of producing unusual optical effects? 3. Was cloud cover broken, allowing bright planets to appear intermittently? 4. Were weather balloons launched nearby that evening? 5. Were strong upper level winds capable of carrying a balloon in the reported direction? Rather than proving a particular explanation, this comparison narrows the range of plausible possibilities using independently recorded environmental evidence. What weather records cannot explain Meteorological evidence is valuable, but it has clear limits. Historical observations are often taken only at scheduled intervals rather than continuously. Small scale cloud formations, local turbulence and brief optical effects may never have been recorded. Likewise, archived weather stations may be many kilometres from the reported sighting location, especially in rural Britain. Witnesses also introduce uncertainty. Reported times may be inaccurate, estimated directions may be mistaken, and descriptions of cloud cover or visibility can differ from professional observations. A mismatch between witness testimony and archived weather records does not automatically invalidate either source; it simply indicates uncertainty that must be considered alongside other evidence. Weather records are also incapable of identifying every aerial object. They can demonstrate whether atmospheric conditions favoured certain explanations, but they cannot independently determine whether an observed light was an aircraft, satellite, astronomical object or something genuinely unidentified . Why weather checks remain an essential part of archive research Within British UFO research, meteorological records function as an independent evidence dataset rather than an explanation in themselves. They allow researchers to test whether reported behaviour is consistent with known atmospheric conditions, evaluate common explanations such as weather balloons or optical effects, and identify cases where environmental evidence contradicts or supports witness accounts. Used alongside Ministry of Defence correspondence, Discovery catalogue records and contemporary observations, historical weather data provide one of the strongest objective tools available for assessing whether a reported UFO can reasonably be explained by ordinary atmospheric phenomena or whether it remains genuinely unidentified on the available evidence. [metoffice.gov.uk] metoffice.gov.uk Met Office National Meteorological Archive Met OfficeNational Meteorological Archive Met Office... The Beaufort Wind Force Scale Objectivity 295 Channel: Objectivity Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Can Weather Records Explain a UFO Sighting?. Use these as the next step if you"},{"title":"Case File Quality","url":"/case-file-quality/","description":"The most useful MUFON files combine original media, precise timing, independent witnesses and a documented process for eliminating explanations.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / MUFON","level":3,"text":"Case File Quality What Makes a MUFON Case File Strong? Case File Quality Case File Quality What Makes a MUFON Case File Strong? The most useful MUFON files combine original media, precise timing, independent witnesses and a documented process for eliminating explanations. The most useful MUFON files combine original media, precise timing, independent witnesses and a documented process for eliminating explanations. UFO Report Databases evidence bounded topic dataset evidence Evidence that adds real analytical value Why longer files are not always better A practical checklist for evaluating case quality ufo report databases mufon case file quality Introduction A strong MUFON case file is not simply one that describes a dramatic sighting. The most useful files are those that preserve enough verifiable information for another investigator to understand what happened, test alternative explanations, and assess whether the evidence supports the conclusions. In practice, that means original media, precise timing and location, documented witness interviews, and a transparent record of how ordinary explanations were examined before a case was left unresolved. MUFON itself states that its investigators begin with the working hypothesis that an event has a conventional explanation and seek evidence to confirm or reject that hypothesis rather than assuming an extraordinary cause. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific Method The Scientific Method MUFON... Because MUFON's Case Management System contains files ranging from brief witness submissions to extensive investigation records, file quality varies considerably. Length alone is a poor indicator of reliability. A concise report with accurate photographs, timestamps and corroborating witnesses may be more valuable than a lengthy narrative built largely on recollection or speculation. [MUFON] mufon.com Search Database Terms and Conditions Search Database Terms and Conditions MUFON... Evidence That Adds Real Analytical Value The strongest MUFON files tend to accumulate several independent forms of evidence that reinforce one another rather than relying on a single witness account. The most valuable elements include: Precise date and time. Exact timing allows investigators to compare the report against aircraft movements, satellite passes, astronomical objects, rocket launches and weather records . Even an error of a few minutes can complicate later verification. Accurate location information. GPS coordinates or clearly identified observation points make it possible to reconstruct viewing angles and sight lines. Original photographs or video. Native image files with intact metadata generally provide far more value than screenshots, edited images or compressed social media uploads. Multiple independent witnesses . Separate accounts collected independently are generally more informative than a jointly produced narrative because investigators can compare agreements and differences. Contemporaneous notes. Reports written immediately after the event usually contain fewer memory distortions than recollections recorded weeks or months later. Environmental observations. Information about weather, cloud cover, visibility, wind, lighting conditions and surrounding activity helps investigators test ordinary explanations. No single item proves that an observation represents an unidentified phenomenon. Instead, confidence increases when independent sources of information point toward the same reconstruction of events. This emphasis on measurable observations is consistent with MUFON's published investigative approach. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific Method The Scientific Method MUFON... Why Witness Interviews Matter A high quality interview often contributes more than an additional page of narrative. Experienced investigators typically clarify: exactly where the witness was standing; the object's apparent direction of travel; elevation above the horizon; apparent angular size; estimated duration; lighting conditions; whether sounds were heard; what attracted the witness's attention in the first place. These details allow later reviewers to recreate the observation rather than relying on general descriptions such as \"moving strangely\" or \"very fast.\" MUFON's published methodology specifically highlights collecting measurable quantities such as angular size, elevation, azimuth and apparent brightness wherever possible. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific Method The Scientific Method MUFON... 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube How a Strong Investigation Handles Ordinary Explanations One hallmark of a reliable case file is not how quickly it reaches an \"unknown\" conclusion but how thoroughly it attempts to eliminate conventional explanations. Good files usually document checks such as: commercial and military aircraft activity; drones; astronomical bodies; satellites; meteor events; atmospheric optics; balloons or lanterns; reflections and photographic artefacts; local weather conditions. Importantly, investigators should explain why these possibilities were accepted or rejected rather than simply stating that they were considered. A written chain of reasoning allows another researcher to evaluate whether the conclusions follow from the available evidence. MUFON's own guidance emphasises that investigators begin by attempting to explain the report through ordinary causes and notes that the large majority of investigated cases ultimately receive conventional explanations. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific Method The Scientific Method MUFON... Why Longer Files Are Not Always Better Length often reflects the complexity of a case rather than its evidential strength. A lengthy file may include: repeated witness statements; speculative discussion; background information unrelated to the observation; correspondence between investigators; administrative paperwork. None of these necessarily improves the evidential value of the case. Conversely, a relatively short investigation can be exceptionally useful if it contains: original image files; precise timestamps; independent witness interviews; documented measurements; a clearly explained elimination process; transparent conclusions. The strongest files maximise the amount of verifiable information rather than the amount of text. 2:00:13 Pro Investigator Reveals UFO Investigation Secrets! Plus some awesome UFO videos Channel: Northland MUFON · Views: 51 · Uploaded: November 2025 · Length: 2 hours Open on YouTube Signs That a Case May Be Weaker Readers should approach conclusions cautiously when a file depends heavily on material that cannot be independently evaluated. Common warning signs include: only one witness with no supporting evidence; uncertain or approximate event timing; missing original media; photographs available only after editing or compression; conclusions that exceed what the documented evidence supports; no explanation of how ordinary possibilities were investigated; missing interview notes; major inconsistencies between successive witness accounts; conclusions based primarily on assumptions rather than documented observations. These weaknesses do not necessarily invalidate a report. They simply reduce the confidence that later researchers can place in any interpretation. The Value of Transparency One of the most useful features of a strong case file is that another investigator can follow the reasoning from beginning to end. Transparent documentation typically includes: 1. the original witness submission; 2. investigator interview notes; 3. media examination; 4. environmental checks; 5. alternative hypotheses considered; 6. reasons for accepting or rejecting those hypotheses; 7. final case disposition. This structure allows independent reviewers to disagree with conclusions while still understanding how they were reached. An unresolved classification should therefore be interpreted cautiously. It generally means that the investigation did not find sufficient evidence for a conventional identification—not that an extraordinary explanation has been established. MUFON's published methodology explicitly distinguishes between an unexplained investigation and proof of any particular cause. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific Method The Scientific Method MUFON... MUFON MEMBER ACCESS TO OUR 145,000 CASE DATABASE Channel: Mutual UFO Network (MUFON) Open on YouTube A Practical Checklist for Evaluating Case Quality When reviewing a MUFON case file, the following questions provide a useful framework: QuestionWhy it mattersIs the event precisely dated and timed?Enables comparison with external records.Is the observation location clearly documented?Allows reconstruction of viewing geometry . Are original photographs or videos available?Preserves metadata and reduces uncertainty.Were witnesses interviewed individually?Helps identify consistency and memory"},{"title":"Historic Records","url":"/historic-records/","description":"Digitization can preserve historic reports, but it cannot recreate missing attachments, undocumented decisions or fields never recorded on paper.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / MUFON","level":3,"text":"Historic Records What MUFON's Digitized Records Cannot Bring Back Historic Records Historic Records What MUFON's Digitized Records Cannot Bring Back Digitization can preserve historic reports, but it cannot recreate missing attachments, undocumented decisions or fields never recorded on paper. Digitization can preserve historic reports, but it cannot recreate missing attachments, undocumented decisions or fields never recorded on paper. UFO Report Databases historical comparative bounded topic event time window From paper files to the online case system Missing media and undocumented investigative steps How historical gaps affect modern comparisons ufo report databases mufon historic records Introduction Digitising MUFON 's older paper records has preserved thousands of historic UFO reports that might otherwise have deteriorated or disappeared. The transition from paper files to searchable digital collections has made decades of civilian reporting easier to locate and compare, while projects such as the Pandora Project, the Case Management System (CMS), and the newer MUFON Archives Research Reporting System (M.A.R.R.S.) have brought large portions of the organisation's historical holdings into electronic form. [mufon.com] mufon.com Through the Ages MUFON Through the Ages MUFON... However, digitisation does not recreate information that was never captured or that has been lost over time. Many historic MUFON files originated as handwritten reports, typed correspondence or investigator folders assembled long before standardised online reporting existed. A digital record can preserve surviving pages, but it cannot recover missing photographs, undocumented investigative conversations, informal annotations or contextual knowledge that was never written down. Understanding these limitations is essential when comparing older MUFON cases with modern reports recorded directly into the CMS. From paper files to the online case system MUFON's archives span several distinct generations of record keeping. Before computerised reporting, case files typically consisted of paper documents submitted by mail, investigator correspondence, sketches, photographs, newspaper clippings and locally maintained files. Record formats varied considerably because reporting standards evolved over decades and individual state organisations often maintained their own collections. [MUFON] mufon.com Through the Ages MUFON Through the Ages MUFON... Beginning in the mid 2000s, MUFON launched major digitisation efforts. The Pandora Project aimed to convert historical reports dating back to the organisation's earliest years into searchable electronic form, while the CMS became the primary repository for newly submitted reports. More recently, Project Aquarius introduced M.A.R.R.S., providing access to thousands of scanned pre computer paper reports alongside historical research material. [mufon.com] mufon.com Through the Ages MUFON Through the Ages MUFON... This creates a mixed archive rather than a uniform one: Recent reports are generally born digital, using structured submission fields. Earlier reports often survive as scanned documents rather than structured database entries. Some historical cases exist only because surviving paper files were scanned. Other cases remain incomplete because portions of the original file no longer exist. [projectaquarius.mufon.com] projectaquarius.mufon.com M.A.R.R.S Repository MUFON's Project Aquarius Library M.A.R.R.S Repository MUFON's Project Aquarius Library As a result, searching the database may give the impression that all records are directly comparable when, in reality, they originated under very different documentation practices. Missing media and undocumented investigative steps The most obvious losses involve physical evidence that never entered the digital archive. Historic files frequently referred to photographs, negatives, film reels, tape recordings or physical objects. In some cases these materials deteriorated, were misplaced, remained with witnesses or investigators, or were simply never archived centrally. A scanned report may therefore mention attached photographs that are no longer present. Digitisation preserves the reference but cannot recreate the missing attachment. [projectaquarius.mufon.com] projectaquarius.mufon.com M.A.R.R.S Repository MUFON's Project Aquarius Library M.A.R.R.S Repository MUFON's Project Aquarius Library Less visible losses can be even more significant. 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube Investigator judgement that never reached the file Older investigations relied heavily on volunteer field investigators whose working methods varied over time. Many conclusions were influenced by telephone conversations, site visits, personal observations and witness interactions that may have been summarised only briefly—or not documented at all. Examples include: impressions about witness credibility; informal discussions with local police or airport staff; unsuccessful attempts to locate additional witnesses; observations made during site visits that were never formally recorded; investigative reasoning omitted from the final written report. If these judgements were never written into the paper file, no digitisation project can recover them. Information outside standard forms Modern online reporting systems prompt witnesses to enter information such as observation time, duration, weather conditions and viewing direction into dedicated fields. Older paper reports often lacked this consistency. Some historic files include remarkably detailed narratives, while others record only a date, location and brief description. Even perfect scanning cannot fill empty fields that were never completed in the original report. Administrative history Paper archives also contain evidence of how an investigation unfolded. Folder order, routing slips, handwritten corrections, filing notes and correspondence chains may reveal when evidence arrived or why a case changed classification. If these administrative materials were discarded before scanning—or never preserved—they disappear from the historical record, even if the main report survives. Why scanning is not the same as structured data Scanning preserves pages as images or PDFs, but researchers often need structured information that can be searched consistently across thousands of reports. A scanned report may mention: witness occupation; estimated altitude; weather conditions; object colour; flight direction. Unless these details are manually indexed, they remain buried within the scanned document rather than becoming searchable database fields. M.A.R.R.S. itself notes that while filenames can be searched, the contents of many scanned files cannot be comprehensively indexed in the way modern database records can. [projectaquarius.mufon.com] projectaquarius.mufon.com M.A.R.R.S Repository MUFON's Project Aquarius Library M.A.R.R.S Repository MUFON's Project Aquarius Library Consequently, historical digitisation improves preservation far more than it improves analytical consistency. MUFON MEMBER ACCESS TO OUR 145,000 CASE DATABASE Channel: Mutual UFO Network (MUFON) Open on YouTube How historical gaps affect modern comparisons These differences matter when researchers attempt long term statistical comparisons. An apparent increase in reports mentioning particular characteristics may reflect improvements in reporting forms rather than changes in the underlying phenomenon. Modern witnesses are routinely asked questions that older witnesses may never have been prompted to answer. Likewise: missing values in older reports do not necessarily indicate that information was unknown; absent photographs do not prove none existed; brief narratives may reflect reporting practice rather than witness uncertainty; inconsistent classifications may arise from changing investigative standards across decades. For this reason, combining paper era records with modern CMS submissions requires careful interpretation rather than assuming every database field has the same meaning across time. Preservation succeeds, but historical uncertainty remains The digitisation of MUFON's historical archive has been an important preservation effort. Thousands of reports that once existed only in filing cabinets can now be located, read and studied by researchers, greatly reducing the risk of permanent loss through deterioration or dispersal. Projects such as M.A.R.R.S. explicitly acknowledge their role in preserving pre computerised reports and making them accessible in digital form. [mufon.com] projectaquarius.mufon.com M.A.R.R.S Repository MUFON's Project Aquarius Library M.A.R.R.S Repository MUFON's Project Aquarius Library At the same time, digitisation has clear limits. It preserves surviving documents rather than reconstructing vanished evidence. Missing attachments remain missing,"},{"title":"Investigator Checks","url":"/investigator-checks/","description":"MUFON investigators compare witness accounts with aircraft, astronomy, weather, satellites and image clues before leaving a case unresolved.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / MUFON","level":3,"text":"Investigator Checks What MUFON Checks Before a Case Stays Unexplained Investigator Checks Investigator Checks What MUFON Checks Before a Case Stays Unexplained MUFON investigators compare witness accounts with aircraft, astronomy, weather, satellites and image clues before leaving a case unresolved. MUFON investigators compare witness accounts with aircraft, astronomy, weather, satellites and image clues before leaving a case unresolved. UFO Report Databases implementation single mechanism mechanism From witness interview to reconstructed timeline Aircraft, astronomy, weather and satellite checks Why unresolved does not mean extraordinary ufo report databases mufon investigator checks Introduction MUFON (the Mutual UFO Network) does not treat a report as unexplained simply because a witness could not identify what they saw. Its published investigative approach starts from the opposite assumption: that the event probably has a conventional explanation, and the investigator's job is to test that hypothesis before considering an unknown classification. According to MUFON, field investigators succeed in identifying an ordinary cause for the large majority of reports, while only a small percentage remain unresolved after investigation. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific Method The Scientific Method MUFON... Within MUFON's investigation process, an \"unexplained\" outcome therefore reflects the limits of the available evidence rather than proof of something extraordinary. Before recommending that a case remain unresolved, investigators reconstruct the event, compare it against known aircraft, astronomical objects, satellites, weather phenomena and optical effects, and examine any photographs or video for signs of misinterpretation or imaging artefacts. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific Method The Scientific Method MUFON... From witness interview to reconstructed timeline The first stage is usually not analysing photographs but improving the quality of the witness account. Online reports often contain incomplete or ambiguous information, so investigators attempt to establish a reliable chronology before evaluating possible explanations. The follow up interview typically seeks to clarify: The precise date and local time of the sighting. The observer's location and viewing direction. The object's apparent elevation and azimuth. Estimated brightness, colour, apparent size and shape. Whether the object changed speed, direction or altitude. Weather conditions and visibility. The number of witnesses and whether their accounts agree. MUFON states that investigators also collect measurable information where possible, including angular size, estimated distance and other observational details. The aim is to replace a general description such as \"a bright light moved across the sky\" with a reconstruction that can be compared against known aerial or astronomical events. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific Method The Scientific Method MUFON... Investigators are also encouraged to contact additional witnesses or reliable third parties. Independent accounts can confirm timing and direction, while inconsistencies sometimes reveal misunderstandings, memory errors or differences in perspective. Training materials likewise emphasise collateral contacts as part of the investigative process. [MUFON] mufon.com Become a Mufon Field Investigator Become a Mufon Field Investigator MUFON... Aircraft, astronomy, weather and satellite checks Once the timeline is established, investigators compare the report against several common categories of misidentification rather than searching immediately for exotic explanations. 50:23 MUFON – The Truth Behind UFOs and Alien Encounters Channel: J. Horton Films · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: January 2023 · Length: 50 minutes Open on YouTube Aircraft and drones Aircraft are among the most frequent sources of mistaken identification. Investigators consider factors such as navigation light patterns, landing lights, approach paths, holding patterns and apparent hovering caused by aircraft flying toward the observer. Modern drone activity has become another routine consideration. Bright LEDs, hovering behaviour and rapid manoeuvres can produce reports that initially appear unusual, particularly at night. MUFON's own guidance lists both aircraft and drones among the ordinary explanations investigators are expected to examine first. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific Method The Scientific Method MUFON... Astronomical objects Bright planets—especially Venus—and prominent stars are well known sources of UFO reports because atmospheric conditions can make them appear unusually bright, colourful or apparently mobile. Investigators compare the reported direction and time against the positions of: Bright planets. Prominent stars. Meteor activity. The Moon. Other predictable celestial objects. If the witness observed a stationary light low above the horizon for an extended period, astronomical explanations become particularly important to evaluate before concluding that the object remained unidentified . [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific Method The Scientific Method MUFON... Satellites and space objects Satellites are another routine elimination step. Bright satellite passes, the International Space Station and groups of recently launched satellites can all surprise observers unfamiliar with their appearance. State MUFON organisations openly recommend using satellite tracking resources and International Space Station tracking tools during investigations, illustrating how civilian investigators attempt to compare reports with predictable orbital activity before classifying a case as unknown. [Official WASHINGTON State MUFON] mufonwa.org Official WASHINGTON State MUFON... Weather and atmospheric conditions Environmental conditions receive similar attention. Investigators assess whether clouds, haze, temperature inversions, ice crystals or other atmospheric effects could account for unusual lights or apparent motion. Training for field investigators specifically includes working knowledge of weather anomalies alongside astronomy and aircraft identification because these natural conditions can substantially alter how ordinary objects appear to observers. [MUFON] mufon.com Become a Mufon Field Investigator Become a Mufon Field Investigator MUFON... Examining photographs and video Images rarely settle a case on their own. Instead, investigators ask whether the camera may have recorded something different from what the witness believed they saw. Typical checks include: Reviewing image metadata where available. Looking for reflections from lights or windows. Identifying lens flare or internal reflections. Checking for motion blur. Assessing digital artefacts from phone cameras or security cameras. Comparing multiple frames to determine whether apparent motion comes from the object or the camera. Some MUFON educational material demonstrates common security camera artefacts that create apparent glowing \"orbs\" through reflections and sensor behaviour rather than physical objects in the sky. The point is not that every image is an artefact, but that imaging limitations must be ruled out before stronger conclusions are reached. [Official WASHINGTON State MUFON] mufonwa.org Official WASHINGTON State MUFON... 2:00:13 Pro Investigator Reveals UFO Investigation Secrets! Plus some awesome UFO videos Channel: Northland MUFON · Views: 51 · Uploaded: November 2025 · Length: 2 hours Open on YouTube Testing ordinary explanations before reaching \"unknown\" A central feature of MUFON's published methodology is that investigators begin with the working hypothesis that the event can be explained conventionally. Examples of commonly investigated possibilities include: Aircraft. Drones. Chinese lanterns or sky lanterns. Bright astronomical objects. Reflected or refracted light. Other forms of witness misinterpretation. Only after these possibilities have been examined—and no satisfactory match is found—may a case be recommended for an unknown or unexplained disposition. MUFON describes this as an application of the scientific method: attempting to prove or disprove an ordinary explanation rather than assuming an extraordinary one from the outset. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific Method The Scientific Method MUFON... Why unresolved does not mean extraordinary Perhaps the most important distinction is that \"unexplained\" is an investigative classification, not a declaration about the object's true nature. A case may remain unresolved for several reasons: The witness reported the event long after it occurred. Critical timing or location information is missing. No additional witnesses can be located. Photographs are too poor for reliable analysis. Several conventional explanations remain plausible but cannot be distinguished with confidence. Conversely, a report can appear remarkable initially yet receive an entirely conventional explanation once flight activity, astronomical conditions"},{"title":"Member Access","url":"/member-access/","description":"Paid access opens searchable reports and selected attachments, but confidential witness details and internal case records remain restricted.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / MUFON","level":3,"text":"Member Access How Much of MUFON's Database Does Membership Unlock? Member Access Member Access How Much of MUFON's Database Does Membership Unlock? Paid access opens searchable reports and selected attachments, but confidential witness details and internal case records remain restricted. Paid access opens searchable reports and selected attachments, but confidential witness details and internal case records remain restricted. UFO Report Databases governance single policy policy intervention Reports and attachments available to members Records reserved for investigators and administrators Copyright limits on copying and redistribution ufo report databases mufon member access Introduction A paid MUFON membership provides substantially more access than the organisation's public facing website, but it does not unlock the organisation's entire investigative archive. Members can search large portions of the Case Management System (CMS), read witness narratives, and, depending on the membership level and the material available for a case, view attachments such as photographs, videos and uploaded documents. At the same time, MUFON maintains additional layers of restricted information for privacy, case management and investigative purposes. Witness contact details, internal investigator notes, administrative records and other sensitive material are not presented as part of ordinary member access. This layered approach is intended to support civilian UFO research while protecting witnesses and preserving the integrity of ongoing investigations. [MUFON] mufon.com Search Database Terms and Conditions Search Database Terms and Conditions MUFON... Reports and Attachments Available to Members MUFON describes database access as a membership privilege rather than a public archive. Its published database terms state that active members may access \"certain parts\" of the Case Management System, specifically including sighting narratives together with uploaded photographs, videos, Microsoft Word documents and PDF files. The wording is notable because it explicitly refers to selected portions of the database rather than unrestricted access to every record held by the organisation. [MUFON] mufon.com Search Database Terms and Conditions Search Database Terms and Conditions MUFON... For a researcher, this access is valuable because it allows examination of more than the brief summaries that often circulate elsewhere. Depending on what a witness submitted, a member may be able to review: the original witness narrative; attached photographs or image files; uploaded video recordings; scanned documents or sketches; PDF and Word document attachments supplied with the report. This gives members the opportunity to compare reports directly, examine visual evidence and search historical cases without relying solely on published case summaries. MUFON's higher Researcher membership also advertises access to Project Aquarius, a separate digital research library and mapping tools that integrate MUFON material with several other historical UFO databases. These are additional research resources rather than unrestricted access to all internal CMS records. [MUFON] mufon.com Join Now! Join Now! MUFON... 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube What Members Do Not See Membership should not be confused with investigator or administrator privileges. MUFON's own documentation consistently distinguishes between member access and confidential case management information. Although members can search many reports, several important categories of information remain restricted. These typically include: witness names, addresses, telephone numbers and other personal identifiers; investigator correspondence with witnesses; [mufonu.com] mufonu.com MUFON Field Investigators are the heart and soul of our organization. To become... internal case management notes; administrative workflow records; information used to assign or supervise investigations; material withheld to protect privacy or ongoing investigations. This distinction reflects MUFON's dual role. The organisation functions both as a public reporting service and as an investigative network. Witnesses often provide personal details with the expectation that investigators—not the general membership—will use them to conduct interviews or request clarification. Restricting that information reduces the risk of unwanted contact and helps preserve witness confidentiality. [MUFON] mufon.com Search Database Terms and Conditions Search Database Terms and Conditions MUFON... The arrangement also means that even paying members cannot independently audit every stage of an investigation. They may see the submitted evidence available through the member database while remaining unable to inspect internal discussions, investigator deliberations or administrative decisions that contributed to a case's eventual classification. Why MUFON Uses Layered Access The membership model balances several competing objectives. First, it supports research by allowing paying members to search a large archive of civilian UFO reports rather than limiting access to isolated published cases. Secondly, it protects witnesses. Many reports contain sensitive personal information or circumstances that witnesses may not wish to become publicly searchable. Restricting identifying information helps encourage reporting while reducing privacy risks. Finally, it supports case management. Investigators need working documents, interview notes and administrative tools that are not intended as public research material. Separating operational records from member facing records reduces the likelihood that incomplete investigations, draft analyses or internal communications will be mistaken for final conclusions. [MUFON] mufon.com Search Database Terms and Conditions Search Database Terms and Conditions MUFON... MUFON MEMBER ACCESS TO OUR 145,000 CASE DATABASE Channel: Mutual UFO Network (MUFON) Open on YouTube Copyright and Limits on Redistribution Access to the database does not transfer ownership of its contents. MUFON's published terms state that reports and media remain protected by United States copyright, with ownership retained by MUFON and the original submitting party where applicable. Members are granted permission to access the material through their membership, not to republish it freely. [MUFON] mufon.com Search Database Terms and Conditions Search Database Terms and Conditions MUFON... The organisation specifically prohibits downloading database content for redistribution through books, magazines, television programmes, films, websites, social media or other commercial or non commercial platforms without prior written permission. According to the published terms, unauthorised redistribution may result in termination of database privileges and could expose users to legal action. [MUFON] mufon.com Search Database Terms and Conditions Search Database Terms and Conditions MUFON... For researchers, this means there is an important distinction between viewing material inside the CMS and reproducing it elsewhere. Even when a member has legitimate database access, images, videos and attached documents cannot automatically be copied into publications or online collections. What Membership Really Unlocks The practical result is that MUFON membership opens a significant research resource without making the organisation's investigative process completely transparent. Members gain searchable access to many witness reports and supporting attachments, allowing historical comparison and independent review of submitted evidence. However, the database remains intentionally layered. Personal witness information, internal investigative records and administrative case files stay behind additional access controls, preserving both witness privacy and the operational needs of MUFON's volunteer investigation system. [MUFON] mufon.com Search Database Terms and Conditions Search Database Terms and Conditions MUFON... Robert Spearing 2024 Interview MUFON’s Project Aquarius & UFO Archives Channel: Nite Muse Media Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to How Much of MUFON's Database Does Membership Unlock?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The UFO Book: Encyclopedia of the Extraterrestrial By Jerome Clark The UFO Book includes a historical overview of UFO phenomenon, an overview of its terminology roots, and in depth information regarding... See on Amazon Book UFOs and Government: A Historical Inquiry By Michael D. Swords Governments around the world have had to deal with the UFO phenomenon for a good part of a century. How and why they did so is the subjec... See on Amazon Book Copyright: What Everyone Needs to Know By Neil Weinstock Netanel In Copyright: What Everyone Needs to Know®, intellectual"},{"title":"Unexplained Labels","url":"/unexplained-labels/","description":"An unexplained MUFON case may reflect unusual evidence, but it can also result from missing data, weak imagery or an unavailable witness.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / MUFON","level":3,"text":"Unexplained Labels What MUFON's Unexplained Label Really Tells You Unexplained Labels Unexplained Labels What MUFON's Unexplained Label Really Tells You An unexplained MUFON case may reflect unusual evidence, but it can also result from missing data, weak imagery or an unavailable witness. An unexplained MUFON case may reflect unusual evidence, but it can also result from missing data, weak imagery or an unavailable witness. UFO Report Databases critique risk single policy policy intervention How MUFON reaches an unexplained disposition Data gaps that can prevent identification Separating investigative outcome from object identity ufo report databases mufon unexplained labels Introduction An \"unexplained\" or \"unknown\" disposition in a MUFON case should not be read as proof that a witness observed an extraordinary craft. It is a description of the investigation's outcome , not a confirmed identification of the reported object. In practice, the label means that the assigned investigator could not confidently match the available evidence to a conventional explanation after completing the investigation. That distinction matters because an unresolved case can result from genuinely unusual observations, but it can also arise from incomplete evidence, conflicting testimony, poor quality imagery, missing contextual information or practical limits on what investigators are able to verify. MUFON itself states that its investigators begin by attempting to explain reports through ordinary causes and only classify cases as unknown after reasonable investigative efforts fail to produce a satisfactory identification. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific Method The Scientific Method MUFON... Within the broader context of MUFON investigations and restricted database access, this distinction is especially important because outside readers often see only the final disposition rather than the investigator's reasoning, supporting evidence or remaining uncertainties. How MUFON Reaches an Unexplained Disposition MUFON describes its investigative process as an application of the scientific method. Investigators begin with the working hypothesis that a reported event has a conventional explanation and attempt to confirm or reject that hypothesis by gathering additional information. This normally includes interviewing witnesses, reviewing photographs or video, checking astronomical and aviation data where available, examining environmental conditions and considering common sources of misidentification such as aircraft, satellites, drones, Chinese lanterns or optical effects. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific Method The Scientific Method MUFON... Only after those avenues fail to produce a convincing explanation may a case receive an unknown or unexplained disposition. Importantly, this is not a declaration that investigators have identified an extraordinary object. It is simply a statement that the available investigation did not produce an adequate conventional explanation. MUFON's own guidance also recognises several other dispositions—including identified flying object (IFO), hoax, insufficient data and information only—which shows that the organisation distinguishes between different reasons an investigation may end without a conventional identification. Recent investigator guidance further divides unknown cases into categories such as \"Unknown – UAV\" and \"Unknown – Other\", emphasising that even within unexplained reports there are different investigative outcomes rather than a single conclusion. [MUFON] mufon.com JULY 2023 MUFON Journal WEB No. 663 $5.99 A NEWCOMER’S GUIDE TO MUFMarch 6, 2026... Published: JULY 2023 Why an Unexplained Case May Still Be Inconclusive One of the biggest misconceptions is that \"unexplained\" means investigators ruled out every ordinary possibility. In reality, investigations are often constrained by the quality and completeness of the evidence. Several common limitations can prevent a confident identification: Witnesses cannot be contacted. If follow up interviews never occur, important details about timing, direction, weather or viewing conditions may remain unknown. Reports arrive long after the event. Memories fade, making precise reconstruction more difficult. Images lack useful information. Blurry photographs, digital compression, missing metadata or unknown camera settings may prevent reliable analysis. The observation was too brief. A few seconds of observation may not provide enough information to distinguish between multiple ordinary explanations. Conflicting testimony. Multiple witnesses may remember key details differently, preventing investigators from reconstructing the event with confidence. Missing external records. Flight information, weather observations or other corroborating data may be unavailable or incomplete for the relevant time and location. None of these circumstances demonstrates that an anomalous object was present. Instead, they illustrate how an investigation can reach the practical limit of what the available evidence allows. 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube Investigative Outcome Is Not Object Identity A useful way to understand MUFON's terminology is to separate two different questions: 1. What did investigators conclude? 2. What was the object actually? An unexplained disposition answers only the first question. The investigation may conclude that available evidence does not support a conventional identification. That does not establish what the object actually was. The true explanation could still be an ordinary aircraft, an atmospheric phenomenon, an optical illusion or another mundane event that simply could not be confirmed with the available evidence. This distinction is familiar in many investigative disciplines. An unsolved criminal investigation does not prove that a particular suspect committed the crime; it indicates only that investigators could not establish a definitive explanation from the available evidence. Similarly, an unexplained MUFON case identifies a limit in the investigation rather than providing positive evidence for any particular hypothesis. Restricted Access Makes Independent Evaluation Difficult The possibility of misunderstanding increases because many detailed MUFON case files are not openly available. Paid members can access substantially more material in the Case Management System than casual readers, while investigator notes and internal correspondence may remain restricted. [MUFON] mufon.com Search Database Terms and Conditions Search Database Terms and Conditions MUFON... As a result, someone encountering a publicly cited \"unexplained\" case may be unable to examine: the witness interview, investigator notes, image analysis, correspondence with specialists, supporting documents, rejected hypotheses, or unresolved uncertainties. Without those materials, readers may incorrectly assume that \"unexplained\" reflects exceptionally strong evidence, when in some cases it simply reflects an inability to gather enough reliable information for a firm conclusion. 50:23 MUFON – The Truth Behind UFOs and Alien Encounters Channel: J. Horton Films · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: January 2023 · Length: 50 minutes Open on YouTube The Label Should Be Read as a Starting Point, Not an End Point For researchers comparing UFO databases, an unexplained MUFON case is often most valuable as a candidate for further examination rather than as a verified anomaly. Cases deserving closer attention generally combine several characteristics: multiple independent witnesses , consistent testimony, high quality original media, documented environmental analysis, elimination of plausible conventional explanations, and transparent investigative reasoning. By contrast, an unexplained label attached to sparse or inaccessible documentation should be treated more cautiously. The same disposition can arise from fundamentally different evidential situations, ranging from a remarkably well documented but unresolved observation to a case where crucial information was never available. What the Label Really Tells You The most accurate interpretation is that MUFON's unexplained designation reflects the limits of an investigation , not necessarily the nature of the reported object. For readers using UFO report databases and catalogues, the disposition should therefore be viewed as one piece of metadata rather than the final answer. It signals that investigators were unable to reach a satisfactory identification using the evidence available to them, but it does not establish that the reported object was extraordinary, nor does it rule out conventional explanations that could not be confirmed with the information at hand. [mufon.com] mufon.com The Scientific Method The Scientific Method MUFON... 2:00:13 Pro Investigator Reveals UFO Investigation Secrets! Plus some awesome UFO videos Channel: Northland"},{"title":"Duplicate Reports","url":"/duplicate-reports/","description":"Several catalogue entries may describe one shared event, one copied account, or genuinely separate observations from different locations.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Search by Date","level":3,"text":"Duplicate Reports Are Five UFO Reports Really Five Witnesses? Search by Date Duplicate Reports Duplicate Reports Are Five UFO Reports Really Five Witnesses? Several catalogue entries may describe one shared event, one copied account, or genuinely separate observations from different locations. Several catalogue entries may describe one shared event, one copied account, or genuinely separate observations from different locations. UFO Report Databases critique risk bounded topic dataset evidence Comparing witness positions and viewing directions Tracing copied narratives across catalogues Grouping statements into distinct incidents ufo report databases search by date duplicate reports Introduction A database showing five UFO reports does not necessarily represent five independent witnesses or even five separate incidents. Public catalogues count different things: individual witness statements, submitted forms, newspaper summaries, investigator files and database records. The same event may appear repeatedly across multiple collections, while several genuinely independent observers may be merged into a single case. Understanding this distinction is essential when using UFO report databases , because duplicated records can exaggerate apparent patterns, whereas overly aggressive merging can hide valuable corroboration. Most established reporting organisations also caution that their databases contain witness reports rather than verified events, making careful source comparison an essential part of any analysis. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 Are Five UFO Reports Really Five Witnesses? The first question is not \"How many reports exist?\" but \"How many independent observations exist?\" An incident may generate multiple records because: each witness submits a separate report; one witness reports to several organisations; investigators create their own case files; newspapers summarise the event; later catalogues import or transcribe earlier collections. Conversely, a single database entry may summarise a mass sighting involving dozens or hundreds of observers. For this reason, experienced researchers distinguish between: Counted itemWhat it actually representsWitness reportOne submitted statementInvestigation fileA case assembled by investigatorsDatabase recordOne catalogue entry, which may combine or split reportsIncidentThe underlying event being described The incident—not the raw number of records—is usually the appropriate unit for comparing UFO databases. Comparing Witness Positions and Viewing Directions Independent witnesses should agree on the existence of an event without simply repeating the same narrative. The strongest corroboration usually comes from observers who describe the phenomenon from different locations before learning about one another. Useful comparison points include: precise observation location; viewing direction (azimuth); elevation above the horizon; estimated movement across the sky; start and finish times; duration; local weather and visibility. If two witnesses several kilometres apart independently report an object moving from the south west towards the north east at nearly the same time, those observations can often be geometrically compared. If the viewing directions are incompatible, the reports may concern different objects despite similar descriptions. Modern investigative guidance also emphasises collecting measurable information such as observer coordinates, angular position and estimated motion because these allow independent reports to be tested against one another rather than compared only by narrative similarity. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific Method MUFONThe Scientific Method MUFON... 5:09 UFO Sightings: How Scientists are Trying to Capture More Data NOVA PBS Channel: NOVA PBS Official · Views: 25.1K · Uploaded: March 2025 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube Agreement matters more than identical wording Ironically, reports that are too similar can be less convincing than reports that agree on essential facts while differing in personal details. Independent observers naturally vary in: colour descriptions; estimated size; emotional reactions; remembered sequence of events. However, they should broadly agree on: time; location; direction; overall movement; duration. Perfectly matching wording often indicates copying rather than genuine independence. Tracing Copied Narratives Across Catalogues Many historical UFO catalogues inherited material from earlier publications. A report may therefore appear in several databases despite originating from a single witness. Warning signs include: identical spelling mistakes; identical punctuation; matching unusual phrases; the same missing details; identical witness quotations reproduced verbatim. Researchers therefore try to identify the earliest known version of a report rather than treating every later appearance as fresh evidence. For example, a witness may: 5:21 Washington man spent last 25 years running National UFO Reporting Center Channel: KREM 2 News · Views: 6.3K · Uploaded: February 2019 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube 1. telephone a reporting centre; 2. submit an online form; 3. appear in a local newspaper; 4. be interviewed by an investigator; 5. later be included in a historical database. Five records may therefore document one witness rather than five witnesses. This issue is especially important because organisations differ in how they manage submissions. NUFORC , for example, primarily publishes witness reports in largely unedited form, whereas organisations with field investigators may produce additional investigative documentation derived from the original account. [NUFORC] nuforc.org 2022 MUFON International Symposium Presentation Abtract NUFORCApril 11, 2022... Published: April 11, 2022 Grouping Statements Into Distinct Incidents Rather than counting reports individually, many researchers attempt to cluster records into single incidents. Typical grouping criteria include: same date and time window; nearby observation locations; compatible flight paths; similar duration; matching environmental conditions; absence of contradictory timing. This approach resembles methods used in other observational sciences. For example, meteor organisations routinely combine many geographically separated witness reports into one fireball event after comparing times, trajectories and viewing geometry rather than treating every submission as a separate phenomenon. The same principle can improve the interpretation of UFO databases by distinguishing multiple observations of one event from multiple unrelated sightings. [CIA] cia.gov How To Investigate a Flying Saucer How To Investigate a Flying Saucer CIA... However, clustering should remain conservative. Two reports filed on the same evening may describe entirely different objects if they occurred hours apart or in opposite directions. Common Sources of False Multiplication Several routine processes can inflate apparent case numbers without increasing the amount of independent evidence. Multiple reporting channels. One witness may report to several organisations. Historical transcription. Older printed catalogues are frequently digitised into newer databases. Media amplification. Newspaper stories can prompt additional retrospective reports from people who had not previously reported their observations. Investigator summaries. Field investigations may produce reports that are separate documents but not independent observations. Database imports. Some catalogues merge earlier datasets, creating duplicate records unless careful de duplication is performed. Each of these mechanisms increases document counts without necessarily increasing the number of witnesses or incidents. 49:30 UFOs In American Culture: A History Of Intrigue And Denial Channel: Connecticut Public Podcasts · Views: 78 · Uploaded: September 2024 · Length: 49 minutes Open on YouTube Why Independence Matters Independent corroboration strengthens a case because it reduces the chance that multiple records merely repeat one person's memory or one published story. It also allows investigators to compare geometry, timing and consistency across observations. By contrast, treating every catalogue entry as independent evidence can create misleading statistical patterns, exaggerate apparent sighting waves and overestimate witness numbers. Some researchers have therefore proposed explicit classifications distinguishing raw witness reports from more fully investigated cases, recognising that collections often combine records with very different evidential status. [Zenodo] zenodo.org Investigation Status for Collections of Reported UFO Cases: Proposal of a Classification ZenodoJanuary 27, 2024... Published: January 27, 2024 When evaluating any UFO database, the most informative question is therefore not how many reports exist, but how many independently documented"},{"title":"Airfield Records","url":"/airfield-records/","description":"Searching nearby bases, airfields, police forces, and military districts can uncover records filed under institutions rather than the witness's town.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Search by Date","level":3,"text":"Airfield Records Was the UFO File Logged Under a Nearby Base? Search by Date Airfield Records Airfield Records Was the UFO File Logged Under a Nearby Base? Searching nearby bases, airfields, police forces, and military districts can uncover records filed under institutions rather than the witness's town. Searching nearby bases, airfields, police forces, and military districts can uncover records filed under institutions rather than the witness's town. UFO Report Databases historical comparative bounded topic entity Identifying nearby civilian and military airfields Searching archive terms beyond the town name Separating sighting dates from memo and filing dates ufo report databases search by date airfield records Introduction A UFO report is not always filed under the town where a witness saw an unusual object. In many historical archives, the decisive factor was the organisation that received, assessed or forwarded the report rather than the location of the event itself. A sighting near an RAF station, military airfield, civilian airport or police headquarters may therefore appear within records created by those institutions instead of under the witness's town name. Recognising this filing logic can transform an apparently unsuccessful archive search into a productive one, especially when working with government collections assembled before modern searchable databases. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... Rather than assuming every record is indexed geographically, treat nearby aviation and defence organisations as potential record creators. Their names, abbreviations and administrative structures often provide better search terms than the place where the sighting occurred. Was the UFO File Logged Under a Nearby Base? Government archives generally preserve records according to the office that created or managed them. A report from a member of the public might pass through a local police force, an air traffic authority, an RAF operations room or an Air Ministry desk before reaching a central UFO file. Each stage could generate correspondence, memoranda or intelligence summaries with different filing conventions. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... This means a search for a sighting over a village may fail even though related documents survive under: the nearest RAF station or United States Air Force installation; a civilian airport or regional air traffic authority; an air defence headquarters; a police constabulary or regional command; the government department responsible for evaluating aerial reports. The UK Ministry of Defence UFO files, for example, include correspondence originating from multiple branches rather than a single continuous \"UFO office\", making institutional searches essential when tracing individual incidents. [National Archives] media.nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFO files at The National Archives The National Archives National Archives UFO files at The National Archives The National Archives 9:47 UFO file release October 2008 Channel: The National Archives UK · Views: 19.5K · Uploaded: October 2008 · Length: 9 minutes Open on YouTube Identifying nearby civilian and military airfields Before searching catalogues, establish which aviation facilities operated within a realistic radius of the reported sighting at the time it occurred. Historical maps and aeronautical references are often more useful than present day airport lists because many wartime and Cold War installations have since closed or changed names. Relevant locations include: active RAF or Air Force bases; former wartime airfields; reserve or auxiliary stations; naval air stations; civilian airports; military training areas with associated flying operations; radar stations linked to nearby airfields. The nearest installation may not have been the one with reporting responsibility. During the Cold War, observations could be forwarded through regional air defence networks or intelligence organisations several administrative layers away from the witness's location. [National Archives] media.nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFO files at The National Archives The National Archives National Archives UFO files at The National Archives The National Archives A practical search should therefore record both the geographical distance and the administrative relationships between nearby aviation facilities. Search Beyond the Town Name Searching only the witness's town encourages false negatives because archive catalogues often index files using institutional terminology. Useful additional search terms include: official station names (for example, \"RAF Bentwaters\" rather than only the neighbouring town); historical names of airfields that later changed designation; command headquarters; operational groups or districts; Air Ministry or Ministry of Defence file references where known; police force names rather than municipal authorities. The UK National Archives specifically recommends searching across several record series, including DEFE, AIR, FCO and other departmental collections using terms such as \"UFO\", \" unidentified flying\" and related expressions, reflecting the fact that relevant material was distributed among multiple departments instead of one central archive. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... The same principle applies in the United States. Project Blue Book records include not only chronological sighting files but also material organised by Air Force Office of Special Investigations districts and overseas commands. Consequently, an event may appear under the responsible investigative organisation as well as under its date and location. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National Archives... 3:56 UFO file release March 2009 Channel: The National Archives UK · Views: 128.6K · Uploaded: March 2009 · Length: 3 minutes 56 seconds Open on YouTube Separate the Sighting Date from the Filing Date Institutional filing frequently introduces delays between the observation and the surviving paperwork. A witness may report an incident immediately, while an intelligence summary, staff memorandum or correspondence file is created days or weeks later. When searching archives, distinguish at least three dates: the date and time of the observation; the date the report reached an authority; the date a memorandum, investigation or filing action was produced. These dates may fall in different months or even different calendar years if historical reviews, parliamentary enquiries or freedom of information requests generated new documentation long after the original event. Searching only the sighting date can therefore overlook valuable administrative records. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... Likewise, a file title dated months after a reported sighting does not necessarily indicate a later incident. It may simply record internal correspondence about an earlier observation. Why Institutional Searches Often Reveal More Than Geographic Searches Large government collections were designed for administrative retrieval rather than public historical research. Officials needed to locate files by office, responsibility or operational function, not by the modern expectations of UFO database users. The surviving UK records demonstrate this clearly. Reports, policy discussions, parliamentary correspondence and military enquiries were generated by several different branches, while many earlier files were destroyed under retention policies before systematic preservation began. Researchers who search only for town names risk concluding that no records exist when relevant documents survive elsewhere in the administrative chain. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFO reports National ArchivesUFO reports The National Archives... Similarly, the U.S. National Archives describes Project Blue Book holdings as including administrative files, chronological case files, Office of Special Investigations records and finding aids that organise material in different ways. Understanding those filing structures allows researchers to locate records that simple place name searches may never reveal. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National Archives... A Practical Archive Strategy When investigating a historical UFO report, expand the search in institutional rather than purely geographic terms: 48:15 The British UFO Files: Secret Government Investigations Revealed Channel: Documentary Overload · Views: 796 · Uploaded: April 2026 · Length: 48 minutes Open on"},{"title":"Time Shifts","url":"/time-shifts/","description":"A sighting near midnight can appear on different dates once local time, daylight saving rules, and UTC are reconciled.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Search by Date","level":3,"text":"Time Shifts Did the UFO Sighting Happen on the Wrong Date? Search by Date Time Shifts Time Shifts Did the UFO Sighting Happen on the Wrong Date? A sighting near midnight can appear on different dates once local time, daylight saving rules, and UTC are reconciled. A sighting near midnight can appear on different dates once local time, daylight saving rules, and UTC are reconciled. UFO Report Databases mechanism single mechanism mechanism Converting the witness's local time to UTC Checking historical daylight saving rules Searching both sides of the midnight boundary ufo report databases search by date time shifts Introduction A UFO sighting recorded at 11:55 pm may not remain on the same calendar date once it is compared with other records. Different databases store times differently: some preserve the witness's local clock time, others convert everything to Coordinated Universal Time (UTC), and some contain only a calendar date with no explicit time zone. Around midnight, those differences can move what appears to be the same event into the previous or following day. For anyone comparing UFO catalogues, astronomical records or aviation data, understanding these time shifts is essential to avoid missing genuine matches or creating false ones. Did the UFO Sighting Happen on the Wrong Date? A change of only a few minutes can alter the calendar day when different time standards are used. Consider a witness who reports a sighting at 11:58 pm on 14 March in eastern North America. Converted to UTC, the event occurred during the early hours of 15 March. A European database storing UTC would therefore index it under 15 March, while a witness oriented catalogue might keep the original local date. The reverse can also happen. A report from Japan at 12:10 am on 15 March converts to the afternoon of 14 March in UTC. Searching only the witness's calendar date could therefore overlook related satellite observations, meteor records or military logs that are indexed by UTC. The practical consequence is straightforward: when investigating a sighting close to midnight, search the reported date and the adjoining calendar days before concluding that no matching record exists. Converting the Witness's Local Time to UTC The most reliable approach is to reconstruct the original observation before comparing databases. Start with: the witness's reported local date the reported local time (or best estimate) the observation location the time zone that applied on that date [iana.org] iana.org It is updated periodi Time ZonesJuly 8, 2026 — TIME ZONES The Time Zone Database (often called tz or zoneinfo ) contains code and data that represent the hi... Published: July 8, 2026 whether daylight saving time (DST) was in effect [iana.org] iana.org Time zone and daylight saving time data Time zone and daylight saving time data... Only then should the local time be converted into UTC. This matters because many scientific datasets—including satellite tracking, astronomical observations and some meteor catalogues—use UTC as their reference standard. Comparing a local witness time directly with a UTC timestamp without conversion can produce an apparent mismatch of several hours and, around midnight, an entirely different calendar date. Where reports provide only an approximate time such as \"around midnight\" or \"just after twelve\", it is often better to search a wider window rather than treating the stated time as exact. 26:12 Experts analyze Pentagon’s UFO file release Channel: CBS News · Views: 315.9K · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 26 minutes Open on YouTube Checking Historical Daylight Saving Rules Historical daylight saving rules cannot safely be inferred from today's clock settings. Governments have repeatedly changed when clocks move forward or back, and those changes vary between countries, regions and years. The public IANA Time Zone Database records these historical civil time rules and is widely used by operating systems and software to calculate the correct UTC offset for past dates. [IANA] iana.org Time zone and daylight saving time data Time zone and daylight saving time data... For example, the United States altered the start and end dates of daylight saving time several times during the twentieth century and again in 2007. Similar changes have occurred across Europe, Australia and many other regions. Applying modern DST rules to an older sighting can shift the converted UTC time by an hour, potentially moving an event across the midnight boundary. [IANA] iana.org How to Read the tz Database How to Read the tz Database... Older reports present additional complications: witnesses sometimes wrote only \"summer time\" or \"standard time\" newspaper editors occasionally normalised times without stating they had done so archived catalogues may omit whether DST was observed political changes have altered both time zone boundaries and UTC offsets over time These factors mean that a one hour discrepancy does not necessarily indicate conflicting reports; it may simply reflect different assumptions about local civil time. Searching Both Sides of the Midnight Boundary When a sighting occurs between roughly 11:30 pm and 12:30 am local time, treat the midnight boundary as uncertain until the timing has been verified. A practical search strategy is to examine: 1. The reported local calendar date. 2. The previous calendar day in UTC indexed sources. 3. The following calendar day in UTC indexed sources. 4. A time window extending at least one hour on either side of the reported time if the witness gave only an estimate. This method is particularly valuable when comparing UFO reports with meteor databases, satellite re entry records or astronomical observations, which commonly store events using UTC for consistency across countries. Common Sources of Date Confusion Several mechanisms can make apparently conflicting dates refer to the same observation: Local versus UTC indexing. Different databases store different reference times. Daylight saving assumptions. One catalogue may apply DST correctly while another assumes standard time. Retrospective transcription. Historical reports copied into later databases may have been converted—or not converted—without documenting the method. Witness recall. People often remember an event as occurring \"late Friday night\", while an investigator may record the official date as Saturday after midnight. Unknown observation time. Reports stating only \"around midnight\" inherently span two possible calendar dates. Recognising these mechanisms helps distinguish genuine inconsistencies from simple differences in timekeeping. Top Aerospace Scientists Suspect UFOs are Biblical Time Machines Diana Walsh Pasulka Channel: Danny Jones Open on YouTube Why Accurate Date Conversion Improves Catalogue Matching Many apparent failures to match UFO reports across catalogues arise not because the events are unrelated, but because the records were indexed under different dates. Converting the witness's original local time using the correct historical time zone and daylight saving rules, then searching both sides of the midnight boundary, substantially reduces the risk of overlooking corresponding reports. For researchers comparing multiple databases, the calendar date should therefore be treated as a searchable attribute rather than an immutable fact. Around midnight, the same sighting can legitimately appear under different dates depending on whether the source preserves local civil time or uses UTC as its reference. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Did the UFO Sighting Happen on the Wrong Date?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Craft of Research By Wayne C. Booth, Gregory G. Colomb et al. Rating: 4.3/5 from 6 Google Books ratings Since 1995, students, researchers, and professionals have turned to The Craft of Research for clear and helpful guidance on how to conduc... See on Amazon Book NightWatch: A Practical Guide to Viewing the Universe By Terence Dickinson A practical guide to viewing the universe. See on Amazon Book The Backyard Astronomer's Guide By Terence Dickinson, Alan Dyer Discusses the practical aspects of stargazing, including how to choose appropriate equipment, contending with light pollution, taking suc... See on Amazon Book Going to the Sources: A Guide to Historical Research and Writing By Anthony Brundage It’s been almost 30 years since the first edition of Going to the Sources: A Guide to Historical Research and Writing was first published... See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: The Craft of Research NightWatch: A Practical to Viewing the Universe books The Backyard Astronomer's books As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. eBay marketplace picks Marketplace Samples Live tested eBay searches with available results related to this page. Shop location Using USA USA USA UK Canada Australia Ireland USA UK Canada Australia Ireland Selected from"},{"title":"Newspaper Check","url":"/newspaper-check/","description":"Local newspapers can reveal police calls, meteors, aircraft activity, outages, and publicity surrounding a reported sighting.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Search by Date","level":3,"text":"Newspaper Check What Did Local Newspapers Report That Night? Search by Date Newspaper Check Newspaper Check What Did Local Newspapers Report That Night? Local newspapers can reveal police calls, meteors, aircraft activity, outages, and publicity surrounding a reported sighting. Local newspapers can reveal police calls, meteors, aircraft activity, outages, and publicity surrounding a reported sighting. UFO Report Databases evidence single dataset dataset evidence Searching period terms such as strange light and fireball Checking neighboring towns and police call coverage Handling OCR errors and inspecting page images ufo report databases search by date newspaper check Introduction Contemporary local newspapers are among the most valuable sources for checking whether a reported UFO sighting coincided with other observable events. Unlike later retellings, newspapers published within hours or days of an incident often record what local journalists, police, emergency services, airports, observatories and residents believed was happening at the time. They can reveal whether multiple people independently reported unusual lights, whether a bright meteor crossed the region, whether electrical failures or military activity attracted public attention, or whether the supposed UFO was barely noticed outside a small circle of witnesses. For researchers using UFO report databases and catalogues, newspapers therefore serve as an independent layer of evidence rather than simply another copy of the same story. They help establish the wider context of a claimed sighting and identify events that may explain—or occasionally corroborate—the reports. Modern digitised newspaper archives also make it possible to search neighbouring communities, compare editions published on successive days and inspect the original page images rather than relying solely on searchable text. [Library of Congress Guides] guides.loc.gov Library of Congress Guides Search Tips Library of Congress GuidesSearch Tips Chronicling America: A Guide for Researchers Research Guides at Library of CongressJuly 8, 2026... Published: July 8, 2026 What Did Local Newspapers Report That Night? A newspaper search should begin with the exact date, approximate time and location of the reported sighting. Rather than searching only for \"UFO\", look for the language journalists of the period were more likely to use. Useful search terms include: strange light mysterious light fireball bright object object in the sky meteor explosion in the sky unusual aircraft police flooded with calls residents report lights Older newspapers frequently avoided the term \"UFO\", especially before it became common in the late 1940s. Even decades later, editors often preferred neutral descriptions until an official explanation emerged. Searching only for \"UFO\" can therefore miss reports that describe exactly the same event using different language. Equally important is searching the following day's edition. A sighting occurring late in the evening might not appear until the morning paper, while investigations or official statements often followed a day or two later. 1:37 New Zealand military declassifies UFO files Channel: Al Jazeera English · Views: 73.9K · Uploaded: December 2010 · Length: 1 minute 37 seconds Open on YouTube Searching Neighbouring Towns and Police Coverage One of the strongest uses of newspapers is determining whether an apparently isolated report was actually part of a wider regional event. A bright meteor, rocket re entry or atmospheric phenomenon may generate reports across dozens of towns. Individual UFO databases sometimes contain these as separate witness accounts, whereas newspapers can show that emergency services in several districts received calls within the same period. Look for coverage such as: police switchboards receiving numerous telephone calls; reports from neighbouring towns describing similar lights; airport or air traffic officials commenting on unusual observations; observatories or meteorological offices issuing explanations; journalists interviewing multiple independent witnesses . Neighbouring newspapers are often as important as the paper serving the reported location. A phenomenon high in the atmosphere rarely respects county or municipal boundaries, and different editorial teams may publish complementary details. One newspaper might report police activity while another interviews airport officials or astronomers, allowing a fuller reconstruction of the night's events. Looking Beyond the UFO Story A newspaper's value often lies outside articles explicitly discussing unidentified objects. The same edition may include reports of: severe thunderstorms producing unusual lightning effects; power failures affecting large areas; military exercises or air displays; rocket launches or satellite related news reported nationally; astronomical events expected that evening; wildfires producing illuminated smoke; emergency incidents drawing aircraft into the area. Because these reports were written independently of later UFO interpretations, they help establish what else was happening locally. Even if none provides a complete explanation, together they build a timeline against which witness accounts can be compared. Researchers should also examine weather pages, shipping news, aviation columns and local briefs, not only the front page. Significant contextual information often appeared in specialist sections rather than headline news. 8:37 UFO file release February 2010 Channel: The National Archives UK · Views: 454.7K · Uploaded: February 2010 · Length: 8 minutes Open on YouTube Handling OCR Errors and Inspecting Original Pages Digitised newspaper archives rely heavily on optical character recognition (OCR), which converts scanned pages into searchable text. Historic newspapers often contain damaged paper, unusual fonts, poor printing and complex layouts, all of which produce OCR errors. As a result, relevant articles may not appear in straightforward keyword searches even when they exist. [The Library of Congress] loc.gov Open source on loc.gov. Good practice includes: searching several alternative spellings and descriptive phrases; using date and location filters before broadening keywords; trying proximity searches rather than exact phrases where supported; checking adjacent issues if no results appear; opening the scanned page image instead of relying only on OCR text. Viewing the original page is especially important because OCR may omit captions, misread headlines or fail to recognise key words entirely. Modern newspaper archive interfaces increasingly allow users to search full pages, apply geographic filters and clip page images directly from the original scans, making verification easier than relying on extracted text alone. [Blog @ the Library of Congress] blogs.loc.gov Open source on loc.gov. Distinguishing Independent Reporting from Repeated Stories A newspaper article is not automatically independent confirmation of a UFO report. Many local papers republished material from wire services or copied reports from neighbouring newspapers with minimal editing. Likewise, later UFO catalogues may quote those newspaper stories rather than original witnesses, creating the appearance of multiple independent sources. When evaluating newspaper evidence, ask: Does the article identify named local witnesses? Does it describe police, airport or official responses first hand? Is it reporting observations made locally or summarising another newspaper's story? Does it contain details absent from later UFO catalogues? Are several newspapers independently reporting the same local events or merely reproducing identical wording? Identifying genuinely independent reporting strengthens the evidential value of newspaper coverage, while recognising syndicated or duplicated stories helps prevent double counting evidence in a reconstructed timeline. 6:57 UFOs discovered in The National Archives Channel: The National Archives UK · Views: 39.7K · Uploaded: May 2008 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube Why Newspaper Checks Matter for UFO Databases UFO report databases are designed to collect sightings, but newspapers capture the broader public record of the same night. Together they provide complementary evidence. A well executed newspaper search can reveal that a reported UFO coincided with dozens of emergency calls, a spectacular fireball, unusual aviation activity or widespread public attention that is only partly reflected in later catalogues. Conversely, the absence of any contemporary local coverage—even when a dramatic event is later claimed—may itself become an important piece of context when assessing the reliability, prominence and historical reconstruction of a reported sighting. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to What Did Local Newspapers Report That Night?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Craft of Research By"},{"title":"Pilot Silence","url":"/pilot-silence/","description":"Pilots may see unusual aerial events more often than most people yet report less because professional stigma can outweigh the value of speaking up.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Reporting Bias","level":3,"text":"Pilot Silence Why Pilots Sometimes Keep UFO Sightings Quiet Reporting Bias Pilot Silence Pilot Silence Why Pilots Sometimes Keep UFO Sightings Quiet Pilots may see unusual aerial events more often than most people yet report less because professional stigma can outweigh the value of speaking up. Pilots may see unusual aerial events more often than most people yet report less because professional stigma can outweigh the value of speaking up. UFO Report Databases critique risk bounded topic population context Career risks and fear of ridicule How confidential reporting changes case counts Why silence cannot be treated as no sightings ufo report databases reporting bias pilot silence Introduction Pilots are often regarded as especially valuable witnesses because they are trained to recognise aircraft, weather phenomena, navigation lights and other features of the sky. Yet aviation professionals appear far less frequently in public UFO and unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) databases than many people assume. That imbalance should not automatically be interpreted as evidence that pilots rarely encounter unusual aerial events. Instead, research and official reviews suggest that professional, cultural and institutional pressures have historically discouraged some pilots from making formal reports, particularly through public UFO reporting channels. [AARO] aaro.mil AARO FAQ... For anyone interpreting UFO report databases and catalogues, this distinction is important. A low number of pilot submitted cases may reflect barriers to reporting rather than an absence of unusual observations. Understanding those barriers helps explain why aviation data can be incomplete and why changes in reporting policy may affect case numbers without any corresponding change in what is actually seen in the sky. Inside NASA's UFO coverup culture Reality Check Channel: NewsNation Open on YouTube Career risks and fear of ridicule Commercial and military pilots operate in professions where judgement, situational awareness and decision making are central to public trust. Historically, many aviators have worried that reporting an unidentified object could expose them to scepticism from colleagues, unwanted media attention or questions about their competence, even if they were simply reporting an unexplained observation rather than claiming an extraordinary explanation. [WIRED] wired.com The agency stressed the need to shift the conversation from sensationalism to science and eliminate the stigma associated with reporting... NASA's 2023 Independent Study Team on UAP concluded that stigma remains a significant obstacle to collecting better quality information. The report argued that the negative perception surrounding UAP reporting discourages witnesses from coming forward, reducing the amount of potentially useful data available for scientific analysis. Rather than treating this as evidence for any particular explanation, the panel identified stigma as a data quality problem. [WIRED] wired.com The agency stressed the need to shift the conversation from sensationalism to science and eliminate the stigma associated with reporting... Military reporting has reflected similar concerns. The U.S. government's 2021 preliminary assessment of UAP noted that aviators and intelligence personnel described \"disparagement\" associated with observing, reporting or discussing unusual aerial events, suggesting that organisational culture itself contributed to under reporting. [AARO] aaro.mil AARO FAQ... For civilian airline pilots, the concern is often less about official punishment than professional reputation. Flight crews routinely make safety reports about weather, equipment failures and operational hazards, but an event that cannot immediately be identified may be perceived differently because of the cultural associations attached to the term \"UFO\". The result can be silence even when a pilot believes an observation deserves documentation. 59:18 NASA UFO report finds no evidence UAP have extraterrestrial origins FULL Channel: Global News · Views: 37.7K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube How confidential reporting changes case counts Modern aviation safety increasingly relies on confidential, non punitive reporting systems. NASA's Aviation Safety Reporting System (ASRS), operating on behalf of the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA), allows pilots and other aviation professionals to submit confidential reports about hazards, incidents and near misses. Reports are de identified before being shared with the wider aviation community, encouraging participation without fear of individual blame. [NASA] nasa.gov Aviation Safety Reporting System Overview Aviation Safety Reporting System Overview NASAMarch 3, 2026... Published: March 3, 2026 This reporting culture demonstrates an important principle: pilots are generally willing to report safety issues when they trust that the system exists to improve safety rather than assign fault. The success of confidential aviation reporting suggests that institutional design can strongly influence reporting rates. [NASA] nasa.gov Aviation Safety Reporting System Overview Aviation Safety Reporting System Overview NASAMarch 3, 2026... Published: March 3, 2026 The same principle is increasingly being applied to UAP reporting. The U.S. Department of Defense's All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) now encourages civilian pilots to report unusual aerial observations through air traffic control, with relevant Pilot Reports (PIREPs) reaching AARO through FAA channels. This represents a shift away from relying solely on informal or specialist UFO organisations and towards integrating unusual observations into established aviation safety processes. [AARO] aaro.mil AARO FAQ... These changes matter when interpreting historical databases. Older public catalogues may under represent professional aviators not because pilots observed less, but because there were fewer trusted, official pathways for reporting events that could not immediately be identified. Why silence cannot be treated as no sightings A common mistake is to assume that few reports from pilots mean few unusual observations by pilots. That conclusion overlooks the difference between an event occurring and an event entering a database. Several factors can interrupt that chain: A pilot may quickly conclude that the object presents no flight safety risk and therefore not pursue further reporting. Air traffic control or operational procedures may already provide an explanation before a formal report is completed. A witness may discuss the event informally with colleagues but never submit it to a public catalogue. Concerns about reputation may outweigh the perceived value of filing a report, particularly if no additional evidence exists. Because of these filters, report databases record reporting behaviour as well as observations. This is a classic example of reporting bias : the probability that an event appears in a catalogue depends partly on social and institutional factors, not solely on what occurred. The issue is not unique to UFO reporting. Aviation safety researchers have long recognised that voluntary reporting systems are influenced by trust, confidentiality and organisational culture. When those factors improve, reporting often increases even if the underlying rate of incidents remains unchanged. [NASA] nasa.gov Aviation Safety Reporting System Overview Aviation Safety Reporting System Overview NASAMarch 3, 2026... Published: March 3, 2026 45:46 Inside the Data: What 1,000+ Pilot Reports Reveal About UAP iConnections Webinar with Ryan Graves Channel: iConnections · Views: 287 · Uploaded: April 2026 · Length: 45 minutes Open on YouTube Better reporting does not validate extraordinary claims Reducing stigma should not be confused with validating extraordinary interpretations of sightings. Most unidentified aerial observations are eventually attributed to familiar causes such as aircraft, balloons, satellites, atmospheric effects or incomplete sensor information. Official bodies including NASA and AARO continue to emphasise that \"unidentified\" simply means there is insufficient evidence for confident identification, not evidence of extraterrestrial technology. [AARO] aaro.mil AARO FAQ... Nevertheless, encouraging pilots to report unusual events has practical value regardless of their eventual explanation. Aviation professionals are trained observers operating in controlled environments with access to radar, cockpit instruments and communication records. Even when a case proves entirely conventional, the associated data can improve understanding of atmospheric phenomena, satellite visibility, sensor limitations or operational hazards. For researchers using UFO report databases and catalogues, the key lesson is straightforward: the absence of pilot reports should not automatically be interpreted as the absence of pilot sightings. Professional reluctance,"},{"title":"Confidential Channels","url":"/confidential-channels/","description":"Confidential reporting systems can produce sudden increases by releasing testimony that witnesses previously withheld, without any rise in sightings.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Reporting Bias","level":3,"text":"Confidential Channels Do Safer Reporting Channels Create More UFO Cases? Reporting Bias Confidential Channels Confidential Channels Do Safer Reporting Channels Create More UFO Cases? Confidential reporting systems can produce sudden increases by releasing testimony that witnesses previously withheld, without any rise in sightings. Confidential reporting systems can produce sudden increases by releasing testimony that witnesses previously withheld, without any rise in sightings. UFO Report Databases governance single policy policy intervention How stigma suppresses witness participation What changes when anonymity is protected Separating newly reported testimony from new events ufo report databases reporting bias confidential channels Introduction A rise in the number of UFO or unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) reports does not necessarily mean that more unusual events are occurring. One of the clearest non astronomical explanations for growing database totals is the creation of reporting channels that reduce the personal, professional or social cost of coming forward. When witnesses believe they can report confidentially, avoid ridicule or receive a fair hearing, people who previously remained silent may begin submitting accounts that describe events from both the present and the past. The result is a larger catalogue of cases without any corresponding increase in the number of objects actually observed. NASA has identified stigma as a major cause of data loss, while recent research on UAP witnesses suggests that only a minority ever report their experiences to an official body or specialist organisation. [narcap.org] narcap.org Platte, John Michael Guttierez, Ted Roe, Ryan Graves, 2023 — National Aviation Reporting Center on Anomalous PhenomenaJune 14, 2023... Published: June 14, 2023 How Stigma Suppresses Witness Participation Every reporting system reflects not only what people observe but also what they are willing to disclose. For decades, UFO reporting has carried a reputation risk that distinguishes it from reporting many other unusual events. Witnesses have described concerns about being labelled unreliable, attracting unwanted publicity or damaging professional credibility. These pressures can be especially important for people whose careers depend on perceptions of judgement and competence. Pilots, military personnel, scientists and law enforcement officers may have stronger incentives to stay silent than members of the general public, even if they regard an observation as genuine or operationally significant. The existence of this \"hidden population\" means public databases inevitably miss some proportion of sightings. Research supports this interpretation. A survey examining self reported UAP experiences found that only around 28% of respondents who claimed to have witnessed a phenomenon had reported it to any organisation, while only 14% had contacted a specialist UFO reporting organisation. Although the study was not designed to estimate national reporting rates, it demonstrates that many experiences never enter the catalogues commonly analysed by researchers. [AARO] aaro.mil AARO Home... NASA's Independent UAP Study Team reached a similar conclusion from an institutional perspective, stating that the negative perception surrounding UAP reporting almost certainly causes \"data attrition\" by discouraging witnesses from submitting reports in the first place. [The Guardian] theguardian.com This initiative aims to collect and analyze data, demystify sightings, and promote a science based perspective. The use of AI and machine... 51:24 Breaking Down UAP Footage with the Head of The Pentagon’s UAP Taskforce, Dr. Jon Kosloski Channel: StarTalk · Views: 1.5M · Uploaded: August 2025 · Length: 51 minutes Open on YouTube What Changes When Anonymity Is Protected Reducing stigma changes witness behaviour rather than the underlying phenomenon. Confidential or protected reporting systems remove barriers that previously filtered cases out before they reached a database. Several mechanisms can increase case numbers: Reduced reputational risk. Witnesses who feared ridicule become more willing to submit accounts. Professional protection. Pilots, government personnel and other professionals may report events they previously discussed only privately. Delayed disclosure. Individuals sometimes report historical experiences years after they occurred once they believe reporting has become acceptable. Improved institutional trust. Witnesses are more likely to contribute when they believe reports will be evaluated seriously rather than dismissed automatically. Importantly, these additional reports may describe events spanning many years. A sudden increase in database entries can therefore reflect the release of previously suppressed testimony rather than a sudden wave of new sightings. 24:37 What I Found Tracking 200,000 UFO Sightings Meet Christian Stepien of the UFO Reporting Center Channel: Profoundly Pointless · Views: 460 · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 24 minutes Open on YouTube Aviation Reporting Illustrates the Principle The aviation community provides one of the clearest examples of how confidentiality and reduced stigma affect reporting behaviour. The National Aviation Reporting Center on Anomalous Phenomena (NARCAP) explicitly modelled aspects of its reporting process on NASA's Aviation Safety Reporting System (ASRS), emphasising confidentiality to encourage pilots, air traffic controllers and airport personnel to submit incidents without fear of professional repercussions. NARCAP argues that longstanding cultural bias has discouraged reporting despite potential aviation safety implications. [narcap.org] narcap.org Platte, John Michael Guttierez, Ted Roe, Ryan Graves, 2023 — National Aviation Reporting Center on Anomalous PhenomenaJune 14, 2023... Published: June 14, 2023 Recent official reporting pathways show a similar awareness of these barriers. The U.S. Department of Defense's All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) provides mechanisms for eligible government personnel to submit protected information and highlights protections against reprisal for authorised reports. While operational reporting still follows established military and aviation procedures, the creation of formal channels reflects recognition that witnesses may be reluctant to come forward without institutional safeguards. [AARO] aaro.mil Submit A Report AARO Submit A Report... The lesson for database analysis is straightforward: improved reporting procedures may increase recorded cases even if the underlying rate of unusual observations remains unchanged. Separating Newly Reported Testimony from New Events Researchers analysing UFO databases must distinguish between when an event occurred and when it entered the catalogue . These dates are often very different. For example, a witness may observe an unusual object in 2018 but wait until 2025—after public attitudes shift or a trusted reporting channel becomes available—to submit an account. A database recording the report date would show an increase in 2025 despite no corresponding increase in sightings that year. This distinction is especially important after: introduction of confidential reporting systems; major government announcements encouraging reporting; highly publicised hearings or official investigations; media coverage that reduces perceived stigma. Without accounting for reporting delay, analysts may incorrectly interpret administrative changes as changes in the external phenomenon. Why Larger Databases Are Not Necessarily Evidence of More UFO Activity From the perspective of database interpretation, safer reporting channels generally improve completeness , not necessarily incidence . A larger catalogue may indicate that: previously silent witnesses are participating; historical cases are finally being documented; professional observers have greater confidence in reporting; organisations have become easier to find or more trusted. These are valuable developments because they reduce missing data and broaden the range of available testimony. However, they also complicate trend analysis. An increase in reports following stigma reduction cannot automatically be interpreted as evidence that more unidentified objects appeared in the sky. For anyone analysing UFO report databases and catalogues, changes in confidentiality, anonymity and witness protection are therefore best understood as changes in the reporting process itself. They affect how many observations become visible to researchers, making database growth an indicator of reporting behaviour as well as of whatever witnesses actually observed. 34:59 AI Found Hidden Patterns in 150,000 UFO Reports ft. Christian Stepien, National UFO Database CTO Channel: TechUnhinged · Views: 18 · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 34 minutes Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Do Safer Reporting Channels Create More"},{"title":"Online Reporting","url":"/online-reporting/","description":"Online forms lowered the effort needed to report a sighting, changing who entered the record and making long-term totals harder to compare.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Reporting Bias","level":3,"text":"Online Reporting How the Internet Changed Who Reports UFOs Reporting Bias Online Reporting Online Reporting How the Internet Changed Who Reports UFOs Online forms lowered the effort needed to report a sighting, changing who entered the record and making long term totals harder to compare. Online forms lowered the effort needed to report a sighting, changing who entered the record and making long term totals harder to compare. UFO Report Databases historical comparative single mechanism policy intervention From hotlines and letters to web forms New access, duplicates and participation gaps Why pre internet and online totals differ ufo report databases reporting bias online reporting Introduction The shift from paper forms, postal mail and telephone hotlines to internet reporting fundamentally changed what UFO databases measure. Online submission forms dramatically reduced the effort required to report a sighting, allowing many more people to contribute within minutes rather than days. As a result, modern catalogues contain far larger numbers of reports than their pre internet predecessors, but they also reflect a broader and more uneven reporting population. Changes in access, technology and reporting behaviour mean that increases in report totals cannot automatically be interpreted as increases in unusual aerial events. Instead, they often indicate that reporting itself became easier, faster and more visible. [mufon.com] mufon.com A Brief History of Mufon A Brief History of Mufon MUFON... From hotlines and letters to web forms Before widespread internet access, reporting a UFO usually required writing a letter, telephoning an organisation or contacting a local investigator. Each option imposed practical barriers. Witnesses needed to know which organisation existed, invest time in making contact and often wait for correspondence or a follow up interview. Many unusual observations therefore disappeared without ever entering a public catalogue. Government archives illustrate this earlier reporting environment. The UK's Ministry of Defence received reports primarily through letters, telephone calls and referrals from other agencies, while archived case files include handwritten correspondence alongside structured report forms that were completed by officials after receiving witness accounts. These processes naturally favoured people who were highly motivated or already familiar with reporting channels. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFO reports National ArchivesUFO reports The National Archives... The spread of the web in the late 1990s and early 2000s removed many of these barriers. Instead of locating an investigator or mailing paperwork, witnesses could complete an online questionnaire immediately after an event. Organisations such as the National UFO Reporting Center ( NUFORC ) and the Mutual UFO Network ( MUFON ) increasingly centred their collection systems on web based submission forms, allowing reports to be received continuously from across the United States and internationally. MUFON's Case Management System automated routing, investigator assignment and database storage in ways that were impossible with paper based systems. [mufon.com] mufon.com A Brief History of Mufon A Brief History of Mufon MUFON... 1:59:47 UFOs have gone Mainstream! Eyewitness Reports Peter Davenport Channel: Cuyamungue Institute · Views: 1.1K · Uploaded: October 2023 · Length: 1 hour 59 minutes Open on YouTube New access, duplicates and participation gaps Lower reporting costs changed more than the number of submissions—they changed who appeared in the record. Online forms made participation easier for occasional witnesses who might never previously have written a letter or searched for a telephone hotline. Reporting also became much faster. Someone could submit an account while an event was still unfolding, often attaching photographs or video captured on a mobile phone. This increased the diversity of reports and reduced delays between observation and submission. [MUFON] mufon.com A Brief History of Mufon A Brief History of Mufon MUFON... The same accessibility also introduced new complications for researchers. A single witness can now submit the same event to multiple organisations within minutes. Large public events may generate dozens or hundreds of separate reports from independent observers, while the same individual may file identical accounts with several databases. Unless catalogues actively cross reference submissions, raw report totals may therefore contain substantial duplication. Different organisations also ask different questions, apply different review procedures and retain different levels of detail, making direct comparison difficult. [narcap.org] narcap.org Platte, John Michael Guttierez, Ted Roe, Ryan Graves, 2023 — National Aviation Reporting Center on Anomalous PhenomenaJune 14, 2023... Published: June 14, 2023 Online reporting also broadened international participation without eliminating participation gaps. Internet access, digital literacy, language, awareness of reporting organisations and trust in those organisations all influence who submits reports. Areas with better connectivity or stronger UFO communities may therefore appear to experience more sightings simply because residents are more likely to know about, and use, online reporting systems. This means geographic patterns partly reflect reporting opportunities rather than only differences in what people observed. [narcap.org] narcap.org Platte, John Michael Guttierez, Ted Roe, Ryan Graves, 2023 — National Aviation Reporting Center on Anomalous PhenomenaJune 14, 2023... Published: June 14, 2023 Another consequence is that online systems lowered the threshold for what people considered worth reporting. Witnesses can now submit uncertain observations with little effort, whereas earlier reporting systems required a greater investment of time. This likely increased the proportion of brief, ambiguous or easily explained reports entering public databases alongside genuinely unusual cases. Organisations such as NUFORC explicitly note that their databases contain a mixture of conventional explanations, hoaxes and unexplained reports, rather than a uniformly vetted collection of anomalous events. [narcap.org] narcap.org Platte, John Michael Guttierez, Ted Roe, Ryan Graves, 2023 — National Aviation Reporting Center on Anomalous PhenomenaJune 14, 2023... Published: June 14, 2023 Why pre internet and online totals differ The transition to internet reporting created a break in the historical record that complicates long term statistical comparisons. An increase in reports after widespread internet adoption does not necessarily indicate that more unusual aerial phenomena were being witnessed. It may instead reflect: a much lower effort required to submit a report; wider public awareness of reporting organisations through search engines and social media; immediate online availability of reporting forms; easier submission of photographs and videos; increased reporting of the same event by multiple independent witnesses ; and greater participation from people who would never previously have contacted a UFO organisation. [mufon.com] mufon.com A Brief History of Mufon A Brief History of Mufon MUFON... For this reason, researchers analysing long term UFO databases generally treat reporting systems themselves as part of the phenomenon being studied. A spike in reports may reflect changes in reporting technology, public awareness or database management just as much as changes in the underlying frequency of unusual observations. The internet therefore reshaped UFO catalogues in two important ways. It expanded public participation and preserved many observations that would previously have gone unrecorded, but it also introduced new forms of reporting bias , duplication and inconsistency. Modern databases are richer and more accessible than earlier collections, yet comparisons across the paper and internet eras require careful adjustment because the process of becoming a recorded case changed as much as the number of cases themselves. [mufon.com] mufon.com A Brief History of Mufon A Brief History of Mufon MUFON... 24:37 What I Found Tracking 200,000 UFO Sightings Meet Christian Stepien of the UFO Reporting Center Channel: Profoundly Pointless · Views: 460 · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 24 minutes Open on YouTube 16:34 Pentagon releases classified UFO files Channel: KPTV FOX 12 Local news, weather Portland, Oregon · Views: 16.9K · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 16 minutes Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to How the Internet Changed Who Reports UFOs. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The UFO Handbook: A Guide to Investigating, Evaluating, and R... By Allan Hendry An explanation of the usual reasons for confusing IFOs (identified flying objects) with UFOs and a survey"},{"title":"Memory Changes","url":"/memory-changes/","description":"News coverage, familiar UFO imagery and repeated retelling can become mixed with an older observation before a witness finally submits it.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Reporting Bias","level":3,"text":"Memory Changes How UFO Memories Change Before They Are Reported Reporting Bias Memory Changes Memory Changes How UFO Memories Change Before They Are Reported News coverage, familiar UFO imagery and repeated retelling can become mixed with an older observation before a witness finally submits it. News coverage, familiar UFO imagery and repeated retelling can become mixed with an older observation before a witness finally submits it. UFO Report Databases mechanism single mechanism mechanism Why recollection is reconstructed over time How media language enters witness accounts What delayed memories can and cannot establish ufo report databases reporting bias memory changes Introduction A UFO report is not a frozen snapshot of what a witness saw. It is a description reconstructed from memory, often days, months or even years after the event. During that interval, news reports, documentaries, social media discussions, familiar UFO imagery and conversations with other people can become woven into the original recollection. This does not necessarily mean a witness is dishonest. Decades of psychological research show that human memory is reconstructive rather than replayed like a recording, making sincere people vulnerable to incorporating later information into what they genuinely believe they experienced. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological ScienceCurrent Issues and Advances in Misinformation Research Current Directions in Psychological Science... For anyone using UFO report databases and catalogues, this distinction is important. A delayed report may faithfully describe what the witness now remembers, while differing in subtle or substantial ways from what was originally observed. Understanding how memory changes helps explain why later accounts often become richer, more coherent or more consistent with popular UFO narratives without requiring deliberate fabrication. Why Recollection Is Reconstructed Over Time Psychologists no longer view memory as a permanent recording that is simply retrieved on demand. Instead, recalling an event involves rebuilding it from stored fragments, expectations, prior knowledge and later experiences. Each act of remembering can also modify the memory before it is stored again, making later recollections slightly different from earlier ones. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological ScienceCurrent Issues and Advances in Misinformation Research Current Directions in Psychological Science... Several factors make this especially relevant for UFO sightings: Most sightings are brief, unexpected and occur under poor viewing conditions. Witnesses rarely expect to memorise precise details at the time. Reports are often submitted long after the event. The event usually lacks independent physical evidence that could refresh memory objectively. Research on eyewitness testimony consistently finds that memories become more vulnerable to post event information as time passes. As the original memory trace weakens, people become less able to distinguish what they actually observed from what they later heard, read or imagined. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The impact of recall timing on the preservation of eyewitness memory PMCJuly 19, 2021... Published: July 19, 2021 This does not mean that every delayed UFO report is inaccurate. Instead, confidence and vividness are not reliable indicators that every reported detail originated in the original observation. Your Brain Has Been Faking Your Memories This Whole Time Channel: MindGlitch Open on YouTube How Media Language Enters Witness Accounts One of the best established findings in cognitive psychology is the misinformation effect . This occurs when information encountered after an event alters later recollection of that event. Laboratory studies have repeatedly shown that witnesses can confidently remember details introduced through later questioning, narratives or discussion rather than direct observation. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Misinformation Effect Misinformation Effect an overview ScienceDirect Topics... In the context of UFO reports, post event information may come from many sources: Television documentaries describing classic flying saucer shapes. News coverage using terms such as \"craft\", \"orb\" or \"triangle\". Internet forums comparing similar sightings. Friends suggesting possible explanations. Books or podcasts discussing famous historical cases. A witness who initially remembered \"a bright light\" may later describe \"a metallic disc with a glowing rim\" after repeated exposure to familiar UFO imagery. Importantly, this change need not be conscious. The person may sincerely believe that the more detailed description reflects what they originally saw because the later information has become integrated into the remembered event. Research also shows that repeated exposure increases the likelihood that misleading information becomes incorporated into memory, particularly when the new information does not strongly conflict with the original experience. [Cambridge University Press] cambridge.org Cambridge University PressRE EVALUATING THE CREDIBILITY OF EYEWITNESS TESTIMONY: THE MISINFORMATION EFFECT AND THE OVERCRITICAL JUROR E... How Reliable Is Your Memory? Dr. Elizabeth (Beth) Loftus TED Talk on False Memories Channel: UCI CNLM Open on YouTube Why Repeated Retelling Changes a Story Every retelling requires reconstruction. Rather than retrieving a perfect copy of the original experience, people rebuild the event into a coherent narrative. Over multiple retellings: uncertain details may become increasingly definite; gaps may be filled using assumptions; dramatic elements may receive greater emphasis because listeners respond to them; awkward inconsistencies may disappear as the story becomes more internally consistent. Psychologists refer to related errors as source monitoring problems, where people remember information but gradually lose track of where it came from. A witness may accurately remember reading about black triangular aircraft in a magazine while mistakenly believing that shape formed part of the original sighting. These processes occur in many forms of eyewitness testimony and are not unique to UFO reports. They reflect normal properties of human memory rather than evidence of deception. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological ScienceCurrent Issues and Advances in Misinformation Research Current Directions in Psychological Science... Delayed Reporting Creates Additional Opportunities for Memory Change Many UFO catalogues contain reports submitted months or years after the alleged observation. Long delays create more opportunities for later information to influence recollection. During that period, witnesses may encounter: anniversaries of famous UFO incidents; television series featuring similar imagery; newly released military videos or government statements; online discussions encouraging reinterpretation of earlier experiences; conversations with family members who remember the event differently. Experimental research on eyewitness memory has found that longer delays increase exposure to misleading information and generally increase susceptibility to memory distortion. This is one reason why legal investigators prefer interviewing witnesses as early as practical after an event. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The impact of recall timing on the preservation of eyewitness memory PMCJuly 19, 2021... Published: July 19, 2021 For UFO databases, the delay between observation and reporting therefore becomes an important piece of metadata rather than merely an administrative detail. How ACCURATE is Eyewitness Testimony? Leading Questions & Post Event Discussion Channel: Bear it in MIND Open on YouTube What Delayed Memories Can and Cannot Establish Delayed witness accounts remain valuable because they document what people remember experiencing and can reveal recurring themes, reporting behaviour and cultural influences. They may also preserve observations that would otherwise have been lost entirely. However, the timing of the report affects how confidently specific details should be interpreted. A delayed report can reasonably establish: that the witness recalls an unusual experience; approximately when and where it occurred, if independently supported; how the experience is presently remembered; how witnesses describe unusual aerial events within a particular cultural period. By contrast, delayed memories alone cannot reliably establish: the exact appearance of an object years earlier; precise sequences of rapidly unfolding events; whether distinctive details originated during the observation or were acquired afterwards; that later added descriptions accurately reflect the original sighting. This"},{"title":"Per Capita Rates","url":"/per-capita-rates/","description":"Dividing sightings by population adjusts for observer numbers but cannot correct unequal sky access, tourism, internet use or willingness to report.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Reporting Bias","level":3,"text":"Per Capita Rates Why Per Capita UFO Maps Can Still Mislead Reporting Bias Per Capita Rates Per Capita Rates Why Per Capita UFO Maps Can Still Mislead Dividing sightings by population adjusts for observer numbers but cannot correct unequal sky access, tourism, internet use or willingness to report. Dividing sightings by population adjusts for observer numbers but cannot correct unequal sky access, tourism, internet use or willingness to report. UFO Report Databases evidence single dataset dataset evidence What population adjustment actually fixes Outdoor time, tourism and unequal sky access Reading report rates as participation measures ufo report databases reporting bias per capita rates Introduction Population adjusted UFO report maps are often presented as an improvement over raw sighting totals because they divide reports by the number of people living in each area. This corrects one obvious distortion: densely populated regions naturally generate more potential witnesses than sparsely populated ones. However, per capita rates remain a measure of reported sightings, not of the true frequency of unusual aerial events. They still depend on who spends time looking at the sky, who notices something unusual, who knows where to report it, and who is willing to submit a report. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)An environmental analysis of public UAP sightings and sky view potential PMCDecember 14, 2023... Published: December 14, 2023 For that reason, a county or country with a high per capita reporting rate should not automatically be interpreted as a place where unexplained phenomena occur more frequently. Instead, it may simply be a place where conditions favour observation and reporting. Population adjustment removes only one source of bias while leaving many others untouched. What Population Adjustment Actually Fixes Dividing report counts by population addresses a genuine statistical problem. A city of one million residents will almost always produce more eyewitness reports than a town of 10,000 simply because there are more people available to observe the sky. Reporting rates expressed as \"reports per 100,000 residents\" therefore make geographic comparisons fairer than raw totals. Yet this adjustment assumes that every resident has roughly the same opportunity and willingness to become a reporter. In practice, that assumption rarely holds. Researchers analysing nearly 100,000 reports from the National UFO Reporting Center ( NUFORC ) found that reporting patterns are influenced by numerous environmental and social variables beyond population alone. Their work examined factors including artificial light, cloud cover, tree canopy, internet availability and opportunities to view the sky, illustrating that report density reflects much more than the number of residents. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)An environmental analysis of public UAP sightings and sky view potential PMCDecember 14, 2023... Published: December 14, 2023 As a result, a per capita map answers only a narrow question: how many reports were submitted relative to the resident population. It does not answer how often unusual aerial events actually occurred. 51:24 Breaking Down UAP Footage with the Head of The Pentagon’s UAP Taskforce, Dr. Jon Kosloski Channel: StarTalk · Views: 1.5M · Uploaded: August 2025 · Length: 51 minutes Open on YouTube Outdoor Time, Tourism and Unequal Sky Access One of the largest remaining biases is unequal exposure to the sky. People differ greatly in how often they are outside after dark, whether they live beneath bright urban lighting, and whether surrounding terrain provides unobstructed views. Someone working night shifts in rural countryside has many more opportunities to notice unusual lights than someone spending evenings indoors in a brightly illuminated city centre. Tourism creates another distortion. National parks, mountain regions, deserts and coastal destinations may host millions of visitors each year despite having relatively small permanent populations. If reports are divided only by resident population, locations with heavy visitor traffic can appear to experience unusually high rates simply because many observers are temporarily present. The opposite effect can occur in dense metropolitan areas. Large populations may generate relatively low per capita reporting rates because tall buildings, heavy light pollution and limited views of the horizon reduce opportunities to notice celestial or atmospheric events. Population adjustment does not account for these differences in observational opportunity. The environmental analysis of NUFORC data found measurable relationships between reporting frequency and variables affecting sky visibility, including cloud cover, artificial sky brightness and forest cover. Such findings indicate that the geography of reporting partly reflects where people can easily observe the sky rather than where unusual phenomena necessarily occur. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)An environmental analysis of public UAP sightings and sky view potential PMCDecember 14, 2023... Published: December 14, 2023 Internet Access and Reporting Infrastructure Still Matter Submitting a UFO report requires more than seeing something unusual. Witnesses must know that a reporting system exists, have internet access or another reporting route, and consider filing worthwhile. The same NUFORC analysis notes that internet availability probably influenced reporting growth after 2000 and that poorer internet access in some rural areas likely introduced reporting bias , particularly during the early years of widespread online reporting . A lower per capita reporting rate in such areas may therefore reflect communication infrastructure rather than fewer observations. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)An environmental analysis of public UAP sightings and sky view potential PMCDecember 14, 2023... Published: December 14, 2023 Reporting organisations themselves also have unequal visibility. An English language database based in one country is naturally more familiar to some populations than others. Consequently, international comparisons based on a single catalogue often mix genuine geographic variation with differences in language, publicity and awareness. Even within the same country, communities differ in familiarity with specialist reporting organisations. Population adjustment cannot compensate for unequal knowledge of where reports should be submitted. 24:37 What I Found Tracking 200,000 UFO Sightings Meet Christian Stepien of the UFO Reporting Center Channel: Profoundly Pointless · Views: 460 · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 24 minutes Open on YouTube Participation Is Not Evenly Distributed Per capita calculations also assume that each resident is equally likely to file a report after witnessing something unusual. Evidence suggests this is not the case. NASA's Independent UAP Study concluded that stigma surrounding unidentified anomalous phenomena discourages reporting and almost certainly causes data loss. The report argues that concerns about ridicule, credibility and professional consequences reduce participation, meaning reported cases represent only a subset of potential observations. [NASA Science] smd cms.nasa.gov Science NASA UNIDENTIFIED ANOMALOUS PHENOMENA Independent... This reluctance is unlikely to be geographically uniform. Communities differ in attitudes towards unusual experiences, trust in institutions and willingness to engage with public reporting systems. Areas with similar numbers of witnesses may therefore generate very different numbers of reports. Population adjustment cannot correct for these differences because they concern reporting behaviour rather than population size. Reading Report Rates as Measures of Participation Per capita UFO maps remain useful when interpreted correctly. They can identify places where reporting activity is unusually high relative to population, helping researchers investigate why those locations produce more submissions. However, they should be understood primarily as indicators of participation in a reporting system rather than direct measurements of unexplained aerial events. High per capita reporting rates may reflect combinations of: Better opportunities to observe the sky. Large transient populations such as tourists. Greater awareness of reporting organisations. Easier internet access and online participation. Lower social stigma surrounding reporting. Stronger local interest in unusual aerial phenomena. Conversely, low rates may indicate barriers to reporting rather than an absence of observations. For researchers using UFO catalogues, the key lesson is that population adjustment improves comparisons but does not eliminate selection bias. Per capita maps are more informative than raw counts, yet they still describe the geography of reported"},{"title":"Choose a Database","url":"/choose-a-database/","description":"The best source depends on whether the goal is to study witness behaviour, investigated cases, official decisions or source trails.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Database Comparison","level":3,"text":"Choose a Database Which UFO Database Fits Your Question? Database Comparison Choose a Database Choose a Database Which UFO Database Fits Your Question? The best source depends on whether the goal is to study witness behaviour, investigated cases, official decisions or source trails. The best source depends on whether the goal is to study witness behaviour, investigated cases, official decisions or source trails. UFO Report Databases implementation bounded topic population context Match the question to the intake system Compare access, investigation and coverage Build a defensible cross database sample ufo report databases database comparison choose a database Introduction Choosing the right UFO database begins with a simple question: what exactly are you trying to study? No single catalogue is the definitive source for every research problem. Some databases primarily collect witness submissions, others publish investigated cases, while still others preserve historical government records or bibliographic references. Using the wrong source can produce misleading conclusions because each database represents a different population, follows different collection rules and records different stages of the reporting process. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 A defensible research design therefore starts by matching the research question to the database's purpose. A study of witness behaviour should not rely on an archive built around official investigations, and a historical analysis of government policy cannot be answered from a modern public reporting system alone. Match the Question to the Intake System The first decision is not which database is largest, but what each database considers a record . If the research question concerns how members of the public describe unusual aerial events , a public reporting system such as the National UFO Reporting Center ( NUFORC ) is generally the best fit. Its reporting form captures witness narratives, dates, locations, object descriptions and other observational details, allowing researchers to examine reporting language, perceived object characteristics, seasonal patterns and reporting behaviour. However, NUFORC explicitly states that reports are public submissions rather than verified events, and not every submission proceeds to investigation. [NUFORC] nuforc.org report a ufo File a UFO Report NUFORCSeptember 13, 2021... Published: September 13, 2021 If the question instead asks how investigators reached conclusions , databases built around investigations are more appropriate. Organisations such as MUFON and France's GEIPAN document investigative processes rather than simply collecting reports. Their value lies in follow up work, evidence review and case assessment rather than the raw number of sightings. If the goal is to understand official government responses , historical collections such as Project Blue Book files and national archives are more suitable because they preserve administrative records, correspondence, investigations and policy decisions rather than contemporary public reporting. Finally, if the objective is to trace where information about a case originated , a bibliographic catalogue such as UFOCAT is often the strongest choice. UFOCAT was designed primarily as a reference database linking published and unpublished sources relating to individual cases rather than providing a single authoritative list of unique incidents. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... 5:43 Project Blue Book: UFO Secrets Hidden Inside Hangar 18 (Season 2) History Channel: HISTORY · Views: 141.0K · Uploaded: March 2020 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube Compare Access, Investigation and Coverage Even databases that appear similar differ substantially in what they provide to researchers. Research needMost suitable type of databaseKey limitationWitness descriptions and reporting trendsPublic reporting databases (for example, NUFORC)Self selected submissions rather than representative samplesInvestigative outcomesInvestigated case collections (for example, GEIPAN or MUFON)Investigation depth varies between casesGovernment policy and historical practiceOfficial archives and Project Blue Book recordsRestricted to specific countries and time periodsSource tracing and literature reviewUFOCAT and archival cataloguesMultiple entries may refer to the same underlying incident Coverage also differs in three important ways. Geographical scope. Some collections focus primarily on one country, while others accept international submissions. A global research question may therefore require combining multiple repositories rather than relying on one database. Time period. Project Blue Book covers only the years during which the programme operated, whereas NUFORC continues to accept reports today. Historical and contemporary datasets therefore answer different questions. Level of processing. A newly submitted witness account, a fully investigated case and an archived government memorandum represent three different stages in the lifecycle of a report. Treating them as equivalent observations introduces avoidable bias. Build a Defensible Cross Database Sample Many research questions cannot be answered by a single database. Cross database sampling can improve robustness, but only if the datasets are combined carefully. A practical approach is to: 1. Define the unit of analysis before collecting data. Decide whether the study concerns reports, incidents, investigations or documentary sources. 2. Apply consistent filters such as date range, country and event type across every database. 3. Identify duplicate incidents that appear in multiple collections. 4. Record which database contributed each observation so differences in collection methods remain visible during analysis. 5. Compare results across databases instead of automatically merging all records into one dataset. This approach reduces the risk of confusing differences in collection practice with differences in the underlying phenomenon. 1:11 'Project Blue Book' Ep. 1 Official Clip UFO SHOWTIME Documentary Series Channel: SHOWTIME · Views: 290.8K · Uploaded: August 2021 · Length: 1 minute 11 seconds Open on YouTube Avoid Common Selection Mistakes Several recurring errors can weaken UFO database research. Assuming larger databases are inherently better. A higher number of records often reflects broader reporting opportunities rather than greater evidential quality. Comparing unlike populations. Witness submissions, investigated files and archival documents measure different things. Ignoring duplicate cases. Major incidents frequently appear in multiple catalogues and publications. Treating classifications as interchangeable. One organisation's investigation outcome may not correspond to another's terminology or standards. Overlooking access restrictions. Some databases provide open public access, while others limit full investigative files or attachments to members or researchers with specific permissions. UFOCAT's own documentation cautions that its catalogue contains multiple references to many incidents because it was designed as a comprehensive source guide rather than a de duplicated case list. It recommends using the database as a path to original documentation instead of relying on simple record counts. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... Choosing the Best Database for Typical Research Questions The strongest choice depends on the question being asked rather than on the reputation of any individual catalogue. How do witnesses describe unusual aerial objects? Public reporting databases such as NUFORC are generally the most appropriate because they preserve original witness narratives. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 How are cases investigated and classified? Investigative databases such as GEIPAN or MUFON provide greater insight into evidence assessment and official or organisational conclusions. How did governments respond during a particular historical period? Project Blue Book records and national archives provide the most relevant primary documentation. Where did information about a famous case originate? UFOCAT is particularly valuable because it links multiple published and unpublished sources relating to the same incident, allowing researchers to reconstruct the documentary trail. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... The most reliable studies therefore begin by selecting databases that match the research question, recognising that different repositories document different parts of the reporting and investigation process rather than"},{"title":"Infrared Effects","url":"/infrared-effects/","description":"Glare, tracking changes and shifting heat contrast can make ordinary objects seem to accelerate, vanish or change shape.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Unidentified","level":3,"text":"Infrared Effects When Infrared Cameras Make UFOs Look Impossible Unidentified Infrared Effects Infrared Effects When Infrared Cameras Make UFOs Look Impossible Glare, tracking changes and shifting heat contrast can make ordinary objects seem to accelerate, vanish or change shape. Glare, tracking changes and shifting heat contrast can make ordinary objects seem to accelerate, vanish or change shape. UFO Report Databases implementation single mechanism mechanism How glare and heat contrast alter apparent shape Why tracking systems can create misleading motion Which sensor metadata investigators need ufo report databases unidentified infrared effects Introduction Infrared (IR) cameras have become central to many modern UFO and unidentified anomalous phenomenon (UAP) investigations because they can detect heat and thermal contrast that are invisible to the human eye. However, infrared footage does not show the world in the same way as an ordinary video camera. It records differences in infrared radiation, applies image processing, and often relies on automated tracking systems that can produce unfamiliar visual effects. As a result, ordinary aircraft, balloons, birds or distant objects can appear to accelerate, rotate, change shape or disappear in ways that seem extraordinary until the sensor's behaviour is understood. Official UAP investigators increasingly evaluate not only what appears in an infrared image, but also how the camera, tracking software and environmental conditions may have influenced that appearance. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... Within UFO report databases , this distinction is important because an infrared recording may remain \"unidentified\" even when investigators conclude that the apparent extreme behaviour is more likely to arise from imaging effects than from extraordinary flight performance. Understanding these mechanisms helps explain why experienced investigators request complete sensor data rather than relying on short video clips alone. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... How glare and heat contrast alter apparent shape Thermal cameras display temperature differences rather than visible colours or surface details. A distant aircraft that appears as a familiar silhouette in daylight may become nothing more than a bright heat source surrounded by cooler air. Fine structural features such as wings or fuselage outlines may disappear completely if the sensor lacks sufficient resolution at long range. Several mechanisms can produce misleading appearances: Infrared blooming: Extremely bright thermal sources can saturate detector pixels, causing the apparent object to appear larger or less sharply defined than its true physical size. Heat contrast inversion: Depending on display settings, hotter objects may appear either bright or dark, making the same object look substantially different between recordings. Atmospheric distortion: Water vapour, haze and varying air temperatures can blur or distort thermal signatures, particularly over long distances. Automatic image enhancement: Many military and commercial infrared systems continuously adjust contrast to maximise visibility, sometimes exaggerating subtle thermal features. These effects can create the impression that an object is changing shape during flight when the underlying object remains unchanged. Investigators therefore avoid treating apparent outline changes in infrared footage as evidence of physical transformation without supporting sensor data. [arXiv] arxiv.org Thermal Infrared Image Colorization for Nighttime Driving Scenes with Top Down Guided AttentionApril 29, 2021... Published: April 29, 2021 A related limitation is that thermal cameras generally provide much less spatial detail than daylight cameras at comparable ranges. When only a few pixels represent the target, small changes in viewing angle or contrast may dramatically alter its apparent form. Why tracking systems can create misleading motion Many well known military infrared recordings come from stabilised targeting pods rather than handheld cameras. These systems automatically keep a selected target near the centre of the display while simultaneously compensating for aircraft motion, changing zoom levels and switching tracking modes. This creates several opportunities for misleading visual impressions. Apparent acceleration If both the observing aircraft and the target are moving, the relative geometry can create strong parallax effects. A distant object may appear to race across the landscape even when travelling at an ordinary speed. Conversely, a nearby fast moving object may appear comparatively slow. AARO's published analyses include examples where geospatial reconstruction concluded that apparent high speed motion was largely explained by viewing geometry rather than exceptional object performance. In its assessment of the well known \"Go Fast\" video, AARO states that perspective effects significantly altered the apparent speed of the object. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... 18:21 Full Uncut Tracking Video of the Infamous 'Jellyfish' UAP over Iraq Channel: Media Magik Entertainment · Views: 76.1K · Uploaded: April 2025 · Length: 18 minutes Open on YouTube Apparent rotation One of the most discussed infrared recordings is the Navy \"Gimbal\" video, in which the thermal image appears to rotate while remaining tracked. Competing explanations remain part of the public debate. Some analysts argue that the apparent rotation is consistent with glare or optical effects interacting with the targeting pod's rotating optical system, while others dispute that interpretation and contend that the available evidence does not fully explain the observation. Importantly, AARO currently classifies the publicly released Gimbal footage as unresolved rather than attributing it to a confirmed extraordinary object or a confirmed sensor artefact. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... This illustrates an important investigative principle: recognising that a sensor effect is plausible is not identical to proving that it occurred in a specific case. Sudden disappearance Infrared targets may appear to vanish because: automatic tracking loses lock; the target falls below the sensor's contrast threshold; the viewing angle changes; background temperatures become similar to the target; the target exits the narrow field of view during a zoom or tracking adjustment. A disappearance in infrared footage therefore does not automatically imply instantaneous acceleration or exotic propulsion. Why heat does not equal propulsion A common misunderstanding is that a bright infrared signature necessarily indicates an unusually powerful energy source. In practice, infrared brightness depends on many factors besides propulsion, including: engine exhaust temperature; reflected solar heating; viewing angle; atmospheric absorption; detector sensitivity; image processing settings. Likewise, some ordinary objects may appear unexpectedly dim because they are viewed from an angle where little engine heat is visible or because environmental temperatures reduce thermal contrast. Investigators therefore avoid estimating propulsion capability from apparent infrared brightness alone. 13:13 Caught On Camera The US Navy UFO Channel: Found And Explained · Views: 4.7M · Uploaded: December 2021 · Length: 13 minutes Open on YouTube Which sensor metadata investigators need Short video clips rarely contain enough information to determine whether unusual behaviour reflects the object or the imaging system. Professional investigations place substantial weight on metadata accompanying the recording. The most valuable information includes: precise sensor type and model; infrared wavelength band; zoom level and field of view; tracking mode and lock status; aircraft position, altitude and heading; target bearing and estimated range; camera gimbal angles; timestamps synchronised with radar or other sensors; weather and atmospheric conditions. Without these data, reconstructing true object motion becomes far more uncertain. AARO's published case analyses frequently combine infrared imagery with radar information, flight records, commercial aircraft databases or geometric reconstruction before reaching conclusions about apparent performance. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... Lessons from recent official case reviews Recent official releases illustrate that infrared imagery alone is rarely sufficient to establish extraordinary behaviour. In one published Puerto Rico case, AARO concluded that an apparent object splitting into two and entering the sea resulted instead from viewing geometry and reconstruction of the sensor's line of sight, finding two separate objects travelling with the wind rather than a single craft exhibiting unusual manoeuvres. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... Similarly, AARO has published several infrared cases initially reported as unidentified that it"},{"title":"Attention Surges","url":"/attention-surges/","description":"News coverage, online interest and wider social conditions can increase UFO submissions without any matching rise in aerial phenomena.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / NUFORC","level":3,"text":"Attention Surges Can UFO News Create Its Own Reporting Wave? NUFORC Attention Surges Attention Surges Can UFO News Create Its Own Reporting Wave? News coverage, online interest and wider social conditions can increase UFO submissions without any matching rise in aerial phenomena. News coverage, online interest and wider social conditions can increase UFO submissions without any matching rise in aerial phenomena. UFO Report Databases evidence single mechanism mechanism Media exposure and delayed submissions Online attention and social conditions Separating publicity effects from event patterns ufo report databases nuforc attention surges Introduction News coverage can increase the number of UFO reports without any corresponding increase in unusual aerial phenomena. This is one of the most important limitations when interpreting large public databases such as the National UFO Reporting Center ( NUFORC ). Rather than acting as a simple count of unexplained objects in the sky, NUFORC records when people decide to interpret an experience as noteworthy and submit a report. That decision is strongly influenced by public attention, media narratives, internet search activity and wider social conditions. Researchers therefore treat attention itself as an observable variable that helps explain reporting surges, rather than assuming every spike reflects a genuine increase in unidentified objects. [Nature] nature.com An environmental analysis of public UAP sightings and sky view potential Scientific ReportsDecember 14, 2023... Published: December 14, 2023 Can news coverage create a reporting wave? The evidence suggests that it can. Publicity changes what people notice, how they interpret ambiguous sights, and whether they think a reporting organisation is worth contacting. A major news story, documentary, government announcement or viral social media discussion can encourage both immediate reports and submissions describing events that occurred weeks, months or even years earlier. This creates a well known feedback mechanism: 1. A widely reported UFO related event attracts public attention. 2. More people begin watching the sky or reconsider earlier experiences. 3. Ambiguous lights, satellites, aircraft or astronomical objects are more likely to be interpreted as unusual. 4. Witnesses become more aware of reporting systems such as NUFORC. 5. Submission numbers rise, even if the underlying rate of aerial phenomena has not changed. Because NUFORC records voluntary submissions rather than systematically sampled observations, these behavioural changes directly affect the database. [Nature] nature.com An environmental analysis of public UAP sightings and sky view potential Scientific ReportsDecember 14, 2023... Published: December 14, 2023 Historical reporting waves The pattern is visible throughout the history of modern UFO reporting. Kenneth Arnold's widely publicised 1947 sighting rapidly introduced the phrase \"flying saucer\" into popular culture and was followed by a dramatic increase in similar reports across the United States. Historians and social scientists have long debated whether this represented a sudden increase in unusual observations or a shift in public interpretation driven by extensive media attention. [WIRED] wired.com How UFO Sightings Became an American Obsession The period following his sighting saw a surge in UFO sightings across the U.S. This surge, or \"flap,\" is linked to societal fears of inva... Later periods show similar dynamics following highly publicised television programmes, books, government disclosures and, more recently, coverage of U.S. military videos and congressional hearings. These episodes repeatedly coincide with renewed public interest and increased reporting activity, although the existence of more reports alone does not establish that more unexplained phenomena occurred. [Nature] nature.com Exploring expert figures in alien related UFO conspiracy theories Humanities and Social Sciences CommunicationsApril 15, 2025... Published: April 15, 2025 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube Media exposure and delayed submissions One feature of NUFORC that complicates time series analysis is that many reports are submitted well after the event itself. Publicity often prompts people to report experiences they had previously dismissed or never mentioned. For researchers, this distinction is critical: Event date estimates when the witness says the observation occurred. Submission date measures when the witness became motivated to report it. A major news story can therefore produce an apparent reporting surge even though many of the underlying observations happened months or years earlier. If analysts examine posting dates alone, publicity driven backlogs can resemble sudden increases in sightings. This is why studies using NUFORC data typically distinguish between occurrence dates and reporting dates wherever possible. [Nature] nature.com An environmental analysis of public UAP sightings and sky view potential Scientific ReportsDecember 14, 2023... Published: December 14, 2023 Online attention and changing social conditions Modern reporting behaviour is influenced not only by traditional news but also by online search activity, social media and wider cultural interest. Researchers increasingly use measures such as Google Trends because internet searches provide an independent indicator of public attention. Rising search volumes for terms such as \"UFO\" or \"UAP\" frequently accompany major media events, allowing investigators to compare changing interest with changes in reporting behaviour rather than assuming reports alone measure unusual aerial activity. [Nature] nature.com Exploring expert figures in alien related UFO conspiracy theories Humanities and Social Sciences CommunicationsApril 15, 2025... Published: April 15, 2025 Social conditions also affect reporting independently of any change in the sky itself. Factors include: greater public awareness of reporting organisations; increased internet access and easier online submission; widespread discussion on social media; documentaries, streaming series and podcasts; government announcements that reduce perceived stigma around reporting; periods when people spend more time outdoors or looking at the night sky. The 2023 national scale analysis of NUFORC data explicitly notes that reporting databases represent volunteered geographic information. Like other crowdsourced datasets, they reflect both environmental opportunity and human reporting behaviour, making social influences part of the data generating process rather than external noise. [Nature] nature.com An environmental analysis of public UAP sightings and sky view potential Scientific ReportsDecember 14, 2023... Published: December 14, 2023 5:21 Washington man spent last 25 years running National UFO Reporting Center Channel: KREM 2 News · Views: 6.3K · Uploaded: February 2019 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube Separating publicity effects from genuine event patterns Researchers do not simply discard reporting spikes because publicity may be involved. Instead, they attempt to distinguish attention effects from possible changes in observation patterns. Common approaches include comparing: reported event dates with submission dates; regional differences rather than national totals; multiple independent databases instead of a single catalogue; known media events against reporting timelines; environmental variables such as cloud cover, light pollution and viewing opportunity. For example, the large scale environmental study of NUFORC reports found that reporting frequency is associated with factors that increase opportunities to observe the sky, including lower cloud cover and reduced tree canopy, alongside human variables such as population and aircraft activity. The authors also note that internet availability likely influenced reporting, particularly during the early years of widespread online reporting . These findings reinforce that reported sightings arise from both external events and human opportunity to observe and report them. [Nature] nature.com An environmental analysis of public UAP sightings and sky view potential Scientific ReportsDecember 14, 2023... Published: December 14, 2023 Researchers are therefore cautious about interpreting isolated national peaks. A sudden increase following a major documentary or government hearing may represent heightened awareness rather than an equivalent increase in unexplained aerial phenomena. What attention surges mean for interpreting NUFORC Media driven reporting waves do not make NUFORC unreliable; they simply define what the database measures. NUFORC is best understood as a record of reported experiences occurring within changing social and cultural environments. For investigators, attention surges are valuable in their own right because they reveal"},{"title":"The 701 Cases","url":"/the-701-cases/","description":"The 701 cases were a mixed residual set of reports with different witnesses, evidence quality and reasons for remaining inconclusive.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Blue Book","level":3,"text":"The 701 Cases What Is Actually Inside Blue Book's 701 Unknowns? The 701 Cases The 701 Cases What Is Actually Inside Blue Book's 701 Unknowns? The 701 cases were a mixed residual set of reports with different witnesses, evidence quality and reasons for remaining inconclusive. The 701 cases were a mixed residual set of reports with different witnesses, evidence quality and reasons for remaining inconclusive. UFO Report Databases evidence single dataset dataset evidence How the final total was calculated Why the cases are not a uniform dataset The different reasons a report could remain unresolved ufo report databases blue book the 701 cases Introduction The 701 unresolved cases in Project Blue Book are often misunderstood as a list of the Air Force's \"best UFO cases\" or as official evidence of extraterrestrial craft. In reality, they represent a residual category within a much larger investigative database. Between 1947 and 1969, the Air Force recorded 12,618 UFO reports. Most were eventually attributed to conventional causes, while others lacked sufficient information for meaningful analysis. The remaining 701 cases were those that investigators believed contained enough information to examine but could not confidently match to a known explanation at the time. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 As a dataset, the 701 \"unknowns\" are valuable because they preserve unresolved observations rather than because they demonstrate a single unexplained phenomenon. They include reports from military personnel, commercial and military pilots, radar operators, police officers and civilians, collected over more than two decades under changing investigative practices. The cases vary enormously in quality, detail and credibility, making the collection far more heterogeneous than the simple number \"701\" suggests. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesPublic Interest in UFOs Persists 50 Years After Project Blue Book Termination National ArchivesDecember 5, 2019... Published: December 5, 2019 How the Final Total Was Calculated The figure of 701 was the cumulative total of reports that remained officially classified as \" unidentified \" when Project Blue Book closed in December 1969. It was not a separate investigation or curated shortlist assembled afterwards. Each case entered the total because it survived the Air Force's evaluation process without receiving a satisfactory conventional identification. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 This number should also be understood alongside Blue Book's broader classification system: Identified: investigators concluded that the object was an aircraft, balloon, astronomical object, meteorological phenomenon or another conventional cause. Insufficient data: the report lacked enough information for a meaningful evaluation. Unidentified: investigators believed the available information was sufficient to attempt identification, yet no explanation adequately fit the reported characteristics. [Pieces of History] prologue.blogs.archives.gov Pieces of History UFOs: Natural Explanations – Pieces of History Pieces of HistoryUFOs: Natural Explanations – Pieces of HistoryApril 16, 2018... Published: April 16, 2018 The distinction between \"insufficient data\" and \"unidentified\" is important. The 701 cases were not simply reports with missing information. At least according to Blue Book's own procedures, they were cases considered detailed enough to investigate but not detailed enough to reach a convincing conventional conclusion. [Pieces of History] prologue.blogs.archives.gov Pieces of History UFOs: Natural Explanations – Pieces of History Pieces of HistoryUFOs: Natural Explanations – Pieces of HistoryApril 16, 2018... Published: April 16, 2018 2:26 Project Blue Book: Declassified The True Story of the D.C. UFO Sightings History Channel: HISTORY · Views: 393.7K · Uploaded: March 2019 · Length: 2 minutes 26 seconds Open on YouTube Why the Cases Are Not a Uniform Dataset One of the biggest misconceptions is that all 701 reports share the same evidential value. They do not. Project Blue Book operated from the late 1940s through the end of the 1960s, during which investigative procedures, staffing, priorities and available technology changed substantially. A case investigated in the early 1950s might have involved different reporting forms, different technical resources and different standards of documentation than one investigated in 1968. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesPublic Interest in UFOs Persists 50 Years After Project Blue Book Termination National ArchivesDecember 5, 2019... Published: December 5, 2019 The unresolved files include reports with widely differing characteristics, such as: single witness observations; multiple independent witnesses ; military pilot sightings; civilian reports; radar only events; combined visual and radar observations; daytime observations with relatively detailed descriptions; brief night time light sightings with fewer identifying features. Some files contain numerous witness statements, maps, weather information and technical correspondence. Others consist of only a few pages. Remaining unresolved therefore says little by itself about the strength of any individual case. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 The Different Reasons a Report Could Remain Unresolved The \"unknown\" category resulted from several different investigative outcomes rather than one common explanation. No Conventional Explanation Fit the Available Evidence Some reports contained sufficient observational detail to rule out the explanations most commonly examined by investigators, including known aircraft, balloons, astronomical bodies, meteors or weather effects. Rather than forcing an uncertain identification, Blue Book sometimes retained the \"unidentified\" classification. [Pieces of History] prologue.blogs.archives.gov Pieces of History UFOs: Natural Explanations – Pieces of History Pieces of HistoryUFOs: Natural Explanations – Pieces of HistoryApril 16, 2018... Published: April 16, 2018 Competing Explanations Were Equally Plausible Certain reports contained evidence pointing in different directions. A witness description might resemble an aircraft while the reported manoeuvres or timing conflicted with available flight information. In these situations investigators occasionally concluded that no single explanation could be supported with confidence. 2:12 UFO Project Blue Book at National Archives Museum Channel: US National Archives · Views: 352.7K · Uploaded: December 2019 · Length: 2 minutes 12 seconds Open on YouTube Investigation Reached Practical Limits Blue Book had finite staff, incomplete historical records and limited access to every possible source of corroboration. Civilian flight information, classified military activities and local environmental conditions were not always fully reconstructable years later. Some cases therefore remained unresolved because investigators exhausted the evidence available to them rather than because the observations necessarily represented something extraordinary. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesPublic Interest in UFOs Persists 50 Years After Project Blue Book Termination National ArchivesDecember 5, 2019... Published: December 5, 2019 Scientific Uncertainty Many observations involved transient atmospheric or optical phenomena that were difficult to reproduce or verify. Even when investigators suspected a natural explanation, the available evidence sometimes fell short of proving it conclusively. 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube What the 701 Cases Do—and Do Not—Represent The unresolved category has often been interpreted in two opposing ways that both oversimplify the evidence. One interpretation treats every unknown as proof of an extraordinary phenomenon. The official Air Force position rejected that conclusion, stating that none of the unidentified cases demonstrated a threat to national security, evidence of technology beyond contemporary scientific understanding or evidence of extraterrestrial vehicles. [Secrets Declassified] secretsdeclassified.af.mil Open source on af.mil. The opposite interpretation assumes that every unresolved report would inevitably be explained if more information were available. That conclusion also goes beyond the historical record. Blue Book closed with 701 cases still lacking satisfactory conventional identifications,"},{"title":"Agency Trail","url":"/agency-trail/","description":"Canadian UFO reports often passed from witnesses to police, defence and scientific offices, creating uneven but traceable documentary trails.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Canada Files","level":3,"text":"Agency Trail How a Canadian UFO Report Became a File Canada Files Agency Trail Agency Trail How a Canadian UFO Report Became a File Canadian UFO reports often passed from witnesses to police, defence and scientific offices, creating uneven but traceable documentary trails. Canadian UFO reports often passed from witnesses to police, defence and scientific offices, creating uneven but traceable documentary trails. UFO Report Databases implementation single mechanism mechanism From civilian witness to local police Military review and airspace concerns Scientific assessment and archival transfer ufo report databases canada files agency trail Introduction Canada never operated a single permanent government bureau dedicated to investigating unidentified flying objects (UFOs). Instead, reports moved through a succession of organisations depending on who received the initial information and what officials believed the sighting might represent. A witness might begin by contacting local police, the Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), an airport, a military installation or another government office. From there, the report could pass to defence authorities, aviation officials or scientific staff before eventually becoming part of the national archival record. This constantly changing administrative pathway explains why Canadian UFO files are scattered across multiple departments rather than preserved in one continuous series. Understanding that chain of custody is essential for interpreting Canada's government UFO archives and for tracing how individual reports evolved from eyewitness accounts into official records. [ISED Canada] ised isde.canada.ca ISED CanadaManagement of Public Reporting of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena in CanadaJune 1, 2025... Published: June 1, 2025 From Civilian Witness to Local Police For much of the Cold War, there was no universal reporting route for Canadians who believed they had seen an unidentified object in the sky. The first recipient depended largely on circumstance: Local police or the RCMP if witnesses believed public safety might be involved. Military authorities if the object appeared near defence facilities or military aircraft. Civil aviation authorities when pilots or air traffic personnel observed unusual aerial activity. Municipal officials or government departments if the report arrived through ordinary correspondence. Police officers often performed the first practical investigation. They interviewed witnesses, recorded dates and locations, gathered sketches where available and noted weather or visibility conditions. Most officers did not attempt to determine whether an object was extraordinary. Instead, they documented what had been reported and forwarded the information when another agency appeared better placed to assess it. This early stage explains why many surviving Canadian UFO files contain routine police paperwork rather than detailed scientific analysis. The police acted primarily as information collectors, preserving observations that could later be reviewed by specialists or defence officials. [Canada] canada.ca s UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada's UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada.ca... Military Review and Airspace Concerns Once a report reached federal authorities, the Department of National Defence (DND) evaluated it mainly through the lens of national security and airspace awareness rather than the search for unusual phenomena. During the early 1950s, concern about unidentified aircraft coincided with Cold War defence planning. Reports that might represent foreign aircraft, balloons or other potential intrusions attracted attention because they could have military significance even if they ultimately proved mundane. This practical focus distinguished Canadian defence reviews from popular portrayals of dedicated UFO investigations. Former Pentagon official discusses UFO sightings in Canada and the United States Channel: CTV News Open on YouTube Attempts to Standardise Reporting One of the clearest examples of inter agency coordination was Project Second Storey , established in 1952 by the Defence Research Board. The committee brought together representatives from military intelligence, the Department of Transport and the National Research Council (NRC), with astronomer Peter Millman serving as chair. Rather than conducting extensive field investigations itself, the committee attempted to improve the flow of information between departments. It produced a standard reporting form intended to make UFO reports more consistent regardless of which agency received them first. However, the form never achieved widespread adoption, and reporting practices continued to vary considerably across departments. After several meetings, the committee concluded that available reports did not justify an ongoing military investigative programme and recommended ending defence led investigations. [ISED Canada] ised isde.canada.ca ISED CanadaManagement of Public Reporting of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena in CanadaJune 1, 2025... Published: June 1, 2025 The Department of Transport's Parallel Role At roughly the same time, the Department of Transport became involved through Project Magnet , led by engineer Wilbert Smith. Although Project Magnet explored ideas about UFO reports and geomagnetic phenomena, it remained a small departmental initiative rather than Canada's central reporting office. Consequently, reports sometimes flowed simultaneously through defence channels and Transport files instead of following a single national process. This overlap contributes to duplicated or complementary records now found in different archival collections. [ISED Canada] ised isde.canada.ca ISED CanadaManagement of Public Reporting of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena in CanadaJune 1, 2025... Published: June 1, 2025 Why Responsibility Shifted to Scientific Assessment By the mid 1960s, the federal government increasingly viewed most UFO reports as scientific or observational questions rather than defence problems. In 1967, responsibility for receiving public UFO reports formally shifted from the Department of National Defence to the National Research Council. This transfer changed both the purpose and handling of reports. Instead of asking primarily whether an object represented a military threat, the NRC sought to determine whether ordinary astronomical or atmospheric explanations could account for the observation. Reports continued to arrive from members of the public, municipalities, the RCMP and other federal departments, but they now entered a scientific review process rather than a defence oriented one. The transfer also included responsibility for earlier records generated by Project Second Storey and Project Magnet, helping consolidate historical material under one scientific custodian. [ISED Canada] ised isde.canada.ca ISED CanadaManagement of Public Reporting of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena in CanadaJune 1, 2025... Published: June 1, 2025 How the National Research Council Processed Reports The NRC did not operate as an investigative field agency. Instead, it functioned as a clearing house that gathered reports from multiple sources and attempted to classify them. Peter Millman and other scientific staff frequently compared witness descriptions with known astronomical or atmospheric events, including: Bright planets and stars. Meteors and fireballs. Satellites. Atmospheric optical effects. Other natural phenomena. Reports were commonly filed as \"Non Meteoric Sightings\", reflecting that they had been distinguished from ordinary meteor observations rather than identified as extraordinary objects. This filing system illustrates the NRC's scientific perspective: a report remained \"unidentified\" only because available information was insufficient for a conventional explanation, not because an exotic explanation had been accepted. [ISED Canada] ised isde.canada.ca ISED CanadaManagement of Public Reporting of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena in CanadaJune 1, 2025... Published: June 1, 2025 Records show thousands of UFO sightings in Manitoba Channel: CTV News Open on YouTube How Reports Became Archival Records Canada's documentary trail did not end when a case was closed. Administrative responsibility eventually shifted again, this time from active government departments to the national archives. In 1995, the NRC stopped collecting UFO reports. The associated files—including historical material inherited from earlier departments—were transferred to Library and Archives Canada (LAC). Today, researchers can trace many reports across successive administrative stages because documents from the Department of National Defence, Department of Transport, National Research Council and RCMP have all been preserved within the national archival collection. Approximately 9,500 documents have been digitised from an estimated collection of around 15,000 pages. [Canada] canada.ca s UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada's UFOs:"},{"title":"Falcon Lake","url":"/falcon-lake/","description":"Falcon Lake produced medical, police and laboratory records, yet conflicting details prevented officials from reaching a final explanation.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Canada Files","level":3,"text":"Falcon Lake Why Falcon Lake Still Defies a Clear Verdict Canada Files Falcon Lake Falcon Lake Why Falcon Lake Still Defies a Clear Verdict Falcon Lake produced medical, police and laboratory records, yet conflicting details prevented officials from reaching a final explanation. Falcon Lake produced medical, police and laboratory records, yet conflicting details prevented officials from reaching a final explanation. UFO Report Databases critique risk single case event time window Stefan Michalak's account and reported injuries Police, medical and laboratory follow up Contradictions and limits in the surviving files ufo report databases canada files falcon lake Introduction The 1967 Falcon Lake incident remains unresolved not because investigators failed to examine it, but because they examined it extensively without finding a single explanation that accounted for all of the available evidence. Unlike many UFO reports that rely almost entirely on witness testimony, the Falcon Lake case generated police reports, medical examinations, laboratory testing, correspondence between government agencies and years of follow up within Canadian federal archives. Yet those records contain a mixture of documented facts, uncertain findings and contradictory details that prevented officials from reaching either a conventional or extraordinary conclusion. As a result, the case occupies a distinctive place within Canada's government UFO archives: not as proof of an unidentified craft, but as an example of how an unusually well documented investigation can still end without a definitive verdict. [Canada] canada.ca s UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada's UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada.caMarch 2, 2026... Published: March 2, 2026 Stefan Michalak's account and the reported injuries On 20 May 1967, amateur prospector Stefan Michalak was searching for quartz near Falcon Lake, Manitoba, when he reported seeing two metallic, glowing objects descend. According to his account, one departed while the other landed on a rocky outcrop. Believing it might be an experimental aircraft, he approached, claimed to hear mechanical sounds and reported seeing an opening before the object suddenly closed. Moments later, he said, a blast of hot air or gas emerged through a grille like vent, igniting his shirt and leaving burns across his chest before the object departed. [Canada] canada.ca s UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada's UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada.caMarch 2, 2026... Published: March 2, 2026 What distinguished the case from many contemporaneous UFO reports was that Michalak sought medical attention almost immediately. Doctors documented burns, nausea, vomiting and weakness, while police interviewed him soon after the incident. He maintained substantially the same narrative over many years despite repeated questioning by journalists, investigators and government officials. That consistency strengthened his credibility in the eyes of some investigators, but consistency alone could not establish the cause of the injuries. [Canada] canada.ca s UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada's UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada.caMarch 2, 2026... Published: March 2, 2026 The injuries themselves became central to the mystery. Medical records confirmed that Michalak had genuine burns and other physical complaints, but they did not identify an obvious mechanism capable of producing them. This distinction became important: officials generally accepted that he had suffered an injury, while remaining unconvinced that the reported encounter explained it. The UFO Case That STILL Has No Explanation… (Falcon Lake 1967) Channel: UAP Files Podcast 🛸 Open on YouTube Police, medical and laboratory follow up The Falcon Lake incident produced one of the largest collections of government records associated with a single Canadian UFO report. Local police, the Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), the Department of National Defence and later the National Research Council (NRC) all became involved at various stages, creating a paper trail that extended well beyond the initial investigation. The surviving archive includes witness interviews, correspondence, medical information, laboratory reports and scientific commentary. [canada.ca] canada.ca s UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada's UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada.caMarch 2, 2026... Published: March 2, 2026 Medical examinations confirmed observable skin damage, but physicians reached no consensus about its origin. Some considered the burns unusual without describing them as unique, while no examination established exposure to a specific source such as chemicals, electricity or ionising radiation. Laboratory testing likewise failed to produce a decisive result. Investigators examined Michalak's clothing and materials associated with the site, but no finding conclusively supported or disproved his account of an encounter with an unidentified craft. [Library and Archives Canada] collectionscanada.gc.ca Library and Archives Canada ARCHIVED Library and Archives CanadaARCHIVED Results Canada's UFOs: The Search for the Unknown Library and Archives Canada... Government interest also persisted longer than many UFO cases. Archived NRC records show that Falcon Lake continued to generate official correspondence and review well into 1968 and 1969 rather than disappearing after the initial reports. This prolonged administrative attention reflects continuing uncertainty rather than confirmation of an extraordinary explanation. [Library and Archives Canada] collectionscanada.gc.ca Library and Archives Canada ARCHIVED Library and Archives CanadaARCHIVED Results Canada's UFOs: The Search for the Unknown Library and Archives Canada... Why the surviving evidence never produced a clear answer Several factors prevented investigators from reaching a final conclusion. The witness and the physical evidence pointed in different directions. Michalak's injuries were documented, yet the physical traces did not independently verify the detailed sequence he described. Officials therefore had evidence that something had happened, but not evidence demonstrating precisely what had caused it. Medical findings were suggestive rather than diagnostic. Doctors confirmed burns and illness but could not identify a unique injury pattern that indicated exposure to an unfamiliar technology. The records therefore neither validated nor ruled out conventional causes. No independent witness observed the critical encounter. Other people later became aware of Michalak's condition, but nobody independently witnessed the alleged close approach to the object or the moment when the burns were supposedly inflicted. The central event therefore remained dependent upon a single primary testimony. Laboratory work excluded little but confirmed even less. Various tests narrowed some possibilities without producing positive evidence for another explanation. This left investigators with a growing collection of negative findings rather than one persuasive alternative. Alternative explanations all had weaknesses. Over the decades, suggestions have included accidental exposure to hot gases, industrial materials, deliberate fabrication, misidentification of an experimental aircraft or a natural event interpreted through unusual circumstances. None has accounted satisfactorily for every documented element of the case, while none has been supported strongly enough to replace the original account as the official explanation. The Falcon Lake Close UFO Encounter in Manitoba, Canada (1967) FindingUFO Channel: FindingUFO Open on YouTube Contradictions within the historical record Part of the case's enduring reputation stems from the fact that later retellings often simplify or embellish details that appear more cautiously in the original files. One recurring example concerns the pattern of the burns. Popular accounts frequently describe a sharply defined grid of injuries matching an exhaust vent. However, researchers comparing contemporary medical records and photographs with later publications have argued that the earliest documentation is less clear cut than later illustrations suggest. This does not eliminate the documented burns, but it demonstrates how the case evolved in public memory and why investigators distinguish between primary records and later reconstructions. [Library and Archives Canada] collectionscanada.gc.ca Library and Archives Canada ARCHIVED Library and Archives CanadaARCHIVED Results Canada's UFOs: The Search for the Unknown Library and Archives Canada... Another difficulty is that the archive accumulated over several years through different agencies pursuing different objectives. Police focused on witness statements, doctors on clinical findings and scientific agencies on possible physical explanations. Their files therefore answer different questions and sometimes leave apparent gaps when read as though they formed a single coordinated investigation."},{"title":"Project Magnet","url":"/project-magnet/","description":"Project Magnet used government facilities to test Wilbert Smith's ideas about geomagnetism, propulsion and reported flying saucers.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Canada Files","level":3,"text":"Project Magnet What Was Project Magnet Really Trying to Prove? Canada Files Project Magnet Project Magnet What Was Project Magnet Really Trying to Prove? Project Magnet used government facilities to test Wilbert Smith's ideas about geomagnetism, propulsion and reported flying saucers. Project Magnet used government facilities to test Wilbert Smith's ideas about geomagnetism, propulsion and reported flying saucers. UFO Report Databases historical comparative single policy policy intervention Wilbert Smith's theory and government approval The Shirley's Bay observation station Why official support ended in 1954 ufo report databases canada files project magnet Introduction Project Magnet was not an attempt by the Canadian government to prove that flying saucers were extraterrestrial craft. Instead, it was a small Department of Transport research programme that asked a more limited question: if some unexplained aerial reports reflected a genuine physical phenomenon, could that phenomenon be measured and perhaps even lead to new ideas about propulsion? Between 1950 and 1954, senior radio engineer Wilbert B. Smith used existing government facilities, scientific instruments and a dedicated observation station to test whether unusual magnetic or electromagnetic effects accompanied reported UFO activity. The project produced no reproducible evidence supporting Smith's propulsion theory, and official backing ended after several years of inconclusive results and growing concern over the project's increasingly speculative public profile. [canada.ca] canada.ca s UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada's UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada.caMarch 2, 2026... Published: March 2, 2026 Wilbert Smith's theory and government approval Project Magnet originated with Wilbert Brockhouse Smith, a respected Department of Transport engineer whose professional work centred on radio communications and measurements. Influenced by the rapid growth of flying saucer reports after 1947, Smith proposed that at least some unexplained sightings might involve technology exploiting the Earth's magnetic field. Rather than beginning with the assumption of extraterrestrial visitors, his proposal framed the subject as a potential problem in applied physics and geomagnetism. If such a propulsion method existed, understanding it could have significant scientific and engineering value. [declassified.ca] declassified.ca Project Magnet Project Magnet DECLASSIFIED... The Department of Transport authorised Project Magnet in late 1950 as a modest, part time investigation that could use existing departmental resources rather than a large dedicated budget. This distinction is important. The government approved research into Smith's hypothesis, but it did not officially endorse his conclusions about UFOs. Project Magnet remained a small experimental initiative largely identified with Smith himself rather than with Canadian government policy. [experiencersupport.org] experiencersupport.org project magnet a chronology of ufo sightings and investigations 1950 1952 Project Magnet: A Chronology of UFO Sightings and Investigations (1950 1952) The Experiencer Support Association... Smith's research programme focused on whether reported UFOs produced measurable physical signatures rather than simply collecting witness testimony. His working assumption was that any advanced propulsion system manipulating magnetic forces should leave detectable traces, making instrumentation more valuable than anecdotal reports alone. [experiencersupport.org] experiencersupport.org project magnet a chronology of ufo sightings and investigations 1950 1952 Project Magnet: A Chronology of UFO Sightings and Investigations (1950 1952) The Experiencer Support Association... 7:16 The renewed interest in UFOs Channel: CBC News: The National · Views: 147.3K · Uploaded: May 2021 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube How Project Magnet tried to test propulsion claims Unlike many contemporary UFO investigations, Project Magnet attempted to move beyond interviews and sighting reports by designing observational tests. Smith's approach included several complementary methods: Instrument monitoring: Equipment such as magnetometers, radio receivers, radiation detectors and gravimetric instruments was assembled to look for unusual physical effects that might coincide with reported aerial phenomena. [mysteriesofcanada.com] mysteriesofcanada.com Project Magnet – Wilbert Smith – Canadian UFO Researcher Project Magnet – Wilbert Smith – Canadian UFO Researcher Correlation of observations: Smith compared instrument readings with visual reports in the hope that independently measured anomalies would support eyewitness accounts. [experiencersupport.org] experiencersupport.org project magnet a chronology of ufo sightings and investigations 1950 1952 Project Magnet: A Chronology of UFO Sightings and Investigations (1950 1952) The Experiencer Support Association... Testing witness reliability: In one experiment, a balloon was deliberately launched over Ottawa to observe how accurately members of the public and the media described an unexpected object in the sky. The exercise demonstrated how easily conventional objects could generate varied UFO reports, highlighting the importance of controlled observation. [science.ised isde.canada.ca] science.ised isde.canada.ca report sky canada project ... These experiments reflected an unusual mixture of conventional scientific practice and speculative theory. On one hand, Smith emphasised measurements, repeatability and instrumentation. On the other, the underlying hypothesis—that advanced craft might exploit geomagnetic forces for propulsion—had little support from established physics and was never experimentally confirmed. The Shirley's Bay observation station Project Magnet's best known facility was the observation station established at Shirley's Bay, west of Ottawa. Located on government property where scientific and defence research already occurred, the site offered relatively dark skies, restricted public access and space for specialised equipment. [mysteriesofcanada.com] mysteriesofcanada.com Project Magnet – Wilbert Smith – Canadian UFO Researcher Project Magnet – Wilbert Smith – Canadian UFO Researcher The station served as an experimental observatory rather than a routine UFO reporting office. Smith equipped it with instruments intended to detect radio disturbances, magnetic fluctuations and other unusual environmental effects while observers simultaneously watched the sky. The underlying idea was straightforward: if an unconventional propulsion system interacted with the Earth's magnetic field, instruments might record objective evidence even when photographs or eyewitness accounts remained ambiguous. [mysteriesofcanada.com] mysteriesofcanada.com Project Magnet – Wilbert Smith – Canadian UFO Researcher Project Magnet – Wilbert Smith – Canadian UFO Researcher Some later accounts describe isolated anomalous readings recorded at Shirley's Bay, particularly during 1954. However, no observations from the station produced repeatable, independently verified evidence demonstrating a novel propulsion mechanism. Contemporary official records and later historical reviews consistently characterise the experimental results as inconclusive rather than confirmatory. [canada.ca] science.ised isde.canada.ca report sky canada project ... 47:59 The Shag Harbour UFO Incident Full Documentary Channel: Ocean Digital Entertainment · Views: 530.1K · Uploaded: September 2015 · Length: 47 minutes Open on YouTube Why official support ended in 1954 Several factors combined to end Project Magnet.( endnote 18 \"Endnote 18\") [majesticdocuments.com] majesticdocuments.com Project Magnet Majestic Documents Project Magnet Majestic Documents First, the experiments failed to generate reproducible scientific evidence supporting Smith's central hypothesis. Despite several years of observations, the programme did not produce results that convinced other government scientists that UFO propulsion had been experimentally demonstrated. [science.ised isde.canada.ca] science.ised isde.canada.ca report sky canada project ... Second, Smith's public statements increasingly moved beyond cautious investigation. By the early 1950s he had become personally convinced that at least some UFOs were extraterrestrial vehicles using advanced technology. Those views attracted media attention, particularly after American UFO author Donald Keyhoe publicised Project Magnet, making it more difficult for the Department of Transport to present the programme as a narrowly focused scientific investigation. [canadaufohistory.com] canadaufohistory.com Canada UFO HistoryCanada UFO History Project Magnet... Finally, departmental managers had little reason to continue funding research that had produced neither practical engineering advances nor persuasive scientific evidence. Official financial support ended during 1954, although Smith continued pursuing"},{"title":"Second Storey","url":"/second-storey/","description":"Project Second Storey reviewed Canadian UFO reports and concluded that a major continuing investigation was not justified.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Canada Files","level":3,"text":"Second Storey Why Canada Declined a Bigger UFO Program Canada Files Second Storey Second Storey Why Canada Declined a Bigger UFO Program Project Second Storey reviewed Canadian UFO reports and concluded that a major continuing investigation was not justified. Project Second Storey reviewed Canadian UFO reports and concluded that a major continuing investigation was not justified. UFO Report Databases governance bounded topic entity How the interdepartmental committee worked What officials hoped classification could reveal Why the committee closed in 1954 ufo report databases canada files second storey Introduction Project Second Storey was Canada's principal interdepartmental effort to determine whether unidentified flying object (UFO) reports justified a permanent, large scale government investigation. Rather than becoming an expanding research programme, the committee reached the opposite conclusion. After reviewing available reports, developing reporting procedures and assessing both scientific and security implications, it determined that the evidence did not justify a broader national inquiry. The committee's work between 1952 and 1954 shaped Canada's official approach for years afterwards: maintain records, investigate only exceptional cases where genuine scientific or defence value might exist, but avoid creating a dedicated national UFO investigation programme. [science.ised isde.canada.ca] science.ised isde.canada.ca report sky canada project February 11, 2026... Published: February 11, 2026 How the interdepartmental committee worked Project Second Storey was established in April 1952 under the Defence Research Board, Canada's military science organisation within the Department of National Defence. It was chaired by astronomer Dr Peter Millman and included representatives from military intelligence, the armed services and other government departments. The committee's purpose was not to prove or disprove extraordinary claims but to advise whether UFO reports warranted a sustained governmental programme. [science.ised isde.canada.ca] science.ised isde.canada.ca report sky canada project February 11, 2026... Published: February 11, 2026 Rather than conducting extensive field investigations itself, the committee concentrated on improving how reports were collected and evaluated. Members examined existing sighting reports, discussed interviewing techniques, considered methods for cataloguing cases and drafted a standard reporting form intended to make information submitted by different departments more consistent. This reflected a practical administrative goal: before deciding whether a major investigation was necessary, officials wanted to know whether reliable evidence could be gathered in a systematic way. [ncas.org] condon.ncas.org Condon Report, Sec V, Chap 3 UFO Study Progs in Foreign Countries Condon Report, Sec V, Chap 3 UFO Study Progs in Foreign Countries The committee therefore functioned primarily as an advisory and coordinating body. Operational collection of reports remained with military and other government organisations that already encountered unusual aerial observations during their normal duties. [condon.ncas.org] condon.ncas.org Condon Report, Sec V, Chap 3 UFO Study Progs in Foreign Countries Condon Report, Sec V, Chap 3 UFO Study Progs in Foreign Countries 4:20 Canada’s UFO survey results released Channel: CTV News · Views: 11.5K · Uploaded: March 2026 · Length: 4 minutes 20 seconds Open on YouTube What officials hoped classification could reveal One reason for establishing common reporting procedures was the expectation that careful classification might expose useful patterns hidden within individual reports. Officials hoped that standardised records could help distinguish between: astronomical objects such as planets and meteors; atmospheric or meteorological phenomena; aircraft, balloons and other conventional objects; reports potentially relevant to Canadian air defence; genuinely unexplained observations deserving closer examination. The underlying assumption was that a sufficiently large and well organised dataset would reveal whether UFO reports represented a coherent phenomenon or simply a mixture of unrelated events. In practice, the committee found no evidence that classification uncovered a distinct class of incidents requiring a specialised government organisation. Instead, most reports could either be explained, lacked sufficient information for analysis or offered little scientific value. [science.ised isde.canada.ca] science.ised isde.canada.ca report sky canada project February 11, 2026... Published: February 11, 2026 The committee's emphasis on standardisation also reflected a broader scientific mindset. Millman later supported the idea of internationally consistent reporting formats, arguing that comparable records were more valuable than isolated anecdotes. However, this preference for better documentation did not translate into support for a permanent Canadian investigative programme. [condon.ncas.org] condon.ncas.org Condon Report, Sec V, Chap 3 UFO Study Progs in Foreign Countries Condon Report, Sec V, Chap 3 UFO Study Progs in Foreign Countries Why officials rejected a larger investigation Several considerations combined to persuade the committee that a major inquiry was unnecessary. First, the available evidence did not indicate a national security threat. During the early Cold War, any unidentified object in Canadian airspace had to be considered in the context of defence, but the committee found no persuasive indication that reported sightings represented hostile aircraft or other military dangers requiring a dedicated investigative organisation. [science.ised isde.canada.ca] science.ised isde.canada.ca report sky canada project February 11, 2026... Published: February 11, 2026 Second, officials saw limited scientific return. Although some cases remained unexplained because of incomplete information, unresolved did not automatically mean extraordinary. Reports generally lacked the quality, consistency or measurable data needed to support sustained scientific research. The committee therefore concluded that large scale investment would probably produce little additional knowledge. [science.ised isde.canada.ca] science.ised isde.canada.ca report sky canada project February 11, 2026... Published: February 11, 2026 Third, the committee distinguished its cautious institutional assessment from the more speculative views associated with Wilbert B. Smith's Project Magnet . Smith explored unconventional ideas about UFO propulsion and geomagnetism, but those views were regarded as his own rather than official Canadian government policy. Project Second Storey evaluated the broader evidence independently and did not endorse Smith's conclusions. [ncas.org] condon.ncas.org Condon Report, Sec V, Chap 3 UFO Study Progs in Foreign Countries Condon Report, Sec V, Chap 3 UFO Study Progs in Foreign Countries Finally, officials judged that existing departments could continue handling reports without establishing a permanent specialist organisation. Occasional investigations of particularly interesting cases could still occur within normal departmental responsibilities, avoiding the cost and bureaucracy of a dedicated national programme. [condon.ncas.org] condon.ncas.org Condon Report, Sec V, Chap 3 UFO Study Progs in Foreign Countries Condon Report, Sec V, Chap 3 UFO Study Progs in Foreign Countries 47:59 The Shag Harbour UFO Incident Full Documentary Channel: Ocean Digital Entertainment · Views: 530.1K · Uploaded: September 2015 · Length: 47 minutes Open on YouTube Why the committee closed in 1954 Project Second Storey held only a small number of meetings over roughly two years before becoming inactive. Its closure reflected completion of its advisory role rather than the resolution of every UFO report. By 1954, the committee had reached its central policy recommendation: no evidence justified a large scale official investigation; reporting procedures had been developed; responsibility for maintaining files could remain within existing government structures; exceptional cases could still receive attention when they appeared to have genuine scientific or operational significance. The standard reporting form produced by the committee was never widely adopted across government, illustrating the limited institutional momentum behind creating a permanent UFO bureaucracy. Meanwhile, Project Magnet also ended in 1954, further reducing official enthusiasm for expanding Canadian UFO research programmes. [canada.ca] science.ised isde.canada.ca report sky canada project February 11, 2026... Published: February 11, 2026 Lasting influence on Canadian UFO archives Although Project Second Storey rejected a larger inquiry, its influence persisted in the way Canada handled UFO reports. Rather than creating a permanent investigative agency, Canada adopted a record keeping model. Departments continued receiving"},{"title":"Shag Harbour","url":"/shag-harbour/","description":"The Shag Harbour files confirm a serious search for a possible aviation emergency without identifying the reported lights.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Canada Files","level":3,"text":"Shag Harbour What Do the Shag Harbour Files Prove? Canada Files Shag Harbour Shag Harbour What Do the Shag Harbour Files Prove? The Shag Harbour files confirm a serious search for a possible aviation emergency without identifying the reported lights. The Shag Harbour files confirm a serious search for a possible aviation emergency without identifying the reported lights. UFO Report Databases evidence single case event time window Witness reports from the night of the incident Why police and military agencies responded What the records leave unresolved ufo report databases canada files shag harbour Introduction The official records relating to the Shag Harbour incident establish something more limited—but also more concrete—than many later retellings suggest. They show that on the night of 4 October 1967, multiple independent witnesses reported unusual lights descending into the water near Shag Harbour, Nova Scotia. Those reports were treated as a possible aviation emergency, prompting responses from the Royal Canadian Mounted Police (RCMP), rescue authorities, the Canadian military and maritime agencies. Searches were conducted because officials initially believed an aircraft might have crashed. What the surviving government files do not establish is the identity of the object or lights that triggered the response. Within Canada's government UFO archives, the significance of the case lies in its well documented official reaction rather than in any official endorsement of an extraordinary explanation. [Canada] canada.ca s UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada's UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada.caMarch 2, 2026... Published: March 2, 2026 Witness reports from the night of the incident The records consistently show that the incident began with reports from numerous civilian witnesses who observed a series of bright lights travelling over the coast before appearing to descend into the Atlantic Ocean near Shag Harbour. Witnesses differed on details such as colour, spacing and movement, but their reports broadly agreed that something unusual seemed to enter the water rather than simply disappear into the sky. [Disclosdex] disclosdex.com 1967 shag harbour canadian government files Shag Harbour Canadian Government Files (1967) DisclosdexOctober 5, 1967... Published: October 5, 1967 These reports reached the RCMP quickly because local residents believed they had witnessed an aircraft accident. The initial assumption recorded in official communications was therefore practical rather than speculative: emergency services needed to determine whether an aircraft had crashed and whether survivors required rescue. That distinction is important when reading the archival files. The documents record an unidentified event requiring investigation, not a conclusion about what caused it. [Canada] canada.ca s UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada's UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada.caMarch 2, 2026... Published: March 2, 2026 Contemporary records also indicate that authorities attempted to determine whether any known aircraft were missing. Available information failed to match the reports with a confirmed aviation accident, yet this lack of identification did not eliminate the need for further searching because eyewitnesses had described what appeared to be an impact on the water. [Disclosdex] disclosdex.com 1967 shag harbour canadian government files Shag Harbour Canadian Government Files (1967) DisclosdexOctober 5, 1967... Published: October 5, 1967 47:59 The Shag Harbour UFO Incident Full Documentary Channel: Ocean Digital Entertainment · Views: 530.1K · Uploaded: September 2015 · Length: 47 minutes Open on YouTube Why police and military agencies responded The government response is one of the strongest parts of the documentary record because it reflects standard emergency procedures rather than an investigation launched specifically into unidentified flying objects. Police officers attended the shoreline after receiving reports from witnesses. Rescue coordination authorities were alerted, local vessels assisted in searching the reported location, and Canadian military assets became involved because an apparent aircraft ditching at sea would have fallen within established search and rescue responsibilities. [Disclosdex] disclosdex.com 1967 shag harbour canadian government files Shag Harbour Canadian Government Files (1967) DisclosdexOctober 5, 1967... Published: October 5, 1967 The surviving files therefore demonstrate several points with reasonable confidence: The reports were treated as credible enough to justify an immediate search. Authorities initially investigated the event as a possible aviation emergency rather than as a UFO case. Multiple government organisations exchanged information during the response, including police and military authorities. Search operations continued after initial efforts failed to locate an aircraft or survivors. [disclosdex.com] disclosdex.com 1967 shag harbour canadian government files Shag Harbour Canadian Government Files (1967) DisclosdexOctober 5, 1967... Published: October 5, 1967 This administrative trail explains why the Shag Harbour incident occupies an unusually prominent place within Canadian government UFO archives. Unlike many sightings that generated only correspondence or witness reports, this case produced operational records documenting an actual emergency response. [Canada] canada.ca s UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada's UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada.caMarch 2, 2026... Published: March 2, 2026 What the records leave unresolved The archival evidence is often stronger regarding what happened procedurally than what was observed physically . The files do not identify the object reported by witnesses. Despite search efforts, officials did not recover a crashed aircraft, wreckage or other material that conclusively explained the event. The absence of a conventional explanation in the files should not be mistaken for evidence supporting an extraordinary one. Instead, it means the investigation did not reach a definitive identification. [Disclosdex] disclosdex.com 1967 shag harbour canadian government files Shag Harbour Canadian Government Files (1967) DisclosdexOctober 5, 1967... Published: October 5, 1967 Later books, documentaries and UFO literature have expanded the story with claims involving underwater tracking, recovered craft, secret naval operations or additional military discoveries. These narratives are not established by the surviving contemporaneous government records. While some later authors argue that additional undocumented events occurred after the initial search, those claims depend on interviews, recollections or secondary sources rather than the official documents that form Canada's archival record. [Disclosdex] disclosdex.com 1967 shag harbour canadian government files Shag Harbour Canadian Government Files (1967) DisclosdexOctober 5, 1967... Published: October 5, 1967 For historians, this distinction is crucial. The government files authenticate the existence of an official investigation and preserve evidence of how authorities responded. They do not authenticate every later claim attached to the incident. Tim Gallaudet Discusses \"Shag Harbour\" & \"Shelburne\" UFO Incidents Channel: VETTED Open on YouTube 30:34 The Canadian UFO Crash That Hasn’t Been Debunked Channel: PaytonMoreland · Views: 214.1K · Uploaded: June 2024 · Length: 30 minutes Open on YouTube Why the case remains important within Canadian government archives Among Canada's archived UFO files, Shag Harbour stands out because it combines several features that rarely occur together: numerous civilian witnesses reporting the same general event; rapid involvement by law enforcement; documented search and rescue activity; participation by military authorities; and a surviving federal documentary trail preserved by Library and Archives Canada. [Canada] canada.ca s UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada's UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada.caMarch 2, 2026... Published: March 2, 2026 That combination makes the case especially valuable for researchers interested in how Canadian institutions handled unusual reports. The files illustrate official decision making under conditions of uncertainty: reports were assessed, emergency procedures were followed, agencies shared information and searches were carried out until no evidence of an aircraft accident could be found. The enduring importance of the Shag Harbour records therefore lies less in proving that an unidentified craft entered the ocean than in documenting a real government response to an unexplained event. The archives establish that officials investigated what witnesses reported, that the search was genuine and extensive, and that the identity of the reported lights remained unresolved in the surviving official record. [canada.ca] canada.ca s UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada's UFOs: The search for the unknown Canada.caMarch 2,"},{"title":"Duplicates","url":"/duplicates-7830f1/","description":"One widely seen light can become many database entries unless reports are carefully grouped into a single event.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Database Comparison","level":3,"text":"Duplicates When One UFO Sighting Becomes Ten Records Database Comparison Duplicates Duplicates When One UFO Sighting Becomes Ten Records One widely seen light can become many database entries unless reports are carefully grouped into a single event. One widely seen light can become many database entries unless reports are carefully grouped into a single event. UFO Report Databases critique risk single mechanism mechanism Why multiple witnesses create multiple rows Clues used to match related reports Why deduplication can also merge real events ufo report databases database comparison duplicates Introduction Headline totals in UFO databases can give a misleading impression of how many separate incidents actually occurred. A single unusual light seen over a city may generate reports from dozens of witnesses, be documented by several organisations, and later appear in books, magazines and online archives. If each account is counted as an independent sighting rather than as one underlying event with multiple sources, apparent UFO activity can be substantially inflated. This is one of the most important reasons why researchers caution against comparing database totals without understanding how records are organised. The challenge is not unique to UFO research. Any system that preserves eyewitness testimony must decide whether to prioritise individual reports or reconstructed events. UFO catalogues often preserve both, making careful deduplication essential before drawing statistical conclusions. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 When One UFO Sighting Becomes Ten Records A public reporting system usually treats each witness submission as a valuable historical document. If ten people independently describe the same object, each account may contain unique details, different viewing angles or conflicting recollections. Deleting nine of them would lose potentially useful information. For that reason, many databases deliberately preserve separate witness records instead of collapsing everything into a single incident. The resulting catalogue therefore measures reports rather than necessarily measuring unique events. A database with 100,000 entries does not automatically represent 100,000 unrelated sightings. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 The effect becomes even larger during highly publicised incidents. Well known mass sightings can produce reports from civilians, police officers, pilots, journalists and investigators across several days. Later publications discussing the same event may themselves become catalogue entries if the database is designed as a bibliographic reference rather than a simple incident list. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... 1:25:35 70 Years of UFO UAP Data: A Scientific Review with Robert Powell (SCU Founder) Channel: QOOSP Lab: multidisciplinary research & dialogue · Views: 658 · Uploaded: October 2025 · Length: 1 hour 25 minutes Open on YouTube Why multiple witnesses create multiple rows Several mechanisms commonly produce duplicate or near duplicate records: Independent witness submissions. Different observers submit separate reports of the same object. Reports sent to multiple organisations. A witness may file with NUFORC , MUFON , CUFOS or local investigators, creating parallel records across repositories. Multiple investigators. Original witness statements, investigator summaries and follow up reports may all be stored separately. Published retellings. Magazine articles, books and later catalogues often create additional references to an already documented case. Delayed reporting. Someone who remembers an event years later may submit a new account that describes the same historical incident already in the archive. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 These duplicates are not necessarily errors. They often preserve the reporting history of an event rather than attempting to produce a single definitive case file. 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube How researchers identify related reports Deduplication is rarely as simple as matching identical dates. Witnesses frequently estimate times differently, use nearby place names or describe the same object in different language. Researchers therefore compare combinations of evidence, including: date and approximate time geographic location object direction and movement duration number of witnesses weather conditions narrative similarity references to the same news coverage or investigation No single field is sufficient. Two unrelated events may occur on the same evening in neighbouring towns, while genuine duplicate reports may differ by an hour because witnesses estimated the time differently. Modern digital projects increasingly supplement these traditional methods with text comparison techniques that identify narratives describing the same event even when the wording differs. Such approaches help reduce duplicate counting while preserving original witness statements for later inspection. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... Why deduplication can also merge real events Removing duplicates is not risk free. Conservative matching avoids overcounting but can accidentally merge genuinely separate incidents. For example, during meteor showers, satellite launches or periods of intense media attention, many unrelated observers may report different objects from nearby locations on the same night. An automated system that relies too heavily on time and place could incorrectly treat these as one event. Likewise, two aircraft seen from different towns may generate reports that appear similar despite referring to different observations. Overly aggressive deduplication reduces the apparent number of incidents just as excessive duplication inflates it. This trade off explains why many research catalogues retain the original reports even after identifying likely links between them. Researchers can reconstruct events without permanently discarding potentially important evidence. 16:15 UFO Data Mining, Part 1: Time Series Models Channel: Cache Lack Stats · Views: 203 · Uploaded: November 2021 · Length: 16 minutes Open on YouTube UFOCAT shows why raw record counts can mislead The design of UFOCAT illustrates the distinction between records and events particularly clearly. The catalogue was developed primarily as a comprehensive reference to published and unpublished UFO material, not simply as a list of unique sightings. As a result, it intentionally contains multiple entries relating to the same underlying case—for example, an original investigation, a magazine article and a later book discussing that incident. CUFOS explicitly warns that simply counting database entries will overestimate the number of cases. To help researchers estimate unique events, UFOCAT includes linking fields that identify primary reports, allowing users to exclude secondary references when appropriate. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... This illustrates an important principle across UFO databases: a large catalogue may reflect extensive documentation rather than unusually high levels of unexplained aerial activity. Why duplicate handling changes statistical conclusions Duplicate reports can distort several common analyses if they are not recognised: Apparent spikes in sightings may partly reflect many witnesses reporting one widely visible event. Geographic \"hotspots\" can be exaggerated when a single incident generates numerous local submissions. Comparisons between databases become unreliable if one counts witness reports while another counts reconstructed events. Claims that one organisation has \"more sightings\" than another may simply reflect different cataloguing practices rather than different underlying phenomena. For this reason, experienced researchers generally distinguish between report counts , case counts and unique events before making historical or statistical comparisons. Understanding which unit a database records is often more important than the headline total itself. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to When One UFO Sighting Becomes Ten Records. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Data Matching: Concepts and Techniques for Record Linkage, En... By Peter Christen Data"},{"title":"Archive Totals","url":"/archive-totals/","description":"Archive catalogues count preserved folders, photographs and correspondence, not a consistent number of sky events.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Database Comparison","level":3,"text":"Archive Totals Why Archive Hits Do Not Equal UFO Sightings Database Comparison Archive Totals Archive Totals Why Archive Hits Do Not Equal UFO Sightings Archive catalogues count preserved folders, photographs and correspondence, not a consistent number of sky events. Archive catalogues count preserved folders, photographs and correspondence, not a consistent number of sky events. UFO Report Databases evidence single dataset dataset evidence What archival catalogues actually describe How preservation and digitisation shape results Using archive counts without inventing event totals ufo report databases database comparison archive totals Introduction Archive search results are often mistaken for counts of UFO sightings, but archival catalogues measure something different. A government archive preserves records created by public bodies, not a definitive inventory of unusual events. A search for \"UFO\" or \" unidentified flying object\" typically returns catalogue entries for files, folders, correspondence, policy papers, photographs, parliamentary questions and administrative records. One catalogue entry may document dozens of reported sightings, while several entries may all relate to the same incident. As a result, archive search totals primarily reflect how records were created, organised, retained and described rather than how many unidentified aerial events actually occurred. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... What archival catalogues actually describe An archival catalogue is an index to preserved records, not a database of individual incidents. Government departments create files to support their own work, and archives preserve those files according to archival standards. The catalogue therefore describes the records themselves rather than every event mentioned within them. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives Our digital cataloguing practices National ArchivesOur digital cataloguing practices The National Archives... Several factors make catalogue totals unsuitable as sighting counts: One file can contain many reports. A folder covering a month's correspondence or a regional investigation may include numerous witness accounts. One event can generate many files. A widely reported incident may produce operational records, ministerial briefings, press enquiries, parliamentary correspondence and later freedom of information material, each catalogued separately. Some records concern policy rather than sightings. Searches often retrieve administrative papers explaining reporting procedures, record management or government policy instead of documenting observations in the sky. Catalogue records describe containers. The searchable item is frequently a file or series rather than the individual documents stored inside it. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... The UK National Archives illustrates this distinction clearly. Its UFO holdings include official policy, parliamentary business, public correspondence and sighting reports, meaning a keyword search mixes operational cases with administrative documentation rather than listing individual observations. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... 4:31 UFO Interview, 1966 Channel: US National Archives · Views: 688.8K · Uploaded: September 2013 · Length: 4 minutes 31 seconds Open on YouTube How preservation and digitisation shape results Archive search totals also depend on what survived long enough to be catalogued. For example, the UK Ministry of Defence destroyed many UFO files under its records management policy before later deciding to retain material because of sustained public interest. Consequently, surviving catalogue entries represent only the preserved documentary record, not every report originally received. Missing files reduce archive totals without implying fewer historical sightings. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... Digitisation introduces another layer of variation. Archives digitise collections gradually, often according to conservation priorities, demand or available funding. Newly digitised material may suddenly appear in catalogue searches even though the underlying records have existed for decades. Likewise, some collections remain available only through physical consultation, making online search results an incomplete view of total holdings. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives Digitisation National ArchivesDigitisation The National Archives... Cataloguing practices also evolve over time. Archivists may: split large files into multiple catalogue entries; merge related material into a single description; improve metadata with additional keywords; revise titles and descriptions to match current archival standards. Each change can alter search results without any change to the number of underlying UFO reports. Modern digital cataloguing systems are designed to improve discoverability of archival records rather than to create statistical datasets of historical events. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives Our digital cataloguing practices National ArchivesOur digital cataloguing practices The National Archives... 9:47 UFO file release October 2008 Channel: The National Archives UK · Views: 19.5K · Uploaded: October 2008 · Length: 9 minutes Open on YouTube Using archive counts without inventing event totals Archive search results remain valuable evidence when interpreted correctly. They can reveal: how extensively a government agency documented UFO related matters; which departments handled reports; when record keeping practices changed; what kinds of correspondence or investigations were preserved; how official interest developed over time. What they cannot reliably show is the total number of sightings. For example, an archive search returning hundreds of catalogue hits does not mean hundreds of separate UFO events occurred. Those results may include duplicate references to the same case, administrative correspondence about earlier reports, press enquiries, policy reviews or files containing multiple unrelated incidents. Conversely, a single catalogue record may contain dozens of witness reports that are invisible until the file itself is examined. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives UFOs National ArchivesUFOs The National Archives... Researchers who need incident totals therefore work from case level databases, investigation registers or individual report forms rather than archival catalogue counts. Archive metadata are best treated as evidence of documentary holdings and institutional record keeping, while estimates of sighting frequency require datasets whose basic unit is the individual reported event rather than the archival file that preserves it. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives Our digital cataloguing practices National ArchivesOur digital cataloguing practices The National Archives... 3:56 UFO file release March 2009 Channel: The National Archives UK · Views: 128.6K · Uploaded: March 2009 · Length: 3 minutes 56 seconds Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Archive Hits Do Not Equal UFO Sightings. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects By Edward J Ruppelt The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects is a book by Edward J. Ruppelt which described the study of UFOs by United States Air Force fro... See on Amazon Book UFOs and Government: A Historical Inquiry By Michael D. Swords Governments around the world have had to deal with the UFO phenomenon for a good part of a century. How and why they did so is the subjec... See on Amazon Book Using Archives: A Guide to Effective Research By Laura A. Millar This new and extensively revised second edition offers an international perspective on archives management, providing authoritative guida... See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects UFOs and Government: A Historical Inquiry Using Archives: A to Effective Research books As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. eBay marketplace picks Marketplace Samples Live tested eBay searches with available results related to this page. Shop location Using USA USA USA UK Canada Australia Ireland USA UK Canada Australia Ireland Selected from UFO poster on eBay.co.uk . Current eBay listing Conspiracy Theory Poster Wall Print Gift UFO Aliens Flat Earth Moon Landing GBP 18.97 Free shipping New Seller: forgevalleyprint (98.6% positive) Ships from: GB View listing on eBay Current eBay listing UFO Over Desert Highway Sci Fi Abduction Poster Print Framed Canvas Wall Art GBP 14.99"},{"title":"Case Counts","url":"/case-counts/","description":"A UFO database row may represent a witness report, an investigated event, a source citation or an entire archival file.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Database Comparison","level":3,"text":"Case Counts What Does One UFO Case Actually Count? Database Comparison Case Counts Case Counts What Does One UFO Case Actually Count? A UFO database row may represent a witness report, an investigated event, a source citation or an entire archival file. A UFO database row may represent a witness report, an investigated event, a source citation or an entire archival file. UFO Report Databases mechanism single mechanism mechanism Reports, events and files How counting units change totals Questions to ask before comparing databases ufo report databases database comparison case counts Introduction Headline figures from UFO databases are often compared as though every repository counts the same thing. In practice, the word case has no universal meaning. One database may count each witness submission, another each investigated event, another every document held in an archive, and another every published source referring to the same incident. As a result, two databases can report dramatically different totals without either being incorrect. Understanding what a single record represents is therefore more important than comparing the size of the collections themselves. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 What Does One UFO Case Actually Count? The key difference lies in the database's unit of record. Reports, events and files A witness report records one person's account. If three people independently report the same light in the sky, a reporting database may contain three separate records because it is preserving individual testimony rather than reconstructing a single event. Systems designed for public reporting, such as the National UFO Reporting Center ( NUFORC ), are primarily organised around submissions from witnesses. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 An investigated event attempts to merge those reports into one incident. Investigators may collect multiple witness statements, photographs and radar information, but publish only a single case file representing the investigation. In this model, several reports become one investigated case. An archival file is different again. Historical collections such as Project Blue Book preserve government paperwork. One file may concern a single sighting, while another may contain correspondence, administrative memoranda or supplementary documents relating to an investigation. Counting archival files therefore measures preserved documentation rather than unique UFO events. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National Archives... A source citation is another possible unit. UFOCAT , maintained by the Center for UFO Studies, was designed as a reference catalogue of publications and documentary sources. Its creators explicitly note that one sighting may appear several times because the catalogue indexes an original investigation, magazine articles, books and other references separately. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube How Counting Units Change Totals Because databases answer different questions, their totals naturally diverge. A reporting database generally grows whenever another witness submits an account. Even if investigators later conclude that several submissions describe the same occurrence, each report still documents a separate observer and remains valuable for studying witness descriptions, reporting behaviour and geographical distribution. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 An investigative database grows more slowly because its unit is usually the incident rather than every individual narrative. Multiple witness statements may be consolidated into one investigation, reducing the apparent number of cases while increasing the amount of information attached to each one. Reference catalogues can appear larger still because they intentionally preserve multiple documentary paths to the same event. UFOCAT specifically warns users that simple record counts will overestimate the number of actual sightings unless duplicate source relationships are taken into account. The catalogue includes linking fields intended to distinguish primary case records from additional references. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... Archival repositories introduce another complication. A government archive may contain thousands of folders or many cubic feet of records, yet those holdings include administrative material alongside investigative files. The size of the archive therefore reflects what was preserved, not necessarily how many distinct aerial events occurred. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National Archives... Why Different Counting Rules Exist These differences are usually intentional rather than accidental. A public reporting organisation wants to preserve every witness account because each submission may contain unique observations, contact details or timing information. An investigative organisation is more interested in reconstructing what happened during a particular incident, making consolidation both practical and desirable. An archive aims to preserve historical documentation in its original form, regardless of whether several folders concern the same event. A bibliographic catalogue seeks completeness of documentation, not uniqueness of sightings. Recording every publication discussing a famous incident allows researchers to trace how the story developed over time, even though that approach inflates simple record totals. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... 2:26 Project Blue Book: Declassified The True Story of the D.C. UFO Sightings History Channel: HISTORY · Views: 393.7K · Uploaded: March 2019 · Length: 2 minutes 26 seconds Open on YouTube Questions to Ask Before Comparing Databases Before treating one database as \"larger\" than another, it helps to answer a few practical questions. What is one row? Is it a witness report, an investigated incident, a document, or a source reference? Are duplicate witness reports merged? Multiple observers may describe the same event. Can one event produce multiple records? Bibliographic databases often allow this by design. Can one record contain many witnesses? Investigative files frequently combine statements into a single case. Are administrative records included? Historical archives may count files that are not individual sightings. Does the database document submissions or verified investigations? Reporting and investigative systems serve different purposes. These questions often explain differences in headline totals far better than assumptions about reporting rates or investigative activity. The Main Lesson A UFO database total is meaningful only when its counting unit is understood. A collection with fewer records may actually describe more thoroughly investigated incidents, while a much larger database may simply preserve every witness submission or every published reference. Comparing raw case totals without first identifying what each record represents risks comparing reports, events, documents and citations as though they were the same measure, when in reality they answer different research questions. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 16:15 UFO Data Mining, Part 1: Time Series Models Channel: Cache Lack Stats · Views: 203 · Uploaded: November 2021 · Length: 16 minutes Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to What Does One UFO Case Actually Count?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book UFOs and Government: A Historical Inquiry By Michael D. Swords Governments around the world have had to deal with the UFO phenomenon for a good part of a century. How and why they did so is the subjec... See on Amazon Book Data Matching: Concepts and Techniques for Record Linkage, En... By Peter Christen Data matching (also known as record or data linkage, entity resolution, object identification, or field matching) is the task of identify... See on Amazon Book Data Quality: The Accuracy Dimension By Jack E. Olson Data Quality: The Accuracy Dimension is about assessing the quality of corporate data and improving its accuracy using the data"},{"title":"Unexplained Rates","url":"/unexplained-rates/","description":"An unexplained rate reflects each program's intake rules, evidence standards and classification language rather than a universal mystery score.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Database Comparison","level":3,"text":"Unexplained Rates Why UFO Unexplained Rates Mislead Comparisons Database Comparison Unexplained Rates Unexplained Rates Why UFO Unexplained Rates Mislead Comparisons An unexplained rate reflects each program's intake rules, evidence standards and classification language rather than a universal mystery score. An unexplained rate reflects each program's intake rules, evidence standards and classification language rather than a universal mystery score. UFO Report Databases historical comparative bounded topic policy intervention Different meanings of unidentified How weak evidence changes the percentage What a fair percentage comparison requires ufo report databases database comparison unexplained rates Introduction The percentage of cases labelled \"unexplained\" is one of the most frequently quoted figures in discussions of UFO databases, yet it is also one of the least suitable for comparing one catalogue with another. An unexplained rate is not a universal measure of mystery. Instead, it reflects the rules under which a particular organisation accepts reports, investigates them, classifies them and revisits earlier conclusions. Two databases can report very different unexplained percentages even if they are examining broadly similar underlying events because they define \" unidentified \" in different ways and apply different investigative thresholds. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPAN Classification GEIPAN... For that reason, headline comparisons such as \"Database A has 20% unexplained while Database B has 3%\" are usually misleading. Before comparing percentages, it is necessary to understand what each organisation means by an unresolved case, which reports enter the database in the first place and how incomplete or low quality evidence is handled. Project Blue Book Special Report No. 14 Explained The Truth About UFO Investigations Channel: D Code Hub Open on YouTube Different Meanings of \"Unidentified\" The word \"unidentified\" does not describe the same outcome across all UFO reporting systems. It is a label created by each programme's own procedures rather than a standard scientific category. France's GEIPAN provides one of the clearest examples. Its classification system separates cases into: A: identified phenomenon. B: probably identified phenomenon. C: insufficient information for a reliable conclusion. D: unexplained after investigation. [youtube.com] youtube.com GEIPAN UFO investigation France unexplained cases France's Official UFO Investigation Agency (GEIPAN) Kryptoscipher... Since 2008, GEIPAN has further divided D cases into D1 and D2 using formal assessments of both the remaining \"strangeness\" of the observation and the consistency of the available evidence. Importantly, cases lacking adequate information are generally placed in Category C rather than being counted as unexplained. Category D is reserved for reports that remain unexplained despite sufficient investigation and reliable evidence. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPAN Classification GEIPAN... Project Blue Book used a different framework. During its statistical work for Special Report No. 14, cases were classified as identified, insufficient information or unknown. The standard for calling a case \"unknown\" was deliberately demanding: analysts required unanimous agreement that no satisfactory explanation could be assigned. Consequently, Blue Book's \"unknown\" category represented the outcome of its own review procedures rather than a directly comparable equivalent of another organisation's unresolved cases. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Project Blue Book Project Blue Book These differing definitions mean that two organisations may both use words such as \"unknown\", \"unidentified\" or \"unexplained\" while referring to substantially different investigative outcomes. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube How Weak Evidence Changes the Percentage The treatment of poor quality evidence has a major influence on unexplained rates. Suppose two organisations each receive 1,000 reports. If one excludes weak cases from its unexplained category by placing them into an \"insufficient information\" class, while the other simply leaves every unresolved submission in its unknown category, their published unexplained percentages will diverge even if the underlying reports are very similar. GEIPAN explicitly illustrates this effect. Its published statistics distinguish between cases that remain unexplained after investigation (Category D) and cases that cannot be evaluated because the available information is inadequate (Category C). Current published figures show that only a small proportion of published cases fall into Category D, while a much larger share remains in Category C because reliable conclusions cannot be drawn. [Geipan] geipan.fr Statistics GEIPANJune 25, 2026... Published: June 25, 2026 Blue Book likewise separated \"insufficient information\" from \"unknown\", but its historical reporting covered a different period, different reporting environment and different investigative resources. Comparing Blue Book's historical unknown percentage directly with GEIPAN's modern D percentage ignores these structural differences. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Project Blue Book Project Blue Book Evidence quality also changes over time. New witnesses, improved astronomical databases, aviation records and digital imagery can allow older cases to be reclassified. GEIPAN explicitly states that both C and D cases may be revisited when new information becomes available, meaning today's unexplained percentage is not necessarily permanent. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPAN Classification GEIPAN... What a Fair Percentage Comparison Requires A meaningful comparison between UFO databases requires far more than placing two percentages side by side. At minimum, researchers should ask whether the databases are comparable in several key respects: Intake rules: Does the organisation accept every public report or only selected investigated cases? Investigation threshold: How much evidence is required before assigning a final classification? Classification scheme: Is there a separate category for insufficient information? Review policy: Can cases be reopened and reclassified as new evidence appears? Time period: Were the reports collected under comparable technological, social and aviation conditions? Publication practice: Does the published database include every case or only completed investigations? Only after accounting for these differences can percentages begin to describe anything comparable. For example, a lower unexplained rate may reflect stricter evidence requirements before a case is accepted for detailed investigation, more extensive follow up work or more frequent reclassification of historical cases. Conversely, a higher unexplained rate may arise because an organisation retains unresolved reports without separating weak evidence from genuinely puzzling investigations. Neither figure, by itself, demonstrates that one country experiences more anomalous events than another. The Air Force’s Best UFO Evidence Was Exposed in Their Own Report: Special Report 14 Channel: America's Strangest History Open on YouTube Why Headline Percentages Mislead The temptation to rank databases by their unexplained percentages stems from treating the figures as though they measured a common phenomenon. In reality, they primarily measure institutional choices. Each reporting system reflects its own mission, historical context, staffing, evidence standards and terminology. GEIPAN's unexplained category is the product of a structured investigative methodology designed to distinguish inadequate evidence from genuinely unresolved investigations. Project Blue Book's unknown category emerged from Cold War era Air Force procedures and statistical conventions. Other civilian databases often record public submissions under entirely different rules. As a result, unexplained percentages are best interpreted as characteristics of individual databases rather than as universal indicators of how often truly anomalous aerial phenomena occur. Without harmonised definitions, identical investigative thresholds and comparable case populations, ranking databases by their unresolved rates produces apparent differences that largely reflect methodology rather than underlying reality. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why UFO Unexplained Rates Mislead Comparisons. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The UFO Enigma: A New Review of the Physical Evidence By Peter A. Sturrock \"A comprehensive investigation of encounters with unidentified flying objects, all the more riveting because it is both skeptical and scr... See on Amazon Book Calling Bullshit: The Art of Skepticism in a Data Driven World By Carl T. Bergstrom, Jevin D. West Bullshit"},{"title":"False Patterns","url":"/false-patterns/","description":"Repeated records can manufacture apparent clusters, surges and statistical significance by giving one well-documented event excessive weight.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Duplicates","level":3,"text":"False Patterns Can Duplicate UFO Reports Create Fake Waves? Duplicates False Patterns False Patterns Can Duplicate UFO Reports Create Fake Waves? Repeated records can manufacture apparent clusters, surges and statistical significance by giving one well documented event excessive weight. Repeated records can manufacture apparent clusters, surges and statistical significance by giving one well documented event excessive weight. UFO Report Databases critique risk single case aspect event time window How repeated records distort maps and timelines Why inflated sample sizes weaken confidence tests The French case that skewed a sidereal time pattern ufo report databases duplicates false patterns Introduction Duplicate records can create convincing looking UFO “waves” that are largely statistical artefacts rather than evidence of repeated independent events. When one incident appears multiple times across catalogues, witness submissions, investigation files and later publications, simple counting methods can transform a single aerial event into an apparent cluster. The result is not merely an inflated total. Maps can develop false geographical hotspots, timelines can suggest sudden surges in activity, and statistical tests may report patterns that disappear once records are consolidated into distinct events. For anyone using UFO report databases and catalogues, distinguishing between the number of database entries and the number of underlying incidents is therefore essential before drawing conclusions about temporal or spatial trends. [Geipan] geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN GeipanMission & Geipan GEIPAN... Can Duplicate Reports Create Fake Waves? Yes—but only when the database treats repeated records as independent observations. The key distinction is between a record and an event . A record is a single database entry. An event is the underlying occurrence that generated one or more reports. If a spectacular fireball, rocket launch or widely observed unidentified light is reported by dozens of people and then copied into several catalogues, the database may contain dozens or even hundreds of rows describing what was, physically, one event. This matters because many statistical methods assume that each observation is independent. Once duplicated records are counted as separate observations, the apparent sample size grows without adding genuinely new information. A pattern that appears highly significant may simply reflect repeated documentation of the same incident rather than repeated occurrence of the phenomenon itself. How Repeated Records Distort Maps and Timelines False hotspots arise when duplicated events accumulate in one location or time period. Several mechanisms contribute to this effect: Multiple databases may each preserve the same witness account. Several publications may receive separate catalogue entries despite describing the same incident. Updated investigations can generate additional records without representing new sightings. Mass sightings produce many witness reports that are independent testimonies but not independent aerial events. When analysts plot raw record counts on a map, these duplicated entries increase the apparent density of sightings around the original event. A city that experienced one highly publicised incident can therefore appear unusually active simply because documentation was unusually thorough. The same distortion affects timelines. A famous incident that generated extensive correspondence, follow up interviews and repeated archival references can dominate a monthly or yearly count, creating what appears to be a localised \"wave\" even if the number of distinct aerial events changed very little. The problem becomes especially important in historical catalogues, where later researchers frequently indexed magazine articles, books and investigation files separately for bibliographic purposes rather than as independent observations. 26:47 UFOs Unlocked: Inside the Pentagon’s secret files This Is America Channel: Al Jazeera English · Views: 99.7K · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 26 minutes Open on YouTube Why Inflated Sample Sizes Weaken Confidence Tests Statistical confidence depends on the assumption that observations contribute independent evidence. Duplicate records violate that assumption. Suppose one genuine event appears twenty times because it was reported by many witnesses and indexed repeatedly. A statistical program that counts twenty observations instead of one will generally calculate narrower confidence intervals and smaller probability values than are justified. The mathematics gives the illusion of greater certainty because the dataset appears larger than it really is. This can affect several common forms of UFO analysis: estimates of annual reporting rates; tests for seasonal or hourly cycles; regional clustering analyses; correlations with environmental or human factors; and comparisons between different catalogues. The apparent precision of these analyses depends less on the number of database rows than on the number of genuinely independent events. Event level datasets therefore provide a much stronger foundation than record level datasets whenever the research question concerns patterns in aerial phenomena rather than patterns in reporting behaviour. The French Case That Skewed a Sidereal Time Pattern One of the clearest examples of the importance of deduplication comes from French UFO research. Jean Pierre Rospars examined claims that French UFO reports showed a statistically unusual concentration at particular sidereal times —time measured relative to the stars rather than the Sun. Such a pattern has occasionally been presented as potentially meaningful because sidereal time shifts relative to ordinary clock time throughout the year. Rospars demonstrated that the apparent pattern depended heavily on how reports were counted. Repeated entries relating to the same underlying incidents could overweight particular observation windows, making a weak pattern appear stronger than warranted. When reports were evaluated at the event level rather than allowing duplicated documentation to accumulate, the claimed sidereal time concentration became substantially less persuasive. The broader lesson extended well beyond sidereal time analysis. Rospars distinguished carefully between repeated reports from the same witnesses, multiple witnesses observing one physical event, and genuinely separate incidents. Those distinctions altered the statistical structure of the dataset itself rather than simply correcting clerical errors. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANStatistics GEIPAN GEIPANStatistics GEIPAN Why Genuine Multiple Witnesses Should Not Simply Be Deleted Avoiding false waves does not mean collapsing every similar report into one record. Multiple witnesses observing the same object from different locations can provide valuable information about: viewing geometry ; timing consistency; estimated trajectory; atmospheric conditions; and differences in perception. Those observations remain independent witness testimonies even though they refer to one physical event. The correct solution is therefore not indiscriminate deletion but careful modelling of the data structure. Researchers should preserve every witness account while linking them to a shared event identifier. This allows analyses to answer different questions correctly—for example, distinguishing \"How many people reported something unusual?\" from \"How many unusual aerial events occurred?\" 35:38 UFOs, drones, mystery sightings: What government reports, NASA, and investigators say Channel: WGN News · Views: 3.0K · Uploaded: March 2026 · Length: 35 minutes Open on YouTube The Practical Lesson for UFO Databases Modern statistical work increasingly separates reports , witnesses and events because each represents a different unit of analysis. Databases that explicitly identify related records are less likely to manufacture artificial hotspots or apparent reporting waves. Likewise, organisations that distinguish testimony counts from investigated cases make it easier to compare results across years without confusing improvements in documentation with increases in unexplained phenomena. The existence of apparent clusters in a UFO database is therefore not, by itself, evidence of repeated unexplained activity. Before interpreting peaks on maps or timelines, researchers must establish whether they reflect many independent incidents or simply many records describing the same few events. Without that distinction, impressive looking statistical patterns may owe more to catalogue structure than to the sky itself. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANStatistics GEIPAN GEIPANStatistics GEIPAN 5:09 UFO Sightings: How Scientists are Trying to Capture More Data NOVA PBS Channel: NOVA PBS Official · Views: 25.1K · Uploaded: March 2025 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Can Duplicate UFO Reports"},{"title":"Matching Reports","url":"/matching-reports/","description":"Reliable matching combines time, place, movement, narrative details and source history rather than relying on one identical field.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Duplicates","level":3,"text":"Matching Reports How Can Two UFO Reports Be Matched? Duplicates Matching Reports Matching Reports How Can Two UFO Reports Be Matched? Reliable matching combines time, place, movement, narrative details and source history rather than relying on one identical field. Reliable matching combines time, place, movement, narrative details and source history rather than relying on one identical field. UFO Report Databases implementation bounded topic policy intervention The strongest clues that records describe one event Why exact matching misses real duplicates When human review should override an automated score ufo report databases duplicates matching reports Introduction Matching duplicate UFO reports is less about finding identical records than deciding whether different reports describe the same underlying event. Witnesses often remember approximate times, use different place names, or rewrite their account when reporting to another organisation. At the same time, genuinely independent witnesses may describe the same object from different locations with conflicting details. Because of these variations, experienced analysts combine several clues—time, location, movement, narrative content, source history and known astronomical or aerospace events—rather than relying on any single matching field. This approach reduces inflated case counts while preserving independent witness testimony that may still be valuable for reconstructing what happened. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... How Can Two UFO Reports Be Matched? The strongest matches emerge when several independent characteristics point to the same event rather than one field matching perfectly. Analysts typically treat duplicate detection as a process of accumulating evidence instead of applying a simple yes or no rule. The most useful indicators include: Time proximity. Reports occurring within minutes of one another are stronger candidates than those separated by hours. Small differences are expected because witnesses estimate times or submit reports long after the event. Geographic consistency. Nearby towns, neighbouring counties or locations with a clear line of sight may all refer to the same aerial event. Analysts consider viewing geometry rather than expecting identical coordinates. Object movement. Direction of travel, altitude changes, duration and apparent speed often remain more consistent across reports than descriptions such as \"disc\", \"triangle\" or \"light\". Shared environmental details. Weather conditions, twilight, visible planets, rocket launches, satellite passes or meteor activity can connect otherwise different narratives. Narrative overlap. Similar sequences of events, unusual manoeuvres or distinctive observations can indicate that reports describe the same incident even when wording differs. Source relationships. A report copied into several databases, or later expanded by the same witness, is treated differently from genuinely independent eyewitness accounts. UFOCAT illustrates this philosophy by deliberately linking multiple published sources describing the same sighting while retaining separate bibliographic records. Rather than deleting duplicates, it groups related records around a primary event so researchers can trace the original documentation. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... The Center For UFO Studies Mark Rodeghier Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 3.4K · Uploaded: January 2025 Open on YouTube Why Exact Matching Misses Real Duplicates Simple database rules such as requiring identical timestamps or identical town names perform poorly on eyewitness reports. Several common problems explain why: Witnesses frequently round times to the nearest five or ten minutes. Different databases may record local time, daylight saving time or estimated times differently. Place names may vary between suburbs, nearby villages or county level locations. One witness may describe \"three lights\", while another remembers \"a triangular craft\" even though both observed the same formation. Reports submitted weeks or years later often contain additional details or corrected information. The National UFO Reporting Center ( NUFORC ) has repeatedly noted that reports of the same highly visible event can differ substantially in timing and description. Its reporting guidance also distinguishes between the event date and the submission date, meaning upload dates alone are unsuitable for identifying duplicates. [NUFORC] nuforc.org report a ufo File a UFO Report NUFORCSeptember 13, 2021... Published: September 13, 2021 These inconsistencies explain why most serious catalogues favour \"fuzzy\" matching rather than requiring exact agreement across all fields. How Automated Matching Produces Candidate Pairs Modern databases usually begin with automated screening before any human review. A typical workflow is: 1. Standardise records. Dates, times, place names and coordinates are normalised where possible. 2. Generate candidate matches. Records close in time or geography are grouped so the system avoids comparing every report with every other report. 3. Calculate similarity. Multiple fields contribute to a combined score instead of relying on one characteristic. 4. Rank uncertain cases. High confidence matches may be merged automatically, while borderline cases are flagged for manual inspection. Some modern UFO datasets apply text similarity techniques that compare descriptions after removing standard boilerplate, combining location filters with fuzzy narrative matching to identify reports that likely describe the same event despite different wording. These systems are designed to propose candidates rather than make irreversible decisions on their own. [groundzeroplus.com] nexus.groundzeroplus.com UFOSIN T Explorer — 614,505 UFO sightings, searchable UFOSIN T Explorer — 614,505 UFO sightings, searchable Dark Skies: A Pictorial History of Lost, Forgotten and Obscure UFO Encounters with Michael Schratt Channel: Danbury Public Library Open on YouTube When Human Review Should Override an Automated Score Automated scoring is valuable for finding likely duplicates, but experienced investigators often overturn algorithmic suggestions. Human judgement becomes especially important when: A mass sighting generates many witnesses. Multiple reports may describe one astronomical or aerospace event, yet each witness provides independent evidence about viewing conditions and perception. Witnesses occupy different locations. Similar reports from opposite sides of a city may represent valuable corroboration rather than redundant records. Narratives evolve. Follow up interviews often clarify estimates, directions or object behaviour without creating a genuinely new event. Copied material is suspected. Investigators distinguish between independent testimony and accounts clearly derived from media reports or earlier publications. The objective is therefore not simply to reduce record counts but to preserve the distinction between duplicate records , duplicate sources , duplicate witnesses and duplicate events . Those categories may overlap, but they are not interchangeable, and confusing them can distort later statistical analyses. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... Balancing False Merges Against Missed Matches Every matching policy involves a trade off. An overly aggressive system may merge separate incidents occurring on the same evening, reducing legitimate observations into one record. A conservative system does the opposite, allowing repeated submissions and copied accounts to inflate apparent sighting numbers or create artificial reporting clusters. For that reason, many researchers prefer keeping an explicit confidence level for each proposed match instead of treating duplicate detection as certain. Records can remain linked as probable versions of one event while preserving the original submissions for future re evaluation if new evidence emerges. This approach provides cleaner statistics without losing the historical reporting trail that often proves important during later investigation. [cufos.org] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... Close Encounters with UFOs Channel: Chicago Public Library Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to How Can Two UFO Reports Be Matched?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Data Matching: Concepts and Techniques for Record Linkage, En... By Peter Christen Data matching (also known as record or data linkage, entity resolution, object identification, or field matching) is the task of identify... See on Amazon Book Entity Resolution and Information Quality By John R. Talburt Entity"},{"title":"Record Multiplication","url":"/record-multiplication/","description":"One sighting can multiply across witness submissions, investigator files and later publications without becoming several independent events.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Duplicates","level":3,"text":"Record Multiplication How One UFO Sighting Turns Into Many Records Duplicates Record Multiplication Record Multiplication How One UFO Sighting Turns Into Many Records One sighting can multiply across witness submissions, investigator files and later publications without becoming several independent events. One sighting can multiply across witness submissions, investigator files and later publications without becoming several independent events. UFO Report Databases mechanism single mechanism mechanism Direct submissions to multiple reporting organisations Multiple witnesses versus multiple events How retellings create new catalogue entries ufo report databases duplicates record multiplication Introduction A single UFO sighting can generate many database records without representing many separate events. The multiplication usually occurs because different organisations collect different kinds of information: one database stores individual witness reports, another preserves investigators' case files, while a catalogue may index every published reference to the incident. If researchers count every record as a distinct sighting, the apparent number of UFO events can become artificially inflated. Conversely, merging records too aggressively can erase genuinely independent witness testimony. Understanding the distinction between a record , a witness statement , a source reference and an underlying event is therefore essential when comparing UFO databases or analysing reporting trends. Many well known catalogues explicitly acknowledge that their databases were designed for documentation and retrieval rather than producing simple counts of unique incidents. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... Direct submissions to multiple reporting organisations The most straightforward form of record multiplication occurs when a witness submits the same experience to more than one reporting organisation. A person who observes an unusual aerial phenomenon may independently report it to organisations such as the National UFO Reporting Center ( NUFORC ), the Mutual UFO Network ( MUFON ), a regional investigation group, or a local newspaper. Each organisation creates its own record using its own submission form, internal numbering system and review process. Although all of these records describe the same event, they exist as separate entries because they belong to different collections. The reports may not even appear identical. One form may request precise coordinates and duration, while another asks for object shape or perceived behaviour. Witnesses often remember additional details when completing a later report or after discussing the event with investigators. As a result, automated matching based solely on wording frequently fails even when the reports originate from the same individual. NUFORC also distinguishes the date on which an event occurred from the date on which it was reported. A historical sighting submitted years later therefore enters the database alongside contemporary reports, making submission dates unsuitable for identifying duplicate events on their own. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 2:12 UFO Project Blue Book at National Archives Museum Channel: US National Archives · Views: 352.7K · Uploaded: December 2019 · Length: 2 minutes 12 seconds Open on YouTube Multiple witnesses versus multiple events A more subtle source of multiplication occurs when many people independently observe the same phenomenon. A bright meteor, rocket launch, satellite re entry or other conspicuous aerial event can be visible across hundreds of kilometres. Witnesses in different towns naturally describe what they saw from different viewing angles, at slightly different times and with different interpretations. Each testimony represents an independent observation, yet all relate to one physical occurrence. From a research perspective, these reports answer different questions: If the question is how many people reported unusual objects , each witness report deserves to remain separate. If the question is how many unexplained aerial events occurred , those reports should be grouped under a single event whenever the evidence supports that conclusion. This distinction explains why simple report totals can be misleading. A spectacular but ordinary astronomical or aerospace event may generate dozens or even hundreds of reports without implying dozens or hundreds of separate UFO incidents. Jacques Vallée noted that different cataloguing systems deliberately choose different units of analysis. Some databases treat every report as a separate entry, whereas others create a single record for the underlying sighting regardless of how many witnesses contributed evidence. [Geipan] geipan.fr UNIDENTIFIED AERIAL PHENOMENA GeipanUNIDENTIFIED AERIAL PHENOMENA... How retellings create new catalogue entries Database multiplication also continues long after witnesses stop reporting. As an incident becomes better known, it often acquires a documentary history: the original witness statement; an investigator's field notes; a regional case file; a magazine article; a book chapter; a later historical catalogue. Each document may receive its own catalogue entry because it represents a distinct source that researchers may wish to consult. These are bibliographic records rather than additional sightings. The Center for UFO Studies (CUFOS) explains that UFOCAT was intentionally designed as a reference catalogue rather than a simple event register. A single sighting may therefore appear several times—once for an original investigation, again for a journal article and again for a later book—allowing researchers to trace how information evolved across publications. CUFOS explicitly cautions that straightforward case counts from UFOCAT will overestimate the number of unique incidents unless linked records are identified. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... 9:22 Project Blue Book: America's Obsession with UFOs Channel: Origins OSU · Views: 1.6K · Uploaded: September 2025 · Length: 9 minutes Open on YouTube Why simple deduplication is difficult Removing duplicates is considerably harder than matching identical text. Reports referring to the same event frequently differ in: reported time by several minutes or even hours; location because witnesses observed from different places; object description; estimated distance and altitude; spelling of names and places; level of detail. Historical cases introduce additional complications because later publications sometimes correct dates, revise locations or incorporate information unavailable in earlier accounts. Two records may therefore disagree while still referring to the same occurrence. For this reason, serious database work increasingly relies on combinations of date, approximate location, witness information, narrative similarity and known source relationships instead of exact matching rules. The objective is usually to preserve every original record while linking related records to a common underlying event rather than deleting information outright. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New Ham... What record multiplication means for UFO statistics Record multiplication does not necessarily indicate poor database quality. In many cases it reflects different collection goals. A witness report database aims to preserve firsthand testimony. An investigative archive records how a case was examined. A bibliographic catalogue documents where information about the case has appeared. Each serves a legitimate research purpose, but they count different units. Problems arise only when those units are treated as interchangeable. Adding together entries from multiple databases without accounting for overlap can exaggerate reporting totals, create artificial geographical clusters or make one event appear to be several independent incidents. Equally, collapsing all similar reports into one record can obscure valuable corroboration from independent witnesses . For this reason, researchers generally distinguish between reports , records , sources and events before attempting any statistical analysis. Recognising how one sighting naturally expands into many database entries is a prerequisite for interpreting UFO catalogues accurately rather than mistaking documentation practices for evidence of additional phenomena. Classified UFO documents released Channel: CNN Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to How One UFO Sighting Turns Into Many Records. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book UFOs and Government: A Historical Inquiry By Michael D. Swords Governments around the world have had to deal"},{"title":"UFOCAT Counts","url":"/ufocat-counts/","description":"UFOCAT preserves multiple references to the same sighting, so its total entries cannot be treated as a count of independent cases.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Duplicates","level":3,"text":"UFOCAT Counts Why UFOCAT Has More Records Than Events Duplicates UFOCAT Counts UFOCAT Counts Why UFOCAT Has More Records Than Events UFOCAT preserves multiple references to the same sighting, so its total entries cannot be treated as a count of independent cases. UFOCAT preserves multiple references to the same sighting, so its total entries cannot be treated as a count of independent cases. UFO Report Databases historical comparative single dataset dataset evidence Why UFOCAT catalogues sources as well as sightings Primary records and additional source references What row counts can and cannot reveal ufo report databases duplicates ufocat counts Introduction UFOCAT is often described as one of the largest UFO databases ever assembled, but its total number of rows should not be mistaken for the number of independent UFO events. The catalogue was designed primarily as a bibliographic research tool rather than a statistical count of unique sightings. Its creators deliberately preserved multiple documentary references to the same incident so that researchers could trace the complete history of a case across original reports, later investigations, magazine articles and books. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... This distinction matters because a simple database query can greatly overstate how many separate incidents occurred. A catalogue containing several records for one sighting is performing exactly as intended: it is preserving the documentary record, not claiming that each row represents a different event. Why UFOCAT Catalogues Sources as Well as Sightings Unlike databases that primarily collect witness submissions, UFOCAT was built to answer a different question: where can researchers find every known source relating to a reported UFO event? The Center for UFO Studies (CUFOS) explicitly describes UFOCAT as a catalogue of both published and unpublished UFO reports. A single sighting may therefore appear several times because the database records distinct documentary sources rather than collapsing them into one entry. For example, one incident may generate: an original investigator's case file; a report in a UFO newsletter or magazine; a later discussion in a reference book; an updated investigation published years afterwards. Each source preserves different information. An early witness interview may contain observations later omitted from published summaries, while a subsequent investigation may correct dates, locations or witness identities. Recording every source allows researchers to reconstruct how knowledge about a case evolved over time rather than relying on a single secondary account. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... This approach reflects the catalogue's historical origins. UFOCAT grew out of efforts beginning during the University of Colorado's Condon Committee project in the late 1960s and evolved over decades as thousands of additional documents were incorporated. As new publications appeared, they were often linked to existing sightings instead of replacing earlier references. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... 2:12 UFO Project Blue Book at National Archives Museum Channel: US National Archives · Views: 352.7K · Uploaded: December 2019 · Length: 2 minutes 12 seconds Open on YouTube Primary Records and Additional Source References A key feature of UFOCAT is its distinction between the principal description of an event and supplementary references. According to the UFOCAT codebook, records describing the same event are grouped together. Within each group, the chronologically earliest report—or the most complete available account—is designated as the primary record . Additional entries document other sources discussing the same event. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New Ham... This structure has several advantages: Preservation of provenance. Researchers can identify exactly where each claim originated rather than treating later retellings as independent testimony. Comparison between versions. Differences between contemporary reports and later publications become visible instead of being hidden by data cleaning. Recovery of missing details. One publication may include witness names, sketches or timing information absent from another source. Importantly, these additional records are not necessarily duplicate mistakes. They are intentional bibliographic links that preserve the documentary trail surrounding a sighting. What Row Counts Can and Cannot Reveal Because UFOCAT stores both events and their associated documentation, raw row totals measure the volume of archived material rather than the number of distinct sightings. CUFOS explicitly cautions users that counting every record will overcount UFO cases because multiple sources frequently exist for the same incident. The organisation instead notes that preliminary event counts should be based on designated primary records rather than every catalogue entry. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... Consequently, different numerical summaries answer different questions: QuestionAppropriate unitHow many documentary references exist?Total UFOCAT recordsHow many separate reported incidents exist?Primary event recordsHow extensively was a case investigated?Number and variety of linked source records Confusing these units can distort historical analyses. A heavily investigated sighting may generate dozens of catalogue entries while representing only one reported event. Meanwhile, a little known case may appear only once despite being equally independent. 9:22 Project Blue Book: America's Obsession with UFOs Channel: Origins OSU · Views: 1.6K · Uploaded: September 2025 · Length: 9 minutes Open on YouTube Why This Design Benefits Historical Research Although multiple records complicate statistical analysis, they strengthen historical research. The preservation of multiple references allows investigators to distinguish between original testimony and later interpretation. Researchers can determine whether a frequently repeated claim originated from numerous independent witnesses or from repeated citation of a single early source. This also makes it possible to evaluate the reliability of specific publications. If later books simply reproduce earlier accounts without new evidence, the documentary chain becomes visible instead of creating the illusion of independent corroboration. For historians of UFO research, this provenance is often more valuable than an aggressively deduplicated dataset. Removing secondary references would make simple counting easier, but it would also erase information about how reports circulated through the UFO literature and how narratives changed over time. Interpreting UFOCAT Correctly UFOCAT is best understood as a hybrid resource: part sightings database and part bibliographic catalogue. Its unusually large size reflects decades of collecting documents, not merely decades of accumulating new incidents. When analysing UFOCAT, the essential distinction is between records and events . A record represents one documentary source within the catalogue. An event represents one underlying sighting that may have generated many such sources. Recognising that difference prevents inflated case counts while preserving the catalogue's greatest strength: its detailed map of the documentary history surrounding reported UFO sightings. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... The Center For UFO Studies Mark Rodeghier Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 3.4K · Uploaded: January 2025 Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why UFOCAT Has More Records Than Events. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book UFOs and Government: A Historical Inquiry By Michael D. Swords Governments around the world have had to deal with the UFO phenomenon for a good part of a century. How and why they did so is the subjec... See on Amazon Book Data Matching: Concepts and Techniques for Record Linkage, En... By Peter Christen Data matching (also known as record or data linkage, entity resolution, object identification, or field matching) is the task of identify... See on Amazon Book Archives: Principles and Practices By Laura A. Millar This new and extensively revised second edition offers an international perspective on archives management, providing authoritative guida... See on Amazon Book The UFO Experience: A Scientific Inquiry By Joseph Allen Hynek, Hynek See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: UFOs and Government: A Historical Inquiry Data Matching: Concepts and Techniques"},{"title":"Witness Counting","url":"/witness-counting/","description":"Independent witnesses may strengthen a case while still representing one event for statistical counting and mapping.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Duplicates","level":3,"text":"Witness Counting Do Five UFO Witnesses Mean Five Sightings? Duplicates Witness Counting Witness Counting Do Five UFO Witnesses Mean Five Sightings? Independent witnesses may strengthen a case while still representing one event for statistical counting and mapping. Independent witnesses may strengthen a case while still representing one event for statistical counting and mapping. UFO Report Databases governance bounded topic population context Separating event counts from witness counts When accounts are independent testimony A three level model for events, reports and sources ufo report databases duplicates witness counting Introduction A database that records five witnesses to the same aerial event should not normally count that as five separate sightings. Instead, it should record one underlying event supported by five independent testimonies. This distinction is fundamental to reliable UFO cataloguing because most statistical questions ask how many distinct incidents occurred, whereas most evidential questions ask how many people independently observed the same incident. Confusing these two units inflates event totals, distorts maps and time series, and can make ordinary mass observation events appear to be clusters of unrelated sightings. At the same time, merging witness accounts too aggressively risks losing valuable information about what different observers actually saw. The solution is to separate events, reports and sources while preserving the relationships between them. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 Do Five UFO Witnesses Mean Five Sightings? In most research contexts, the answer is no. Five witnesses who independently observed the same physical occurrence represent one event with five observations, not five separate events. This distinction matters because a single visible phenomenon—a meteor, satellite re entry, rocket launch or genuinely unexplained aerial occurrence—may be seen across a wide area. Each witness contributes evidence from a different viewpoint, but the underlying occurrence remains singular. Counting every witness as a separate sighting would artificially increase the apparent frequency of UFO events. Equally, a database should not discard the additional testimony. Independent accounts can strengthen or weaken confidence in a reconstruction by revealing where descriptions agree, where they differ, and whether those differences are consistent with viewing angle, distance, lighting or memory rather than indicating multiple unrelated events. Separating Event Counts from Witness Counts A well governed catalogue treats event totals and witness totals as different measurements answering different questions. For example: Event count: How many distinct aerial incidents occurred? Witness count: How many people independently observed those incidents? Report count: How many submissions were received? Source count: How many documents describe those submissions? These figures should never be treated as interchangeable. Suppose ten people across neighbouring towns independently report the same rocket launch. A database designed for statistical mapping should normally record: One event. Ten witness reports. Potentially more than ten documentary sources if newspapers, investigators and later catalogues all discuss the incident. Keeping these layers separate allows researchers to study witness behaviour without exaggerating the number of underlying aerial events. This principle is reflected in long running catalogues such as UFOCAT , which explicitly warns that multiple entries may relate to one sighting because its purpose is bibliographic completeness rather than event counting. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... 1:10:06 Data drives disclosure: Creating a civilian UFO reporting network Reality Check Channel: NewsNation · Views: 163.4K · Uploaded: March 2026 · Length: 1 hour 10 minutes Open on YouTube When Accounts Are Truly Independent Testimony Independent testimony is valuable precisely because it is separate evidence about the same occurrence. Witnesses should generally remain distinct when they: submitted their reports independently rather than copying one another; observed the event from different locations or viewing angles; describe overlapping timings that are consistent with one event; provide complementary rather than duplicated information. Keeping testimonies separate allows investigators to compare details such as: estimated direction of travel; apparent altitude; colour and brightness; duration; sound or lack of sound; weather and visibility. Agreement across independently produced accounts can increase confidence that an unusual observation occurred, even if witnesses disagree about secondary details. Conversely, substantial inconsistencies may reveal reporting errors, multiple unrelated objects, or simple differences in perception. The key point is that independence strengthens evidence without requiring multiplication of the event count. A Three Level Model for Events, Reports and Sources Modern catalogue design benefits from treating UFO information as three linked but distinct entities. Level 1: The event This is the presumed physical occurrence, whether ultimately explained or unexplained. Every event receives a unique identifier representing one incident in space and time. Level 2: Individual reports Each witness submission is stored separately and linked to the event. Reports retain their original wording, timestamps, locations and observational details. Multiple reports can therefore belong to a single event without losing witness specific information. 11:49 Data Mining Black Triangle UFOs Channel: Enigmatic Devices · Views: 2.4K · Uploaded: July 2021 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Level 3: Documentary sources The same report may later appear in an investigator's file, newspaper article, archive, website or book. Each source documents the report but does not create a new event. UFOCAT follows a closely related philosophy by preserving multiple references to the same case so researchers can trace every available source while cautioning that simple record totals will overcount incidents. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... 7:09:56 National UFO Reporting Center (1976) Part One Channel: Eclecticopedia · Views: 62.7K · Uploaded: January 2017 · Length: 7 hours 9 minutes Open on YouTube Why Multiple Witnesses Can Improve Evidence Without Inflating Statistics Separating witness counts from event counts offers several advantages. First, it preserves corroboration. Researchers can identify whether an event was observed by one person or hundreds without altering the number of incidents. Second, it improves geographical analysis. A meteor visible across several counties should appear as one widespread event rather than dozens of unrelated sightings. Third, it allows better quality assessment. A case supported by several independently submitted reports may justify closer investigation than an isolated account, even though both contribute one event to statistical analyses. Finally, it avoids false trends. Periods containing widely visible astronomical or aerospace events often generate surges in public reporting. Counting witnesses instead of events would falsely suggest a dramatic increase in unexplained aerial phenomena rather than an increase in observations of the same occurrence. NUFORC 's catalogue structure likewise distinguishes reports from the underlying occurrence and emphasises that reports are reviewed individually rather than assumed to represent unique incidents. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 Practical Governance Rules for Database Curators For event level catalogues, a consistent governance policy should normally follow these principles: Assign one event identifier to a single physical occurrence whenever the available evidence supports that interpretation. Preserve every independent witness account as a separate report linked to that event. Preserve every publication or archival reference as a separate source linked to the relevant report or event. Allow later review if new evidence shows that what appeared to be one event actually comprised multiple unrelated incidents. Record uncertainty explicitly instead of forcing either a split or a merge when the evidence is inconclusive. This approach keeps statistical analyses honest while retaining the evidential value of independent testimony. Rather than choosing between event counting and witness counting, it recognises that both measurements are meaningful—but only when they are clearly distinguished and consistently recorded. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides"},{"title":"Aircraft Cases","url":"/aircraft-cases/","description":"GEIPAN tests whether lighting, perspective and terrain can make an ordinary aircraft appear stationary, saucer-shaped or beam-like.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / GEIPAN","level":3,"text":"Aircraft Cases When an Aircraft Looks Nothing Like an Aircraft GEIPAN Aircraft Cases Aircraft Cases When an Aircraft Looks Nothing Like an Aircraft GEIPAN tests whether lighting, perspective and terrain can make an ordinary aircraft appear stationary, saucer shaped or beam like. GEIPAN tests whether lighting, perspective and terrain can make an ordinary aircraft appear stationary, saucer shaped or beam like. UFO Report Databases mechanism single mechanism mechanism Matching reports to flight and radar records Reconstructing lighting and viewing geometry Explaining the C 130 saucer like sighting ufo report databases geipan aircraft cases Introduction One of GEIPAN 's most distinctive investigative practices is that it does not stop when it identifies a likely aircraft. Instead, it asks a second question: why did an ordinary aircraft appear extraordinary to the witness? This distinction is central to the French government's approach to investigating unidentified aerospace phenomena. Rather than dismissing a report once radar or flight records identify an aeroplane, investigators reconstruct the exact viewing conditions—observer position, aircraft movement, lighting, terrain and human perception—to explain how a conventional object could genuinely appear stationary, saucer shaped, triangular or capable of projecting a beam of light. This reconstruction based method turns a simple identification into a testable explanation and illustrates why many initially dramatic reports ultimately receive an identified or probably identified classification. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMethodology GEIPAN GEIPANMethodology GEIPAN Matching Reports to Flight and Radar Records GEIPAN begins by comparing the witness account with independent operational data rather than relying solely on testimony. Investigators examine: Civil and military flight movements. Air traffic and defence radar records where available. The reported time, location and viewing direction. Aircraft type, altitude and route. Weather and astronomical conditions. Access to defence radar is particularly valuable because it allows investigators to determine whether an aircraft occupied precisely the part of the sky described by witnesses. Rather than asking whether an aircraft was generally nearby, the investigation asks whether a specific aircraft's position and timing match the observation closely enough to account for it. GEIPAN's agreements with French defence authorities allow it to consult radar traces during appropriate investigations, making this reconstruction considerably more rigorous than relying on publicly available flight data alone. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Finding a radar match is treated as the beginning of the explanation rather than its conclusion. GEIPAN explicitly states that merely informing witnesses they observed an aircraft is insufficient; investigators seek to explain every unusual feature that made the object appear anomalous. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube Reconstructing Lighting and Viewing Geometry Many aircraft cases depend less on the aircraft itself than on the geometry between observer, aircraft and environment. GEIPAN reconstructs factors including: The observer's exact position and direction of travel. Aircraft heading relative to the witness. Moon position and illumination. Terrain features such as hills and tree lines. Atmospheric visibility. Relative motion between observer and aircraft. This reconstruction often reveals that several ordinary effects combine into an unexpected visual impression. One recurring mechanism is the pivot effect . When both the observer and the aircraft are moving, the changing line of sight can rotate around a nearly fixed point. To the observer, the aircraft appears almost motionless despite travelling at normal speed. The effect is particularly convincing when viewed from a moving vehicle because the observer naturally judges movement against nearby landscape rather than against distant airspace. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Lighting introduces additional distortions. Depending on the angle of illumination, a transport aircraft's rounded fuselage may remain brightly lit while its flatter wings reflect little light towards the observer. The illuminated fuselage can therefore appear as an isolated glowing disc rather than a recognisable aircraft. Terrain can reinforce the illusion: moonlight reflecting from treetops or mist may resemble a downward cone or beam apparently originating from the object itself. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Rather than treating these effects separately, GEIPAN evaluates how they interact to reproduce the witness's actual description. Explaining the C 130 Saucer like Sighting GEIPAN's investigation of the Conches en Ouche sighting on 23 February 2016 has become one of its clearest demonstrations of reconstruction methodology. Four witnesses observed what they believed was an extraordinary aerial object: Three occupants of a moving car described a stationary flying saucer emitting a cone of light. A fourth witness, watching from a stationary vehicle, reported an enormous slow moving triangular object. Radar records quickly established that a Lockheed C 130 Hercules transport aircraft had passed through the area at the relevant time. The challenge was explaining why none of the witnesses recognised it as an aeroplane. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPAN reconstructed the observation in detail. For the three moving witnesses: The pivot effect made the aircraft appear stationary. Indirect full moon illumination reflected diffusely from the aircraft's rounded fuselage while the wings remained comparatively invisible. Moonlight illuminating the tops of nearby trees produced a bright halo interpreted as a cone of light beneath the object. The combined result closely matched the witnesses' description of a hovering flying saucer. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The fourth witness experienced a different illusion despite observing the same aircraft. Because the observer remained stationary, the pivot effect was absent. Instead, GEIPAN concluded that the aircraft's unusually large size, lighting and unfamiliar viewing angle prevented immediate recognition. Investigators also pointed to a perceptual bias in estimating angular speed: larger aircraft often appear to move more slowly because observers unconsciously judge motion by the time taken for the aircraft's tail to occupy the position previously held by its nose. The same physical aircraft therefore generated two distinctly different UFO reports through differing viewing circumstances. [Geipan] geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Mission & Geipan GEIPAN... Dr. Doug Buettner “Aviation Safety and Recommended Steps to Reduce Space Object Caused UAP Reports” Channel: Scientific Coalition for UAP Studies Open on YouTube 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why Reconstruction Matters More Than Identification Alone This approach illustrates an important principle in GEIPAN's methodology. Witnesses are not assumed to be dishonest or careless. Instead, investigators distinguish between accurately reporting what was perceived and correctly identifying the external cause . An explanation is considered stronger when it accounts simultaneously for: The reported shape. Apparent motion. Brightness. Duration. Direction of travel. Surrounding environmental features. Simply naming an aircraft would leave much of the testimony unexplained. By reconstructing lighting, geometry and perception together, GEIPAN attempts to demonstrate how an ordinary object could genuinely produce an extraordinary visual experience. This emphasis on explaining the perceived strangeness, rather than merely assigning a conventional label, is a defining feature of its investigative practice. [Geipan] geipan.fr Mission & Geipan GEIPAN Mission & Geipan GEIPAN... Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to When an Aircraft Looks Nothing Like an Aircraft. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Invisible Gorilla: How Our Intuitions Deceive Us By Christopher Chabris, Daniel Simons NEW YORK TIMES BESTSELLER • Our minds don’t work the way we think they do. Two renowned psychologists explain how and why our intuitions... See on Amazon Book Human Factors in Aviation By Eduardo Salas, Dan Maurino Fully updated and expanded, the second edition"},{"title":"Reclassification","url":"/reclassification/","description":"Reinvestigations, better tools and stricter standards can move old Class D files into identified or inconclusive categories.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / GEIPAN","level":3,"text":"Reclassification Why GEIPAN's Unexplained Total Keeps Changing Reclassification Reclassification Why GEIPAN's Unexplained Total Keeps Changing Reinvestigations, better tools and stricter standards can move old Class D files into identified or inconclusive categories. Reinvestigations, better tools and stricter standards can move old Class D files into identified or inconclusive categories. UFO Report Databases historical comparative bounded topic event time window How old Class D cases are reopened The 2017 reclassification of 50 cases Why unidentified is a provisional outcome ufo report databases geipan reclassification Introduction GEIPAN’s total number of unexplained cases is not a fixed figure. Unlike many UFO databases that leave historical classifications untouched, France’s official investigation unit periodically revisits older files when new evidence, better analytical methods or improved reference data become available. As a result, the number of cases classified as unexplained ( Class D , now divided into D1 and D2) can fall even if no new reports are received. This approach reflects GEIPAN’s view that an unexplained case represents the best conclusion available at a particular moment rather than a permanent verdict. Official guidance explicitly states that classifications may be revised whenever new information emerges or older investigations are re examined. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPAN Classification GEIPAN... How old Class D cases are reopened GEIPAN treats a Class D classification as provisional rather than final. Older investigations may be reopened for several reasons: previously unavailable witness information is received; improved astronomical, meteorological or aviation records become accessible; advances in image analysis or digital mapping provide new clues; investigators identify overlooked correlations with known phenomena; historical files are systematically reviewed using updated investigative procedures. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPAN Classification GEIPAN... The organisation's current methodology also differs from that used during earlier decades. Modern investigations rely on more detailed witness questionnaires, cognitive interviews, digital reconstruction tools and broader access to external databases. These improvements mean that some cases which could not be satisfactorily explained years ago can now be linked to conventional causes that were previously difficult to verify. [Geipan] geipan.fr baisse cas d Forte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années GEIPANDecember 14, 2016... Published: December 14, 2016 Importantly, reclassification does not imply that earlier investigators made mistakes. In many historical cases, the original enquiry simply lacked evidence that later became available. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube The 2008 classification changes made reviews easier A major step towards more consistent reclassification came with GEIPAN's revised classification procedure introduced in 2008. Instead of relying solely on the traditional A, B, C and D categories, the agency: established explicit criteria for assigning classifications; distinguished between insufficient evidence ( Class C ) and genuinely unexplained observations (Class D); divided Class D into D1 (moderately consistent unexplained cases) and D2 (high consistency unexplained cases). The purpose was to produce a classification that more accurately reflected the quality of the available evidence rather than simply whether investigators had found an explanation. The revised framework also made later reassessment more systematic because investigators could compare old files against clearly defined criteria. [Geipan] geipan.fr evolution classification des cas Evolution de la CLASSIFICATION DES CAS GEIPANDecember 10, 2008... Published: December 10, 2008 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube Why the unexplained percentage fell One of GEIPAN's most notable findings is that the proportion of unexplained cases has declined substantially over time. In a 2016 analysis, GEIPAN explained that this reduction should not be interpreted as evidence that unusual aerial phenomena themselves became rarer. Instead, several institutional changes affected the statistics: the number of investigated reports roughly tripled after the organisation expanded its public reporting system and online presence; many additional reports involved readily identifiable objects such as sky lanterns, digital imaging artefacts or other commonplace phenomena; classification standards became stricter; investigations became more thorough through improved digital tools and investigative methods. Because the overall number of processed cases increased dramatically while the absolute number of unexplained cases decreased, the percentage of Class D cases fell sharply. GEIPAN reported that the average number of unexplained cases declined from about 6.8 per year between 1975 and 2004 to roughly 2.5 per year after 2005. During the most recent decade examined in that report, unexplained cases represented only about 2% of newly processed files, compared with roughly 10% across the earlier historical dataset. [Geipan] geipan.fr baisse cas d Forte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années GEIPANDecember 14, 2016... Published: December 14, 2016 GEIPAN also cautioned that these figures describe changes in investigation practices and reporting patterns as much as any underlying change in unusual observations themselves. The 2017 reclassification of 50 cases The most visible demonstration of GEIPAN's review policy came during 2017, when investigators completed fresh examinations of approximately 50 older Class D cases that had originally remained unexplained. According to GEIPAN, these reinvestigations resulted in previously unexplained files being assigned conventional classifications after additional analysis. The work formed part of a broader effort to create a more uniform catalogue of unexplained cases by removing those that no longer satisfied the criteria for Class D under modern standards. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPAN Classification GEIPAN... The review programme illustrates an important feature of the database: historical statistics are not simply accumulated year after year. Instead, they are periodically refined as older cases are reassessed. Why \"unidentified\" is a provisional outcome A common misunderstanding is that a Class D case represents permanent proof that an event defies explanation. GEIPAN explicitly rejects that interpretation. Official documentation states that D classifications may be revised whenever: new witness testimony is received; additional technical evidence becomes available; experts identify a previously overlooked explanation; historical cases are revisited as part of systematic review programmes. Similarly, the organisation notes that D cases require periodic re evaluation and that reclassification remains possible whenever significant new information appears. [Geipan] geipan.fr Classification GEIPAN Classification GEIPAN... This makes the unexplained category fundamentally different from a closed historical archive. Instead of treating uncertainty as permanent, GEIPAN regards it as the current state of knowledge after investigation. What changing totals mean for readers Because classifications evolve, readers should avoid comparing unexplained case totals from different years without considering how the database itself has changed. Three factors influence the published figures simultaneously: newly investigated reports enter the database; older files may be reassessed and reclassified; classification criteria and investigative methods continue to improve. Consequently, the number of unexplained cases reflects both the observations being reported and GEIPAN's evolving ability to investigate them. Rather than weakening the database, this willingness to revise earlier conclusions demonstrates that its statistics are intended to represent the best available assessment at any given time, not a permanently fixed catalogue of mysteries. [Geipan] geipan.fr baisse cas d Forte baisse des CAS D sur les 10 dernières années GEIPANDecember 14, 2016... Published: December 14, 2016 Jacques Vallée on the Status of a Classified UAP Database The Sol Forum Channel: The Sol Foundation Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why GEIPAN's Unexplained Total Keeps Changing. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The UFO Enigma: A New Review of the Physical Evidence By Peter A. Sturrock \"A comprehensive investigation of encounters with unidentified flying objects, all the more riveting because it is both skeptical and"},{"title":"Class C","url":"/class-c/","description":"Class C means investigators lacked reliable evidence, not that they found a strong mystery that resisted explanation.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / GEIPAN","level":3,"text":"Class C Why Class C Does Not Mean Unexplained GEIPAN Class C Class C Why Class C Does Not Mean Unexplained Class C means investigators lacked reliable evidence, not that they found a strong mystery that resisted explanation. Class C means investigators lacked reliable evidence, not that they found a strong mystery that resisted explanation. UFO Report Databases critique risk single policy policy intervention What evidence makes a case Class C How weak documentation blocks testing Why Class C should be separated from Class D ufo report databases geipan class c Introduction A common misunderstanding in discussions of the French government's UFO investigation programme is that any case GEIPAN cannot identify is automatically an \"unexplained\" mystery. That is not how the organisation classifies reports. A Class C case does not mean investigators found a highly unusual phenomenon that resisted explanation. Instead, it means the available evidence was too limited, incomplete or unreliable to support a meaningful conclusion. By design, GEIPAN separates these poorly documented reports from Class D cases, where investigators conducted a substantive assessment yet still could not reach a satisfactory explanation. This distinction is central to understanding the evidential quality of the GEIPAN database and avoiding the common mistake of treating missing information as evidence of an anomalous event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN What evidence makes a case Class C? GEIPAN defines Class C as a phenomenon \"not identified due to lack of data or information.\" The emphasis is on the quality of the evidence rather than the apparent strangeness of the reported event. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN A report may receive a Class C classification when investigators cannot establish enough reliable facts about the observation. Typical limitations include: an incomplete or vague witness account; uncertain date, time or location; insufficient information about direction, altitude or duration; missing photographs, videos or physical evidence where such material would be important; contradictory or internally inconsistent testimony; inability to reconstruct the viewing conditions because essential details were never recorded. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN In these situations, investigators cannot properly compare the report against ordinary explanations such as aircraft, astronomical objects, atmospheric phenomena or perceptual effects. The classification therefore reflects an evidential limitation rather than a judgement that the phenomenon itself is extraordinary. 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube How weak documentation blocks meaningful testing GEIPAN's methodology is built around two linked concepts: Residual strangeness , meaning how unusual the observation remains after testing possible explanations. Consistency , meaning the quantity and reliability of the available evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN Consistency is not simply the number of witnesses. It also includes factors such as the precision of answers, the independence of witnesses, coherence between different accounts, and the availability of supporting material including photographs or videos. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN If consistency is too low, investigators cannot confidently evaluate competing hypotheses. A witness may sincerely describe an extraordinary looking event, but without enough dependable information there is no sound basis for concluding whether the object was an aircraft, a planet, an atmospheric effect or something genuinely unusual. GEIPAN therefore treats insufficient evidence as a separate outcome rather than forcing a speculative explanation or declaring the case genuinely unexplained. Why Class C should be separated from Class D The distinction between Class C and Class D is one of the most important features of GEIPAN's classification system. Class CClass DEvidence is insufficient for meaningful analysis.Evidence is sufficiently consistent to permit a thorough investigation.No reliable conclusion can be reached because essential information is missing.Investigators have examined available hypotheses but no satisfactory explanation remains.Represents an evidential problem.Represents a residual investigative problem after analysis. GEIPAN explicitly states that stronger claims require stronger evidence. Its methodology follows a simple principle: the more unusual a reported phenomenon appears, the stronger and more consistent the supporting evidence must be before it can legitimately be regarded as unexplained. If that evidential threshold is not met, the observation is classified as Class C rather than Class D. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN This approach reduces the risk of turning poor quality reports into apparent mysteries. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why Class C is not a \"hidden mystery\" category Outside the official classification system, lists of \"unexplained UFOs\" sometimes include every report lacking a conventional identification. GEIPAN deliberately avoids this practice. A Class C classification does not imply: investigators exhausted every possible explanation; the event demonstrated unknown technology; the report was too mysterious to solve; the case is evidence for extraterrestrial activity. Instead, it means investigators lacked enough dependable information to test explanations properly. An unresolved investigation and an uninvestigable report are treated as fundamentally different outcomes. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN This distinction protects the integrity of the database because uncertainty arising from missing evidence is not confused with uncertainty that remains after substantial investigation. Class C cases can change if new evidence appears A Class C classification is not necessarily permanent. GEIPAN states that both Class C and Class D cases may be revisited if new information becomes available after the original investigation. Additional witness statements, higher quality images, newly available radar information or other documentary evidence may allow investigators to reassess the case. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN This means the classification reflects the evidence available at the time rather than an immutable judgement about the underlying event. Why the distinction matters for interpreting the GEIPAN database When analysing UFO report databases , treating every unidentified report as equally significant creates a misleading picture. GEIPAN's methodology instead separates two different kinds of uncertainty: Insufficient evidence , where reliable testing is impossible (Class C). Residual unexplained observations , where investigation has been possible but no satisfactory explanation remains (Class D). [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN That separation helps researchers, journalists and readers avoid overstating the number of genuinely anomalous cases. It also explains why approximately one third of published GEIPAN cases fall into Class C while only a small minority are classified as Class D: many reports simply do not contain enough dependable information to support a defensible conclusion, and GEIPAN's methodology explicitly recognises that \"we do not know because the evidence is inadequate\" is a different—and often more scientifically honest—answer than \"we investigated thoroughly and still cannot explain it.\" [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN Jacques Vallée on the Status of a Classified UAP Database The Sol Forum Channel: The Sol Foundation Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Class C Does Not Mean Unexplained. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book UFO: The Inside Story of the US Government's Search for Alien... By Garrett M. Graff \"The full story of our national obsession with UFOs and the covert, decades long search by scientists, the United States military, and t... See on Amazon Book The UFO Experience: A Scientific Inquiry By Joseph Allen Hynek The author belives that a number"},{"title":"Class D","url":"/class-d/","description":"A sighting reaches Class D only when its residual strangeness is high and the supporting information is judged sufficiently consistent.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / GEIPAN","level":3,"text":"Class D What Makes a GEIPAN Case Truly Class D Class D Class D What Makes a GEIPAN Case Truly Class D A sighting reaches Class D only when its residual strangeness is high and the supporting information is judged sufficiently consistent. A sighting reaches Class D only when its residual strangeness is high and the supporting information is judged sufficiently consistent. UFO Report Databases governance single policy policy intervention Residual strangeness after testing explanations Consistency of witnesses and records The distinction between D1 and D2 ufo report databases geipan class d Introduction A GEIPAN Class D classification is not simply a label for an unexplained UFO report. It is the outcome of a structured investigative process in which conventional explanations have been systematically tested and found insufficient, while the remaining evidence is judged reliable enough to justify leaving the case officially unidentified . This makes Class D a deliberately narrow category: most reports are instead identified (Classes A and B) or judged impossible to assess because of inadequate information ( Class C ). Only a small proportion of published GEIPAN investigations remain in Class D after review, reflecting the organisation's emphasis on exhausting ordinary explanations before concluding that a case is genuinely unresolved. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN Residual strangeness after testing explanations The central requirement for Class D is what GEIPAN calls residual strangeness . Rather than asking whether a witness sincerely believes they saw something unusual, investigators ask a different question: how much of the reported phenomenon remains unexplained after all plausible known causes have been examined? [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN Since 2008, GEIPAN has formalised this process through a methodology based on two quantitative concepts: Residual strangeness (E): the degree to which the observation remains inconsistent with the best available conventional explanation after investigation. Consistency (C): the quality and reliability of the available evidence supporting the report. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN Residual strangeness is not measured by how spectacular a sighting appears. Instead, investigators generate competing explanatory hypotheses—including astronomical objects, aircraft, atmospheric phenomena, perception effects and psychological factors—and assess which explanation best accounts for the observation. The stronger the best conventional explanation, the lower the residual strangeness. Only when no known explanation appears sufficiently probable does the case reach the threshold required for consideration as Class D. GEIPAN describes this as residual strangeness exceeding the point at which \"no hypothesis seems appropriate.\" [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN This approach is designed to distinguish between \"currently unexplained\" and \"never explainable.\" A Class D designation records that investigators did not find an adequate explanation with the available evidence; it does not constitute evidence for extraterrestrial technology or any specific extraordinary hypothesis. GEIPAN explicitly states that unexplained cases should not automatically be interpreted as alien spacecraft. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube Consistency of witnesses and records High residual strangeness alone is insufficient for Class D. GEIPAN also requires that the supporting information be internally consistent and sufficiently robust. Consistency combines both the quantity and quality of available information. Investigators consider factors including: the number of independent witnesses ; the precision of witness statements; agreement between different accounts; photographs, videos or other physical records where available; the overall reliability and coherence of the testimony; whether witnesses may have influenced one another. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN Importantly, consistency does not mean witnesses must agree perfectly. Instead, investigators assess whether differences are compatible with normal observation from different locations or perspectives while looking for contradictions that undermine reliability. This requirement explains why some apparently remarkable reports never become Class D. A dramatic account supported only by vague memories, uncertain timing or poor documentation is more likely to be classified as Class C , meaning there is insufficient information for a reliable conclusion. GEIPAN therefore separates absence of evidence from evidence of an unresolved phenomenon . [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN Why strong evidence matters more as strangeness increases GEIPAN deliberately links residual strangeness and consistency rather than treating them as separate checklists. Its methodology follows a straightforward principle: the more unusual the remaining phenomenon appears, the stronger the supporting evidence must be before investigators accept that it is genuinely unexplained. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN This balance serves several purposes: It reduces the chance that poor quality observations are mistaken for unexplained phenomena. It discourages assigning Class D simply because investigators cannot reconstruct an event from limited information. It makes the unidentified category more conservative and therefore more credible. The consequence is that many reports with intriguing features never qualify for Class D because the available evidence is not considered reliable enough to sustain an unexplained conclusion. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube The distinction between D1 and D2 Since the 2008 revision of its methodology, GEIPAN has divided Class D into two subcategories that reflect different degrees of residual strangeness while maintaining the requirement for strong consistency. D1: Strange but limited in residual strangeness A D1 case remains unexplained after investigation and possesses sufficient consistency, but the level of residual strangeness is relatively moderate. There may still be several conceivable explanations, although none fits the evidence well enough to justify classification as an identified case. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN D2: Very high residual strangeness A D2 case combines highly reliable documentation with a particularly high level of unexplained residual strangeness. These represent the most unusual files in GEIPAN's catalogue, not because investigators endorse extraordinary interpretations, but because conventional explanations appear especially inadequate given the available evidence. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN The distinction therefore concerns how much unexplained content remains after investigation , rather than public interest or media attention. Class D is not a permanent verdict GEIPAN does not regard Class D as immutable. Its methodology explicitly allows for cases to be re examined whenever new evidence becomes available. Additional witness statements, improved image analysis, newly available aviation or astronomical records, or advances in investigative methods may all justify reclassification . D cases are therefore subject to periodic review. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN The organisation has also reported revisiting historical unexplained cases, with some ultimately being transferred into explained categories after new information emerged. This reflects GEIPAN's policy that an unexplained classification should persist only while the evidence genuinely supports it. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN Why only a small fraction become Class D The rarity of Class D follows directly from GEIPAN's methodology rather than from the number of unusual reports it receives. Most investigations end in one of three ways: a convincing explanation is identified (Class A or B); the available information proves too incomplete for a reliable conclusion (Class C); only a small remainder satisfy"},{"title":"Case Statistics","url":"/case-statistics/","description":"GEIPAN's percentages describe classified reports and evidence quality, not the frequency of unexplained objects in French airspace.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / GEIPAN","level":3,"text":"Case Statistics What GEIPAN's UFO Percentages Really Tell US Case Statistics Case Statistics What GEIPAN's UFO Percentages Really Tell US GEIPAN's percentages describe classified reports and evidence quality, not the frequency of unexplained objects in French airspace. GEIPAN's percentages describe classified reports and evidence quality, not the frequency of unexplained objects in French airspace. UFO Report Databases evidence single dataset dataset evidence The balance of Classes A, B, C and D Why report statistics are not prevalence estimates How methods and evidence quality shape the totals ufo report databases geipan case statistics Introduction GEIPAN 's published percentages are often misunderstood as measuring how many unidentified objects are flying over France. They do not. Instead, they describe the outcomes of investigations into the subset of reports that have been completed, classified and published in GEIPAN's archive. The figures therefore measure the distribution of investigation results—not the prevalence of unexplained aerial phenomena in French airspace. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANStatistics GEIPANJune 25, 2026... Published: June 25, 2026 This distinction matters because the database combines cases with very different evidential value. Some reports are conclusively identified, others are judged probably identified, many cannot be assessed because too little reliable information exists, and only a small minority remain unexplained after investigation. Understanding what these categories represent is essential before drawing conclusions from GEIPAN's statistics. Jacques Vallée on the Status of a Classified UAP Database The Sol Forum Channel: The Sol Foundation Open on YouTube The Balance of Classes A, B, C and D GEIPAN's public statistics are calculated from classified cases published on its website. As of 25 June 2026, the archive contained 3,368 published classified cases distributed as follows: Class A (28.0%) – the phenomenon was identified with a high degree of confidence. Class B (38.8%) – the investigators concluded that a known explanation is the most probable. Class C (30.1%) – the case could not be resolved because the available information was insufficient or unreliable. Class D (3.1%) – the phenomenon remained unidentified after investigation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANStatistics GEIPANJune 25, 2026... Published: June 25, 2026 These percentages therefore describe how investigated reports ended , not what witnesses originally believed they had seen. A witness may report a spectacular \"UFO\", yet the investigation may ultimately classify it as A or B after identifying an aircraft, astronomical object or another conventional explanation. Conversely, a report may remain in Class C not because it is mysterious, but because investigators lacked enough dependable evidence to reach any conclusion. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN Another important point is that GEIPAN's published figures are dynamic statistics . Since 2016, they have been calculated from the set of classified cases currently published online. Files still under investigation are excluded until classification, and older cases can be reclassified if new evidence emerges. As a result, the percentages are not fixed historical constants but can change over time. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANStatistics GEIPANJune 25, 2026... Published: June 25, 2026 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube Why Report Statistics Are Not Prevalence Estimates A common mistake is to interpret the approximately 3% of Class D cases as meaning that roughly 3% of all unusual aerial events in France are genuinely unexplained. GEIPAN does not make that claim. Several reasons explain why such an interpretation is incorrect: The database contains reported events, not every aerial phenomenon. Many observations are never reported, while others are screened out before becoming published investigation files. The denominator is published classified cases. The percentages do not refer to all public contacts, all sightings or all events in French airspace. Cases differ enormously in evidential quality. A detailed multi witness investigation and a brief report with little supporting information each contribute one classified case. Classification reflects investigative outcome rather than objective rarity. The figures measure how often investigators reached each type of conclusion under the available evidence. [cnes geipan.fr] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANStatistics GEIPANJune 25, 2026... Published: June 25, 2026 The large proportion of Class C cases illustrates this particularly well. These reports are not counted as \"explained\", but neither are they counted as genuinely unexplained phenomena. They occupy a separate category precisely because the evidence was inadequate for either conclusion. Treating Class C as evidence for unknown objects would substantially misrepresent what GEIPAN's classifications are intended to convey. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN How Methods and Evidence Quality Shape the Totals GEIPAN's published statistics are inseparable from its investigative methodology. Classification depends not only on whether investigators can find a plausible explanation but also on the consistency and quality of the available evidence. Since 2008, GEIPAN has based classifications on two principal factors: Residual strangeness —how poorly known explanations account for the reported observation after investigation. Consistency —the quantity and reliability of the available evidence, including witness statements, documentation, photographs and corroborating information. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN GEIPANMission & Geipan GEIPAN This means that a report can remain unresolved for two very different reasons: because there is too little reliable evidence (Class C); or because the evidence is considered sufficiently strong but no satisfactory conventional explanation has been established (Class D). [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN The methodology deliberately prevents these situations from being merged into a single \"unknown\" category. That separation makes the published statistics more informative, because they distinguish investigative uncertainty from residual unexplained cases . GEIPAN also periodically revisits older files. Improvements in digital tools, accumulated investigative experience and newly available information have led to the reclassification of some historical Class D cases into explained categories. Consequently, the proportion of unexplained cases reflects not only incoming reports but also continuing review of the archive. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube What the Published Percentages Can—and Cannot—Support Properly interpreted, GEIPAN's statistics provide insight into the performance and outcomes of an investigative process rather than evidence about the frequency of extraordinary phenomena. The figures can legitimately support conclusions such as: most published investigated cases receive an identified or probably identified explanation; a substantial fraction of reports remain inconclusive because evidence is insufficient; only a small proportion remain unidentified after investigation; classifications are provisional in the sense that they can change if new evidence emerges. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANStatistics GEIPANJune 25, 2026... Published: June 25, 2026 By contrast, the statistics cannot demonstrate: how many unexplained objects actually occur in French skies; the probability that any new sighting represents an unknown phenomenon; evidence for extraterrestrial technology or any specific hypothesis regarding Class D cases. GEIPAN itself emphasises that an unexplained classification is an investigative outcome, not proof of an exotic cause. A Class D case simply means that, given the available evidence and current knowledge, investigators did not reach a satisfactory conventional explanation. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANClassification GEIPAN GEIPANClassification GEIPAN Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to What GEIPAN's UFO Percentages Really Tell US. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Calling Bullshit: The Art of Skepticism in a Data Driven World By Carl T. Bergstrom, Jevin D. West Bullshit isn’t what it used to be. Now, two science professors give us the tools to dismantle misinformation and think clearly in a world... See on Amazon Book How to Lie with Statistics By Darrell Huff 'A great"},{"title":"Go Fast Parallax","url":"/go-fast-parallax/","description":"Reconstructing viewing geometry showed that the GoFast object's apparent speed was partly an effect of motion parallax.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Unidentified","level":3,"text":"Go Fast Parallax Did Go Fast Really Move as Fast as It Looked? Unidentified Go Fast Parallax Go Fast Parallax Did Go Fast Really Move as Fast as It Looked? Reconstructing viewing geometry showed that the GoFast object's apparent speed was partly an effect of motion parallax. Reconstructing viewing geometry showed that the GoFast object's apparent speed was partly an effect of motion parallax. UFO Report Databases mechanism single case aspect event time window What the original footage appeared to show How altitude and viewing geometry changed the estimate Why apparent speed is not measured speed ufo report databases unidentified go fast parallax Introduction The US Navy's GoFast infrared video became one of the best known modern UFO recordings because it appeared to show an object racing just above the ocean at extraordinary speed. Subsequent geometric reconstruction, however, showed that much of this apparent velocity was an illusion created by motion parallax —the visual effect that occurs when a fast moving observer views an object against a distant background. Rather than demonstrating extreme performance, the footage illustrates how aircraft speed, camera zoom and viewing angle can dramatically distort intuitive impressions of motion. This case has become an important example within UFO investigations because it demonstrates why an unidentified object should not be assumed to possess extraordinary capabilities simply because it appears to move rapidly on video. [aaro.mil] aaro.mil AARO FAQ... What the original footage appeared to show When the video was first released, many viewers interpreted it as showing a small object skimming across the sea at remarkable speed while being tracked by the targeting system of a US Navy F/A 18F Super Hornet. Several features encouraged that interpretation: The object appeared to streak across the water. The infrared targeting display remained locked onto the object. The ocean surface seemed to rush beneath it, reinforcing the impression of low altitude flight. The aircrew's reaction added to the perception that they were observing something unusual. None of these visual cues, however, directly measured the object's speed. The video alone records only the changing line of sight between the aircraft and the target. Determining true motion requires reconstructing the geometry of the encounter using aircraft position, sensor orientation and range information rather than relying on visual appearance. [Metabunk] metabunk.org NASA panel analyzes GO FAST Metabunk NASA panel analyzes GO FAST Metabunk... 51:24 Breaking Down UAP Footage with the Head of The Pentagon’s UAP Taskforce, Dr. Jon Kosloski Channel: StarTalk · Views: 1.5M · Uploaded: August 2025 · Length: 51 minutes Open on YouTube How altitude and viewing geometry changed the estimate The key development came when researchers reconstructed the encounter using information displayed on the targeting system itself, including aircraft altitude, viewing angle and target range. Instead of placing the object close to the ocean surface, the reconstruction indicated that it was much higher—roughly 13,000 feet (about 4 km) above sea level. At that altitude, the apparent motion across the water changes dramatically because the background ocean lies much farther behind the object than viewers intuitively assume. [Metabunk] metabunk.org NASA panel analyzes GO FAST Metabunk NASA panel analyzes GO FAST Metabunk... As the fighter travelled at several hundred miles per hour, its own movement caused the viewing angle to change continuously. Against the distant ocean, even a slow moving object could appear to race sideways through the camera's field of view. The consequence was significant: the aircraft's own motion generated much of the apparent speed; the object's projected movement across the background was amplified by perspective; the video did not by itself demonstrate extreme acceleration or extraordinary velocity. Independent analyses reached similar conclusions that the observed motion could be explained without invoking high performance flight. NASA's independent UAP study discussed the geometry behind the GoFast recording, while later Pentagon analysis likewise concluded that the apparent speed was largely a parallax effect. [metabunk.org] metabunk.org NASA panel analyzes GO FAST Metabunk NASA panel analyzes GO FAST Metabunk... 24:46 Breakdown of the Pentagon UFO videos with Mick West Channel: Cool Worlds · Views: 365.2K · Uploaded: April 2020 · Length: 24 minutes Open on YouTube What motion parallax means in this case Motion parallax is a familiar visual phenomenon. When travelling in a moving vehicle, nearby objects such as roadside signs appear to sweep past rapidly, while distant mountains seem almost stationary. The difference arises because the observer—not necessarily the objects—is moving. The GoFast video applies the same principle under more complex conditions: the observing aircraft was flying at high speed; the infrared camera used a narrow field of view; the object lay between the aircraft and the distant ocean background; slight changes in viewing angle translated into large apparent sideways motion on the display. Because the camera compresses depth into a two dimensional image, viewers cannot accurately judge distance or true velocity from the footage alone. AARO notes that this effect becomes especially pronounced when fast moving observers view relatively nearby objects through narrow fields of view. [AARO] aaro.mil AARO FAQ... Why apparent speed is not measured speed One of the central lessons from GoFast is the distinction between apparent motion and physical motion . A video camera records angular movement across its field of view, not an object's actual speed through three dimensional space. Converting angular motion into true velocity requires reliable information about: distance to the object; aircraft position and speed; sensor pointing angle; observation geometry over time. Without those variables, impressive looking motion can easily be misinterpreted. Analyses based on the available telemetry suggested that the object's true speed could have been only a few tens of miles per hour—consistent with movement at prevailing wind speeds rather than extraordinary propulsion. Although uncertainty remains about the object's exact identity, the geometry showed that exceptional speed was not required to explain the video. [Metabunk] metabunk.org NASA panel analyzes GO FAST Metabunk NASA panel analyzes GO FAST Metabunk... What changed in the official interpretation The evolution of the GoFast case illustrates an important distinction between identifying an object and explaining its observed behaviour. For several years, the object remained officially unidentified because the available evidence did not establish exactly what it was. However, later analysis by the US Department of Defense's All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) concluded that the video did not demonstrate anomalous flight performance. According to AARO, detailed geospatial reconstruction showed with high confidence that the object was substantially higher than it appeared, and that motion parallax accounted for its seemingly exceptional speed. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... This distinction is significant. An object may remain unattributed while the behaviour originally thought to be extraordinary is explained through ordinary geometry and optics. Why GoFast remains an important case The GoFast video has become one of the clearest demonstrations that striking imagery alone cannot establish unusual flight characteristics. Its importance lies less in determining exactly what the object was than in showing how careful reconstruction can overturn first impressions. The case reinforces several broader principles used in modern UFO and UAP investigations: sensor footage must be interpreted alongside metadata rather than by appearance alone; perspective effects can create convincing illusions of extreme speed; an unresolved identity is not evidence of extraordinary performance; viewing geometry should be analysed before drawing conclusions about anomalous technology. [aaro.mil] aaro.mil AARO FAQ... 10:07 The Truth Behind Famous UFO Sightings Channel: StarTalk Plus · Views: 25.9K · Uploaded: October 2025 · Length: 10 minutes Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Did Go Fast Really Move as Fast as It Looked?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The UFO Book By Jerome Clark The UFO Book includes a historical overview of UFO phenomenon, an overview of its terminology roots, and in depth information regarding... See on Amazon Book UFOs By Leslie Kean NEW YORK TIMES BESTSELLER • Impeccably researched, this riveting journalistic investigation separates fact from fiction, and documents th... See on Amazon Book The UFO Handbook By Allan Hendry An explanation of the usual"},{"title":"Starlink Pilots","url":"/starlink-pilots/","description":"Five commercial pilots reported unusual Pacific lights later matched to newly launched Starlink satellites under rare viewing conditions.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Unidentified","level":3,"text":"Starlink Pilots How Starlink Fooled Five Experienced Airline Pilots Unidentified Starlink Pilots Starlink Pilots How Starlink Fooled Five Experienced Airline Pilots Five commercial pilots reported unusual Pacific lights later matched to newly launched Starlink satellites under rare viewing conditions. Five commercial pilots reported unusual Pacific lights later matched to newly launched Starlink satellites under rare viewing conditions. UFO Report Databases evidence single case event time window What the pilots reported over the Pacific How launch timing and illumination matched the sightings Why expert witnesses can still meet unfamiliar sky displays ufo report databases unidentified starlink pilots Introduction The August 2022 Pacific airline sightings provide one of the clearest modern examples of why an observation can remain unidentified to experienced witnesses without implying anything extraterrestrial. Five commercial pilots flying on two separate trans Pacific flights independently reported unusual bright lights displaying unfamiliar movement and changing brightness. Some crew members photographed and filmed the phenomenon, and the reports were initially treated as possible unidentified aerial phenomena (UAP). Subsequent reconstruction using aircraft flight tracks, satellite orbital data and launch information showed that the lights closely matched a newly launched train of SpaceX Starlink satellites seen under an unusual illumination geometry. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... For UFO report databases and catalogues, this incident is valuable because it demonstrates that reliable witnesses can accurately describe what they observed while still lacking the information needed to identify its cause. The event illustrates the difference between a genuine observation and a correct interpretation of that observation. What the pilots reported over the Pacific The incident occurred on 10 August 2022, when two commercial aircraft operating over the Pacific Ocean encountered a series of bright lights unlike the aircraft, stars and satellites the crews normally expected to see. Across the two flights, five pilots independently described unusual luminous objects, and two mobile phone photographs together with a short video were recorded from one cockpit. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... Several aspects made the sightings unusual from the pilots' perspective: The lights appeared exceptionally bright. Their brightness changed dramatically over short periods. Multiple lights appeared together. The apparent motion did not resemble the familiar steady track of a conventional satellite. The event occurred in a part of the Pacific where there are relatively few visual reference points at night. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... Importantly, none of these observations necessarily indicated extraordinary technology. They described exactly what the witnesses saw, but not what caused it. 2:07 Train of Satellites in the Sky over California Looks like UFOs SpaceX Starlink Train 2023 Channel: Attractions 360° · Views: 50.2K · Uploaded: July 2023 · Length: 2 minutes 7 seconds Open on YouTube How launch timing and illumination matched the sightings The decisive evidence came from reconstructing the event rather than relying on witness impressions alone. Researchers combined several independent datasets: Automatic Dependent Surveillance–Broadcast (ADS B) records showing the precise positions of the aircraft. Two Line Element (TLE) orbital data describing the locations of satellites. The launch schedule for the relevant Starlink mission. Astronomical modelling of the Sun's position relative to both aircraft and satellites. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... The analysis found that a Starlink satellite train had been launched earlier the same day. At the time of the sightings, the satellites occupied precisely the region of sky reported by the crews. More importantly, the geometry between the Sun, the satellites and the aircraft produced intense specular reflections—mirror like flashes of sunlight directed toward the cockpits. Instead of appearing as faint moving points, the satellites brightened dramatically, faded, and brightened again as successive satellites reflected sunlight toward the observers. To pilots unfamiliar with this recently recognised optical effect, the changing sequence could appear as independent lights manoeuvring in unexpected ways. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... The photographs and video were likewise found to be consistent with this reconstruction rather than with unknown airborne objects. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... Why experienced pilots could still be fooled The incident has sometimes been misrepresented as evidence that pilots cannot be trusted as observers. The research points to the opposite conclusion. Commercial pilots are trained to notice unusual traffic, atmospheric conditions and hazards. Their reports in this case were internally consistent and mutually corroborating across two independent aircraft. What they lacked was access to detailed space situational awareness—specifically, the positions and illumination characteristics of thousands of rapidly increasing low Earth orbit satellites. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... Several factors contributed to the misidentification: Novel appearance. Recently launched Starlink satellites can produce lighting effects unlike traditional satellites. Changing reflections. Specular flashes make stationary orbital motion appear irregular as brightness rapidly varies. Limited operational information. Pilots generally do not receive real time predictions of visible satellite flares. Human perception. At night, against a featureless sky, changing brightness can be interpreted as changes in speed or direction. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... The case therefore illustrates an important principle in UFO investigations: accurate observation does not guarantee accurate identification. Passage of Starlink Satellites Aug 28, 2023 Channel: AmazingSky Open on YouTube Why this case became influential The Pacific event has become an important case study because it moved beyond speculation and tested competing explanations against measurable data. Rather than dismissing the reports or accepting them as anomalous, researchers attempted to reproduce exactly what the pilots would have seen from their cockpit windows. The close agreement between witness reports, photographs, orbital calculations and launch timing substantially strengthened the conventional explanation. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... The authors argued that improving space situational awareness could reduce similar incidents by providing pilots and investigators with predictive information about visible satellite trains and extreme reflection events. They suggested that governments and satellite operators could make visibility forecasts more accessible, allowing unusual lights to be recognised more quickly during flight operations. [arXiv] arxiv.org Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk: A Single Case Study in the Misidentification of a Recently Launched Starlink... 6:06 Curious Pilot: N661LF Oregon UAP / UFO sighting explained, December 7, 2024 Channel: Philip Giacalone · Views: 2.1K · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube What this case shows about unidentified reports Within UFO report databases, this incident demonstrates why an \"unidentified\" report should be treated as a temporary investigative status rather than evidence for an extraordinary explanation. At the moment the pilots made their reports, the lights were genuinely unidentified from their perspective. Only after combining independent orbital data, launch records and aircraft tracking could investigators identify the source with confidence. The witnesses were not fabricating their observations, nor were investigators forced to choose between believing the"},{"title":"Unresolved Totals","url":"/unresolved-totals/","description":"An unresolved percentage mixes strong resistant cases with reports that simply lack enough information for confident identification.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Unidentified","level":3,"text":"Unresolved Totals What UFO Unresolved Percentages Actually Tell You Unidentified Unresolved Totals Unresolved Totals What UFO Unresolved Percentages Actually Tell You An unresolved percentage mixes strong resistant cases with reports that simply lack enough information for confident identification. An unresolved percentage mixes strong resistant cases with reports that simply lack enough information for confident identification. UFO Report Databases historical comparative single dataset dataset evidence How classification rules shape the final percentage Why weak and strong cases are often counted together What to check before comparing different catalogues ufo report databases unidentified unresolved totals Introduction A headline claiming that “20% of UFO reports remain unexplained” or that “thousands of cases are still unresolved” sounds dramatic, but the percentage alone tells you very little. Within most UFO and unidentified anomalous phenomenon (UAP) databases, an unresolved case is an administrative outcome rather than a measure of how extraordinary the underlying event was. The same unresolved category can include well documented observations that resisted extensive investigation alongside reports that simply lacked enough information for any confident conclusion. [Geipan] geipan.fr Statistics GEIPANJune 25, 2026... Published: June 25, 2026 This distinction is essential when reading UFO report catalogues. Different organisations apply different classification rules, investigate to different standards and decide to leave cases open for different reasons. As a result, unresolved percentages are poor measures of either mystery or evidence unless the reader first understands how those percentages were created. How classification rules shape the final percentage An unresolved percentage is not an objective property of the reports themselves. It is the product of a catalogue's definitions, investigative methods and closure criteria. Some databases distinguish between reports that cannot be identified because the evidence is inadequate and reports that remain unexplained despite substantial investigation. Others combine these situations into a single residual category. Two catalogues may therefore report similar unresolved percentages while describing very different collections of cases. France's GEIPAN illustrates why this matters. Rather than treating all unidentified reports equally, it separates: Category C: insufficient information to identify the phenomenon. Category D: unidentified after investigation because available evidence did not support a satisfactory explanation. Its current published statistics show that roughly 30% of classified cases fall into Category C (lack of data), while only about 3% fall into Category D (unidentified after investigation). Looking only at a combined \"unidentified\" total would conceal this major difference in evidential quality. [Geipan] geipan.fr Statistics GEIPANJune 25, 2026... Published: June 25, 2026 Modern US practice demonstrates a similar issue from another direction. The All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) publishes unresolved cases that range from footage judged insufficient for determination to observations involving ordinary looking physical objects whose exact identity cannot yet be established. The unresolved label therefore reflects the current state of attribution rather than a judgement that the object displayed extraordinary characteristics. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... 37:21 Why Congress Is Seriously Investigating UFOs Channel: Astrum · Views: 1.2M · Uploaded: July 2025 · Length: 37 minutes Open on YouTube Why weak and strong cases are often counted together The biggest weakness of headline unresolved percentages is that they flatten fundamentally different kinds of investigations into a single number. An unresolved case might remain open because: the witness reported too little information; photographs or video were too poor for analysis; important sensor data were unavailable; investigators lacked sufficient contextual information; multiple conventional explanations remained plausible; the case genuinely resisted the explanations examined despite relatively good evidence. These situations have very different evidential value, yet they are frequently counted together. For example, AARO's public case library includes unresolved videos where analysts explicitly state that the available imagery is insufficient to reach a determination. Other unresolved entries describe physical objects with unremarkable behaviour that simply cannot yet be attributed to a specific aircraft, balloon or other source. Still others remain open pending additional information. These all contribute to unresolved totals despite representing different investigative situations. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... Consequently, an unresolved percentage does not reveal how many reports are \"high quality mysteries\". It often reflects a mixture of incomplete reports, technically unattributable observations and a much smaller number of genuinely resistant investigations. Why comparisons between catalogues often fail Readers frequently compare unresolved percentages across different databases as though they measured the same phenomenon. In practice, they rarely do. Several factors make direct comparison unreliable: Different intake standards. Some catalogues accept virtually every public submission, while others screen reports before formal investigation. Different evidential thresholds. One organisation may require only a probable explanation to close a case, whereas another may demand stronger confirmation. Different investigative resources. Access to radar, classified sensors, meteorological records or aviation databases varies widely. Different closure policies. Some agencies revisit old cases when new evidence emerges; others rarely reopen files. These differences mean that an organisation reporting a lower unresolved percentage is not necessarily better at solving cases. It may simply use broader identification criteria or classify uncertain reports differently. Likewise, a higher unresolved percentage does not automatically indicate more anomalous events. It may reflect stricter evidential standards, limited investigative resources or a deliberate decision to separate \"insufficient information\" from probable identifications. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube A single percentage can change without the sky changing Unresolved percentages are also sensitive to administrative decisions rather than changes in the underlying phenomenon. Several factors can alter the published proportion: revised classification systems; reopening historical cases; improved image analysis techniques; access to new radar or satellite data; changes in reporting volume following media attention; publication timing before investigations are complete. GEIPAN explicitly notes that classifications can change when new information becomes available, and its published statistics are dynamically updated as investigations progress. [Geipan] geipan.fr Statistics GEIPANJune 25, 2026... Published: June 25, 2026 Similarly, AARO's portfolio evolves as cases receive additional analysis. Reports may move from unresolved to resolved, while new reports enter the database before complete investigation. A published percentage therefore represents a snapshot of an evolving catalogue rather than a permanent measure of unexplained phenomena. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... What to check before comparing different catalogues Before drawing conclusions from unresolved percentages, readers should examine the catalogue itself rather than the headline number. Useful questions include: Does the database separate insufficient information cases from investigated unidentified cases? What evidence is required before investigators close a report? Are unresolved cases periodically reviewed? Does the catalogue contain raw public submissions or investigated files? Are percentages calculated from all reports or only completed investigations? Have the classification rules changed over time? These questions often explain differences between catalogues more effectively than the percentages themselves. UFO Hearing LIVE Pentagon Stumped by 21 Sightings, 700+ New Cases US News UFO News Channel: Hindustan Times Open on YouTube The more useful question than \"How many remain unresolved?\" Within UFO report databases , the unresolved percentage is best understood as a measure of classification outcomes, not as a measure of extraordinary phenomena. The most informative catalogues distinguish between cases that remain unidentified because evidence is weak and those that remain unidentified despite substantial investigation. Without that distinction, a single unresolved percentage combines"},{"title":"Missing Metadata","url":"/missing-metadata/","description":"A striking UFO video may remain unresolved when distance, camera settings, platform motion or original files are missing.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Unidentified","level":3,"text":"Missing Metadata Why UFO Videos Stay Unidentified Without Metadata Missing Metadata Missing Metadata Why UFO Videos Stay Unidentified Without Metadata A striking UFO video may remain unresolved when distance, camera settings, platform motion or original files are missing. A striking UFO video may remain unresolved when distance, camera settings, platform motion or original files are missing. UFO Report Databases critique risk bounded topic dataset evidence Which missing details prevent reliable analysis How compression and platform motion distort interpretation What a useful original video record should preserve ufo report databases unidentified missing metadata Introduction A video can remain officially unidentified without providing evidence of an extraordinary object. In many UFO and unidentified anomalous phenomenon (UAP) investigations, the limiting factor is not what appears on screen but what is missing behind it. Without the original file, reliable timestamps, camera settings, platform position, lens information or sensor telemetry, investigators often cannot measure an object's true distance, size, speed or motion. The result is an unresolved case rather than a confirmed anomaly. This distinction is important when reading UFO report databases and catalogues. An impressive looking clip may genuinely resist identification because the technical information needed to test ordinary explanations has been lost, stripped away or never recorded. Missing metadata therefore explains why some videos remain unresolved while also preventing responsible investigators from treating them as evidence of extraordinary technology. Which Missing Details Prevent Reliable Analysis? A video frame records only part of an event. To determine what was actually observed, investigators need contextual information that is often absent from publicly shared clips. The most valuable metadata typically includes: Exact date and time , allowing comparison with satellite passes, aircraft movements, astronomical objects and weather records . Location and viewing direction , including camera position, altitude and compass heading. Camera and lens information , such as focal length, zoom level, sensor size and field of view. Exposure settings , including shutter speed, frame rate, gain and infrared or visible light mode. Platform motion , especially for footage recorded from aircraft, drones or moving vehicles. Original file integrity , including embedded metadata, compression history and chain of custody. Each missing element removes constraints that would otherwise eliminate conventional explanations. For example, apparent speed depends heavily on knowing both the camera's own motion and the object's distance. A small nearby object crossing the field of view can appear to move dramatically faster than a distant aircraft. Without accurate range information, calculating true velocity becomes impossible. Similarly, apparent acceleration can result from changes in zoom, camera tracking or stabilisation rather than from the object's movement. Without records of lens adjustments or sensor commands, analysts cannot distinguish between target motion and camera motion with confidence. Government investigators frequently encounter exactly this problem. The US All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) has published videos that remain unresolved while simultaneously noting that the available imagery does not permit a definitive identification. In several released cases, the office distinguishes between an unresolved identity and evidence of anomalous behaviour, illustrating that insufficient contextual information often limits the investigation rather than demonstrating extraordinary performance. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... How Compression and Platform Motion Distort Interpretation Many UFO videos circulate only after being uploaded to social media, re encoded multiple times or extracted from edited compilations. These processes remove information that forensic analysts rely upon. 8:14 Are they aliens? Channel: ReasonTV · Views: 20.7K · Uploaded: October 2022 · Length: 8 minutes Open on YouTube Compression Changes What Viewers See Modern video codecs discard visual information to reduce file size. While this usually has little effect on ordinary viewing, compression can create misleading artefacts around small, high contrast or infrared targets. These effects include: blurred edges; artificial trails; shimmering outlines; block like distortions; apparent shape changes between frames. AARO has publicly documented one example in which an infrared object initially appeared to exhibit an unusual atmospheric wake. After examining the full motion video, additional imagery recorded with a longer focal length and commercial flight data, investigators concluded that the object was probably a conventional aircraft and that the apparent wake resulted from video compression artefacts rather than a physical phenomenon. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... Video forensics research similarly shows that repeated encoding, editing and transcoding alter both image content and embedded metadata, making later reconstruction of the original recording conditions substantially more difficult. [arXiv] arxiv.org arXiv Forensic Analysis of Video Files Using Metadata Forensic Analysis of Video Files Using MetadataMay 13, 2021... Published: May 13, 2021 12:52 UFO Videos Explained: Mick West's Expert Analysis Channel: TWiT Tech Podcast Network · Views: 2.5K · Uploaded: February 2025 · Length: 12 minutes Open on YouTube Camera Motion Can Mimic Object Motion Aircraft mounted sensors introduce another layer of complexity. A stabilised infrared camera constantly pans, tilts and zooms to keep a target centred. If telemetry describing those movements is unavailable, viewers may incorrectly attribute the camera's own motion to the object. This can produce misleading impressions of: sudden acceleration; sharp turns; hovering; rapid changes in altitude; impossible speed. Parallax further complicates interpretation. A nearby balloon, bird or drone may appear to race across the landscape when viewed from a fast moving aircraft, while a distant aircraft can appear almost stationary. Determining which interpretation is correct requires accurate knowledge of both the observing platform's motion and the target geometry—information rarely preserved in short internet clips. Why Original Files Matter More Than Viral Clips Investigators consistently place greater evidential value on original recordings than on copies shared online. An original video may preserve: embedded timestamps; camera model and firmware; encoding history; frame timing; GPS information (where available); sensor specific metadata; [scribd.com] scribd.com Source details in endnotes. uninterrupted frame sequences. These details allow analysts to verify whether a file has been edited, identify its recording device and reconstruct the circumstances of capture. Multimedia forensics increasingly relies on metadata within video containers alongside image analysis to detect editing history and establish provenance. [arXiv] arxiv.org arXiv Forensic Analysis of Video Files Using Metadata Forensic Analysis of Video Files Using MetadataMay 13, 2021... Published: May 13, 2021 By contrast, social media uploads commonly: strip embedded metadata; reduce resolution; lower frame rate; recompress the video; crop the original field of view; remove audio; shorten the recording. Each transformation removes information that could otherwise support or eliminate conventional explanations. What a Useful Original Video Record Should Preserve From an investigative perspective, a valuable UFO video is not simply the sharpest image. It is the recording that preserves enough context for independent analysis. Useful evidence typically includes: the complete, unedited original recording rather than a clipped highlight; continuous footage before and after the observed event; original file metadata without re encoding; accurate recording time and location; information about the recording platform, whether handheld, tripod mounted, aircraft or drone; witness notes describing direction of observation, weather conditions and duration; corroborating material such as radar data, ADS B aircraft records or additional witness recordings where available. Digital evidence standards developed for forensic work similarly emphasise preserving original recordings together with associated metadata because this substantially improves later analysis and reduces ambiguity introduced during file conversion. [NIST] nist.gov digital video exchange standards Digital Video Exchange Standards NISTNovember 7, 2016... Published: November 7, 2016 Pentagon UFO Footage Looks Impossible. Here’s the Catch Channel: SciQuest Open on YouTube Why Metadata Gaps Keep Cases in the \"Unidentified\" Category An unidentified classification often reflects the limits of the available"},{"title":"Review Limits","url":"/review-limits/","description":"NUFORC categories and quality tiers add useful context, but they do not turn witness estimates into uniformly investigated conclusions.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / NUFORC","level":3,"text":"Review Limits Does a Detailed UFO Report Count as Verified? NUFORC Review Limits Review Limits Does a Detailed UFO Report Count as Verified? NUFORC categories and quality tiers add useful context, but they do not turn witness estimates into uniformly investigated conclusions. NUFORC categories and quality tiers add useful context, but they do not turn witness estimates into uniformly investigated conclusions. UFO Report Databases governance single policy policy intervention Witness claims versus editorial classifications Limits of estimated size speed and distance What independent verification would require ufo report databases nuforc review limits Introduction A detailed entry in the National UFO Reporting Center ( NUFORC ) database should not automatically be treated as a verified event. The database is designed primarily to preserve witness reports, organise them into a searchable form, and apply editorial review so that readers have more context about the submission. Those editorial labels can help distinguish between reports that appear more or less worthy of further attention, but they do not convert witness observations into independently established facts. [NUFORC] nuforc.org about us About Us NUFORCAugust 17, 2021... Published: August 17, 2021 This distinction is essential for anyone using NUFORC as a research source. Review categories can indicate that staff considered a report internally consistent, unusual or potentially credible, yet most reports are not accompanied by the kind of forensic investigation needed to verify estimated distances, speeds, sizes or extraordinary manoeuvres. Understanding exactly what the review process does—and does not—establish helps prevent readers from overstating the evidential value of individual cases. Witness Claims Versus Editorial Classifications NUFORC reviews incoming submissions before deciding whether to publish them. The organisation states that reports are analysed, obvious hoaxes may be rejected, and staff may contact witnesses for additional information where appropriate. Before submitting a report, witnesses are also encouraged to rule out common sources of misidentification such as Starlink satellite trains, rocket launches and well known camera artefacts. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Report Confirmation NUFORC Report Confirmation NUFORC... That review serves an editorial purpose rather than acting as a scientific certification. A published report generally indicates that: a submission was received and retained; it passed whatever editorial screening NUFORC applied; the narrative was considered suitable for inclusion in the public database; and in some cases, reviewers regarded the witness or circumstances as deserving additional attention. It does not necessarily establish that: the object existed exactly as described; the witness correctly identified its position or behaviour; extraordinary performance characteristics actually occurred; or alternative explanations have been conclusively eliminated. Recent versions of the NUFORC website describe a structured quality review system that assigns reports to review tiers intended to distinguish stronger and weaker cases. According to the published description, these tiers reflect the reviewers' assessment of the report's evidential interest rather than proof that the reported object behaved as claimed. Reports predating the introduction of the system generally were not originally graded under those criteria. [WikiDisc] wikidisc.org Wiki Disc National UFO Reporting Center (NUFORC) Wiki Disc National UFO Reporting Center (NUFORC) WikiDiscJanuary 10, 2026... Published: January 10, 2026 This is an important distinction in governance terms: editorial classifications communicate how NUFORC wishes readers to interpret a submission, not whether an external investigation has confirmed the underlying event. 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube Why Estimated Size, Speed and Distance Remain Uncertain Many NUFORC reports contain detailed numerical estimates. Witnesses often provide an estimated altitude, closest approach, apparent size, speed, duration or direction of travel. These fields make reports more useful for comparison, but they remain observational estimates unless independently measured. Several practical problems limit their reliability. First, humans have difficulty judging distance to lights or poorly defined objects in the night sky. Without knowing an object's actual distance, apparent size cannot be converted into physical size. Second, estimates of speed depend on both distance and viewing angle. An object that appears to move rapidly across the sky could be relatively slow but nearby, or very fast but distant. A witness rarely has enough information to distinguish between those possibilities. Third, eyewitness perception is affected by darkness, atmospheric conditions, expectation, and the absence of familiar reference points. Even experienced observers can misjudge angular motion or apparent acceleration. For these reasons, a report describing an object \"travelling at thousands of miles per hour\" or \"the size of a football field\" should be understood as recording what the witness perceived rather than a measured physical quantity. NUFORC preserves these estimates because they form part of the witness account, not because the organisation has independently verified them. [NUFORC] nuforc.org about us About Us NUFORCAugust 17, 2021... Published: August 17, 2021 What Independent Verification Would Require Moving from a credible witness report to a verified event requires evidence beyond the witness narrative itself. Depending on the circumstances, meaningful independent verification could include: multiple independent witnesses whose accounts agree without prior coordination; radar or air traffic control records matching the reported time and location; satellite or astronomical data excluding conventional celestial explanations; weather observations ruling out atmospheric phenomena; authenticated photographs or video with preserved metadata; flight records eliminating known aircraft; physical traces that can be documented and independently examined; and repeatable corroboration from official records or instrumented observations. The more of these independent sources agree, the stronger the evidential case becomes. Conversely, a report relying solely on one observer's recollection—even if highly detailed—cannot usually establish the physical characteristics of the reported object with confidence. Historically, NUFORC has investigated selected cases in greater depth, including witness interviews and contacts with outside parties, but such investigations represent only a small fraction of the overall database. The organisation itself processes a very large number of reports, making comprehensive forensic investigation impractical for every submission. [NUFORC] nuforc.org National UFO Reporting Center Report a UFO Report a UAP National UFO Reporting Center Report a UFO Report a UAP... 5:21 Washington man spent last 25 years running National UFO Reporting Center Channel: KREM 2 News · Views: 6.3K · Uploaded: February 2019 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube How Researchers Should Interpret Review Labels For research purposes, NUFORC review labels are best understood as indicators of editorial judgement rather than evidence grades equivalent to those used in scientific or legal investigations. A review label can reasonably support conclusions such as: the report was screened before publication; reviewers considered it more or less noteworthy than other submissions; obvious spam or prank material may have been filtered out; and the case may deserve further investigation. It should not be treated as demonstrating that: the witness's estimates are accurate; extraordinary flight characteristics have been confirmed; conventional explanations have been exhaustively excluded; or the event has been independently verified by external authorities. This distinction matters when analysing patterns across the NUFORC database. Researchers can legitimately compare reporting trends, report characteristics and editorial categories, but they should avoid treating every detailed or highly rated report as an established observation of an objectively measured phenomenon. Does a Detailed UFO Report Count as Verified? The answer is generally no. Detail and review status improve the usefulness of a report, but they do not by themselves establish that the reported object's properties are factual. A lengthy narrative with carefully completed fields provides more information for later analysis than a brief anonymous submission. Likewise, an editorial classification signalling reviewer interest offers useful context about how NUFORC assessed the report. Neither substitutes for independent corroboration. The most defensible interpretation is therefore a"},{"title":"Date Gaps","url":"/date-gaps/","description":"A UFO report's event date and posting date can tell very different stories about when sightings and reporting surges occurred.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / NUFORC","level":3,"text":"Date Gaps When Did the UFO Sighting Really Enter the Record? NUFORC Date Gaps Date Gaps When Did the UFO Sighting Really Enter the Record? A UFO report's event date and posting date can tell very different stories about when sightings and reporting surges occurred. A UFO report's event date and posting date can tell very different stories about when sightings and reporting surges occurred. UFO Report Databases historical comparative bounded topic dataset evidence Why event and submission dates diverge How backlogs create artificial spikes Best practices for building report timelines ufo report databases nuforc date gaps Introduction When analysing the National UFO Reporting Center ( NUFORC ) database, one of the most important methodological decisions is whether to use the event date or the submission (or posting) date . Although both appear to describe the same report, they answer different questions. An event date estimates when the witness says the sighting occurred. A submission or posting date records when the report entered NUFORC's system and became available to researchers. Because NUFORC accepts both recent sightings and recollections from many years earlier, the gap between these dates can range from hours to decades. Researchers who overlook this distinction risk mistaking reporting behaviour or database processing patterns for changes in the underlying frequency of reported sightings. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 Why event and submission dates diverge The two dates diverge for several reasons, many of which have little to do with the reported aerial event itself. First, witnesses often delay reporting. Some submit an account immediately after an experience, while others wait weeks, months or even decades before deciding to contact NUFORC. Personal uncertainty, fear of ridicule, renewed media interest, retirement, or discovering NUFORC long after the event can all encourage retrospective reporting. As a result, an event from the 1980s may first appear in the public database in the 2020s. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org 722 New UFO Reports Posted NUFORC 722 New UFO Reports Posted NUFORC Secondly, submission and publication are not identical. After reports are received, NUFORC reviews and processes them before adding them to the public database. The organisation explicitly notes that reports are posted periodically rather than continuously, meaning the public posting date reflects editorial workflow as well as witness behaviour. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 Finally, historical reporting practices have changed over time. Before widespread internet access, many reports arrived by telephone or post. Modern online reporting has reduced delays for many contemporary sightings, but it has also made it easier for people to record memories from much earlier periods, producing a database that mixes contemporary observations with historical recollections. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube How backlogs create artificial spikes One of the most common analytical mistakes is interpreting a sudden increase in posted reports as evidence that more sightings occurred during that same period. In practice, large publication batches often combine recent sightings with historical accounts. For example, NUFORC's July 2023 update announced 722 newly posted reports , explaining that 489 described contemporary sightings while the remainder concerned events from earlier years that witnesses had only recently reported . The posting date therefore represented a publication event rather than a single surge of sightings. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org 722 New UFO Reports Posted NUFORC 722 New UFO Reports Posted NUFORC Similarly, later updates have distinguished between reports from the current reporting period and older sightings submitted retrospectively. A December 2023 update stated that most newly published reports described sightings from that year, while others related to previous years. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org 422 new ufo reports Posted NUFORCDecember 9, 2023... Published: December 9, 2023 Regular batch publication also creates visible clusters in the posting date index. A day showing hundreds of newly available reports may simply indicate that staff completed processing accumulated submissions rather than that hundreds of UFO sightings occurred on that day. NUFORC's public database even maintains separate indexes by event date and date posted , recognising that the two timelines serve different analytical purposes. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org NUFOR C Reports by Date Posted NUFORC Reports by Date PostedAugust 20, 2023... Published: August 20, 2023 What each timeline actually measures Choosing the correct date depends entirely on the research question. If the research asks...Preferred dateWhen did witnesses report that they observed something unusual?Event dateWhen did reports enter the public record?Submission or posting dateWhen did public interest or reporting activity increase?Submission or posting dateDid a news event coincide with an increase in reports?Compare both timelines This distinction becomes especially important during periods of heightened public attention. Media coverage, congressional hearings, military incidents or highly visible satellite launches may encourage people both to report new observations and to submit memories of older experiences. A spike in submission dates therefore reflects changing reporting behaviour as much as any change in reported observations themselves. 12:52 UFO Videos Explained: Mick West's Expert Analysis Channel: TWiT Tech Podcast Network · Views: 2.5K · Uploaded: February 2025 · Length: 12 minutes Open on YouTube Memory and retrospective reporting Older reports remain valuable, but they should not automatically be treated as equivalent to accounts recorded immediately after an event. Memory research consistently shows that recollections can change over time. Dates become approximate, durations may be misremembered, and later experiences or cultural narratives can influence how an event is described. This does not mean retrospective reports are necessarily inaccurate or fabricated; rather, they represent a different type of evidence with different strengths and limitations. For historians, delayed reports may preserve experiences that would otherwise have been lost entirely. For statistical analyses seeking to reconstruct temporal patterns, however, the increasing uncertainty associated with long reporting delays should be recognised and, where possible, measured. Best practices for building report timelines Researchers using NUFORC or derivative datasets can reduce analytical errors by following several straightforward practices. Separate occurrence from publication. Never assume the posting date indicates when the sighting happened. Retain both fields during data cleaning. Removing one date discards information about reporting behaviour. Measure reporting lag. Calculating the interval between event and submission can reveal periods of delayed reporting or changes in witness behaviour. Identify publication batches. Large groups of reports posted together should not automatically be interpreted as real world surges in sightings. Document the chosen timeline. Any published analysis should clearly state whether charts are based on event dates, submission dates or posting dates. Perform sensitivity checks. Comparing results under both dating methods helps determine whether observed trends are genuine or artefacts of reporting delays. These practices are particularly important when combining NUFORC with other UFO catalogues, since different organisations may record submission, investigation or publication dates differently. 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube Why the distinction matters for interpreting UFO databases The difference between an event date and a submission date is not a minor technical detail but a defining characteristic of UFO reporting databases. Event dates provide the closest available estimate of when witnesses say something occurred, while submission and posting dates document when those experiences became part of the historical record. Understanding both timelines allows researchers to distinguish between changes in reported observations, changes in public willingness to report, and changes in database processing. Without that distinction, apparent peaks and trends may reflect administrative workflows or delayed reporting rather than meaningful changes in the pattern of reported UFO sightings."},{"title":"Hotspot Bias","url":"/hotspot-bias/","description":"Clusters of NUFORC reports may arise from population, dark skies and viewing opportunity as much as from unusual aerial events.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / NUFORC","level":3,"text":"Hotspot Bias Do UFO Hotspots Show Objects or Better Viewing? NUFORC Hotspot Bias Hotspot Bias Do UFO Hotspots Show Objects or Better Viewing? Clusters of NUFORC reports may arise from population, dark skies and viewing opportunity as much as from unusual aerial events. Clusters of NUFORC reports may arise from population, dark skies and viewing opportunity as much as from unusual aerial events. UFO Report Databases mechanism single mechanism mechanism Population and exposure behind raw counts Tree cover and night sky brightness How to interpret a sighting hotspot map ufo report databases nuforc hotspot bias Introduction Maps of UFO reports can create the impression that certain places are centres of unusual aerial activity. However, one of the most important limitations of the National UFO Reporting Center ( NUFORC ) database is that apparent \"hotspots\" may reflect where people have the best opportunity to observe the sky rather than where unusual objects are actually more common. Population size, local geography, weather, darkness and outdoor viewing habits all influence how many reports are submitted. For that reason, researchers increasingly treat sighting clusters as evidence of where observations are most likely to occur, not direct evidence that unexplained phenomena are geographically concentrated. Understanding this distinction is essential when interpreting NUFORC maps or comparing one region with another. [NUFORC] nuforc.org University of Utah and Recent AARO Director Publish Study of NUFORC Data NUFORCDecember 15, 2023... Published: December 15, 2023 Population and exposure behind raw counts The simplest explanation for many UFO hotspots is exposure. A sighting requires someone to be outside, looking at the sky, noticing something unusual and deciding to report it. Areas with more potential observers naturally produce more reports than sparsely populated regions, even if the underlying rate of unusual events were identical. This is why raw report totals can be misleading. A city with one million residents offers far more opportunities for observations than a rural county with 20,000 people. Likewise, tourist destinations, recreational areas and places where people spend more time outdoors at night may accumulate more reports simply because more eyes are directed upwards. NUFORC itself notes that its interactive world map broadly follows population distribution, particularly in countries where the organisation is well known. That pattern is consistent with reporting opportunity rather than direct measurement of aerial phenomena. [NUFORC] nuforc.org New! UFO Sightings Map NUFORCDecember 18, 2023... Published: December 18, 2023 Researchers therefore often prefer measures such as reports per capita or statistical models that adjust for population before identifying unusually active locations. Even then, population alone cannot explain every cluster, because the ability to see the night sky varies dramatically between places. [NUFORC] nuforc.org University of Utah and Recent AARO Director Publish Study of NUFORC Data NUFORCDecember 15, 2023... Published: December 15, 2023 5:21 Washington man spent last 25 years running National UFO Reporting Center Channel: KREM 2 News · Views: 6.3K · Uploaded: February 2019 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube Tree cover and night sky brightness Visibility conditions differ enormously across landscapes. Artificial light pollution reduces contrast between faint objects and the background sky. In brightly lit urban areas, many stars disappear from view and subtle atmospheric or astronomical phenomena become much harder to detect. By contrast, darker rural skies reveal far more celestial objects and make moving lights easier to notice. Global studies estimate that the overwhelming majority of people in North America and Europe live beneath light polluted skies, illustrating how uneven observing conditions have become. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness PubMed Central (PMC)The new world atlas of artificial night sky brightness PMCJune 10, 2016... Published: June 10, 2016 Physical geography matters as well. Open deserts, plains, coastlines and high elevation locations often provide wide, unobstructed horizons. Dense forests, mountainous valleys or heavily built urban environments can block large portions of the sky, reducing opportunities to notice distant objects. Weather adds another layer. Persistent cloud cover, haze and humidity shorten the number of nights suitable for observation, while regions with frequent clear skies offer many more viewing opportunities over the course of a year. The 2023 environmental analysis of nearly 100,000 NUFORC reports found that variables including cloud cover and light pollution help explain geographic differences in reported sightings, alongside cultural and demographic influences. [NUFORC] nuforc.org University of Utah and Recent AARO Director Publish Study of NUFORC Data NUFORCDecember 15, 2023... Published: December 15, 2023 Importantly, these factors affect the probability of observation rather than the presence or absence of whatever produced a sighting. Why dark sky locations can become apparent hotspots Some locations gain reputations as UFO hotspots partly because they combine several favourable viewing conditions: relatively dark skies with limited artificial lighting; broad, unobstructed horizons; frequent clear weather; regular visitors who spend time outdoors at night; established local interest that encourages reporting. A remote desert, for example, may generate a disproportionately high number of reports not because more unusual objects appear there, but because observers can detect faint lights that would be invisible from a brightly illuminated city. This mechanism is familiar in astronomy. Professional observatories are deliberately built in dark, dry locations because those environments maximise visibility. The same environmental characteristics can increase the likelihood that members of the public notice satellites, meteors, aircraft lights or genuinely unidentified aerial events. [Nature] nature.com A systematic light pollution modelling bias in present night sky brightness predictions Nature AstronomyMarch 20, 2023... Published: March 20, 2023 UAP & Orb Investigations Doug Buettner Channel: Tim Ventura Open on YouTube How to interpret a sighting hotspot map Hotspot maps are valuable, but they answer a narrower question than many readers assume. A cluster of reports demonstrates that many observations were recorded from a particular area. It does not, by itself, establish why those observations occurred there. When interpreting a hotspot, it is useful to ask several questions: Is the area densely populated? Does it have unusually dark skies? Are there wide, unobstructed views of the horizon? How many clear nights occur annually? Are people especially likely to spend time outdoors after dark? Is there a local culture of reporting unusual aerial sightings? Only after considering these factors can researchers evaluate whether a cluster appears unusual relative to its viewing conditions. Recent spatial analyses of NUFORC data similarly emphasise that hotspots should be treated as starting points for investigation rather than proof of concentrated unexplained activity. Statistical clustering identifies where reports occur together, but additional environmental, demographic and observational context is needed before drawing broader conclusions. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Spatial Hotspots in NUFORC Reports – An Analysis NUFORC Spatial Hotspots in NUFORC Reports – An Analysis NUFORC... The key limitation for NUFORC research Within the broader limitations of NUFORC as a report database, sky visibility represents an important observational bias. The database records where people report seeing unusual objects, not where those objects objectively occur. Consequently, apparent hotspots may emerge because some places provide better opportunities to notice the night sky. Population density, darkness, weather, landscape and reporting behaviour all shape the final map. Researchers who compare regions without accounting for these factors risk confusing differences in observation conditions with differences in the underlying phenomenon itself. I analyzed 80000 UFO sightings from the NUFORC database Channel: The Hidden Truth Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Do UFO Hotspots Show Objects or Better Viewing?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book How to Lie with Maps By Mark Monmonier An updated edition of the \"humorous, informative and perceptive\" guide to how maps can lead us astray ( Toronto Globe and Mail). An insta... See on Amazon Book The Visual Display of Quantitative Information By Edward R. Tufte See on Amazon Book NightWatch: A Practical Guide to Viewing the Universe By Terence"},{"title":"Self Selection","url":"/self-selection/","description":"NUFORC totals reflect who chooses to report, not a random sample of everyone who sees something unusual in the sky.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / NUFORC","level":3,"text":"Self Selection Who Reports UFO Sightings and Who Stays Silent? NUFORC Self Selection Self Selection Who Reports UFO Sightings and Who Stays Silent? NUFORC totals reflect who chooses to report, not a random sample of everyone who sees something unusual in the sky. NUFORC totals reflect who chooses to report, not a random sample of everyone who sees something unusual in the sky. UFO Report Databases critique risk single mechanism population context How witnesses enter the database Why reporters differ from non reporters Claims that self selected data cannot support ufo report databases nuforc self selection Introduction Counts of reports in the National UFO Reporting Center ( NUFORC ) database do not measure how many people have seen something unusual in the sky. They measure how many people chose to submit a report to that particular organisation. That distinction is fundamental. Every entry depends on a chain of personal decisions: the witness must notice the event, decide it is unusual, know that NUFORC exists, feel motivated to report it, and complete the submission process. Anyone who stops at any stage remains invisible to the database. As a result, NUFORC is best understood as a record of self selected reports , not a random sample of all unidentified aerial experiences. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 This self selection does not make the database unhelpful. It does mean that researchers must interpret report totals carefully. Apparent increases, regional differences or changes over time may reflect shifts in reporting behaviour as much as changes in what people actually observed. How witnesses enter the database Unlike official reporting systems that may receive mandatory notifications from professionals, NUFORC relies almost entirely on voluntary participation. Witnesses decide for themselves whether to contact the organisation through its online reporting form, and the report is then reviewed before possible publication. NUFORC itself emphasises that reports are submitted voluntarily, may be published after review, and should not be treated as independently verified accounts. [NUFORC] nuforc.org File a UFO Report NUFORC File a UFO Report NUFORC... Each published report therefore represents several selection steps: Someone noticed an event. They judged it unusual enough to merit reporting. They knew about NUFORC or were directed there. [nuforc.org] nuforc.org about us about us They were willing to describe the experience publicly or semi publicly. Their submission passed NUFORC's processing before appearing in the public database. At every stage, potential witnesses are filtered out. Many people who see unfamiliar lights never report them. Others decide the event probably had an ordinary explanation. Some tell friends but never contact a reporting organisation. Others may report to police, aviation authorities or social media instead of NUFORC. The public database therefore contains only the subset of experiences that successfully passed through this voluntary reporting pathway. [NUFORC] nuforc.org Data Bank NUFORC Data Bank NUFORC Latest UFO SightingsSeptember 6, 2021... Published: September 6, 2021 51:24 Breaking Down UAP Footage with the Head of The Pentagon’s UAP Taskforce, Dr. Jon Kosloski Channel: StarTalk · Views: 1.5M · Uploaded: August 2025 · Length: 51 minutes Open on YouTube Why reporters differ from non reporters Self selection matters because people who submit reports are unlikely to be representative of everyone who witnessed the same phenomenon. Awareness is one obvious factor. A witness cannot report to NUFORC without knowing it exists. Publicity surrounding UFO topics, media coverage, documentaries and online communities can increase awareness independently of any change in unusual aerial events. Motivation also varies considerably. Someone who finds an observation emotionally significant or puzzling may invest time writing a detailed report, whereas another witness to the same event may simply move on. People who already have an interest in astronomy, aviation or unidentified aerial phenomena may also be more inclined to document what they saw. Privacy concerns create another filter. Reporting involves sharing personal information and a written narrative. Some witnesses avoid reporting because they fear embarrassment, scepticism from others or unwanted attention. These concerns are especially relevant for experiences that people believe may be socially stigmatised. Practical barriers also matter. Internet access, language, digital literacy and available time all influence participation. Consequently, the probability of entering the database varies across individuals even when they witness similar events. This is a classic example of self selection bias, where participation depends on characteristics of the participants rather than random sampling. [Nature] nature.com Data derived metrics describing the behaviour of field based citizen scientists provide insights for project design and modelling b... Why report counts cannot be treated as population totals Because participation is voluntary, raw counts cannot be interpreted as the prevalence of unexplained aerial observations in the wider population. For example, if reports double over a decade, several explanations are possible: More people genuinely witnessed unusual phenomena. More people became aware of NUFORC. Media attention encouraged reporting. [nature.com] nature.com Source details in endnotes. Online submission became easier. Public attitudes towards discussing UFOs became less negative. A combination of all of these factors. The database alone cannot separate these possibilities. Researchers therefore avoid treating annual report totals as a direct measure of how frequently unidentified phenomena actually occurred. Instead, they analyse the reports as observations influenced by both sky events and human reporting behaviour. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect On the dynamics of reporting data: A case study of UFO sightings On the dynamics of reporting data: A case study of UFO sightings ScienceDirect... The same caution applies to geographical comparisons. A state or region with more reports may simply contain more people, greater awareness of NUFORC, stronger local UFO communities or easier internet access. Several statistical studies of NUFORC data have found strong relationships between report numbers and population distribution, reinforcing the need to adjust for demographic factors before drawing conclusions. [SSRN] papers.ssrn.com A Preliminary Analysis of Historical UFO Report Data by Mark Carlotto:: SSRNJune 10, 2021... Published: June 10, 2021 10:10 The Government's Unresolved UAP Reports Channel: StarTalk Plus · Views: 69.1K · Uploaded: October 2025 · Length: 10 minutes Open on YouTube Lessons from other volunteer datasets The challenge is not unique to UFO reporting. Many citizen science projects rely on volunteers who decide when, where and what to record. Ecological recording schemes, biodiversity databases and similar projects routinely experience spatial, temporal and participant biases because volunteers are not randomly assigned observation locations. Research on citizen science consistently shows that volunteer collected datasets reflect recorder behaviour as well as the underlying phenomenon being studied. Participants cluster around accessible locations, contribute unevenly over time and differ substantially in experience and motivation. Modern statistical methods often attempt to model these participation biases rather than ignore them. [Nature] nature.com Data derived metrics describing the behaviour of field based citizen scientists provide insights for project design and modelling b... This broader methodological literature provides a useful comparison for NUFORC. The database shares many strengths of volunteer reporting—large scale, rich narratives and long term coverage—but also many of the same limitations arising from non random participation. Data & AI Ethics (NEW COURSE) Channel: Maven Analytics Open on YouTube What self selected data can and cannot support Recognising self selection changes the kinds of conclusions that can be drawn from NUFORC data. The database is well suited to questions such as: How witnesses describe unusual aerial experiences. Which reported shapes or characteristics become more or less common over time. How reporting activity responds to major media events or technological changes. Where clusters of reports may warrant closer investigation. By contrast, the database alone cannot reliably answer questions such as: What proportion of the public has witnessed an unidentified aerial phenomenon. Whether one region truly experiences more unexplained events than another. Whether report increases necessarily indicate more anomalous objects in the sky. The true frequency of unexplained aerial"},{"title":"Investigation Checks","url":"/investigation-checks/","description":"Investigators compared reports with flights, balloons, astronomy, weather and radar records before deciding whether a case remained unresolved.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Blue Book","level":3,"text":"Investigation Checks How Blue Book Tried to Explain a Sighting Investigation Checks Investigation Checks How Blue Book Tried to Explain a Sighting Investigators compared reports with flights, balloons, astronomy, weather and radar records before deciding whether a case remained unresolved. Investigators compared reports with flights, balloons, astronomy, weather and radar records before deciding whether a case remained unresolved. UFO Report Databases implementation single mechanism mechanism What local Air Force personnel collected first Which conventional causes investigators checked Where the verification process commonly stopped ufo report databases blue book investigation checks Introduction Project Blue Book did not begin by asking whether a reported object was extraordinary. Its working method was to eliminate ordinary explanations first. A sighting was only left officially “ unidentified ” after investigators believed they had compared the available evidence against plausible conventional causes and could not make a satisfactory match. That distinction is important when reading Blue Book records and catalogues: an unresolved file reflects the limits of the investigation, not an official conclusion that an exotic craft had been observed. The investigation process therefore centred on systematic verification—checking aviation activity, balloons, astronomical objects, weather conditions, radar information and witness testimony before deciding whether a conventional explanation was adequate. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 What local Air Force personnel collected first Blue Book investigations usually started with the Air Force installation nearest the reported event rather than at headquarters. The quality of this first response often determined whether a case could later be explained. Investigators attempted to establish the basic facts while memories were still fresh. Typical case files included: Exact date and time of the observation. The observer's location and viewing direction. Estimated altitude, speed, size and duration. Weather and visibility conditions. Sketches showing the object's apparent movement. Statements from additional witnesses. Photographs or film if any existed. Witnesses were questioned about what they actually observed rather than what they believed it was. Investigators also looked for inconsistencies between different accounts, since differences in estimated direction, altitude or timing could indicate ordinary observational errors instead of an unusual aerial event. These initial records accompanied the case when it was forwarded to Project Blue Book headquarters at Wright Patterson Air Force Base for further analysis if no immediate explanation emerged. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 Which conventional causes investigators checked Rather than relying on a single explanation, Blue Book investigators compared each report against several independent sources of information. Their objective was to determine whether known aircraft, natural phenomena or observational effects could account for the reported behaviour. Aircraft and scheduled flights One of the first checks concerned aviation activity. Military operations, commercial airline routes, private aircraft and training flights were compared with the reported location and time. Investigators examined whether: Aircraft lights could have appeared stationary because they were approaching directly towards the observer. Banking turns produced sudden changes in brightness. High altitude aircraft remained sunlit after sunset. Refuelling, formation flying or military exercises created unfamiliar lighting patterns. Because many witnesses lacked experience estimating altitude or distance at night, an ordinary aircraft could appear unusually fast, slow or large depending on viewing conditions. Flight records therefore provided an important independent test rather than relying solely on eyewitness impressions. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 2:26 Project Blue Book: Declassified The True Story of the D.C. UFO Sightings History Channel: HISTORY · Views: 393.7K · Uploaded: March 2019 · Length: 2 minutes 26 seconds Open on YouTube Balloons and research launches Weather balloons, research balloons and classified balloon programmes were frequent comparison points. Investigators considered whether balloon drift matched reported movement by checking launch schedules and upper air wind data. High altitude balloons could reflect sunlight long after sunset, appear motionless for extended periods, or seem to change direction as wind layers differed with altitude. In several historical cases, balloon records provided a better fit than aircraft because reported speeds were low, motion followed prevailing winds and observations occurred during known launch periods. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 Astronomy and celestial objects Astronomical explanations formed another routine stage of the investigation. Investigators compared reports with: The positions of Venus and bright planets. Bright stars near the horizon. Meteor showers. Fireballs. The Moon's phase and position. Satellite passes when applicable during the later years of Blue Book. Venus became a particularly common explanation because, when viewed low above the horizon, atmospheric refraction and changing cloud layers could produce apparent movement or flickering colours. Investigators therefore checked astronomical tables before concluding that a genuinely unknown object had been observed. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 Weather and atmospheric effects Meteorological records were consulted whenever the reported behaviour might be explained by atmospheric conditions. Investigators examined: Cloud cover. Temperature inversions. Wind direction at different altitudes. Visibility. Thunderstorms. Ice crystals and other optical effects. Temperature inversions were especially important in radar related reports because they could bend radio waves and create misleading radar returns. Optical effects such as halos, mirages and reflections from ice crystals could also alter the appearance of bright lights or the Sun and Moon. Rather than assuming these explanations, investigators compared weather observations from the relevant time and location to determine whether conditions were suitable for such phenomena. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 2:12 UFO Project Blue Book at National Archives Museum Channel: US National Archives · Views: 352.7K · Uploaded: December 2019 · Length: 2 minutes 12 seconds Open on YouTube Radar and technical records When radar observations accompanied visual reports, Blue Book did not automatically treat radar as independent confirmation. Investigators attempted to determine: Which radar systems detected the object. Whether multiple stations recorded the same target. Whether operators reported equipment problems. Whether atmospheric conditions could have produced anomalous returns. Whether known aircraft occupied the same airspace. This cross checking recognised that radar contacts could result from propagation effects, electronic interference or misinterpretation, while visual observations alone could also be mistaken. Cases combining consistent radar and visual evidence generally received closer scrutiny than reports based on a single source. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 How investigators compared competing explanations Blue Book's approach was less about proving one explanation than testing whether a proposed explanation accounted for the reported details. An explanation generally had to match several elements simultaneously: Timing. Direction of travel. Apparent brightness. Estimated duration. Witness location. Weather conditions. Independent records such as flight or balloon logs. A proposed explanation that matched only one characteristic but conflicted with others was less persuasive. For example,"},{"title":"Unidentified Label","url":"/unidentified-label/","description":"An unidentified ruling meant investigators lacked a confident conventional match, not that the Air Force had confirmed alien technology.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Blue Book","level":3,"text":"Unidentified Label What Did Blue Book Really Mean by Unidentified? Unidentified Label Unidentified Label What Did Blue Book Really Mean by Unidentified? An unidentified ruling meant investigators lacked a confident conventional match, not that the Air Force had confirmed alien technology. An unidentified ruling meant investigators lacked a confident conventional match, not that the Air Force had confirmed alien technology. UFO Report Databases governance single policy policy intervention The official definition of an unidentified case How unidentified differed from insufficient data Why unresolved did not mean extraterrestrial ufo report databases blue book unidentified label Introduction Project Blue Book 's \" Unidentified \" label is often misunderstood as an official admission that the US Air Force encountered alien spacecraft. It was not. Within Blue Book's investigative system, an unidentified case simply meant that, after investigators examined the available evidence, they could not confidently match the reported object or event to a known aircraft, astronomical object, weather phenomenon, balloon, or other conventional explanation. It was a statement about the limits of that particular investigation, not a positive conclusion about what had been observed. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 This distinction matters because the Blue Book archive is frequently cited in discussions of UFO databases and catalogues. The 701 officially unidentified cases remain historically significant, but they represent unresolved investigations rather than confirmed examples of extraordinary technology. Understanding how the label was defined—and how it differed from other case categories—is essential for interpreting the records accurately. What Did Blue Book Really Mean by \"Unidentified\"? Project Blue Book classified reports according to the evidence available after investigation. Official guidance distinguished three broad outcomes: Identified: investigators found sufficient evidence to attribute the sighting to a known object or phenomenon. Insufficient data: the report lacked essential information needed for meaningful evaluation. Unidentified: investigators believed enough information existed to examine conventional explanations, yet none could be established with sufficient confidence. [Pieces of History] prologue.blogs.archives.gov Pieces of History UFOs: Natural Explanations – Pieces of History Pieces of History UFOs: Natural Explanations – Pieces of History The key point is that \"unidentified\" was a residual category , not an affirmative one. It meant that conventional explanations had not been demonstrated to the investigators' satisfaction, not that an unconventional explanation had been established. The Air Force's own published fact sheet later reinforced this interpretation by stating that no evidence from unidentified cases demonstrated technology beyond contemporary scientific knowledge or extraterrestrial origin. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 54:51 UAP Data Analysis On 80,000 Sightings! Don Tadaya Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 54 minutes Open on YouTube How \"Unidentified\" Differed from \"Insufficient Data\" One of the most common misconceptions is that every unexplained report was automatically labelled \"unidentified.\" Official Blue Book guidance says otherwise. An insufficient data case lacked the information necessary to conduct a meaningful investigation. Essential details such as observation time, viewing direction, duration, weather conditions, or witness descriptions might be missing or contradictory. In those cases investigators could not reasonably test competing explanations. [Pieces of History] prologue.blogs.archives.gov Pieces of History UFOs: Natural Explanations – Pieces of History Pieces of History UFOs: Natural Explanations – Pieces of History An unidentified case, by contrast, was intended to contain enough information for investigators to compare the report against known possibilities. Investigators might examine: aircraft movements; balloon launches; astronomical objects; meteorological conditions; satellite passes; radar information where available; and witness consistency. Only when those checks failed to produce a convincing match would the report remain officially unidentified. The category therefore implied that an investigation had reached the limits of available evidence—not that investigators had discovered something impossible to explain. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 2:26 Project Blue Book: Declassified The True Story of the D.C. UFO Sightings History Channel: HISTORY · Views: 393.7K · Uploaded: March 2019 · Length: 2 minutes 26 seconds Open on YouTube Why Unresolved Did Not Mean Extraterrestrial Logically, an unexplained observation does not prove any particular explanation. Blue Book investigators recognised several reasons why a case might remain unresolved without implying extraordinary technology: witnesses may have observed a genuine but poorly documented conventional event; available records such as flight logs or weather observations could have been incomplete; observational conditions may have prevented reliable identification; multiple ordinary factors could have combined into an unusual appearance; evidence collected after the event might simply have been insufficient to distinguish among competing explanations. An unidentified classification therefore reflected uncertainty , not confirmation of an alternative hypothesis. The Air Force made this explicit when Project Blue Book ended in 1969. Its official conclusions stated that: no investigated UFO had represented a threat to national security; no unidentified case demonstrated technology beyond contemporary scientific knowledge; and no unidentified case provided evidence of extraterrestrial vehicles. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 Those conclusions did not claim every case had been fully explained. Rather, they stated that unresolved cases had not produced evidence sufficient to support extraordinary claims. Why the Number \"701 Unidentified Cases\" Is Often Misused The figure of 701 unidentified reports is frequently presented without context. Standing alone, the number can suggest that the Air Force documented hundreds of confirmed mysteries. In reality, it represented approximately six percent of the 12,618 reports examined between 1947 and 1969. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 Several important limitations affect interpretation of those cases: investigative methods changed over more than two decades; documentation quality varied substantially between reports; some files contain extensive witness interviews, while others are comparatively sparse; new information sometimes emerged after cases had been classified; investigators worked under practical limits of time, staffing and available records. For historians, the unidentified cases are therefore best viewed as a catalogue of investigations that ended without a satisfactory conventional identification—not as a catalogue of confirmed anomalous objects. 2:12 UFO Project Blue Book at National Archives Museum Channel: US National Archives · Views: 352.7K · Uploaded: December 2019 · Length: 2 minutes 12 seconds Open on YouTube The Role of Scientific Judgement Blue Book's classifications depended heavily on investigator judgement rather than mechanical rules. Scientific adviser J. Allen Hynek later criticised aspects of Blue Book's procedures, arguing that some investigations were superficial and that genuinely puzzling reports deserved more careful analysis. His criticisms concerned investigative quality and methodology rather than claiming that unidentified cases constituted proof of extraterrestrial spacecraft. The existence of methodological debate illustrates why the unidentified category should not be treated as a definitive scientific finding in either direction. [National Archives] archives.gov Open source on archives.gov. Likewise, later historians have pointed out that unresolved cases can arise in any observational database without implying a single underlying cause. Aviation accident investigations, astronomy surveys and intelligence reporting all contain events"},{"title":"Cold War Context","url":"/cold-war-context/","description":"Air defence concerns influenced which sightings drew attention, what investigators checked and how military context entered the surviving files.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Blue Book","level":3,"text":"Cold War Context How the Cold War Shaped Blue Book's Case Files Cold War Context Cold War Context How the Cold War Shaped Blue Book's Case Files Air defence concerns influenced which sightings drew attention, what investigators checked and how military context entered the surviving files. Air defence concerns influenced which sightings drew attention, what investigators checked and how military context entered the surviving files. UFO Report Databases historical comparative bounded topic event time window Why unfamiliar objects raised air defence concerns Why reports near sensitive sites received attention How military priorities shaped the surviving archive ufo report databases blue book cold war context Introduction Project Blue Book was created during the height of the Cold War, and that strategic setting profoundly influenced the records it produced. Although the programme is often remembered today as a catalogue of UFO sightings, the Air Force's first concern was national defence rather than the search for extraordinary phenomena. Unknown objects in American airspace could represent Soviet bombers, reconnaissance aircraft, experimental missiles or intelligence gathering balloons. As a result, Blue Book's surviving files reflect military priorities as much as unexplained observations. Understanding this Cold War context helps explain why some incidents generated extensive investigations while others received relatively little attention. It also clarifies why Blue Book case files contain detailed discussions of radar, aircraft movements, weather balloons, missile tests and military installations. These records were shaped by the practical question facing investigators: whether an unidentified object posed a potential security threat. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesPublic Interest in UFOs Persists 50 Years After Project Blue Book Termination National ArchivesDecember 5, 2019... Published: December 5, 2019 Why unfamiliar objects raised air defence concerns When Blue Book began in March 1952, relations between the United States and the Soviet Union were becoming increasingly tense. Long range bombers, nuclear weapons and guided missiles had transformed air defence into a national priority. Air defence commanders could not simply dismiss reports of unidentified objects because even an apparently unusual sighting might indicate hostile reconnaissance or a previously unknown aircraft. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesPublic Interest in UFOs Persists 50 Years After Project Blue Book Termination National ArchivesDecember 5, 2019... Published: December 5, 2019 This security mission shaped the programme's official objectives. The Air Force repeatedly stated that Blue Book existed primarily to determine whether reported objects threatened national security and only secondarily to examine any scientific significance. Consequently, investigators routinely compared reports against: military and civilian aircraft operations; weather balloon launches; astronomical objects; radar returns; missile activities; atmospheric and meteorological phenomena. These checks were intended to eliminate known military or natural explanations before a case could remain unresolved. The emphasis was therefore not simply on identifying \"UFOs\" but on ensuring that no genuine security threat went unrecognised. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National Archives... The Cold War also explains why some apparently mundane explanations recur throughout Blue Book records. High altitude balloon programmes, for example, expanded dramatically during the 1950s for meteorological research and military purposes. Since many such programmes were classified or only partially disclosed, investigators sometimes had to balance operational secrecy with the need to explain public reports. 4:19 UFO Report Project Blue Book 1952 Channel: tvdays · Views: 972 · Uploaded: July 2021 · Length: 4 minutes 19 seconds Open on YouTube Why reports near sensitive sites received attention Not every sighting carried equal strategic importance. Reports occurring near military bases, nuclear facilities, missile ranges, aircraft factories or radar stations often attracted greater attention because those locations were considered potential targets for Soviet intelligence gathering. In these cases, investigators commonly attempted to reconstruct several aspects of the event: precise location and viewing direction; local weather conditions; aircraft operating nearby; radar observations; timing relative to military exercises; possible balloon launches or missile tests. The objective was not merely to explain the witness's experience but to determine whether the observation suggested unauthorised activity around strategically important facilities. This explains why some Blue Book files are unusually extensive. Incidents involving multiple military witnesses, simultaneous radar reports or observations close to defence installations could generate correspondence among different Air Force units, intelligence organisations and technical specialists. By contrast, isolated civilian reports from less sensitive locations often received shorter investigations. The famous Washington, D.C., radar and visual incidents of July 1952 illustrate this priority. Although they became widely known as UFO events, they immediately concerned military authorities because unidentified radar targets appeared within protected airspace surrounding the nation's capital. The response involved air defence interceptors as well as detailed technical analysis of radar conditions. The incident demonstrates how Cold War air defence procedures became embedded within Blue Book documentation. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesPublic Interest in UFOs Persists 50 Years After Project Blue Book Termination National ArchivesDecember 5, 2019... Published: December 5, 2019 How military priorities shaped the surviving archive Blue Book's archive was never intended to become a balanced scientific database. Instead, it developed from an operational reporting system serving military needs. That origin influenced both what was preserved and how cases were documented. Several characteristics of the surviving records reflect this military perspective. Security first. Files frequently contain detailed information about aircraft identification, radar operations, weather observations and intelligence assessments because these directly affected defence decisions. Variable documentation. Cases perceived as operationally significant often accumulated many pages of supporting material, while reports judged unlikely to involve a security issue might consist of only brief correspondence. Changing investigative emphasis. As Cold War technologies evolved—from bomber threats during the early 1950s to missiles, satellites and increasingly sophisticated reconnaissance systems later in the decade—the kinds of conventional explanations considered by investigators also changed. Administrative classification. The designation \"unidentified\" did not automatically imply exceptional performance or unexplained technology. Rather, it indicated that available evidence had not permitted a satisfactory identification after the investigative procedures then considered appropriate. These patterns mean researchers must interpret the archive as a product of defence administration rather than as a neutral catalogue of unexplained aerial phenomena. The records document what military investigators considered important enough to record, verify and preserve under the security priorities of their time. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National Archives... 2:56 17th December 1969: Project Blue Book, the United States' study of UFOs, officially terminated Channel: HistoryPod · Views: 574 · Uploaded: December 2023 · Length: 2 minutes 56 seconds Open on YouTube Cold War secrecy and its effect on interpretation Another consequence of the Cold War was the widespread classification of military projects. Experimental aircraft, electronic warfare systems, surveillance programmes and intelligence operations could not always be discussed openly. In some cases, investigators themselves had only limited access to classified information outside their immediate responsibilities. This produced an enduring interpretive challenge. Some sightings that remained unexplained within Blue Book may simply have lacked access to information about classified programmes, while other reports were eventually explained through ordinary causes despite initial uncertainty. Historians therefore caution against assuming that every unresolved case represents the same type of phenomenon. The reverse is also true: secrecy sometimes prevented straightforward public explanations,"},{"title":"Report 14","url":"/report-14/","description":"The statistical study found differences between known and unknown reports but could not determine whether those differences reflected unusual objects or","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Blue Book","level":3,"text":"Report 14 Did Blue Book's Biggest Study Explain the Unknowns? Report 14 Report 14 Did Blue Book's Biggest Study Explain the Unknowns? The statistical study found differences between known and unknown reports but could not determine whether those differences reflected unusual objects or The statistical study found differences between known and unknown reports but could not determine whether those differences reflected unusual objects or UFO Report Databases critique risk single dataset dataset evidence How thousands of reports were coded and compared What differences appeared between knowns and unknowns Why the findings support competing interpretations ufo report databases blue book report 14 Introduction Project Blue Book Special Report No. 14 remains one of the most frequently cited documents in debates over unidentified flying objects because it combined the largest statistical analysis of Air Force sighting reports undertaken during the early Cold War with conclusions that have never been universally accepted. Rather than ending arguments about unexplained sightings, the report became a focal point for competing interpretations. Skeptics point to its overall conclusion that there was no persuasive evidence of technology beyond contemporary scientific knowledge, while proponents emphasise that the statistical analysis found measurable differences between reports classified as \"known\" and those classified as \"unknown\". Both readings draw on genuine elements of the report, which is why it has remained controversial for decades. [theblackvault.com] theblackvault.com Project Blue Book Special Report 14 – May 5, 1955 The Black VaultSeptember 17, 2020... Published: May 5, 1955 Within the broader Project Blue Book archive, Special Report No. 14 is best understood as a sophisticated attempt to organise thousands of reports into a comparable database. It provided valuable statistical evidence about the characteristics of the reports themselves, but it could not determine what the unexplained observations actually represented. How thousands of reports were coded and compared The Air Force commissioned the Battelle Memorial Institute to analyse approximately 3,200 UFO reports collected between 1947 and the end of 1952. Instead of treating every case as an isolated mystery, Battelle developed a standard coding system that converted each report into roughly thirty measurable variables suitable for IBM punched card analysis. [theblackvault.com] theblackvault.com Project Blue Book Special Report 14 – May 5, 1955 The Black VaultSeptember 17, 2020... Published: May 5, 1955 The evaluation process attempted to separate three distinct groups: Knowns , where investigators believed a conventional explanation had been established. Unknowns , where the available information appeared sufficient for analysis but no satisfactory explanation could be agreed upon. Insufficient information , where the report lacked enough detail for a reliable judgement. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com decade 1970s project blue book special report 14 1955 pbbsr14 UFO TransparencyProject Blue Book Special Report 14, 1955, U.S. Air Force / Battelle Memorial Institute · 1976 · UFO Transparency... An important feature of the methodology was that classifying a case as \"unknown\" required unanimous agreement among multiple reviewers. This made the threshold relatively conservative. Reports were also graded by quality, allowing analysts to examine whether better documented cases behaved differently from poorer ones rather than simply counting unexplained reports. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com decade 1970s project blue book special report 14 1955 pbbsr14 UFO TransparencyProject Blue Book Special Report 14, 1955, U.S. Air Force / Battelle Memorial Institute · 1976 · UFO Transparency... The study then compared known and unknown cases across observable characteristics such as shape, colour, brightness, speed, duration and the number of reported objects, using statistical tests to determine whether the two groups appeared similar or systematically different. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com decade 1970s project blue book special report 14 1955 pbbsr14 UFO TransparencyProject Blue Book Special Report 14, 1955, U.S. Air Force / Battelle Memorial Institute · 1976 · UFO Transparency... 2:12 UFO Project Blue Book at National Archives Museum Channel: US National Archives · Views: 352.7K · Uploaded: December 2019 · Length: 2 minutes 12 seconds Open on YouTube What differences appeared between knowns and unknowns? The statistical comparisons produced the finding that has fuelled debate ever since: reports classified as unknown did not appear to be a random subset of the identified cases. Instead, the unknown category showed statistically significant differences across several measured characteristics. Furthermore, higher quality reports were proportionally more likely to remain unidentified than poorer quality reports. That observation challenged the simple assumption that unexplained cases resulted primarily from inadequate evidence or careless reporting. [theblackvault.com] theblackvault.com Project Blue Book Special Report 14 – May 5, 1955 The Black VaultSeptember 17, 2020... Published: May 5, 1955 This distinction is often misunderstood. The report did not conclude that unidentified objects represented extraterrestrial spacecraft or advanced secret technology. It only showed that, within the dataset analysed, the reports placed in the unknown category differed in measurable ways from reports that investigators successfully explained. That finding can support more than one interpretation. It might indicate that some observations reflected genuinely unusual phenomena deserving further study. Equally, it could reflect limitations in contemporary investigative methods, incomplete knowledge of atmospheric effects, classified aircraft activity, observer behaviour, or biases in the reporting process. The statistical analysis alone could not discriminate between those possibilities. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com decade 1970s project blue book special report 14 1955 pbbsr14 UFO TransparencyProject Blue Book Special Report 14, 1955, U.S. Air Force / Battelle Memorial Institute · 1976 · UFO Transparency... Why the findings supported competing interpretations The report's principal conclusion stated that it was \"highly improbable\" that the unidentified reports represented technological developments beyond then current scientific knowledge. At first glance, this appears to settle the matter. However, that conclusion was an interpretive judgement rather than a direct statistical result derived from the numerical comparisons. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com decade 1970s project blue book special report 14 1955 pbbsr14 UFO TransparencyProject Blue Book Special Report 14, 1955, U.S. Air Force / Battelle Memorial Institute · 1976 · UFO Transparency... This distinction lies at the centre of the continuing debate. Those who regard the report as broadly supportive of conventional explanations argue that: most reports ultimately received ordinary identifications; no physical evidence demonstrated extraordinary technology; statistical differences alone cannot establish the existence of a new phenomenon. Conversely, researchers who view the report as more significant argue that: the unknown cases survived a deliberately conservative review process; better quality reports were not easier to explain; statistically distinct unknowns suggested that the unexplained category could not simply be dismissed as observational error or poor documentation. [theblackvault.com] theblackvault.com Project Blue Book Special Report 14 – May 5, 1955 The Black VaultSeptember 17, 2020... Published: May 5, 1955 The same dataset therefore became evidence for different conclusions because participants disagreed about what statistical separation actually implied. The controversy over the public presentation Another reason Special Report No. 14 failed to settle the issue was the way its findings were communicated publicly. When the Air Force announced the report in 1955, press materials emphasised that only a very small percentage of cases remained unexplained and suggested that additional information would probably eliminate the remainder. Critics later argued that this presentation conflated the report's \"insufficient information\" category with genuinely analysed unknowns and therefore understated how many cases had resisted explanation after full evaluation. [theblackvault.com] theblackvault.com Project Blue Book Special Report 14 – May 5, 1955 The Black VaultSeptember 17, 2020... Published: May 5, 1955 Whether one accepts those criticisms or not, the discrepancy between the statistical tables and the simplified public messaging encouraged suspicion among UFO researchers. Rather than ending controversy, the announcement prompted decades of reanalysis by historians, statisticians and UFO"},{"title":"Blue Book Files","url":"/blue-book-files/","description":"Blue Book case files let readers compare original testimony with the Air Force's explanation, paperwork and investigative priorities.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Reports vs Files","level":3,"text":"Blue Book Files What Blue Book Files Reveal Beyond the Sighting Reports vs Files Blue Book Files Blue Book Files What Blue Book Files Reveal Beyond the Sighting Blue Book case files let readers compare original testimony with the Air Force's explanation, paperwork and investigative priorities. Blue Book case files let readers compare original testimony with the Air Force's explanation, paperwork and investigative priorities. UFO Report Databases historical comparative single dataset dataset evidence Questionnaires and witness descriptions Control sheets and official conclusions Administrative records versus case evidence ufo report databases reports vs files blue book files Introduction Project Blue Book case files are valuable not because they preserve UFO reports alone, but because they document how those reports were investigated. Whereas a witness narrative records what someone believed they saw, a complete Blue Book file often includes the Air Force's standard questionnaire, internal summaries, correspondence, technical assessments, routing paperwork and the final official conclusion. Together, these records allow readers to compare the original testimony with the institutional process used to interpret it during the Cold War. Rather than treating a sighting as an isolated story, the files reveal how evidence was collected, which explanations were considered, what information investigators regarded as important and where uncertainties remained. The National Archives preserves both the individual case files and separate administrative records documenting how Project Blue Book itself operated. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesBulk Downloads for Records Related to Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) National ArchivesApril 24, 2025... Published: April 24, 2025 What Blue Book Files Reveal Beyond the Sighting A stand alone witness report captures perception. A Blue Book case file captures an investigation. For many incidents, the Air Force retained much more than the initial account. A typical file may include the witness questionnaire, letters exchanged with investigators, newspaper clippings, photographs, laboratory or photographic assessments where available, and a control sheet summarising the report alongside the official explanation. Looking at these documents together allows researchers to distinguish between what the witness actually reported and what investigators later inferred. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesPublic Interest in UFOs Persists 50 Years After Project Blue Book Termination National ArchivesDecember 5, 2019... Published: December 5, 2019 This distinction is historically important because it exposes both the evidence available at the time and the reasoning used to classify the event. Whether or not one agrees with the conclusion, the file documents the decision making process rather than only its outcome. 1:53 Project Blue Book 1969 Movietone Moment 17 December 2021 Channel: British Movietone · Views: 20.1K · Uploaded: December 2021 · Length: 1 minute 53 seconds Open on YouTube Questionnaires and Witness Descriptions The standard Air Force questionnaire added structure that many public UFO databases cannot provide. Besides a narrative description, investigators sought information such as: exact date and time; observing location; weather and visibility; apparent direction of travel; estimated elevation and duration; number of witnesses; the observer's occupation and experience; sketches or diagrams when available. These standardised questions make many Blue Book cases easier to compare with one another than free form reports submitted decades later. They also help modern researchers identify where an interpretation rested on measurable observations and where it depended on estimates or memory. Just as importantly, the original questionnaire sometimes preserves details that disappear from later summaries. Comparing the witness's own wording with the Air Force's condensed description can reveal whether nuances were omitted, emphasised or reinterpreted during the investigation. 1:11 'Project Blue Book' Ep. 1 Official Clip UFO SHOWTIME Documentary Series Channel: SHOWTIME · Views: 290.8K · Uploaded: August 2021 · Length: 1 minute 11 seconds Open on YouTube Control Sheets and Official Conclusions One of the most distinctive documents in many Blue Book files is the control sheet. This is not another witness statement but an administrative summary prepared during the investigation. Typically, the control sheet records: the essential facts of the reported sighting; internal case identifiers; investigative routing; the official explanation, if one was assigned; the final disposition, such as identified, insufficient information or unidentified . Because the control sheet places the witness account beside the Air Force's conclusion, it provides a direct comparison between observation and official interpretation. A reader can therefore evaluate whether the explanation appears to address the reported details or whether significant questions remained unresolved. This is particularly useful in cases classified as \"unidentified\". Within Blue Book, that label generally meant investigators lacked sufficient evidence for a confident identification after applying the available information. It did not constitute an official finding of extraordinary technology or extraterrestrial origin. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Do Records Show Proof of UFOs? National Archives National ArchivesDo Records Show Proof of UFOs? National ArchivesFebruary 9, 2018... Published: February 9, 2018 Administrative Records Versus Case Evidence Blue Book's administrative records answer different questions from the case files themselves. Individual case files document specific sightings. Administrative records document how the programme functioned: staffing decisions, reporting procedures, correspondence between offices, policy discussions, budgets, public information planning and changes in investigative practice. These records help historians understand institutional priorities rather than individual sightings. The National Archives preserves these administrative files separately from the chronological case files, allowing researchers to examine both operational policy and case level evidence. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesBulk Downloads for Records Related to Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAPs) National ArchivesApril 24, 2025... Published: April 24, 2025 Reading both together can clarify issues that would otherwise remain obscure. For example, procedural memoranda may explain why certain reports required additional review, how cases were assigned, or how investigators were instructed to classify recurring categories such as astronomical objects, balloons or aircraft. Administrative paperwork therefore provides context for patterns seen across hundreds of individual investigations. Why the Complete File Matters A witness report answers the question, \"What did someone say they saw?\" A complete Blue Book file answers additional questions: What follow up questions were asked? Which evidence was gathered? Were multiple witnesses interviewed? Was a conventional explanation investigated? How did the Air Force justify its conclusion? What information was missing when the case was closed? Those additional layers make Blue Book fundamentally different from collections that preserve only public testimony. Researchers can compare raw observations with official analysis instead of relying solely on either. This also allows historians to study the investigative culture itself. The files show which forms of evidence were considered persuasive, how much effort different reports received, and how administrative practices evolved over the life of the project. Even when a conclusion is disputed, the surviving paperwork provides an unusually transparent record of the government's investigative process during a period of sustained public interest in UFO reports. [National Archives] archives.gov National ArchivesPublic Interest in UFOs Persists 50 Years After Project Blue Book Termination National ArchivesDecember 5, 2019... Published: December 5, 2019 1:34:32 Ex Pentagon Official: The U.S Isn't Telling The Truth! Top Secret UFO Encounters Finally Uncovered! Channel: The Diary Of A CEO · Views: 6.3M · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 1 hour 34 minutes Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to What Blue Book Files Reveal Beyond the Sighting. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects By Edward J Ruppelt The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects is a book by Edward J. Ruppelt which described the study of UFOs by United States Air Force fro... See on Amazon Book UFOs and Government: A Historical Inquiry By Michael D. Swords Governments around the world have had to deal with"},{"title":"Independent Witnesses","url":"/independent-witnesses/","description":"Several reports strengthen a UFO case only when witnesses observed separately and did not shape one another's accounts.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Reports vs Files","level":3,"text":"Independent Witnesses When Do Multiple UFO Witnesses Really Corroborate? Reports vs Files Independent Witnesses Independent Witnesses When Do Multiple UFO Witnesses Really Corroborate? Several reports strengthen a UFO case only when witnesses observed separately and did not shape one another's accounts. Several reports strengthen a UFO case only when witnesses observed separately and did not shape one another's accounts. UFO Report Databases critique risk single mechanism mechanism How witness independence is established Shared viewing and story contamination Why separate vantage points matter ufo report databases reports vs files independent witnesses Introduction Multiple witness reports can strengthen the evidential value of a UFO or unidentified anomalous phenomenon (UAP) case, but only when the witnesses are genuinely independent. A database containing ten reports of the same event is not automatically ten times stronger than a single report. If the observers discussed what they saw before reporting it, watched the same media coverage, or relied on one person's interpretation, the accounts may no longer represent independent lines of evidence. The central question is therefore not simply how many people reported the event , but how much of each account originated from the witness's own observation . For investigators and database curators, this distinction is crucial. Independent testimony can reveal consistent details that are difficult to attribute to chance alone, while non independent testimony can unintentionally amplify shared errors, assumptions or memories. Modern scientific guidance on UAP research similarly stresses that eyewitness reports become more valuable when they can be compared with independent observations and, ideally, corroborating sensor data rather than being treated as repeated versions of the same story. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science NASA UNIDENTIFIED ANOMALOUS PHENOMENA... When Do Multiple Witnesses Really Corroborate? True corroboration means that separate observers provide overlapping descriptions without influencing one another. Investigators are less interested in whether every detail matches than whether the core observations remain consistent despite independent reporting. Evidence is generally stronger when witnesses: observed the event from different locations or viewing angles; recorded or reported their observations before communicating with each other; were interviewed separately; describe the same sequence of events using their own language rather than identical phrasing; agree on central features while differing in expected peripheral details. Perfect agreement is not necessarily a sign of reliability. In eyewitness research, entirely identical narratives can indicate that witnesses have compared stories or that later discussion has harmonised their memories. Genuine independent recollections usually contain both common observations and natural differences in emphasis or detail. Research on investigative interviewing has consistently found that memory can be influenced by social interaction and questioning, making early independent accounts particularly valuable. [National Institute of Justice] nij.ojp.gov National Institute of JusticeInterviewing Victims and Witnesses of Crime National Institute of JusticeJanuary 1, 1995... Published: January 1, 1995 1:06:54 The UFO Evidence I'm Taking Seriously Channel: Astrum Extra · Views: 495.5K · Uploaded: October 2025 · Length: 1 hour 6 minutes Open on YouTube How Investigators Test Independence Experienced investigators try to establish independence before comparing witness statements. Typical questions include: Who first noticed the object? Were witnesses together or apart? Did they discuss the event before giving statements? When was each report first recorded? Did later media coverage introduce new details? Are similarities confined to directly observable features, or do they extend to interpretations? Databases that preserve original submission dates and unedited witness narratives are especially useful because they allow researchers to distinguish contemporaneous observations from later retellings. Shared Viewing Does Not Automatically Mean Independent Evidence Many UFO sightings involve groups of people looking at the same object. Although this may seem inherently stronger than a single witness report, group observation creates opportunities for memory contamination. One person may point towards the sky and announce, \"It's changing direction,\" prompting others to interpret ambiguous movement in the same way. During or immediately after the event, people naturally exchange impressions, ask questions and fill gaps in one another's memories. Psychological research shows that such post event discussion can alter subsequent recollections without any deliberate dishonesty. [National Institute of Justice] nij.ojp.gov National Institute of JusticeInterviewing Victims and Witnesses of Crime National Institute of JusticeJanuary 1, 1995... Published: January 1, 1995 This means that a bus full of passengers, a family, or a group of friends may collectively provide valuable testimony, but not necessarily multiple independent testimonies. Their accounts remain important, yet they cannot automatically be counted as separate confirmations of every reported detail. Why Separate Vantage Points Matter Observations from different locations provide an important safeguard against shared perceptual errors. If observers several kilometres apart independently report: the same time of appearance, the same direction of travel, similar brightness changes, and compatible movement across the sky, their combined observations allow investigators to reconstruct the event geometrically. Differences in viewing angle can help estimate altitude, speed and direction, and may reveal whether an apparently unusual manoeuvre was actually a perspective effect. Separate vantage points also reduce the chance that all observers were affected by the same local environmental conditions, such as reflections, nearby lights or atmospheric distortions. Conversely, if accounts from different locations cannot be reconciled geometrically, investigators may conclude that witnesses were observing different objects or interpreting the same phenomenon differently. 41:41 Are These UFO Sightings Real Evidence? The Proof Is Out There Channel: HISTORY · Views: 192.0K · Uploaded: April 2026 · Length: 41 minutes Open on YouTube Multiple Database Entries Are Not the Same as Multiple Witnesses Large UFO catalogues often contain several records relating to a single event. These may include: separate reports from different witnesses; duplicate submissions by the same witness; later follow up reports; reports copied between organisations; media summaries entered as new cases. Without careful database management, one widely reported incident can appear to represent numerous independent observations when it actually reflects repeated documentation of the same underlying testimony. Researchers therefore distinguish between: multiple records of one case , and multiple independent witnesses within one case . The latter potentially increases evidential strength; the former mainly reflects archival practice. Independence Strengthens a Case—but Does Not Decide It Independent witnesses reduce the likelihood that an observation resulted solely from one person's misperception or memory error. They do not, however, determine what was observed. Several independent observers can all accurately report an unusual light while still collectively misidentifying a satellite train, an aircraft seen under uncommon lighting conditions, or an atmospheric phenomenon. Likewise, genuinely unidentified cases remain unidentified because available information is insufficient for a confident explanation—not because multiple witnesses automatically imply an extraordinary cause. NASA's independent UAP study emphasises this distinction. Eyewitness reports can reveal useful patterns and deserve careful preservation, but by themselves they do not provide repeatable scientific evidence. Their value increases substantially when independent testimony is combined with calibrated sensor data, precise timing, photographs, radar records or other objective measurements. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science NASA UNIDENTIFIED ANOMALOUS PHENOMENA... Why UFOs Are Hiding In Our Oceans & How The CIA Trained Psychic Spies Admiral Tim Gallaudet Channel: Dr. Mayim Bialik Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to When Do Multiple UFO Witnesses Really Corroborate?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Invisible Gorilla: How Our Intuitions Deceive Us By Christopher Chabris, Daniel Simons NEW YORK TIMES BESTSELLER • Our minds don’t work the way we think they do. Two renowned psychologists explain how and why our intuitions... See on Amazon Book Eyewitness"},{"title":"Distance Errors","url":"/distance-errors/","description":"Without a reliable distance estimate, dramatic claims about a UFO's physical size or speed cannot be calculated securely.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Reports vs Files","level":3,"text":"Distance Errors Why UFO Size and Speed Estimates Go Wrong Reports vs Files Distance Errors Distance Errors Why UFO Size and Speed Estimates Go Wrong Without a reliable distance estimate, dramatic claims about a UFO's physical size or speed cannot be calculated securely. Without a reliable distance estimate, dramatic claims about a UFO's physical size or speed cannot be calculated securely. UFO Report Databases mechanism single mechanism mechanism Apparent size versus physical size Angular motion and assumed distance Common visual reference failures ufo report databases reports vs files distance errors Introduction When a UFO witness describes an object as \"the size of a house\" or \"travelling thousands of miles per hour\", the statement often reflects perception rather than a measurable quantity. The critical missing variable is usually distance. Unless investigators know how far away the object was, they cannot reliably convert its apparent size into a physical size or its apparent motion across the sky into a true speed. This is one of the most important limitations in UFO report databases and catalogues because witness reports commonly record what an object looked like, not where it was in three dimensional space. NASA's review of unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) likewise emphasises that incomplete observational data prevent robust scientific conclusions and highlights the need for better calibrated measurements rather than subjective estimates. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 Why UFO Size and Speed Estimates Go Wrong Apparent size versus physical size Human observers naturally estimate how large something appears, not how large it actually is. The apparent or angular size of an object is the angle it occupies in a person's field of view. Geometry shows that the same angular size can be produced by either a small nearby object or a much larger distant one. Only if the distance is known can the object's physical dimensions be calculated. [Coe College] coe.edu College Angular Size Coe College Coe CollegeAngular Size Coe College... This creates a fundamental ambiguity in many UFO reports. Consider two possibilities: A drone 2 metres across at a distance of 100 metres. An aircraft 40 metres across at a distance of 2 kilometres. Both can occupy a very similar angular size in the observer's vision despite differing enormously in their true dimensions. Without an independent estimate of range, neither interpretation can be ruled out from apparent size alone. [Coe College] coe.edu College Angular Size Coe College Coe CollegeAngular Size Coe College... This is why experienced investigators usually separate a witness's statement that an object \"looked enormous\" from any claim that it was enormous. The first is a direct observation; the second is an inference that depends on information often missing from the original report. 10:35 Congress Fell for a BALLOON? MICK WEST Breaks Down The Hellfire Missile UFO Channel: The Zach Show · Views: 302 · Uploaded: October 2025 · Length: 10 minutes Open on YouTube Angular motion and assumed distance The same problem affects estimates of speed. A witness usually sees an object move through an angle across the sky rather than measuring the distance it actually travelled. For example, if an object appears to move 20 degrees across the sky in five seconds, its angular motion can be estimated. Its true speed, however, depends entirely on how far away it was. If that object were: 200 metres away, the corresponding speed might be modest. 20 kilometres away, the same apparent motion would imply a vastly greater velocity. Hundreds of kilometres away, the implied speed could become physically extraordinary. Because speed is proportional to assumed distance, even small errors in range produce proportionally large errors in calculated velocity. Without an independently established distance, dramatic speed estimates have no secure mathematical foundation. [Coe College] coe.edu College Angular Size Coe College Coe CollegeAngular Size Coe College... This explains why many sensational numerical claims in older UFO literature depend less on what was directly observed than on assumptions made afterwards about how distant the object must have been. Common Visual Reference Failures Witnesses rarely observe an unfamiliar object against a background that provides reliable depth information. Several recurring situations reduce the ability to judge distance accurately. Featureless skies. A bright point against a dark sky offers almost no depth cues. The observer cannot easily distinguish whether the light is hundreds of metres away or many kilometres distant. Unknown object size. Humans often estimate distance by unconsciously comparing an object's apparent size with its expected real size. When the object is unfamiliar, this comparison becomes impossible. Night time viewing. Darkness removes landmarks, shadows and perspective, making distance estimation substantially more difficult. Single observation point. A lone witness usually lacks stereoscopic or triangulated measurements over long distances. Two observers separated by a known baseline can sometimes estimate range through parallax, but isolated reports cannot. [Imagine the Universe] imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov Imagine the Universe Fermi Learning Center Imagine the UniverseFermi Learning CenterAugust 20, 2018... Published: August 20, 2018 These limitations explain why investigators place much greater confidence in observations supported by radar, multiple synchronised cameras or independently located witnesses than in visual impressions alone. 5:32 The SHOCKING Math Error Behind Viral UFO Videos Mick West Channel: The Zach Show · Views: 126 · Uploaded: October 2025 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube Why Witnesses Can Be Completely Sincere Distance errors do not imply dishonesty or poor observation. They arise because the human visual system evolved to estimate distances using environmental cues that are often absent when looking into the sky. Research on visual perception shows that perceived size depends strongly on perceived distance. When reliable distance cues disappear, people naturally rely more heavily on apparent angular size, increasing uncertainty in judgments of both size and motion. Well known visual phenomena, including the Moon illusion, demonstrate that even familiar celestial objects can appear dramatically larger or closer under certain viewing conditions despite their physical properties remaining unchanged. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Explaining the moon illusion PubMed Central (PMC)Explaining the moon illusion PMCJanuary 4, 2000... Published: January 4, 2000 Consequently, two honest observers may produce different estimates of the same object's size or speed even when describing the same event. Angular diameter Channel: Audiopedia Open on YouTube What Investigators Look For Instead Because eyewitness estimates of distance are inherently uncertain, modern investigations seek independent evidence that constrains range before calculating physical characteristics. Useful indicators include: Multiple observers at known locations, allowing geometric triangulation. Simultaneous photographs or videos from different positions. Radar returns that provide distance directly. Aircraft transponder data. Astronomical or satellite tracking information. Terrain references showing the object passed behind or in front of known landmarks. When these data are unavailable, investigators generally report only observable quantities such as angular position, direction of travel, duration and apparent brightness, while avoiding confident statements about physical size or velocity. This approach is consistent with recommendations that higher quality, standardised observational data are essential for scientifically evaluating UAP reports. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 Why This Matters for UFO Databases Large UFO catalogues preserve valuable witness descriptions, but the absence of reliable distance measurements means many reports cannot support precise calculations of an object's dimensions or speed. A database entry stating that an object \"filled half the windscreen\" or \"crossed the sky in seconds\" records an authentic perception, yet those observations alone cannot determine whether the object was a nearby small object, a distant conventional aircraft or something genuinely unusual. For researchers comparing reports across decades, this distinction is crucial. Apparent size and apparent motion are useful observational data, whereas derived claims about extraordinary dimensions or impossible velocities remain hypotheses unless supported by independent evidence that establishes the object's distance. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides"},{"title":"Viewing Geometry","url":"/viewing-geometry/","description":"Maps, compass bearings and visible horizons can test whether a reported UFO path matches aircraft, satellites or celestial objects.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Reports vs Files","level":3,"text":"Viewing Geometry How Investigators Rebuild a UFO Sighting Reports vs Files Viewing Geometry Viewing Geometry How Investigators Rebuild a UFO Sighting Maps, compass bearings and visible horizons can test whether a reported UFO path matches aircraft, satellites or celestial objects. Maps, compass bearings and visible horizons can test whether a reported UFO path matches aircraft, satellites or celestial objects. UFO Report Databases implementation bounded topic policy intervention Fixing the witness position and direction Mapping the visible sky and horizon Comparing reconstructed paths with known objects ufo report databases reports vs files viewing geometry Introduction A witness account becomes far more useful when investigators can reconstruct exactly where the observer stood, which direction they were facing and how the reported object moved across the visible sky. This process, known as reconstructing the viewing geometry, transforms a subjective description into a testable model. Instead of asking only, \"What did the witness think they saw?\", investigators ask, \"What could have occupied that exact position in the sky at that exact time?\" Within UFO report databases and catalogues, viewing geometry is one of the most valuable forms of follow up investigation because it allows reports to be compared objectively with aircraft tracks, satellite passes, astronomical objects and terrain. Even when a case remains unidentified , a well documented geometric reconstruction makes later reanalysis possible as new data or identification tools become available. NASA's UAP study likewise emphasises that precise location, timing and observational metadata are essential if reports are to support scientific investigation rather than anecdotal description. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 Fixing the Witness Position and Direction The first task is to establish the observer's position as accurately as possible. Investigators prefer exact coordinates rather than a town name or postcode because even small changes in location can alter the apparent relationship between a sighting and the horizon. When possible, investigators revisit the site with the witness. Standing in the original position allows them to determine: the precise observing location using GPS or mapping software; the witness's facing direction (azimuth); the estimated angle above the horizon (elevation); whether the witness moved during the observation; the duration of each stage of the event; and whether nearby buildings, trees or hills blocked parts of the sky. Direction estimates given during interviews are frequently checked rather than accepted at face value. Witnesses often describe an object as travelling \"east\" or \"over the hill\", but a site visit may show that the apparent direction was closer to north east or that the landmark itself was misidentified. Many investigation protocols therefore encourage recording measurable quantities such as compass bearing, angular elevation and apparent angular size instead of relying solely on descriptive language. MUFON 's published investigation guidance, for example, lists azimuth, elevation, angular size and other measurable observational parameters among the information collected during follow up interviews. [MUFON] mufon.com The Scientific Method The Scientific Method MUFON... Calculating the Distance to the Moon Channel: Science360 Beyond the Sky Open on YouTube Mapping the Visible Sky and Horizon Once the observer's location is fixed, investigators reconstruct the sky exactly as it appeared at the reported time. This reconstruction combines several independent sources of information: astronomical software showing the positions of planets, bright stars and the Moon; topographic maps identifying hills, ridgelines and valleys; aerial imagery revealing buildings or vegetation; historical weather records indicating cloud cover and visibility; and photographs taken from the witness location to document the visible horizon. The horizon matters because many common misidentifications occur close to it. Bright planets such as Venus can appear unusually large through atmospheric distortion, while aircraft approaching directly towards an observer may seem stationary before changing apparent direction. A witness's memory of an object's height above the horizon may therefore be more informative than their estimate of its distance. Investigators often express an object's position in angular terms. Holding an arm fully extended provides rough field estimates: a clenched fist spans approximately 10 degrees of sky, while a finger width covers roughly one degree. Although imprecise, such estimates can later be refined against photographs or site measurements. Comparing Reconstructed Paths with Known Objects The reconstructed geometry is then compared with independent records to determine whether known objects occupied the same part of the sky. Typical comparisons include: Aircraft: Flight tracking records can establish whether commercial or private aircraft followed the reported path. Navigation lights viewed head on may appear nearly stationary before suddenly moving sideways as the aircraft changes relative position. Satellites: Satellite prediction databases help determine whether a reported moving light matches the timing and direction of a satellite pass or a sequence of Starlink satellites. Astronomical objects: Bright planets, stars and the Moon are compared with the reconstructed line of sight. Venus, Jupiter and Sirius have all generated numerous UFO reports because of their brightness and atmospheric effects near the horizon. Meteors and fireballs: Reported rapid streaks can be compared with meteor observations and fireball networks. Weather phenomena: Balloon launches, illuminated clouds and atmospheric optical effects are evaluated where consistent with the reconstructed geometry. Rather than asking whether a description sounds similar, investigators ask whether the known object's calculated position matches the witness's reconstructed line of sight within reasonable uncertainty. If the geometry does not fit, that explanation becomes less convincing regardless of superficial similarity. NASA's public guidance similarly notes that careful consideration of celestial objects, satellites, aircraft and atmospheric phenomena resolves many initially puzzling observations. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science Identifying UFOs and UAPs NASA ScienceIdentifying UFOs and UAPs NASA Science... Stellar parallax and measuring distance Channel: Las Cumbres Observatory Open on YouTube Why Viewing Geometry Often Changes a Case Reconstructing viewing geometry frequently alters an initial interpretation because human perception provides poor estimates of distance, speed and size without external reference points. Several recurring effects illustrate why geometry is so important: A distant aircraft flying directly towards an observer can appear almost motionless before seeming to accelerate sideways. Two unrelated lights separated by many kilometres may appear close together from one viewing angle. A satellite fading into Earth's shadow can appear to stop abruptly or disappear instantaneously. Bright planets near the horizon may seem to hover over trees or buildings despite being astronomically distant. A witness turning their head while tracking an object can unintentionally exaggerate apparent motion. By reconstructing the observer's viewpoint, investigators can distinguish genuine changes in an object's apparent position from changes caused by perspective or observer movement. Limits of Geometric Reconstruction Viewing geometry is powerful but not infallible. Its accuracy depends on the quality of the original report. Common limitations include uncertain timing, vague location descriptions, delayed reporting, inaccurate compass directions and imperfect human memory. Even a difference of several minutes can significantly affect comparisons with satellites or fast moving aircraft. Likewise, if a witness cannot identify where they were standing or which landmarks they used, multiple geometric solutions may remain possible. Investigators therefore treat reconstructed geometry as an evidence based model rather than a perfect recreation. Well documented reports usually include confidence estimates or acknowledge uncertainty in the measured bearings and elevations rather than presenting them as exact values. Why Geometry Matters for UFO Databases For UFO databases and catalogues, viewing geometry adds enduring value because it converts a narrative into structured observational data. Bearings, elevations, observer coordinates and reconstructed paths can be re examined years later using improved flight records, satellite catalogues or astronomical software that may not have been available during the original investigation. This makes viewing geometry one of the most"},{"title":"Unexplained Cases","url":"/unexplained-cases/","description":"A case may remain open because evidence is missing, contradictory or genuinely resistant to identification after investigation.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / Reports vs Files","level":3,"text":"Unexplained Cases What Does an Unexplained UFO Case Actually Mean? Reports vs Files Unexplained Cases Unexplained Cases What Does an Unexplained UFO Case Actually Mean? A case may remain open because evidence is missing, contradictory or genuinely resistant to identification after investigation. A case may remain open because evidence is missing, contradictory or genuinely resistant to identification after investigation. UFO Report Databases governance single policy policy intervention Insufficient data versus failed identification How contradictory testimony blocks conclusions Why unexplained is not proof of the exotic ufo report databases reports vs files unexplained cases Introduction An \"unexplained\" UFO case is not a single, uniform category. In UFO report databases and catalogues, the label can describe very different situations: a sighting with too little information to investigate properly, a case containing contradictory evidence that prevents a firm conclusion, or a report that remained unidentified despite a thorough investigation. Understanding which meaning applies is essential when reading catalogues or comparing statistics. An unresolved entry does not automatically imply an extraordinary object; it often reflects the limits of the available evidence, the standards of the investigating organisation, or the quality of the original report. Modern scientific reviews, including NASA's work on unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP), emphasise that incomplete or inconsistent data frequently make reliable conclusions impossible. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 What Does an Unexplained UFO Case Actually Mean? Different organisations define \"unexplained\" in different ways, which is why the same sighting might receive different classifications depending on the catalogue. A useful example comes from the French space agency CNES through its GEIPAN investigation programme. Rather than treating every unresolved case alike, GEIPAN separates reports into categories that distinguish between a lack of information and a failed identification after investigation. Cases classified as C remain unidentified because investigators did not receive enough reliable evidence, while D cases remain unidentified despite an investigation that found no satisfactory conventional explanation. This distinction prevents readers from assuming that every unresolved case has the same evidential value. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN This approach illustrates an important principle for anyone using UFO databases: an unresolved entry is a description of the investigative outcome, not necessarily of the phenomenon itself. QUE SAIT ON RÉELLEMENT DES OVNIS ? Channel: Xavier Tytelman Open on YouTube Insufficient Data Versus Failed Identification The most common source of misunderstanding is treating all unresolved reports as equally mysterious. A case may remain open because essential information is missing. Typical problems include: no precise time or location; only a single witness with no corroboration; photographs or videos lacking context or metadata; uncertain estimates of distance, altitude or speed; delayed reporting that makes independent verification impossible. In these situations, investigators cannot reliably compare the report with aircraft movements, satellite passes, astronomical objects or weather conditions. The absence of an explanation reflects the absence of usable evidence rather than the presence of unusual evidence. NASA has repeatedly stressed that most UAP reports contain insufficient high quality data to permit scientific conclusions, making improved data collection a higher priority than speculation about origins. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP FAQs NASA ScienceUAP FAQs NASA Science... A different situation occurs when investigators possess relatively complete information yet still cannot confidently match the observation to a known object or phenomenon. Such cases may include multiple witnesses, supporting documentation or technical analysis, but every tested conventional explanation remains unsatisfactory. These are fundamentally different from cases where investigation cannot proceed because the evidence is incomplete. GEIPAN's distinction between Categories C and D was designed specifically to preserve that difference. [GEIPAN] cnes geipan.fr GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN GEIPANHow does GEIPAN classify observation cases? GEIPAN 11:56 Meeting France's UFO detectives • FRANCE 24 English Channel: FRANCE 24 English · Views: 48.3K · Uploaded: October 2024 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube How Contradictory Testimony Blocks Conclusions An investigation can also remain unresolved because the available evidence points in conflicting directions. Witnesses observing the same event often disagree about: colour, brightness or shape; direction of movement; duration of the sighting; estimated distance or altitude; whether the object appeared stationary or moving rapidly. These differences do not necessarily indicate dishonesty. Human perception varies with viewing angle, lighting conditions, stress and expectations. Memory also changes over time, particularly if witnesses discuss an event with one another or encounter media coverage before providing detailed statements. Contradictory physical evidence can create similar problems. A photograph may suggest one explanation while witness descriptions suggest another. Radar information may not align precisely with visual observations, or environmental data may remain incomplete. Rather than forcing a conclusion, responsible investigative practice often records the case as unresolved until stronger evidence becomes available. Some cataloguing systems therefore allow cases to be revisited and reclassified when new witnesses, photographs or technical information emerge. GEIPAN explicitly notes that unresolved cases can be re examined if additional evidence is later obtained. [Geipan] geipan.fr FAQ GEIPAN FAQ GEIPAN... Why Unexplained Is Not Proof of the Exotic Perhaps the most persistent misunderstanding is the assumption that \"unidentified\" means \"identified as something extraordinary.\" From an investigative perspective, these are entirely different statements. \"Unidentified\" simply means investigators could not establish a reliable identification using the available evidence. It does not establish extraterrestrial technology, unknown physics or any other specific explanation. This distinction has been maintained across multiple official investigations. NASA states that current public evidence does not support claims that UAP represent alien technology, while also acknowledging that many reports cannot be conclusively explained because of limited data. The emphasis is therefore on improving observations rather than drawing conclusions from uncertainty. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP FAQs NASA ScienceUAP FAQs NASA Science... Historical government investigations reached similar conclusions. The final findings of Project Blue Book reported that some cases remained officially unidentified, but also concluded that the investigated reports provided no evidence of extraterrestrial vehicles or technology beyond established scientific knowledge. The persistence of unresolved cases was treated as an investigative limitation rather than affirmative evidence for an exotic hypothesis. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 UAP Evidence & Analysis Mick West Channel: Tim Ventura Open on YouTube Reading UFO Catalogues More Carefully When comparing UFO databases, readers should therefore ask a simple question before interpreting an unresolved case: why is it unresolved? The answer may be one of several possibilities: the report lacked enough information for meaningful investigation; available evidence was internally inconsistent; all tested conventional explanations proved inadequate despite substantial investigation; new information may later permit reclassification . Recognising these distinctions makes catalogue statistics more informative. A database with many unresolved cases does not necessarily contain stronger evidence for extraordinary phenomena; it may simply use broader reporting criteria, receive less complete witness information or apply a classification system that separates uncertainty from positive identification. Understanding how each catalogue defines \"unexplained\" is therefore as important as the number of unexplained cases it contains. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to What Does an Unexplained UFO Case Actually Mean?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper"},{"title":"Archive Differences","url":"/archive-differences/","description":"UFOCAT links records across private files, publications and official documents, while government archives preserve custody and administrative context.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / UFOCAT","level":3,"text":"Archive Differences UFOCAT or Government Archive: What Does Each Preserve? Archive Differences Archive Differences UFOCAT or Government Archive: What Does Each Preserve? UFOCAT links records across private files, publications and official documents, while government archives preserve custody and administrative context. UFOCAT links records across private files, publications and official documents, while government archives preserve custody and administrative context. UFO Report Databases historical comparative bounded topic population context Cross source linking versus archival custody What civilian networks preserve outside official systems When researchers need both kinds of collection ufo report databases ufocat archive differences Introduction Civilian UFO catalogues and government archives preserve different kinds of value. A catalogue such as UFOCAT is designed to help researchers discover, compare and connect reports that may be scattered across private files, books, magazines, newspapers and official records. A government archive, by contrast, is primarily concerned with preserving the records created or received by a public body, maintaining their provenance, custody and authenticity as official documents. These are complementary rather than competing approaches. A civilian catalogue excels at linking information across institutional boundaries, while an official archive provides stronger evidence of where a record came from, who created it and how it has been preserved over time. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 UFOCAT or Government Archive: What Does Each Preserve? Although both contain material relating to unidentified aerial phenomena, their purposes differ fundamentally. UFOCAT functions as a research index rather than a repository of original records. A single incident may be represented by several entries because the catalogue records different sources discussing the same event. Those sources can include investigator files, specialist publications, newspaper reports, books and references to official documents. Its objective is to show where evidence exists and how accounts relate to one another, even when the original material is held by different organisations. Government archives preserve records because they were created during the conduct of official business. National archives generally retain the administrative structure of the creating agency, preserving original filing systems, transfer histories and metadata that document how records entered archival custody. In the case of the US Air Force's Project Blue Book files, the National Archives preserves the investigation records as an official government collection rather than reorganising them into a thematic database. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 This distinction affects how each resource should be interpreted. A UFOCAT entry points researchers towards evidence; an archival record represents evidence that has remained within a documented chain of government custody. Cross source Linking Versus Archival Custody The greatest strength of civilian catalogues lies in cross source integration. Because civilian researchers were not limited to one organisation's paperwork, they could combine information from multiple origins, including: private investigator correspondence; civilian UFO organisations; newspaper coverage; later scholarly or historical publications; official files released decades after the original investigations. This allows researchers to trace how an individual case evolved over time. For example, an incident might first appear in a local newspaper, later receive a civilian field investigation, then be discussed in a specialist journal and finally emerge in declassified government records. UFOCAT attempts to connect these separate references into a navigable research trail. Government archives generally avoid this kind of editorial synthesis. Archivists preserve records according to the principle of provenance, keeping documents associated with the agency that created them rather than rearranging them by topic. This practice protects historical context because researchers can understand how and why records were produced, rather than seeing them only as isolated pieces of information. [National Archives] nationalarchives.gov.uk National Archives Records management code National ArchivesRecords management code The National Archives... The trade off is clear: civilian catalogues improve discoverability across collections, while official archives preserve documentary integrity within a single collection. What Civilian Networks Preserve Outside Official Systems Civilian UFO organisations have historically collected material that governments either never gathered or never retained. These collections often include witness correspondence, investigator notebooks, interviews, sketches, unpublished reports, organisational newsletters and regional case files. Many sightings reported to civilian groups were never submitted to military or police authorities at all, meaning they exist only in private collections. Civilian researchers have also preserved information after official investigations ended. Following the closure of Project Blue Book in 1969, organisations such as the Center for UFO Studies (CUFOS), APRO and NICAP continued collecting reports and conducting investigations. As a result, civilian databases provide chronological continuity beyond the lifespan of particular government programmes. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 However, these strengths come with limitations. Documentation standards vary considerably between organisations, duplicate reporting is common, and some underlying files remain in private custody or have been lost. A catalogue entry therefore does not guarantee that the associated evidence survives in complete form. Why Government Archives Remain Essential Official archives contribute qualities that civilian catalogues cannot fully replace. Government records usually include documented transfer histories, administrative metadata and legal responsibilities governing preservation and public access. Researchers can often establish: which office created a document; when it entered the archival system; whether records have been redacted or restricted; how the files relate to the wider administrative record. For Project Blue Book, the National Archives preserves not only case files but also administrative records, finding aids and associated documentation explaining how the records were transferred and organised after declassification. The archive's responsibility extends beyond access to maintaining the authenticity and evidential value of the records themselves. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 This emphasis on documented custody makes official archives especially valuable when assessing provenance or evaluating claims about altered or fabricated documents. When Researchers Need Both Kinds of Collection Historical UFO research is strongest when civilian catalogues and government archives are used together. A researcher might begin with UFOCAT to identify every known reference to a particular sighting across newspapers, private investigations and official reports. Those references can then be followed into archival collections to examine the original government records in their administrative context. Conversely, someone working from an official archive may discover that the government file represents only one part of the surviving documentary record. Civilian collections may contain witness interviews, later correspondence or investigative material that never entered official custody. Rather than answering the same questions, the two systems solve different research problems. Civilian catalogues maximise connections across dispersed evidence, while government archives maximise confidence in provenance, record custody and historical authenticity. Used together, they provide a fuller understanding of both the documentary history of individual UFO reports and the institutions that preserved them. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to UFOCAT or Government Archive What Does Each Preserve?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects By Edward J Ruppelt The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects is a book by"},{"title":"Database Rescue","url":"/database-rescue/","description":"UFOCAT survived through copied tapes, damaged backups and format conversions carried out across informal research and institutional networks.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / UFOCAT","level":3,"text":"Database Rescue How UFOCAT Survived Lost Tapes and Dead Software Database Rescue Database Rescue How UFOCAT Survived Lost Tapes and Dead Software UFOCAT survived through copied tapes, damaged backups and format conversions carried out across informal research and institutional networks. UFOCAT survived through copied tapes, damaged backups and format conversions carried out across informal research and institutional networks. UFO Report Databases historical comparative bounded topic event time window The mainframe catalogue and its storage crisis Reconstructing the damaged backup Moving the data into modern formats ufo report databases ufocat database rescue Introduction UFOCAT survived not because it was preserved by a single institution with a formal digital archive, but because civilian researchers repeatedly rescued it from obsolete computer systems and ageing storage media. During the 1980s and early 1990s, the catalogue faced a genuine risk of partial or permanent loss after it became too expensive for the Center for UFO Studies (CUFOS) to maintain on an IBM mainframe. What exists today is the result of a series of recoveries involving magnetic tapes, incomplete floppy disk copies and painstaking reconstruction rather than a seamless technological upgrade. That rescue effort illustrates a broader challenge faced by many early research databases: preserving the data often depended more on committed individuals than on institutional infrastructure. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... The Mainframe Catalogue and Its Storage Crisis When David Saunders transferred UFOCAT to CUFOS in 1976, the catalogue was maintained on an IBM mainframe computer at a nearby computing facility. During this period, updates were managed by Fred Merritt, while routine protection relied on magnetic tape backups—the standard archival technology for large computer systems of the era. Magnetic tape offered reliable long term storage but required specialised hardware, software and operators to read and maintain it. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... For a small civilian research organisation, those arrangements gradually became unsustainable. By 1982, the costs of operating and updating the database on a commercial mainframe exceeded CUFOS's resources. Rather than continue active maintenance, the catalogue was removed from everyday use and archived onto magnetic tapes. This decision preserved the information in one sense, but also trapped it inside a technological environment that was rapidly disappearing as personal computers replaced institutional mainframes. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... The storage problem was therefore not simply physical preservation. The tapes themselves survived, but accessing their contents increasingly required obsolete equipment and compatible software. Like many digital archives created during the 1970s and early 1980s, UFOCAT faced the risk that functioning readers would disappear before anyone could migrate the data into newer formats. [IBM] ibm.com Magnetic tape IBM Magnetic tape IBM... 6:20 First Ever UFO Database Reveals Strange Pattern (S21) Ancient Aliens History Channel: HISTORY · Views: 118.8K · Uploaded: September 2025 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube Reconstructing the Damaged Backup The decisive rescue began in 1990 when Donald Johnson obtained a copy of UFOCAT distributed across ten 3.5 inch floppy diskettes. Those diskettes had been created by John Derr of the U.S. Geological Survey, who had converted one of the original magnetic tape backups for his own research. However, the copy proved incomplete because the first portion of the tape could not be read during the conversion, leaving approximately the first 10,000 records missing. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New Ham... Instead of accepting the damaged copy, Johnson searched for additional surviving material. Mark Rodeghier, then Director of CUFOS, located an older magnetic tape backup that still contained the missing records. Johnson merged the two independent versions, using each to compensate for the weaknesses of the other. The result was a reconstructed catalogue that closely matched the database as it had existed when active maintenance ceased in 1982. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New Ham... This recovery demonstrates an important feature of early digital preservation. The successful reconstruction did not depend on a perfect master copy surviving intact. Instead, it relied on preserving multiple imperfect copies created at different times and for different purposes. Only by comparing and combining those versions could researchers restore a substantially complete catalogue. The episode also highlights how informal research networks became part of the preservation process. The recovery involved university researchers, CUFOS staff and a scientist at the U.S. Geological Survey, none of whom were operating within a dedicated digital preservation programme. Their overlapping personal copies ultimately became the archive's safety net. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New Ham... Moving the Data into Modern Formats Once the missing records had been restored, the greater challenge was preventing the same crisis from recurring. Rather than continue relying on mainframe era file structures, Johnson migrated UFOCAT into contemporary desktop database software, first using dBase IV and later Microsoft Access. These systems could run on ordinary personal computers, dramatically lowering maintenance costs while making routine updates practical once again. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... The migration involved more than copying records into a new program. Earlier versions of UFOCAT used many abbreviated, single character codes inherited from the constraints of older computing environments. During conversion, these fields were expanded into more descriptive names, new data fields were introduced and considerably greater emphasis was placed on documenting the bibliographic source for every catalogue entry. These changes made the database easier to understand while preserving compatibility with earlier records wherever possible. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... The catalogue continued evolving after the initial migration. Later releases were distributed in Microsoft Access format alongside spreadsheet versions, allowing researchers without specialist database software to examine the underlying records. By moving away from proprietary mainframe environments and towards widely supported desktop formats, the custodians substantially reduced the risk that UFOCAT would again become inaccessible solely because of obsolete hardware or software. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... Did the Air Force obstruct Roswell and UAP investigations? Reality Check Channel: NewsNation Open on YouTube The Center For UFO Studies Mark Rodeghier Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 3.4K · Uploaded: January 2025 Open on YouTube Why the Rescue Matters The survival of UFOCAT illustrates a broader lesson in digital preservation. Digital information does not remain accessible simply because storage media endure. Hardware becomes obsolete, software disappears, file formats change and undocumented encoding systems become difficult to interpret. Preservation therefore requires periodic migration rather than passive storage. [Harvard Gazette] news.harvard.edu Gazette Saving the digital record — Harvard Gazette Harvard GazetteSaving the digital record — Harvard Gazette... UFOCAT also shows how fragile early research databases could be outside large institutional archives. The catalogue persisted because multiple researchers retained independent copies, recognised their historical value and invested considerable effort in reconstructing damaged data. Without those overlapping backups and successive format conversions, one of the world's largest bibliographic catalogues of civilian UFO reports might have remained stranded on unreadable magnetic tapes or been lost altogether. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to How UFOCAT Survived Lost Tapes and Dead Software. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Theory and Craft of Digital Preservation By Trevor Owens A guide to managing data in the digital age."},{"title":"Duplicate Entries","url":"/duplicate-entries/","description":"UFOCAT may list one event several times because each record can represent a different source in the same documentary chain.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / UFOCAT","level":3,"text":"Duplicate Entries Why One UFO Sighting Can Have Many Records UFOCAT Duplicate Entries Duplicate Entries Why One UFO Sighting Can Have Many Records UFOCAT may list one event several times because each record can represent a different source in the same documentary chain. UFOCAT may list one event several times because each record can represent a different source in the same documentary chain. UFO Report Databases critique risk single dataset dataset evidence How incident blocks group related records What the X2 field says about source distance Why filtered totals still require human review ufo report databases ufocat duplicate entries Introduction UFOCAT is a reference catalogue rather than a census of unique UFO sightings. Its records are designed to preserve the documentary history of an incident by linking together investigation files, magazine articles, books, newspapers and later summaries that describe the same event. As a result, one sighting may legitimately generate several database entries. Counting every row as a separate incident therefore produces inflated totals and can misrepresent both the frequency and the quality of the underlying evidence. The Centre for UFO Studies (CUFOS), which maintains UFOCAT, explicitly warns that simple record counts overstate the number of cases and recommends using the catalogue as a guide to original documentation rather than as a standalone statistical dataset. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... How incident blocks group related records The key to understanding UFOCAT is that the basic unit of organisation is not always the individual database row but the group of linked records describing the same reported event. Each record receives its own permanent identifier, yet it can also be connected to a common primary record representing the best available source for that incident. The codebook explains that the Primary Record Number (PRN) points researchers towards the record judged to contain the most primary information. Filtering by that PRN retrieves the associated documentary chain rather than unrelated sightings. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New Ham... A single incident might therefore include: an original investigator's case file; a report in a civilian UFO journal; coverage in a local newspaper; a later chapter in a UFO book; a catalogue entry derived from one of those publications. These are separate bibliographic records because they preserve different versions or stages of transmission, not because they document different aerial events. This structure allows researchers to compare how an account evolved over time, identify where later authors relied on earlier publications, and locate the closest surviving source to the original witness testimony. CUFOS describes this as one of UFOCAT's principal purposes. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... The Center For UFO Studies Mark Rodeghier Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 3.4K · Uploaded: January 2025 Open on YouTube What the X2 field says about source distance One of the most important fields for avoiding duplicate counting is X2 , which the UFOCAT codebook describes as indicating the \"handedness\" of a record—that is, how close that record is to the original source. The coding distinguishes several levels of documentary separation: X2 = 0 : the selected primary record for the incident. X2 = 1 : an independent investigation of the same event that was not chosen as the primary record. X2 = 2 : a secondary source that refers to the primary report or another source. X2 = 3 : a source even further removed, relying only on secondary reporting. Rather than measuring witness reliability, X2 measures the relationship between records within the documentary chain. A magazine article copied from an investigator's report is not treated the same way as the investigation itself, even though both concern the identical sighting. Likewise, two independent investigations of one event may each receive separate records because they contain distinct documentary evidence while still referring to the same occurrence. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New Ham... This distinction is particularly valuable when tracing citation networks. Researchers can see whether a widely repeated story ultimately depends on one original investigation or whether multiple independent enquiries produced overlapping evidence. Why filtered totals still require human review The UFOCAT documentation notes that filtering for X2 = 0 provides the standard starting point for statistical work because it removes many duplicate references and leaves only the designated primary entries. CUFOS specifically recommends this approach when researchers need preliminary case counts without obvious over counting. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New Ham... Even so, filtering cannot eliminate every ambiguity. Several factors still require manual assessment: Independent investigations: More than one investigator may have documented the same event. Although the coding attempts to identify a preferred primary record, complex historical cases sometimes require judgement. Incomplete linkage: UFOCAT has grown over decades from numerous archives and catalogues. Some relationships between records may remain unidentified or uncertain, especially where original documentation has been lost. Changing source availability: Newly discovered files or revised historical research can clarify that apparently separate reports actually describe the same incident, or vice versa. Coding uncertainty: The codebook includes fields expressing the coder's confidence in relationships between linked records, recognising that some documentary connections are probable rather than certain. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New Ham... For these reasons, even an X2 filtered dataset should be regarded as a starting point for analysis rather than a definitive count of unique sightings. PASCAGOULA 73 Official Trailer (2025) Alien Abduction Documentary Film 4K Channel: MovieGasm‍․com Open on YouTube UFOs and life beyond with Don Schmitt FOX 10 Talks Channel: FOX 10 Phoenix Open on YouTube Why duplicate records improve, rather than weaken, the catalogue At first glance, duplicate looking entries may appear to be a flaw. In practice they are one of UFOCAT's defining research features. Because UFOCAT functions as a bibliographic reference system, preserving multiple records for the same incident enables researchers to: identify the earliest surviving documentation; compare later retellings with original investigations; determine whether a claim rests on firsthand or derivative reporting; locate additional archival material connected with an event. The design therefore prioritises documentary transparency over numerical simplicity. Problems arise only when users ignore the catalogue's relational structure and assume that every row represents an independent sighting. CUFOS repeatedly cautions against this interpretation, emphasising that UFOCAT is intended as a guide to original sources rather than a self contained statistical inventory of UFO events. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why One UFO Sighting Can Have Many Records. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The UFO Handbook: A Guide to Investigating, Evaluating, and R... By Allan Hendry An explanation of the usual reasons for confusing IFOs (identified flying objects) with UFOs and a survey of the tools used to investigat... See on Amazon Book The UFO Encyclopedia: The Phenomenon from the Beginning By Jerome Clark \"A comprehensive encyclopedia that describes the UFO phenomenon from the beginning and offers the first calibrated scientific proof for t... See on Amazon Book Data Quality: The Accuracy Dimension By Jack E. Olson Data Quality: The Accuracy Dimension is about assessing the quality of corporate data and improving its accuracy using the data profiling... See on Amazon Book Data Matching By Peter Christen Data matching (also known as record or data linkage, entity resolution, object identification, or field matching) is the task of identify... See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: The UFO : A to Investigating, Evaluating, and Reporting UFO Sightings books The UFO Encyclopedia: The Phenomenon from the Beginning Data Quality: The Accuracy Dimension As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. eBay marketplace picks Marketplace Samples Live tested eBay searches with available results related to this page. Shop location Using USA USA USA UK Canada Australia Ireland USA UK"},{"title":"Access Levels","url":"/access-levels/","description":"UFOCAT distinguishes open files, restricted witness records and confidential cases, shaping what researchers can verify for themselves.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / UFOCAT","level":3,"text":"Access Levels Which UFOCAT Sources Can Researchers Actually Check? Access Levels Access Levels Which UFOCAT Sources Can Researchers Actually Check? UFOCAT distinguishes open files, restricted witness records and confidential cases, shaping what researchers can verify for themselves. UFOCAT distinguishes open files, restricted witness records and confidential cases, shaping what researchers can verify for themselves. UFO Report Databases governance single policy policy intervention Published, private and organisational source types Restricted witness details and confidential cases How access limits affect verification ufo report databases ufocat access levels Introduction UFOCAT is more than a catalogue of reported UFO events: it is also a catalogue of sources, and those sources are not equally accessible. One of the database's most important but often overlooked features is that it distinguishes between published material, privately held investigator files and records subject to confidentiality restrictions. This means that a UFOCAT entry tells researchers not only where information originated, but also whether they are likely to inspect the underlying evidence themselves or whether access depends on the permission of an individual investigator or organisation. The result is that two records describing similar incidents may differ greatly in how independently verifiable they are. The UFOCAT codebook explicitly treats these access distinctions as part of the database's design rather than an afterthought. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New HamNovember 7, 2025... Published: November 7, 2025 Which UFOCAT Sources Can Researchers Actually Check? The practical value of a UFOCAT source reference depends on what type of material it identifies. The catalogue separates sources according to their origin because each category carries different expectations about availability, completeness and independent verification. Published, private and organisational source types Published sources are generally the easiest to verify. These include newspapers, magazines, books, journals and organisational newsletters that can often be located in libraries, archives or digital collections. When a UFOCAT record cites published material, another researcher can usually inspect the same document and compare interpretations directly. Private investigator files occupy a different category. These may include: witness interview notes; correspondence; sketches and photographs; field investigation reports; unpublished case summaries. Such material may contain substantially more detail than a published article, but it often remains in the custody of the investigator or the organisation that collected it. Access therefore depends on whether the owner is willing to share the records. UFOCAT preserves references to these files even when they are not publicly available because they document that additional evidence exists, although not necessarily that every researcher can inspect it. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New HamNovember 7, 2025... Published: November 7, 2025 Organisational archives occupy an intermediate position. Collections maintained by groups such as the Center for UFO Studies (CUFOS) may preserve decades of historical investigation files, yet access can still be governed by archive policies, donor agreements or privacy commitments rather than open public inspection. Consequently, a catalogue reference may identify a real archival source without implying unrestricted availability. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New HamNovember 7, 2025... Published: November 7, 2025 1:06:47 The Fifth Witness: UFO Crash Retrievals Uncovered Channel: J. Horton Films · Views: 104.8K · Uploaded: November 2025 · Length: 1 hour 6 minutes Open on YouTube Restricted witness details and confidential cases One distinctive feature of the UFOCAT codebook is its formal treatment of confidentiality. Rather than simply omitting sensitive material, the database assigns availability levels that specify how much information may be released. The codebook explains that contributors can choose different \"levels of availability\" for unpublished material. Published records remain freely available, while unpublished records may be restricted in several ways, beginning with the removal of witness names and extending to broader limitations on access. This system was designed to respect promises made to witnesses and investigators who supplied their files on the understanding that sensitive information would not automatically become public. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New HamNovember 7, 2025... Published: November 7, 2025 In practice, this means a researcher may encounter entries where: witness names have been replaced by initials; identifying personal information has been removed; the source file exists but cannot be freely copied; permission from the contributing investigator is required before additional documentation can be examined. The codebook also notes that contributors concerned about inappropriate use of their material could effectively classify their submissions by choosing more restrictive availability levels. At the same time, CUFOS reserved the right to reject submissions carrying excessive restrictions, recognising that a research database loses value if its contents become largely inaccessible. According to the codebook, fewer than ten per cent of primary records imposed restrictions beyond withholding witness names, with the intention of reducing that proportion over time. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New HamNovember 7, 2025... Published: November 7, 2025 Did the Air Force obstruct Roswell and UAP investigations? Reality Check Channel: NewsNation Open on YouTube How Access Limits Affect Verification These access categories have important consequences for anyone evaluating UFO evidence. First, a catalogue citation does not guarantee that the underlying documentation can be independently inspected. A published newspaper article may be available immediately, whereas an investigator's original interview notes may remain inaccessible decades after the case entered UFOCAT. Second, access restrictions affect reproducibility. Historical research relies on other investigators being able to examine the same documents and judge whether conclusions are justified. When only summaries or secondary accounts are publicly available, independent verification becomes more difficult because the original interview records, correspondence or photographs cannot necessarily be reviewed. Third, differences in accessibility should not automatically be interpreted as differences in credibility. Confidentiality often reflects ethical obligations rather than evidential weakness. Witnesses may have requested anonymity, investigators may have promised privacy, or organisations may be preserving personal records under archival agreements. The existence of restricted material therefore says more about record governance than about whether a reported event was accurately described. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New HamNovember 7, 2025... Published: November 7, 2025 For this reason, experienced researchers distinguish between three separate questions: Does the catalogue identify a source? UFOCAT usually answers this clearly. Can the original source be examined today? The answer depends on its availability category. Can independent researchers verify the claims directly? That depends on whether the cited material is publicly accessible or remains under private control. Understanding these distinctions helps prevent a common misunderstanding of UFOCAT. The database is not a repository in which every cited document can be opened and inspected. Instead, it is a structured guide showing where information originated, while also signalling the practical limits that privacy, ownership and archival policy place on independent examination. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New HamNovember 7, 2025... Published: November 7, 2025 The Center For UFO Studies Mark Rodeghier Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 3.4K · Uploaded: January 2025 Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Which UFOCAT Sources Can Researchers Actually Check?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Craft of Research By Wayne C. Booth, Gregory G. Colomb et al. Rating: 4.3/5 from 6 Google Books ratings Since 1995, students, researchers, and professionals have turned to The Craft of Research for clear and helpful guidance on how to conduc... See on Amazon Book Archives: Principles and Practices By Laura A. Millar This new and extensively revised second edition offers an international perspective on archives management,"},{"title":"Source Trails","url":"/source-trails/","description":"Direct and indirect source fields help readers follow a published UFO story toward earlier interviews, witness forms and investigator files.","breadcrumb":"UFO Report Databases / UFOCAT","level":3,"text":"Source Trails Can UFOCAT Lead Back to the Original Report? Source Trails Source Trails Can UFOCAT Lead Back to the Original Report? Direct and indirect source fields help readers follow a published UFO story toward earlier interviews, witness forms and investigator files. Direct and indirect source fields help readers follow a published UFO story toward earlier interviews, witness forms and investigator files. UFO Report Databases implementation single mechanism mechanism Direct sources versus credited sources Reading page, date and file location fields Building a source trail from books to witnesses ufo report databases ufocat source trails Introduction UFOCAT is most useful when treated as a guide back to earlier evidence rather than as the final record of a UFO sighting. Instead of asking only what happened , the catalogue also records where this version of the story came from . By preserving both direct source information and credited or indirect source information, UFOCAT allows researchers to reconstruct the path a report followed as it moved from witness statements to investigation files, magazines, books and later catalogues. That source trail often reveals how an account evolved, which details were added or omitted, and whether later writers relied on original documentation or merely repeated earlier publications. CUFOS describes UFOCAT as a bibliographic reference designed to lead researchers towards the underlying records rather than replace them. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... Can UFOCAT Lead Back to the Original Report? In many cases, yes—but only by following several linked references rather than expecting a single definitive record. A typical UFO report may have been investigated by a civilian organisation, summarised in a newsletter, expanded in a book years later and eventually entered into several catalogues. UFOCAT often contains separate entries representing these different appearances. Rather than treating them as independent sightings, the database records enough source information to help identify that they refer to the same event. This backwards approach differs from a conventional incident database. Instead of stopping at the latest published version, researchers can work from a modern compilation back through progressively earlier documentation until they reach the closest surviving record to the witness or field investigator. Where original files still exist, this may lead to interview notes, witness questionnaires, correspondence or investigator reports. Where the original material has been lost, the trail at least shows the earliest surviving publication and the chain of authors who relied upon it. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies UFOCAT Center for UFO StudiesUFOCAT Center for UFO Studies... 6:20 First Ever UFO Database Reveals Strange Pattern (S21) Ancient Aliens History Channel: HISTORY · Views: 118.8K · Uploaded: September 2025 · Length: 6 minutes Open on YouTube Direct Sources versus Credited Sources The mechanism depends largely on two complementary source fields. The direct source identifies the document or archive from which the UFOCAT record itself was entered. This is the publication, investigation file or collection that supplied the catalogue entry. The indirect source (stored in the ISOURCE field) records the source credited by that publication's author. In other words, it answers the question: where did the author say this information came from? According to the UFOCAT codebook, this field serves two principal purposes: helping group records that refer to the same event; identifying primary entries and indicating how close they are to original witness material. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies THE USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New Ham The codebook also specifies conventions for recording different kinds of credited sources. For example: an investigator's written field report receives a special priority code; a completed witness report form is distinguished from a later narrative; witness letters and unstructured interviews receive separate indicators; newspaper accounts are marked differently from formal investigation records. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies THE USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New Ham These distinctions matter because two records may describe the same incident while resting on very different evidential foundations. A magazine article based on a newspaper story carries a longer chain of transmission than an investigator's original interview notes, even if both ultimately describe the same sighting. Reading Page, Date and File Location Fields Source tracing rarely depends on one field alone. Researchers normally combine several pieces of bibliographic information. Useful fields typically include: Source abbreviation , identifying the publication or archive. Author information , helping distinguish between multiple works by the same researcher. Publication date , showing whether an account is contemporary or retrospective. Volume and page references , allowing the original printed discussion to be located. File or collection identifiers , where available, pointing towards archived investigation material rather than published summaries. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies THE USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New Ham Taken together, these details allow researchers to reconstruct a citation much as a historian would follow footnotes through successive publications. For example, a book published in the 1990s may cite an article from a UFO journal published in the 1970s, which itself credits an investigator's report prepared shortly after the event. UFOCAT's fields make that chain visible, allowing the reader to move step by step towards the earliest surviving documentation instead of accepting the newest summary at face value. UFO Witness Now Streaming on discovery+ Channel: discovery plus Open on YouTube The Center For UFO Studies Mark Rodeghier Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 3.4K · Uploaded: January 2025 Open on YouTube Building a Source Trail from Books to Witnesses A typical reconstruction follows a sequence rather than a single lookup. 1. Begin with the UFOCAT entry describing the version of interest. 2. Identify its direct source—the publication or archive used to create the catalogue record. 3. Examine the credited indirect source recorded by the author. 4. Locate that earlier publication or investigation file. 5. Repeat the process until reaching the earliest available witness document or investigator record. Center for UFO Studies Not every chain reaches an original witness interview. Older investigations may have been discarded, private files may remain inaccessible, or publications may simply cite \"an investigator\" without precise references. The codebook explicitly notes that indirect source identifiers are not always unique because authors sometimes provide incomplete citations. Even so, recording imperfect references is preferable to losing the chain altogether, since it preserves clues for later archival research. Center for UFO Studies Why Source Trails Matter Following a report backwards often changes how a case is interpreted. A dramatic claim appearing in a later book may prove to be a condensed version of a much longer interview in which uncertainty, alternative explanations or contradictory witness statements were discussed. Conversely, a brief newspaper article may lead to a detailed investigator's file containing sketches, timings and follow up interviews that were never published. The source trail also helps distinguish between genuinely independent evidence and repeated retellings. If several books all cite the same investigation file, they do not represent multiple independent confirmations of the event. Instead, they document the influence of one original source. Recognising this prevents researchers from mistakenly treating repeated publications as repeated observations. For this reason, UFOCAT functions less as a catalogue of isolated sightings than as a map of documentary relationships. Its greatest strength is not deciding which reports are true, but showing how published accounts connect to earlier records and helping researchers locate the closest surviving evidence to the original witness testimony. Center for UFO Studies Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Can UFOCAT Lead Back to the Original Report?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Craft of Research By Wayne C. Booth, Gregory G. Colomb et al. Rating: 4.3/5 from 6 Google Books ratings Since 1995, students, researchers, and professionals have turned to The Craft of Research for clear and helpful guidance on how to conduc... See on Amazon Book The Chicago Guide to Fact Checking By Brooke Borel Why we fact check What we fact"}]};
